          "name": "Jan Vicente",
          "search_text": "jan vicente divergence of the cryptobenthic sponge community across the hawaiian geologic gradient cryptobenthic sponge communities are dominant components of coral reefs that play an integral role in sustaining biodiversity and maintaining reef resilience. sponges of the hawaiian archipelago are mainly cryptobenthic and comprise over 200 species. previous sponge surveys using autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms) focused on kāneʻohe bay increased the known diversity of sponges for the entire archipelago by 3-fold from a single patch reef. seeing the success of arms at capturing the hidden diversity of cryptobenthic sponges, we used a similar approach to test whether the geologic gradient between the main hawaiian islands (mhi) and northwestern hawaiian islands (nwhi) influence the community composition of sponge species. we used 28s metabarcoding primers on homogenized arms scrapings from the sessile invertebrate community from 25 sites in the two island regions. the 28s primer showed sponges to be abundant (25% relative abundance) and rich (125 operational taxonomic units) constituents of the total metazoan community. differences in community composition were evident between the two island regions with the mhi having significantly higher diversity and abundance of sponges than the nwhi. sponge orders dictyoceratida and tetractinellida were mainly responsible for these differences. overall, this study supports the hypothesis that complex island topography exhibited by the mhi provide more nutrient input from rivers, and groundwater discharge that feed sponges, where sponges from reefs in low lying atolls of the nwhi are devoid of these terrestrially derived nutrient sources resulting in less rich and dense sponge communities within the cryptobiota. anthropogenic sources of nutrients derived from agriculturally modified landscapes in the mhi but absent in the nwhi might also explain these differences. our findings could mirror similar biodiversity patterns from other island archipelagos of oceania with similar geologic histories and underscore the need for management at regional scales.",
