      "search_text": "commonwealth scientific and industrial research organisation christopher doropoulos javier porobic christopher doropoulos scaling the footprint of larval based coral restoration accelerating coral reef restoration at ecologically meaningful scales remains a global challenge. propagule-based approaches scale well in plant systems, but larval-based coral restoration has been difficult, particularly for delivering competent larvae to reefs. across a series of trials, we develop and test techniques to collect, culture, and deploy coral larvae at ecologically relevant scales. demographic modelling indicates that harvesting wild coral spawn slicks, culturing them to release competent larvae offers a low-impact pathway to produce and translocate genetically diverse larvae over large areas. collection scales to >30 million embryos per pass using active pumping onto a small barge, and hundreds of millions via repeated passes with hand-held skimmers. culturing in pools yields hundreds of millions of competent larvae. to deploy larvae across large spatial footprints, we developed the larval seedbox: a modular, passive delivery system that enables delayed release of competent larvae near the benthos, increasing substrate encounter rates without net enclosures that previously limited seeding to small areas. an initial trial at a clear-water mid-shelf reef delivered ~14 million larvae using five seedboxes across ~2 ha. settlement occurred on 85% of tiles with densities 9–56x above background, with spatial modelling estimating enhancement over ~3,000 m² via local retention and tidally driven dispersal. a second trial at a turbid inshore reef delivered ~6 million larvae using ten seedboxes across ~1.6 ha of reef. preliminary analysis shows settlement was low throughout the bay (26% of tiles with 1 or more spat), hotspots of settlement remained high (>100x above background) but were largely restricted to tiles adjacent to seedboxes. knowledge gaps remain on optimal seedboxe placement depending on reef topography, fine-scale currents, and benthic predators or competitors. seedboxes are simple, modular, and readily transferable to operators, offering a practical tool for scaling coral larval restoration over large, degraded areas. javier porobic one matrix is not enough: exploring kernel approaches to variability in reef connectivity. modelling connectivity between habitats is crucial for comprehending the dynamics of spatially structured ecosystems, particularly those dominated by sessile species such as corals. the level of connectivity between fragmented habitats is a key input to ecological models and is fundamental for predicting population dynamics and, in some cases, for ecosystem management. this study focuses on the great barrier reef (gbr), a highly spatially structured ecosystem consisting of almost 4000 coral reefs that face increasing cumulative pressures from tropical cyclones, heat waves, and predation by crown-of-thorns starfish (cots). while hydrodynamic models have been combined with particle-tracking techniques to derive reef connectivity matrices for the gbr, previous approaches have been limited by the snapshot effect of using single connectivity matrices derived from a limited number of spawning events, thereby underestimating the range of potential connections and their temporal variability. we addressed this issue by deriving connectivity kernels that replace explicit pairwise connectivity matrices with a more general description based on probability density functions. our approach explicitly accounts for differences in release times, incorporates bootstrapping techniques that sample from the temporal distribution of spawning events for each coral family, and evaluates how variations in larval competence and decay rates influence connectivity estimates. by integrating these temporal and biological sources of variability, we quantify their combined effect on final connectivity patterns and avoid the snapshot effect inherent in single-matrix approaches. connectivity kernels not only reveal how connection strength and patterns change across the reef ecosystem, but also offer a wide range of realistic connection combinations to support ensemble ecosystem modelling. capturing the full range of potential connectivity variability is becoming increasingly crucial as ecosystem models are used to provide future projections of reef health and evaluate the efficacy of proposed restoration and adaptation strategies.",
