      "search_text": "cooperative institute for marine and atmospheric studies ashley rossin ashley rossin leveraging sexual reproduction of corals from urbanized environments for conservation and restoration of natural reef populations caribbean coral populations have experienced substantial declines in coral cover and associated reef infrastructure, particularly among species that are susceptible to stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) and thermal stress. resilient populations of ecologically important species, however, have been found thriving in environmentally marginal, urbanized environments such as commercial ports in southeastern florida which experience highly variable water quality, temperature extremes, and periodic acidification. these persistent ‘urban coral’ populations may serve as sources of stress-tolerant genotypes for the restoration of degraded reefs. we assessed genetic connectivity of six species of corals spanning urbanized and reef habitats using 2brad snp genotyping, finding evidence of distinct genetic lineages and inbreeding despite higher genetic diversity in urban corals. next, we investigated reproductive capacity, fecundity, and spawning periodicity with histology and in situ spawning observations, determining that urban corals produce and release viable gametes during documented spawning seasons, but that asynchronous game release between urban and reef sites is likely limiting natural gene flow among populations. finally, we evaluated the efficacy of incorporating urban corals of opportunity into asexual and sexual propagation pipelines with restoration partners, producing >1,500 urban x reef crosses which are being gene-banked, propagated for outplanting, and screened for heritability of resilience characteristics using lab-based stress-testing experiments. by quantitatively identifying hotspots of genetic diversity and self-sustaining populations in urbanized environments, we suggest that urban coral populations represent an untapped reservoir of resilient genotypes for conservation and restoration despite local and global stressors. as anthropogenic impacts continue to cause declines in natural reef populations, corals in urbanized habitats can be a source of resilient genotypes promoting genetic adaptation to a changing climate.",
