          "search_text": "layla iijima nutrition as a boost to improve coral thermotolerance human-induced climate change is intensifying marine heatwave events, triggering the breakdown of symbiosis that leads to coral bleaching. bleaching might be lethal for corals, as photosynthate transfer represents the main energy source for the majority of scleractinian corals. however, several laboratory studies have shown that corals benefiting from an external food supply (heterotrophy) are more resistant to bleaching or recover better. still, the influence of heterotrophy on coral thermotolerance remains poorly studied in the field settings. in new caledonia, areas rich in plankton and organic matter (mesotrophic reef) provide natural laboratories for studying the role of nutrition in coral thermotolerance. to test the hypothesis that food availability in the water column improve physiological performances of corals thriving in mesotrophic reefs, we transplanted coral colonies of acropora tenuis from oligo- to mesotrophic reefs. after five months of transplantation, skeletal growth was measured and was generally similar among transplantation treatments. acute heat stress assay (cbass) was also conducted on a. tenuis, which consists of four temperature regimes (28, 30, 33, and 36℃), with the maximum temperature maintained for three hours. one hour post-stress at 36°c, mesotrophic coral colonies exhibited the highest maximum photosynthetic efficiencies (fv/fm ≒ 0.55). this trend persisted 12 hours after the stress, with oligotrophic corals exhibiting severe damage to the photosystem (fv/fm < 0.2). our results suggest that higher exogenous food availability enable corals to mitigate photophysiological damage during thermal stress. ongoing physiological and isotopic analyses will enable us to further elucidate acclimatization mechanisms to mesotrophic reefs and their role in coral fitness.",
