      "search_text": "maritime and marine science centre of excellence, pattimura university cilun djakiman cilun djakiman does habitat heterogeneity predict high ecological diversity? a comparative analysis from indonesia and the philippines the habitat heterogeneity hypothesis suggests that complex reef-scale habitat arrangements support a multitude of ecological niches and thus high species diversity, contributing to highly adaptive and resilient ecological communities. global-scale satellite derived metrics may provide practical and scalable tools to identify such areas of high biodiversity, but to date this relationship remains unclear. these findings are relevant for informing reef management efforts that support small-scale reef fishers in indonesia and the philippines. we test this relationship in the biodiversity hotspot of the coral triangle: indonesia and the philippines. using fine resolution (5m x 5m) benthic and geomorphic maps, we derived habitat heterogeneity estimates for  60 sites across four regions. we used habitat heterogeneity to predict the diversity of reef fishes, invertebrates, algae,coral benthos and reef fish biomass collected through underwater visual census. we ran generalized linear mixed models (glmm) using r to examine the relationship between habitat heterogeneity and our ecological response variables. our results show a significant positive relationship between satellite-derived habitat heterogeneity metrics and fish ecological metrics in indonesia. in contrast, relationships were unclear for sites in the philippines. preliminary findings suggest that philippine sites are generally algae-dominated, whereas indonesian sites have higher coral cover. high coral cover is correlated with stronger biodiversity and habitat heterogeneity relationships. model refinement, along with the inclusion of relevant environmental data layers, enhanced the characterization of site-level differences and strengthened our ability to pinpoint areas that support high biodiversity and ecological resilience. overall, these findings indicate that remotely sensed habitat heterogeneity metrics may be able to serve as proxies for reef biodiversity in conservation decision making and marine spatial planning, with the possibility of broader geographical application.",
