          "name": "Shannon Ruseborn",
          "search_text": "shannon ruseborn empowering community-based restoration in the pacific islands reeframe: innovative restoration of hawaiian reefs via permanent undersea coral nurseries effective, scalable restoration requires sustained local capacity and community stewardship. the national oceanic and atmospheric administration (noaa) restoration center (rc), a us federal resource agency, plays a critical, behind-the-scenes role in empowering local partners and community groups to actively plan and implement coral reef restoration efforts across the us pacific islands (american samoa, cnmi, guam, and hawaii).\r\n\r\nthis presentation outlines noaa rc’s strategy for building sustainable community-based restoration capacity in this vast and culturally diverse region. our approach centers on providing essential resources and technical expertise while prioritizing local autonomy and indigenous knowledge. key mechanisms include: (1) funding support: implementing grant competitions designed to prioritize projects with strong community involvement and locally-driven objectives, thereby providing the necessary financial infrastructure. (2) training and workshops: developing and implementing specialized regional workshops focused on critical restoration skills, such as restoration planning, rubble stabilization, structure-from-motion (sfm), and sexual propagation. these training sessions ensure partners have access to cutting-edge science and best practices. (3) technical expertise and assistance: providing direct technical support and co-developing restoration plans, ensuring projects are scientifically robust, scalable, and tailored to local conditions.\r\n\r\nby focusing on facilitating local capacity and supporting partners in implementing restoration, the noaa restoration center demonstrates how a federal agency can serve as a vital catalyst, empowering pacific island communities to become the primary stewards and implementers of successful and enduring coral reef restoration. this model is crucial for accelerating recovery and fostering long-term resilience in the face of a changing climate. waikīkī is the most iconic beach in hawai’i, yet is one of the most degraded reefs in the islands, with low coral cover, structural complexity, and fish abundance. reeframe is a collaborative coral restoration project to foster coral growth and improve reef habitat by using innovative concrete technology to create naturally-shaped structures providing ideal habitat for corals, fishes, and other reef life. the permanent structures will be placed about 0.75 mi off waikīkī beach on sand flats ranging from 40 to 60 feet deep. corals of opportunity (colonies dislodged by disturbance) will be attached to the structures as nurseries for outplanting, and eventually natural succession will result in reeframe structures becoming de facto coral reefs.  a preliminary feasibility study, initiated in 2016, has already demonstrated the effectiveness of concrete structures in this location. the test structures have successfully attracted naturally settling coral larvae, fish, and invertebrates. furthermore, both naturally settled colonies and corals of opportunity transplanted onto the test structures have shown high survival and growth. this success provides confidence that a scaled-up nursery will be effective for restoring waikīkī reefs. the restoration of this environmentally and culturally significant ‘āina is central to reeframe’s mission, guided by the hawaiian proverb, e hāpai wa‘a kākou – if we all help to lift and carry the canoe, we can move it forward. reeframe is a three-year project led by conservation international, the university of hawai‘i at mānoa, the hawai‘i division of aquatic resources, natrx, oceanit, climbhi, and pacific consulting services inc., and noaa’s office of habitat conservation.",
