      "search_text": "state of hawai'i division of aquatic resources christina jayne christina jayne a decade of development: lessons from innovative land-based restoration practices for hawai‘i’s unique coral reef challenges as the most geographically isolated island chain in the world, with urbanized coastlines and nearshore fringing reefs, hawaiian coral reefs are extremely unique and face distinct challenges for coral restoration. unlike most other reef ecosystems in the pacific and around the globe, hawaiian reefs exhibit low species diversity, high endemism, and lack dominance by fast-growing branching corals. these characteristics have required hawai‘i-based restoration practitioners to create novel techniques for working with slow-growing massive corals, rare species, and restoration in environments with heavy tourism and human use. to address these hurdles, the state of hawai‘i division of aquatic resources (dar) established the hawai‘i coral restoration nursery (hcrn) in 2015 to pioneer multiple innovative approaches to expedite the growth of hawaiian coral species to large sizes in a significantly shorter duration compared to natural growth rates. \r\n\r\nas the first land-based coral restoration nursery in hawai'i, the hcrn initiated a combination of microfragmentation and advanced aquarium techniques to produce large corals for restoring damaged and degraded reef sites and enhancing natural ecosystem services and functions. the 1-2 year process produces 40-cm and 1-m coral colonies which represent 20-100+ years of natural growth. with over a decade of developments, dar will share lessons learned, experimental results, and novel techniques from the nursery’s process and history. from microfragment sizing and species-specific light regimes to site-specific acclimation techniques, the hcrn has tested and proven a suite of methods to improve coral growth and outplant success. these protocols were developed through small scale experiments, trial and error, and collaborations with researchers and practitioners. dar aims to share lessons learned from the evolution of the hcrn to support information sharing, best practices, and collaboration between coral restoration practitioners across our oceans.",
