      "search_text": "the nature conservancy - us virgin islands caitlin lustic caitlin lustic establishing the ideal rearing conditions for early-stage recruits of diploria labryinthiformis: does an extended lab-rearing period post-settlement translate to increased resilience post-outplanting? solving the operational challenge of scaling coral restoration: case study in the united states virgin islands assisted sexual reproduction is an effective strategy for enhancing fertilization rates in coral populations with reduced density and for increasing genetic diversity, improving adaptability to environmental stressors such as bleaching. since 2020, the nature conservancy’s coral innovation hub in the u.s. virgin islands has implemented this approach, primarily rearing recruits in field-based nurseries until 2024. following the 2023 mass bleaching event and in anticipation of further thermal stress in 2024, a subset of diploria labyrinthiformis recruits settled in may 2023 at our hub was transferred to a land-based nursery for protection. after nearly one year, these lab-reared recruits exhibited substantially greater growth compared to counterparts maintained in field nurseries. to assess whether this growth advantage translates to improved survival and resilience post-outplanting, both treatment groups (field-reared and lab-reared) were transplanted to the reef and monitored for six months. this presentation will review comparative results on growth, retention, and health metrics, informing optimal rearing strategies for post-outplant success. tropical coral reefs have suffered devastating damage in recent decades. pollution, overfishing, disease, and ever-increasing impacts from climate change have all led to substantial losses in live coral cover and diversity. despite these severe declines, local reefs may still have the capacity to recover, and coral restoration has become a common practice in reef conservation. considering the rate and extent of coral reef decline, the scale of restoration needs to match the scale of the impacts. however, conducting reef restoration over large areas can be prohibitively expensive and operationally challenging, making coral restoration at the ecosystem level particularly difficult. governments globally have begun to recognize this challenge, with funding now being directed towards coral reef conservation. leveraging this funding opportunity, the nature conservancy in the virgin islands developed a program aimed at scaling coral production and restoration through our coral innovation hub on the island of st. croix. through innovative developments to overcome operational challenges of large-scale coral reef restoration, our program is conducting this work across more than 90 acres of reef habitat, outplanting ~10,000 corals annually and generating millions of genetically diverse coral larvae through assisted sexual reproduction. in this presentation we will describe the methods implemented to tackle the challenges of scaling restoration, technological successes such as monitoring large areas using structure-from-motion technology, and lessons learned for building thermal resilience into coral restoration work.",
