      "search_text": "university of amsterdam petra visser petra visser benthic cyanobacterial mats on curaçaon reefs: abundance, nitrogen fixation, and toxicity on changing reefs benthic cyanobacterial mats (bcms) are expanding across caribbean reefs as coral mortality from bleaching and sctld creates extensive bare substrate rapidly colonised by these groups. these mats are highly active microbial assemblages that can strongly influence reef biogeochemistry through nitrogen fixation, organic matter production, and the release of bioactive secondary metabolites. yet, their ecological roles and potential risks remain poorly quantified.\r\nwe surveyed 28 sites along the leeward coast of curaçao (10 m depth) to assess bcm distribution, biomass, nitrogen fixation, and toxicity. average bcm cover was 10%, dominated by red mats. biomass analyses of the most abundant mat types enabled calculation of ash-free dry weight per m² of reef. nitrogen fixation rates were quantified using the acetylene-reduction assay (per unit dry weight) and extrapolated to reef-scale estimates. culturing of dominant cyanobacterial strains revealed substantial variability in n-fixation potential, identifying species most relevant to the reef nitrogen budget.\r\nmost bcm morphotypes exhibited toxic potential in thamnocephalus platyurus bioassays, with lc₅₀ values indicating the presence of bioactive or toxic secondary metabolites. untargeted metabolomic analyses (lc-ms/ms) confirmed diverse chemical profiles and candidate compounds likely contributing to toxicity.\r\ntogether, these results show that bcms on curaçao’s reefs are abundant, metabolically active, and often toxic. their capacity for substantial nitrogen input, combined with secondary metabolite production, suggests that bcms already play a significant role in shaping reef biogeochemistry and may influence benthic community dynamics. understanding the ecological functions and impacts of bcms is essential for predicting future reef trajectories in an era of coral decline.",
