          "name": "Matlyn Ua",
          "search_text": "matlyn ua quantifying direct and indirect effects of cryptic macrofauna on hawaiian coral calcification rates in a heating ocean coral reefs are complex ecosystems that promote biodiversity, provide shoreline protection and are a cultural foundation in hawaiʻi, where the coral polyp is respected as an akua (deity). recent work highlights the important role that cryptic meiofauna serve in promoting coral growth through species interactions (e.g., sediment removal by the common guard crab trapezia intermedia). however, the full understanding of cryptic meiofauna (e.g.,  brittle stars) effects remains understudied. the objective of this project is to quantify the effects of both direct and indirect interactions between  corals and common coral-associated invertebrates to assess how known interactions modulate coral growth rates. corals (pocillopora meandrina and porites lobata), and invertebrates (trapezia intermedia and ophicoma erianceus) were collected from auwili, hawaiʻi and incubated in a fully orthogonal experiment with meiofaunal species (t. intermedia and o. erinaceus), association (direct and indirect interaction) as fixed factors. after a 7 week incubation, modulation of calcification was assessed. guard crabs are expected to have a stronger impact on branching corals and brittle stars on mounding corals, with direct interaction. similarly, with indirect interaction, guard crabs are expected to have stronger impacts on branching corals and brittles on mounding corals, though in a smaller magnitude. by understanding the intricacies of species interactions on coral reefs, a better framework for adequate conservation efforts can be enacted to protect coral reefs.",
