      "distance_km": 16285.0,
      "search_text": "v-corals dr dor shefy dor shefy chimerism in a model coral species: from ecology perspectives to active restoration chimerism is increasingly recognized as a major contributor to intraspecific genetic-variation in reef-building corals. stylophora pistillata, a brooding branching species from the red-sea, acts as a model for exploring the ontogeny and ecological perspectives of allorecognition. here, we synthesize a suite of experimental and field-based studies that illuminate the mechanisms and consequences of chimerism in this species and its application in active restoration. vital staining of coral-larvae and kinship-tracking experiments revealed that s. pistillata planulae consistently settle into dense aggregations regardless of relatedness, forming more aggregates than expected by random settlement. similar clustering was observed in natural juvenile populations, confirming that such interactions are ecologically common. during an early alloimmune window of <6 months, these contacts lead to either fusion or structured rejection boundaries, with the probability of fusion decreasing as the temporal distance from first contact increases, reflecting the combined influence of age and kinship on allogeneic outcomes in forming chimeric or rejecting entities. long-term in situ monitoring observations demonstrated that young chimeras outperform genetically homogeneous colonies in size and survivorship, where chimeric entities achieved larger ecological volume, initiated distinct early branching patterns, and exhibited significantly higher survival during the first two years. these results suggest a fusion effect on overall performance, supported by transcriptomic analysis showing that chimeric colonies frontload stress-associated pathways that promote greater tolerance during early development, further preparing them for environmental change. together, these findings help explain why early allogeneic interactions may be less physiologically costly than previously assumed. considered together, the results position s. pistillata as a powerful model system and support the idea that chimerism is a natural strategy for resilience and phenotypic variability among corals. integrating chimerism into restoration activities may improve adaptive capabilities and enhance long-term restoration outcomes in a rapidly changing ocean.",
