          "name": "Dr Kelly Gomez-Campo",
          "search_text": "kelly johanna gomez campo validation of optical traits measurements using diving-pam-ii mini-spec reflectance geometries thermal history associated with coral recovery trajectories via proteostasis capacity after heat-stress light absorption capacity and efficiency are fundamental traits governing coral performance, photoacclimation potential, and susceptibility to environmental change. these optical properties emerge from multiple interacting features such as polyp/corallite morphology, tissue pigmentation, symbiodiniaceae cell density/pigmentation, and skeletal light scattering. absorptance (a) determinations in multicellular, highly scattering tissues such as corals is difficult, as it requires full control of scattering sources to prevent over-estimations. historically, integrating spheres were the only reliable solution for these determinations. subsequent developments, including miniature spectrophotometers (e.g. ocean optics usb2000/4000/flame), enabled more flexible optical configurations for measuring both transmission and reflectance (r) in laboratory settings. the underwater chlorophyll fluorometer diving-pam-ii is equipped with a miniature spectrometer (mini-spec), calibrated to measure reflectance directly in mesocosms or in situ. the system uses a halogen lamp and a dedicated reflection optic (spec/r) to record spectra normalized to a diffuse reflectance standard, providing wavelength-resolved reflectance measurements across the par range. however, its ability to produce accurate, physiologically meaningful optical descriptors has not been validated against established more robust methodologies. here, we evaluate whether mini-spec reflectance can yield reliable optical determinations. we measured reflectance of five coral species and a crustose coralline alga (cca colonized aquarium tiles) using both, a standard miniature spectrophotometer (flame-t-uv–vis, ocean optics inc.) with fixed validated optical geometry, and the mini-spec. for each sample, we quantified surface area, pigment content, symbiont cell density, and host protein content, enabling parallel estimation of absorptance (a) and specific absorption coefficients normalized to chlorophyll (a*chla), symbiont number (a*sym), and host protein (a*m). this comparison provides a rigorous assessment of the accuracy, precision, and operational limits of the mini-spec protocol. when properly implemented, the diving-pam-ii-mini-spec can substantially enhance our current capacity to characterize coral phenotypes and link host phenomics to host genomic/epigenomic/metabolomic responses in experimental biology. decades of coral reef watch data have revealed a strong latitudinal gradient in thermal stress accumulation across the caribbean, shaping the realized thermal niche of coral populations. to examine the effect of contrasting thermal histories on thermal performance, we conducted standardized cbass thermal profile assays on colonies of the endangered reef-building coral, acropora palmata from curaçao (highest frequency of heat-stress events), u.s. virgin islands and the florida keys (prior to the functional extinction of a. palmata at this site). colonies were exposed to a control+6°c temperature ramp up (0–120 min), a heat-load (180–360 min), and a subsequent 10-hour recovery phase (ending at 1020 min). physiological phenotyping (ed5, ed50, ed95, decline width, tissue absorptance) and inferred transcriptional stress trajectories using slingshot (top 2,000 most variable genes) consistently identified curaçao as the most thermally resilient population. the dominant axes of inferred stress progression and recovery, in correlation with gene expression, resolved regulatory programs associated with complete (thermally tolerant) versus incomplete (sensitive) returns to homeostasis. colonies from the thermally tolerant population were characterized by a dominant set of positively correlated genes representing a single, highly integrated protein-quality control / proteostasis (pcp) network. the core temporal gene markers of this pathway included canonical heat-shock proteins (hsps), co-chaperones, ubiquitin ligases, deubiquitinases, and proteasome components. our results show that the presence and strong induction of hsps and ubiquitination-related genes is not just a signature of excessive stress but a signature of resilience and recovery. curaçao colonies maintain an expanded and extremely efficient, constitutively primed pcp network that enables efficient damage control, mitigation of the toxicity of aberrant proteins, and recovery of nuclear functions under thermal stress conditions. complementary snp-based analysis confirms curacao colonies harbor important genetic diversity for survival under a high frequency of heat-stress events and are therefore obvious targets for conservation action.",
