      "search_text": "alligator head foundation bethany dean bethany dean first attempt of restoration based on coral sexual propagation in jamaica: successful experiences and lessons learnt macrofragmentation and outplanting methodology, a strategy to minimize extraction and maximise the outcomes of asexual propagation of orbicella faveolata assisted sexual coral propagation is crucial in rebuilding jamaica’s coral resilience as it results in greater genetic diversity, increasing the chances of coral survivorship as they face unprecedented challenges. restoration in jamaica has been heavily biased towards asexual coral propagation, which can result in a loss of biodiversity and decline in species resilience in response to increasing and frequent stressors, such as disease, storms and bleaching events. the alligator head foundation in partnership with the nature conservancy under the iki coralcarib project, became the first institution to successfully execute a coral assisted fertilization programme in jamaica. here, we provide a detailed description of the processes and results of the programme, including the lessons learned during its implementation.  \r\nin september 2025, orbicella faveolata and orbicella annularis gamete bundles were successfully collected and fertilised from 28 colonies across 3 different sites in the east portland fish sanctuary. approximately 1500 sexual coral recruits were developed in ahf’s ex-situ laboratory and were seeded on 3 different shaped substrates. variability was observed in spat settlement numbers as they ranged from 0-103 spats per substrate and in spatial preference on substrates. substrates were monitored and allowed to develop both ex-situ and in-situ in 25m2 experiment plots.  \r\nthe program has shown that it is possible for jamaica to successfully engage in assisted sexual propagation techniques increasing the genetic diversity within our restoration programs and local reefs. we hope through the establishment of ahf’s programme that other local entities may learn and be able to replicate components and contribute to the expansion of sexual coral restoration efforts in jamaica. \r\n\r\nkey words: assisted coral sexual propagation; restoration; orbicella faveolata; orbicella annularis; jamaica over the past four decades, coral reef health in east portland, jamaica have been negatively affected by compounding stressors. historically, local restoration efforts focused on asexual reproduction of acroporids with varied success, and little attention to slow growing reef builders. after the massive 2023 bleaching event affected reefs, including our iki coralcarib project site in east portland, there was a need to adapt and change previous restoration plans \r\nthree different morphologies of orbicella faveolata were observed in east portland, all displaying resilience towards stony coral tissue loss disease and high sea temperatures. morph 1, a rugose colony with a surface forming multiple bumps, was selected for macrofragmentation as each bump potentially could be a colony outplant. in 2025, 3-6 ofav bumps approximately 5-10cm in length were ‘macrofragmented’ per colony in-situ using a mallet and chisel. wounds were sealed with 2-part epoxy sculpt putty and ofav fragments were secured to the reef within 5km of the donor colonies. sixty-five colonies across two sites were macrofragmented. at site 1, an average 63.8% of the wound area was healed over 9 months, while at site 2, 58.2% of the wound area was healed over 4 months. \r\na sample of 6 donor colonies from each site, alongside non-donor colonies were monitored for spawning in september 2025. 75% of the colonies spawned, with at least two donor colonies spawning on both september 13 and 14.\r\nthis methodology is a low-tech sustainable technique which can be used to restore slow growing species on reefs without the need for a nursery. results have so far shown that colonies can heal, remain healthy and maintain reproductive potential. \r\n\r\nkeywords: macrofragmentation; wound healing; restoration; orbicella faveolata, jamaica",
