          "name": "Somok Banerjee",
          "search_text": "somok banerjee real-time microbial interactions in coral mucus coral reef health depends on microbial partnerships, yet the interactions that sustain or destabilize these symbioses occur in a hidden world we have rarely observed directly. although sequencing has illuminated the taxonomic composition of coral microbiomes, it cannot capture the fleeting, intercellular behaviors that determine whether microbial partners coexist, aggregate, or enter competitive conflict. these microscale processes ultimately influence microbiome stability on the reef, but they remain poorly understood. here we establish a new experimental framework to coral microbiome ecology, allowing real-time visualization of community dynamics that were previously inaccessible in coral systems. we study microbial communities sampled from diploria labyrinthiformis, green and pink phenotypes dervied from reefs in bermuda. to do so, we use microfluidics coupled to automated timelapse microscopy, enabling visualization and pertubation of cell-to-cell interactions between microbial types within controlled microenvironments. this approach allows us to observe emerging behaviours such as mixed-species aggregation, directional movement toward neighbouring groups, and even antagonistic encounters in which one phenotype disrupts established microcolonies of microbial communtieis. by quantifying these\r\ninteraction patterns, we can begin to determine the behavioral mechanisms like cooperation, competition, aggregation, and interference that regulate how coral-associated microbial communities form and persist within mucus on the reef. these mechanistic measurements, in turn, reveal how microbiomes from different coral phenotypes assemble and maintain distinct community structures on the reef. linking microscale behaviors to patterns observed in natural mucus samples can help bridge sequencing datasets and allows us to identify which interaction strategies promote stability, exclude opportunistic taxa, or predispose communities to dysbiosis under stress. by revealing the hidden dynamics of mucus-associated bacteria, this work opens new avenues for understanding themolecular mechanisms underlying microbial symbioses and help identify microbial traits that could be leveraged in future to support coral survival under environmental stress.\r\nkeywords: coral microbiome, microbial interactions, microfluidics, symbiosis.",
