          "name": "Ian Enochs",
          "search_text": "ian enochs cross-basin assessment of bioerosion and accretion reveals widespread carbonate loss and key drivers of reef persistence the ecological and societal benefits of coral reefs are dependent on their complex calcium carbonate frameworks, which support high biodiversity, sustain fisheries, and protect coastlines. yet survey-based assessments have indicated that these structures are declining, jeopardizing the goods and services they provide. although regional studies have quantified bioerosion and accretion, a broad-scale evaluation of the multifaceted processes that govern reef carbonate persistence has yet to be undertaken. here, for the first time, we deployed more than 500 bioerosion accretion replicates (bars) across the pacific and atlantic oceans, encompassing eight geographic regions and more than 53 sites across 18 islands. each bar, which began as a piece of clean coral skeleton, was simultaneously degraded by communities of bioeroders and overgrown by calcifiers during its multi-year deployment. using high-resolution computed tomography (ct) before and after deployment, we precisely quantified the contribution of multiple functional groups operating on each bar, including calcifiers, grazers, macroborers, and microorganisms. we further evaluated environmental and ecological drivers of carbonate persistence, including local conditions, parrotfish community composition, and human proximity. ultimately, the majority of sites were net erosional, with american samoa, a region with a particularly elevated aragonite saturation state, representing a net-accreting exception. erosion was dominated by surface grazing, highlighting the destructive actions of some species of otherwise valuable herbivores. macroboring, primarily by heterotrophic invertebrates, was correlated with distance to human populations, suggesting links to water quality. collectively, these findings provide the first large-scale quantification of reef carbonate persistence and reveal the widespread prevalence of framework erosion. because constructive and erosive processes respond to distinct environmental and ecological drivers, promoting reef habitat growth will require multifaceted strategies. these include addressing global stressors, improving local water quality, and strategically managing herbivore populations to minimize their impacts on reef carbonate persistence.",
