          "name": "Jonas Dreesmann",
          "search_text": "jonas dreesmann the microbiome of the plastid-bearing sea slug elysia viridis is stable amidst plastid replacement sacoglossa sea slugs are particularly renowned for their ability to incorporate functional \r\nchloroplasts from their algal diet into their tissues, a process known as kleptoplasty. \r\nconsiderable research has focused on the physiological and molecular mechanisms of the \r\nslugs and the alien organelles within the slugs that underpin kleptoplasty. however, other \r\nfactors potentially relevant to establishing kleptoplasty, such as the composition of the \r\nmicrobiome and whether the microbiome is food-dependent, remain poorly understood.   \r\nto shed light on the origin of the microbiome in sacoglossa, we analyzed elysia viridis – a \r\npolyphagous and well-established model in the research of kleptoplasty. we sampled natural \r\npopulations and also conducted a 21-day food switch experiment to understand the influence \r\nof algal diet on the associated microbial community of the slug. microbial communities were \r\nthen analyzed via dna metabarcoding of the bacterial 16s v1v2 region to assess taxonomic \r\ndiversity and community structure. simultaneously, we determined the origin and abundance \r\nof chloroplasts in the slugs using a novel approach based on dna metabarcoding. \r\nour results demonstrate that the microbiome of e. viridis varies depending on the collection \r\nsite, but is not altered by our food switch experiment despite a complete exchange of \r\nkleptoplasts after 14 days. the latter further underlines the ability of e. viridis to adapt to \r\ndifferent food sources and provides exclusive insights into the dynamics of a food switch. \r\nalthough the results regarding the microbiome were not as explicit, gammaproteobacteria, \r\nactinobacteria, and bacteroidia were identified as the most abundant classes in all samples. \r\nhowever, no time-spot dependent pattern could be observed.",
