          "name": "Yvonne Sawall",
          "search_text": "yvonne sawall community-scale mesocosm testing of pulsed artificial upwelling as a coral bleaching mitigation tool evaluating the impacts of pulsed artificial upwelling on primary production and calcification rates in coral reef communities under heat stress the effect of pulsed artificial upwelling during a simulated heat wave on the recruitment success of the coral porites astreoides novel reef interventions require thorough testing before implementation. we evaluated the efficacy of pulsed artificial upwelling (au)–the uplift of cooler, deeper water from within the euphotic zone–as a tool to reduce coral thermal stress. pulsed rather than continuous upwelling was applied to moderate, but not eliminate, thermal stress and promote gradual acclimatization to warming oceans. in summer 2025, we conducted a simulated au experiment in bermuda using an outdoor, community-scale mesocosm system. the 7-week experiment used twelve 500-l flow-through seawater basins: four ambient (28°c), four heated (30.5°c), and four heated+au (30.5°c interrupted by 28°c for 8 h per day). each basin contained common reef taxa, including adult and juvenile hard corals, soft corals, gorgonians, and juvenile fishes. we measured coral and fish metabolic rates and coral photosynthetic integrity (fv/fm) as indicators of physiological plasticity and stress.\r\nthe reef-building corals diploria labyrinthiformis and montastraea cavernosa showed high photosynthesis, respiration, and fv/fm in ambient and heated+au, but reduced performance under heat alone. porites astreoides exhibited minimal responses, and spat maintained consistently high fv/fm. the hydrozoan millepora alcicornis showed strong declines in metabolic rates and fv/fm in both heated treatments. calcification generally declined with temperature (ambient → heated+au → heated), except in d. labyrinthiformis, which calcified least under heated+au. the gorgonian gorgonia ventalina and soft coral plexaura flexuosa showed low susceptibility to heat. among fishes, only the princess parrotfish increased respiration with temperature. the four-eye butterflyfish, however, experienced high mortality (50%), with more deaths under heated+au.\r\noverall, au reduced coral bleaching, though benefits varied among taxa and some trade-offs occurred (e.g., d. labyrinthiformis calcification). most other sessile cnidarians and reef fishes showed limited temperature responses, except butterflyfish. au may serve as a localized, mid-term intervention to mitigate coral thermal stress during marine heatwaves. effective reef management in warming oceans may require novel strategies that both reduce acute bleaching during marine heatwaves and promote long-term thermal acclimatization. here we present an experimental test evaluating the impacts of one such intervention: pulsed artificial upwelling (au). this periodic transport of cooler water from depth to warmer, shallow reefs can deliver short-term thermal refugia while maintaining moderated heat stress exposure. manipulation experiments on the reef-community scale are generally scarce compared to organism-scale experiments on corals, however understanding the response of reef communities to thermal stress and mitigation strategies is necessary to identify potential wide-ranging ecological impacts. in summer 2025, a 7-week experiment was conducted in natural flow-through seawater basins containing simplified reef communities in bermuda. three treatments were applied across twelve basins: 4x ambient (28℃), 4x heated (30.5℃), and 4x simulated au (30.5℃ with daily 8-hour cooling periods at 28℃). simplified reef communities included reef-building corals, sea fans, soft corals, fish, snails, hermit crabs, and “live rock” with crustose, turf, and fleshy algae. while various individual species-level responses were assessed, here we focus on community-scale metabolic rates. initial results indicate that, relative to ambient conditions, elevated temperatures reduced primary production rates, and au maintained primary production rates comparable to those in ambient treatments. conversely, heat stress alone had no effect on community calcification, yet rates in the au basins were lower than both other treatment groups. these findings suggest that pulsed artificial upwelling may sustain high community primary production rates during thermal stress, but at the cost of calcification, highlighting a potential trade-off of its implementation on reef communities. climate-driven increases in the frequency and intensity of heat stress events threaten the persistence of coral reef ecosystems, emphasizing the need for innovative mitigation strategies. artificial upwelling (au), which is the pulsed delivery of cooler, deep seawater to shallow coral reefs, has been proposed as a potential thermal refuge during acute heat stress events. as part of a larger community-scale au project, we investigated the effects of heat stress, au, and biotic interactions on early life stages of the brooding coral porites astreoides. using an ex-situ mesocosm system, we established 12 reef-community basins exposed to ambient (28 °c), heat (30.5 °c), or heat + au (alternating 30.5–28 °c) conditions over two months. 24 adult colonies were collected from the bermuda rim reef and split between ambient and heat treatments during their spawning period, and larvae released over 12 days were settled onto tiles and reared under their respective spawning temperatures. recruits originating from heat-stressed parents were subsequently distributed between heat and heat + au treatments. to assess the compounding effects of coral-algae competition and grazing, and predation, half of the recruits in each basin were caged. survivorship, growth, and photosynthetic integrity (fv/fm) were monitored weekly using photography and pam fluorometry, respectively. initial results indicate, in contradiction to our expectation, that survivorship was higher in heated treatments compared to ambient and in heat + au. fv/fm values were also surprisingly consistent between all treatments. recruit survival was strongly reduced on uncaged substrate due to predation by butterflyfish, independent of treatment. so far, we conclude that heat stress with or without au has no negative effect on the very early life stages of p. astreoides, while the monitoring of longer-term legacy effects is currently still ongoing.",
