      "search_text": "biodiversity research center, academia sinica wei-chen tung wei-chen tung fine-scale soundscape mapping reveals biological sound hotspots in degraded shallow coral reefs reef features and their configuration drive hotspots of biodiversity and ecological functioning within coral reef ecosystems, particularly for fishes and crustaceans. increasing disturbances from coral mortality and extreme weather have accelerated habitat degradation, yet it remains unclear how substrate characteristics in degraded reefs support soniferous crustaceans and reef fishes at fine spatial scales. traditional visual surveys often underestimate cryptic species, whereas passive acoustic monitoring (pam) offers a promising alternative for assessing biological communities across broad areas. however, the relationship between fine-scale habitat characteristics and acoustic biodiversity in shallow, structurally heterogeneous reefs remains poorly quantified. in this study, we conducted snorkel-towed surveys that integrated gps-referenced benthic mapping with simultaneous acoustic recordings across a degraded reef system in okinawa, japan. using a deep-learning fish sound detection model, we extracted low-frequency fish calls along with high-frequency shrimp snaps, enabling high-resolution spatial comparisons between acoustic activity and benthic features. our results reveal distinct spatial patterns in sound production between fishes and snapping shrimps. diversity and richness hotspots of fish calls overlapped with areas dominated by tabular and branching corals.  in contrast, snapping shrimp sound intensity was highest in areas of mixed sandy bottoms and dead corals, reflecting different habitat preferences. notably, areas with dead but intact coral skeletons and adjacent sandy bottoms still served as acoustic hotspots for fishes and shrimps respectively, underscoring the ecological function of residual structures in supporting these communities after disturbance. these findings demonstrate that integrating pam with benthic structural mapping effectively helps identify ecologically valuable hotspots within degraded seascapes. the use of dead colonies by soniferous communities highlights the value of remnant coral frameworks for maintaining acoustic biodiversity. this suggests that soundscape-based indicators can inform precision habitat management in degraded shallow reef environments.",
