          "name": "Benjamin Glass",
          "search_text": "benjamin glass extreme marine heatwaves threaten resilience through legacy effects in stress-tolerant corals during the 4th global coral bleaching event (gcbe4; january 2023–september 2025), an unprecedented marine heatwave occurred on the bocas del toro reef complex (btrc) in panama. we characterized how this heatwave impacted the health and holobiont communities of the stress-tolerant coral siderastrea siderea at four sites across the btrc. tagged colonies at each site (n = 30–53 colonies per site) were visited before, during, and after the heatwave (early may 2022, mid-august 2023, and late april 2024, respectively) for the collection of images and dna samples. in situ temperature logger data showed that sites reached maxima of 32.1–33.9°c in october 2023, resulting in the accumulation of ~12–20 maximum °c-heating weeks (dhws). over this period, s. siderea colonies displayed widespread bleaching (i.e., the loss of algal endosymbionts), with an increase from 8.6–33% of colonies bleached per site in may 2022 to 33–70% in august 2023, followed by a decline to 15–63% by april 2024. colony-level partial mortality increased significantly between 2022–2024 at three of the four sites, and was observed even in colonies that were not bleached in august 2023. at three sites that experienced > 12 dhws during the heatwave, corals hosting cladocopium spp. algal symbionts in 2022 shifted towards less diverse communities dominated by heat-tolerant breviolum and durusdinium spp., and corals maintained these modified symbiont communities for months. the heatwave also reshaped corals’ bacterial microbiomes, including increases in α-diversity and abundances of pathogenic taxa (e.g., vibrionaceae), and these shifts were similarly persistent following the heatwave. together, these findings demonstrate that gcbe4 had lasting impacts on s. siderea holobiont health across the btrc, underscoring that record-breaking heat can compromise even stress-tolerant coral species and induce legacy effects that will likely affect future resilience. rapid action to minimize further ocean warming is thus necessary to safeguard reef ecosystems.",
