      "search_text": "california academy of sciences, auckland pim bongaerts pim bongaerts cryptic diversity and ecological differentiation in caribbean reef-building corals: a reefscape genomics perspective molecular ecological studies have been instrumental in advancing our understanding of coral reefs. although initially focused primarily on broad-scale connectivity patterns, they have gradually uncovered extensive local genetic structuring and cryptic diversity. genome-wide sequencing has accelerated the discovery of these patterns, but because studies are typically conducted on individual species with population-level metadata, it has remained challenging to evaluate the broader ecological relevance of this biodiversity. in this study, we assess cryptic diversity and associated fine-scale niche partitioning using a reefscape genomics approach, where we conducted reef-wide photogrammetric mapping and genotyping across 25 scleractinian coral species in curaçao (southern caribbean). reduced-representation genome sequencing revealed cryptic diversity across the vast majority of species, highlighting the extent of cryptic diversity across caribbean corals. for more than half of the species, cryptic lineages showed distinct depth distributions, underscoring the central role of depth in generating and maintaining this diversity. using a newly developed package to streamline the extraction of environmental metrics from photogrammetry data, we further characterized 3d microhabitat associations for >2,000 genotyped corals. these results confirmed that nominal species exhibit much stronger niche differentiation than is apparent from depth distributions alone, and that cryptic lineages themselves are separated by marked ecological differences. overall, these results highlight how much coral species biodiversity has been underestimated on caribbean reefs, and the degree of environmental specialization underlying this cryptic diversity. they stress the importance of overcoming the methodological barriers to detecting cryptic lineages in situ, ensuring that our attempts to understand these rapidly changing ecosystems are not confounded by species units that do not accurately reflect underlying biological diversity.",
