          "name": "Dr Ale Hernández",
          "search_text": "alejandra hernández-agreda not all brooders are the same: hidden vulnerabilities in rare caribbean corals global threats to coral reefs are driving severe population declines in many scleractinian coral species. species with low abundances and brooding reproductive modes are hypothesized to be particularly vulnerable due to their limited dispersal and nonlinear genetic erosion. yet the risk faced by these corals remains poorly understood due to limited information on their population dynamics, genetic status, and ecological traits. using photogrammetry and reduced-representation sequencing, we conducted an integrated ecological and genomic assessment of seven caribbean coral species to evaluate how low abundance and brooding reproductive mode interact to shape vulnerability in three of the rarest and most threatened genera (helioseris, scolymia, and mycetophyllia). across species, we observed high clonality, low genetic diversity, and extremely low abundances, with mycetophyllia ferox, mycetophyllia lamarckiana, and scolymia lacera among the rarest taxa with only 1–4 colonies across the total surveyed area (17,500 m² across 7 locations on curaçao). despite these species reported as brooders, they exhibited a wide range of dispersal capacities, which largely explained observed patterns of inbreeding. critically endangered helioseris cucullata and scolymia cubensis showed short-distance dispersal (<100s of meters), resulting in high inbreeding and strong geographic structure with isolation-by-distance. in contrast, the least concern species mycetophyllia aliciae displayed “broadcast-comparable” dispersal (>10s of km), leading to low inbreeding and no detectable spatial differentiation. cryptic species were identified only in h. cucullata, revealing two ecologically distinct lineages with evidence of recent hybridization. our results suggest that some rare brooders may face vulnerability levels comparable to other highly threatened caribbean genera, such as acropora and dendrogyra, where critically small population sizes jeopardize successful sexual reproduction. taken together, our findings show that distinct life-history traits can generate highly divergent population dynamics even among species sharing the same reproductive mode, underscoring the necessity for species-specific ecological and genomic assessments to guide conservation interventions.",
