          "name": "Cheryl Logan",
          "search_text": "cheryl logan mechanisms of thermal tolerance in galápagos corals corals in the eastern tropical pacific (etp) inhabit harsh conditions outside the typical range of most tropical reefs, experiencing high temperature variability, high nutrient levels, and relatively acidified waters. in the galápagos, upwelling and recurrent enso events further expose corals to extreme cold and warm anomalies that can lead to bleaching. to examine how corals persist under such extremes, we conducted an archipelago-wide survey of galápagos coral tolerance to heat and cold stress using a portable bleaching stress system. thermal tolerance assays on pocillopora and pavona across seven islands revealed clear spatial patterns: corals from the coolest region (isabela) were the most heat-sensitive, while those from the warmer northern region (darwin and wolf) exhibited the broadest thermal tolerances. we then tracked the fate of the relatively vulnerable isabela corals at a new restoration site over a two-year period. we found higher coral genetic diversity, variation in growth rates, and more diverse coral-symbiont associations in pocilloporids than previously reported. when combined with the archipelago-wide results, these findings suggest that isabela pocilloporids have higher adaptive potential and suitability for restoration than expected. our findings highlight the galápagos as a natural laboratory for understanding how corals persist in extreme and fluctuating environments and reveal physiological traits that may shape coral resilience under future ocean conditions.",
