      "search_text": "cawthron institute kirsty smith kirsty smith gambierdiscus (dinophyta: alveolata) community structure shapes ciguatoxin risk in a tropical lagoon ecosystem ciguatera poisoning (cp) caused by ciguatoxins (ctxs) from marine microbial eukaryotes (gambierdiscus spp., dinophyta, alveolata) has significant worldwide economic and social impacts and is directly impacted by climate change. however, significant knowledge gaps remain in understanding the link between gambierdiscus community ecology and ctx concentrations in fishes within a local area, inhibiting prevention strategies.  to examine site-specific ctx risk factors, we coupled a tailored high-throughput sequencing metabarcoding approach and quantitative pcr, with ctx determination in novel gambierdiscus strains, in an omnivorous fish (maito, ctenochaetus striatus) with a limited home range as well as fishes with wider distributions, in rarotonga (cook islands), which has amongst the world’s highest incidence rate of cp. of the novel gambierdiscus strains, only g. polynesiensis isolates produced described pacific ctxs (ctx3b, -3c, -4a, -4b, iso-ctx3b/c, -4a/b). while dinoflagellate assemblages were heavily dominated by gambierdiscus spp., only sites with higher abundances of g. polynesiensis closely reflected the ctx3b levels in c. striatus, unrelated to fish age or size. artificial and macroalgal substrate sampling gave similar assemblage alpha diversity of dinoflagellates, which was significantly lower at a site near a major tourist hub, with relatively lower water quality. using neuroblastoma cell-based assays and liquid chromatography tandem mass spectrometry, ctxs were detected in samples from nine carnivorous and herbivorous fish taxa, with levels varying among species. based on these results, site-specific information on sentinel fish species and regular quantification of targeted gambierdiscus taxa using molecular approaches could play an important role in cp prevention. we are ultimately working towards fit-for-purpose monitoring techniques that are rapid, cheap, and easy to use for the communities that need them.",
