      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Luis Calderon-Aguilera",
          "search_text": "luis calderon-aguilera taxonomic and functional diversity of mesophotic communities of the mexican pacific the mexican pacific spans from 15–32° n and serves as a natural laboratory for studying climate-change effects on ecosystem structure and function. the region already exhibits projected future conditions–including lower alkalinity, rising sea-surface temperatures, increasing frequency and intensity of extreme meteorological events, and coastal upwelling that enhances nutrient supply and primary production. these conditions generate turbid waters with low light penetration, producing a shallow mesophotic zone (16–63 m), depending on site and season. between 2021 and 2023, we conducted 384 visual transects using remotely operated vehicles (rovs) across shallow (>10% incident light) and mesophotic (<10% incident light) strata to examine fish assemblages and assess potential refuge capacity in three mesophotic locations. we also collected water samples at multiple depths for edna and carbonate-system analyses. we identified 141 fish species across all sites and depths: 85 were shared between strata, 37 were exclusive to shallow waters, and 19 to mesophotic depths. we calculated taxonomic (α-diversity, shannon, simpson, β-diversity) and functional (richness, divergence, evenness, mean pairwise distance) diversity indices and found no significant differences (p > 0.05) between depth strata. the edna analysis of 81 samples produced 16,441,008 reads and identified 66,246 otus, 74.8% of which corresponded to cellular organisms. eukaryotic diversity included protists, metazoans, and fungi. the results supported the deep-refuge hypothesis, with 4,262 otus shared between shallow and mid-water layers, 1,747 between shallow and deep layers, and 914 between mid-water and deep layers. deep samples showed lower species richness, suggesting more homogeneous communities. overall, the observed similarity across depths likely reflects the characteristically shallow mesophotic zones of the eastern tropical pacific and does not necessarily indicate that the mesophotic zone functions as an ecological refuge.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Taxonomic and functional diversity of mesophotic communities of the Mexican Pacific",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "90fcbc6b-361a-4db0-b070-d0c58cb03821"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00268",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Miel Rodríguez-jiménez",
          "search_text": "miel a. rodríguez-jiménez physiological markers of photosynthetic efficiency in two morphospecies of pocillopora in the gulf of california the physiological response in the symbiotic dinoflagellates of the symbiodiniaceae family is essential for the maintenance of scleractinian corals in the face of environmental variation. therefore, the characterization of physiological indicators such as chlorophyll a concentration and symbiont density are key markers for assessing the overall coral health and resilience. in the tropical mexican pacific, the genus pocillopora are the most representative reef-building corals. in addition, the environmental heterogeneity to which they are exposed can elicit different physiological response. this study compared the chlorophyll a concentration and symbiont density of p.cf. meandrina and p.cf. capitata in two contrasting environments within bahía de la paz in the year 2023. six colonies per morphospecies were sampled, and chlorophyll concentration was determined using the methods described elsewhere, while symbiont density was obtained by counting cells with a hemocytometer. chlorophyll data were analyzed using two-way anova, while symbiont density data were analyzed using a kruskal-wallis test. the results indicate significant differences in chlorophyll a concentration between morphospecies (f = 5.55, df =1, p = 0.03), with higher concentrations of chlorophyll in p.cf. capitata (µ =17.54  ± 12.35 µg cm-²) compared to p.cf. meandrina (µ = 9.43  ± 4.39 µg cm-2). meanwhile, symbiont density showed significant differences between sites (x²= 6, df = 1, p = 0.01), with higher chl a in pocillopora corals at punta galeras (µ = 2.48 x 108 ± 2.35 x 108 cells·cm-²) compared to isla gaviota (µ = 6.68 x 107 ± 4.89 x 107 cells·cm-²) . these results indicate specific acclimatization responses in the pocillopora genus according to the morphospecies. it is expected that the analysis of other physiological indicators, such as total protein and/or lipid concentration, will allow the identification of specific pathways of acclimatization response to different local environments and by morphospecies in bahía de la paz.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Physiological markers of photosynthetic efficiency in two morphospecies of Pocillopora in the Gulf of California",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "509db5ce-9598-4f91-af61-52f6cdc189c8"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01479",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 2,
