          "name": "Márcio Coelho",
          "search_text": "márcio coelho speciation in recently diverged lineages of atlantic paramuricea: insights from phylo- and population genomics recent work on a species complex of closely related gorgonians in the genus paramuricea in the ne atlantic and mediterranean has identified three main genetic lineages: p. clavata in the mediterranean, and two colour morphotypes of p. cf. grayi (purple and yellow) along the portuguese coast. although the two atlantic morphotypes share identical mitogenomes, multilocus and phylogenomic analyses reveal clear genomic segregation consistent with lineage divergence. their distributions along the portuguese coast show strong spatial structure, with the lineages occurring parapatrically with depth in western portugal and the purple lineage virtually absent in the south. these patterns, together with differences in reproductive timing and mode, suggest partial reproductive isolation. here, we integrate new transcriptomic data, including samples from the remote gorringe seamount, to deepen understanding of lineage segregation and evolutionary trajectories within this complex. phylogenomic analyses of 2,766 single-copy orthologues (pomo species tree and astral) consistently resolved three well-supported paramuricea lineages: mediterranean p. clavata and two ne atlantic lineages of p. cf. grayi (yellow and purple). gorringe seamount samples formed a distinct monophyletic subgroup within the purple lineage, confirming their genetic uniqueness and supporting their reclassification as p. grayi. population-genomic analyses (pca, admixture, hybrid-triangle plots) further distinguished the three lineages and revealed that individuals of the p. cf. grayi purple lineage carry variable ancestry from the two parental lineages (p. clavata and p. cf. grayi yellow). their intermediate genomic positions indicate a detectable hybridization signal among these taxa, with admixture proportions varying geographically and showing stronger p. cf. grayi yellow ancestry in mainland populations than in gorringe. together, these results provide new insight into the evolutionary history of atlantic–mediterranean paramuricea, illustrating how fine-scale geographic structure, reproductive differences, and hybridization interact in the diversification of anthozoans, an important step toward resolving speciation in groups where boundaries are often difficult to define.",
