          "name": "David Paz-Garcia",
          "search_text": "david paz-garcia marginal seasonality shows greater differentiation in gene expression than extreme hydrothermal vent activity in a reef-building coral cryptic diversity and high morphological plasticity in pocillopora reef-building corals in the eastern tropical pacific the acclimation abilities of reef corals living in variable environments depend not only on their own genomic response to stressful conditions but also on the reinforcing and complementary responses of their algal symbionts. here, we analyze the gene expression profiles from populations of porites panamensis and its endosymbiotic cladocopium algal communities of a hydrothermal vent in bahia concepción, mexico, where temperature and ph variation are the highest in the gulf of california. seasonal changes (winter versus summer) triggered 12-18 times more differentially expressed genes (degs) for the coral host and its zooxanthellae (575 and 3312, respectively) than did proximity to hydrothermal vents (47 and 180). weighted co-expression analysis revealed host reproduction and immunity modules were sensitive to warm summer and on-vent conditions, while nutritional shifts to lipid reserves occurred in oligotrophic winter regimes. zooxanthellae up-regulated chloroplast biogenesis and amino acid transport in summer, while metabolism and chaperone activity were up-regulated in winter. chaperon and ribosome sensitivity were also linked to temperature and nutrient availability in the algal symbionts. complementary transcriptomic changes between host and zooxanthellae allowed holobiont acclimatization to the marginal environmental variability of bahia concepción. the complementary modular responses facilitated acclimatization to the hydrothermal system with immune and metabolic trade-offs, showing that mutualistic symbiotic relations in coral p. panamensis may prevail with reduced fitness to end of century climate change projections. accurate species identification is fundamental to biological diversity assessments and critical for understanding ecological and evolutionary dynamics. high morphological variation in scleractinian corals have collapsed our understanding of species boundaries. pocillopora corals are the main reef builders in the eastern tropical pacific (etp), despite growing under broad interannual changes that are unfavorable for reef development. associated with these environmental conditions, pocillopora corals exhibit cryptic diversity and highly variable skeletal morphology, leading to confusion and debate over the taxonomic validity of morphologically defined species. in this study, we found that most of the nuclear genes analyzed present low resolution to delimit species, but the histone loci allow us to identify most of the species present in etp. based on mitochondrial and nuclear data, we found that up to five species could be distributed in the etp: p. cf. effusa, p. villosa, p. cf. verrucosa, and two cryptic species of the p. grandis-meandrina complex. we found that the colonial morphology and micro-morphology of corallites are not useful for identifying pocillopora species. we characterize in situ morphological plasticity of pocillopora corals and examine associated changes of environmental conditions in the gulf of california and morphological variation along etp. more than 200 tagged colonies of p. meandrina/eydouxi species complex (mtorf type 1) were followed for 15 years (2011-2025) in locations with different flow conditions. during the study, several patterns of morphological plasticity were documented. coral colonies switched between morphological species in as little as 6-8 months (short-term plasticity) or in two-three years (long-term plasticity). this study shows that much of the morphological diversity seen in pocillopora corals in the etp is driven by changes in environmental conditions independently of genetic lineage.",
