      "search_text": "chulalongkorn university dr apple chavanich niranjan divakaran voranop viyakarn suchana apple chavanich scaling coral restoration in developing countries: lessons from low-cost technologies and cross-sectoral collaboration in thailand coral reef restoration urgently requires innovative yet accessible technologies that can be applied across diverse socio-economic contexts. in thailand, we demonstrate how cross-sectoral partnerships can drive practical and scalable restoration solutions by integrating scientific innovation with low-cost technologies suitable for developing countries. this study presents a restoration framework developed through collaboration among universities, the plant genetic conservation project under the royal initiative of her royal highness princess maha chakri sirindhorn, the royal thai navy, government agencies, private sector partners, and coastal communities. our team has advanced sexual coral propagation, microbial enhancement, and the early development of cryopreserved gamete banks to increase genetic diversity and climate resilience. importantly, we also developed a low-technology hatchery system, using simple materials, gravity-fed seawater flow, low-energy filtration, and locally sourced components, that significantly reduces operational costs while maintaining high survival of coral larvae and recruits. this system has enabled reliable annual production of thousands of coral juveniles at samaesan island in the upper gulf of thailand, making sexual propagation more accessible to regions with limited resources. these innovations, co-designed with navy divers and coastal communities, have resulted in improved settlement success, enhanced early growth, and the integration of thermally resilient broodstock into restoration plots. the low-tech hatchery model has further empowered community partners to participate in coral culture, strengthening local capacity, stewardship, and long-term monitoring. niranjan divakaran presence of microplastics in the corals of samae sarn islands, upper gulf of thailand marine plastics represent a major pollutant along thailand’s coasts, accounting for over 45% of total marine debris. this study examined microplastic (mp) contamination in ten coral species; acropora millepora, astreopora myriophthalma, diploastrea heliopora, echinopora lamellosa, favia pallida, favites halicora, galaxea fascicularis, pocillopora damicornis, porites lutea, and turbinaria decurrens, which collected from samae sarn island, chonburi province. the results showed that all coral species contained mps, with favia pallida showing the highest contamination (0.78 mps /polyp) and echinopora lamellosa the lowest (0.015 mps /polyp). fibers dominated (89.3%), followed by films, granules, and styrofoam. most mps were <1 mm (65%), and black particles were most common (41.7%). six polymer types were identified, mainly nylon (43.2%), followed by polypropylene, polyester, pet, polystyrene, and pvdc. larger polyps exhibited higher mp abundance, suggesting ingestion is influenced by mouth size. the dominance of fibrous black nylon reflects local anthropogenic sources such as tourism and fishing. these findings, the first for samae sarn, highlight microplastics as an emerging threat to coral health and underscore the urgency of targeted management and awareness initiatives in thailand’s coastal regions. voranop viyakarn a low-technology coral hatchery model for affordable sexual propagation in thailand developing accessible and cost-effective coral propagation technologies is essential for scaling reef restoration in resource-limited settings. at the samae san island in the upper gulf of thailand, we established a low-technology coral hatchery system designed to support reliable sexual propagation using simple, low-budget equipment. the system employs gravity-fed seawater flow, low-energy filtration, inexpensive settlement substrates, and readily available local materials, enabling consistent larval rearing without the need for advanced infrastructure. over multiple spawning seasons, the hatchery has produced thousands of coral settlers annually, with stable survival rates comparable to higher-tech facilities. this approach demonstrates that high-impact restoration outcomes can be achieved using practical, low-cost engineering solutions. the simplicity of the system also allows for easy training and participation by community volunteers, navy divers, and students, fostering long-term stewardship and expanding human capacity for restoration. the samae san low-tech hatchery provides a scalable model suitable for developing countries, marine protected areas with limited budgets, and regions seeking to initiate coral sexual propagation without extensive laboratory resources.",
