          "name": "Dr Diana Morales",
          "search_text": "diana morales-de-anda exploring links between functional diversity and ecosystem processes in coral reef fishes: scale, trait selection, and an emerging analytical framework functional diversity is commonly used to describe coral reef fish communities and to infer aspects of ecosystem functioning. integrating multiple traits into analyses has been highly valuable and increasingly popular, particularly in reef systems, providing critical information on species characteristics and responses to environmental conditions. nonetheless, as several authors have highlighted, this approach still remains weakly connected to the ecosystem functions and processes it aims to represent. furthermore, interpretation is strongly shaped by methodological decisions such as spatial scale and trait selection. here, we use data from the marietas islands reef system, a site the research group has consistently studied across multiple projects within a long-term monitoring program (every two months from 2010 to 2024, with the same surveyor and semi-fixed sites). in addition to traditional descriptors, we are incorporating traits associated with ecosystem processes, such as nutrient cycling and biomass-mediated energy flow, to distinguish community traits from response and ecosystem effect traits. in this study, we present an analytical workflow under development to examine how functional patterns vary across spatial scales and different trait sets. we also assess the potential of emerging modeling tools, including supervised machine learning, to support trait-based analyses. preliminary analyses indicate strong scale dependence and moderate correlations among spatial levels, highlighting the importance of integrating both spatial resolution and trait selection. overall, this work contributes to a broader effort to explicitly link functional approaches to ecosystem processes in coral reef fishes, while promoting the collaborative development of trait datasets explicitly linked to ecological processes.",
