      "search_text": "citizens of the reef andy ridley andy ridley the evolution of a human-ai hybrid platform for coral reef conservation - learnings and opportunities effective coral reef monitoring increasingly depends on scalable underwater imaging workflows, yet many community-driven and conservation-focused organizations face persistent gaps in equipment quality, data consistency, and analytical capacity. citizens of the reef has spent the past five years evolving a human–ai hybrid image processing system designed to overcome these barriers in resource-limited contexts. the great reef census integrates citizen scientist-sourced underwater imagery, often generated with assorted camera equipment, inconsistent lighting, and minimal calibration with a suite of machine-learning tools. by uniting industry and academic expertise, this collaboration delivers cutting-edge solutions that keep the program at the forefront of technological and scientific progress. virtual volunteers annotate coral reef images to morphology level that have been validated to achieve 97-99% accuracy compared to human expert analysis of the same images, for estimates of total coral cover and key coral groups. through an accessible model of community participation, the great reef census delivers accurate, high-impact scientific reconnaissance data and achieves a substantial return on investment.\r\n\r\nin this presentation, we will discuss the iteration of the great reef census platform, how we overcame multiple and varying issues with all elements of the program, from image capture to ai model development and image processing. we will also explain the new generation machine learning programs we are deploying as part of the expansion to hawai’i, the red sea and coral triangle.",
