      "search_text": "clemson university sarah rider sarah rider cryptofauna biodiversity and settlement patterns in the florida keys national marine sanctuary healthy coral reef ecosystems are a product of abundant and diverse species assemblages. coral reefs’ most abundant and diverse residents are small invertebrates (<5 centimeters) such as worms, clams, snails, shrimp, and crabs, collectively known as cryptofauna. cryptofauna are critically important because they constitute the base of coral reef food webs and nutrient cycles. understanding cryptofauna community dynamics, including the composition of species from different trophic levels and functional roles, is critical to understanding the fundamental health of coral reefs. little is currently known about cryptofauna communities in the florida keys national marine sanctuary. we are investigating cryptofauna settlement, succession, and biodiversity patterns in the middle florida keys at two reefs situated along an environmental gradient: coral gardens (a nearshore reef with high rugosity and high coral cover) and stag party (an offshore reef with low rugosity and low coral cover). we deployed a total of 10 autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms) with attached onset hobo temperature and light data loggers across these reefs in july 2024. one arms was retrieved from each reef in august 2024, january 2025, june 2025, and june 2026 after 1-month, 6-month, 1-year, and 2-year immersions, respectively. the remaining arms will be retrieved in june 2027 after a 3-year immersion. in accordance with smithsonian institute protocols, arms processing involves (1) identifying motile organisms to the lowest possible taxonomic level, and (2) photographing arms plates to annotate in coralnet and determine percent cover of sessile organisms. results from this project will improve our understanding of how cryptofauna communities are influenced by environmental conditions (water temperature, light availability, reef location, coral cover, and rugosity), and how sampling results are influenced by arms immersion duration. we aim to contribute valuable information to help inform arms survey design and better enable replication and standardization of future cryptofauna studies.",
