          "name": "Charlotte Moritz",
          "search_text": "charlotte moritz shining a light on daytime coral spawning synchrony across oceans the variety of coral taxa and environmental cues triggering broadcast spawning led to contrasting theories on the mechanisms underpinning reproductive synchrony. porites rus, a gonochoric, daytime-spawning species, provides novel insights into these processes across oceans owing to its broad indo-pacific and red sea distribution. despite its ecological relevance, particularly a common presence in coral reefs and multi-stress resistance, its reproductive biology has remained poorly documented. establishment and expansion of a transoceanic observation network enabled the compilation of the largest dataset for a single daytime-spawning species. statistical analyses of decadal data across spatial scales (from individual colonies to ocean basins) and varying depths reveal a striking pattern of high-precision spawning synchrony, for colonies separated by meters to >15,000 km, consistently 5 days after the full moon over an extended, uninterrupted period from october to april. significant linear correlations between depth, light, water temperature and spawning time after sunrise are consistent across spatial and temporal scales, and for both males and females spawning ≃20 minutes apart. interestingly, individual colonies spawn across consecutive days and months, reflecting a substantial energetic investment in reproduction at the polyp and colony levels. this work enables minute-scale predictive accuracy of spawning onset according to environmental conditions, facilitating in situ monitoring and controlled experimentation. porites rus emerges as a robust and tractable model species for exploring coral reproductive rhythms across interconnected systems, from local reefs to biogeographic scales. ongoing international collaborations are integrating genetic, developmental, behavioural, histological, population dynamics and connectivity analyses to investigate how broad-scale reproductive synchrony can be maintained in a changing ocean. studying this resilient species promises significant advances in understanding reproductive strategies, with applications across taxa. finally, p. rus constitutes a powerful model to anticipate and predict the future structure and functioning of coral reefs shaped by the survival of stress-tolerant and plastic species.",
