      "search_text": "college of charleston heather spalding piper zola heather spalding tissue nutrients and biomass of the red algal invader chondria tumulosa in the papahānaumokuākea marine national monument, hawaiʻi the cryptogenic red algal invader chondria tumulosa (chondria) was discovered in the papahanaumokuakea marine national monument (pmnm) in 2015 and forms dense mats that quickly overgrow native corals and algae. the objectives of this study were to determine the distribution, abundance, and biomass of chondria mats within the pmnm. chondria’s distribution includes the three most northern atolls of the pmnm, each of which has a high abundance of herbivorous fish and intact trophic systems. by 2023, 65% of sites surveyed at manawai (pearl and hermes atoll) contained attached or unattached chondria mats in the lagoon, back reef, and fore reef from 1 to 21 m depths. chondria percent cover reached up to 95% over a 25 x 1 m area, with in situ canopy heights of up to 21 cm. chondria mat biomass was determined in twenty 10 x 10 cm quadrats from 2 to 13 m depths at 8 sites. chondria biomass was similar or less than that of other invasive mat-forming algae, but its dense mats absorbed up to 99% of available light, depending on mat canopy height. stable isotope analyses of chondria tissue δ15n ranged from 2.2 to 3.4‰, suggesting its distribution was not influenced by anthropogenic or natural shore-based processes, such as seabird guano. however, the %n in algal tissue ranged from 0.95 to 2.75%, suggesting enrichment is occurring in situ within some mats. this invasive alga poses a serious threat to coral reefs in the pacific due to its dense, mat-forming morphology, lack of herbivory, fragmentation, attachment to nets and other algae, and high abundance in nearly pristine, nutrient-poor environments. piper zola estimating infection and bioerosion rates of the boring sponge cliona celata via direct contact and larval dispersal with applications to reef carbonate budgets the process of bioerosion by boring sponges plays a crucial role in shaping biogenic carbonate reef systems, including mollusc beds and coral reefs, through calcium carbonate recycling, sediment creation, and reef accretion control. despite their ecological importance, few studies have compared infection intensity and bioerosion capabilities between the different mechanisms of boring sponge infection (ie., direct contact versus larval recruitment) onto substrate among carbonate reefs. to assess differences in infection mechanisms by the boring sponge cliona celata, a field experiment was deployed in charleston harbor (charleston, sc, usa). clean oyster shells, which served as calcium carbonate substrate, were either directly exposed to sponge via physical attachment to infected substrate or to sponge larval recruitment via larvae present in the water column. results revealed greater boring hole size, percent surface area infected, and number of boring holes in substrate exposed to physical contact with sponge infection, suggesting a higher infection rate and intensity compared to infection via larval settlement alone. results also reveal that infection via physical contact generated a higher bioerosion rate compared to larval settlement. we estimate that c. celata in direct contact with new substrate has a bioerosion rate of 0.730 ± 0.080 kg caco3 m-2 yr-1. boring sponge infection has important ramifications for the function and structure of carbonate reef habitats around the world. our findings suggest that contact-based spread can accelerate substrate loss and contribute to net reef degradation.",
