      "search_text": "columbia university arjun krishnan arjun sai krishnan population genomics of the stony coral acropora millepora and its symbionts across the great barrier reef rising ocean temperatures and associated bleaching events pose an existential threat to coral reefs worldwide. an adaptive response to climatic change will necessarily rely on standing heritable variation in reef-building corals and their symbionts, but the association between their genetics and variation in bleaching susceptibility remains to be well understood, as does the relationship of adaptive genetic variation to salient environmental gradients.\r\n\r\nwith these goals in mind, we focused on acropora millepora, a stony coral found throughout the indo-pacific, in which inter-individual variation in heat resistance among aposymbiotic larvae has been shown to be heritable. we sampled 1081 colonies across 40 reefs along a 1600 km stretch of the great barrier reef (gbr), spanning a wide range of thermal, bathymetric, and other environmental conditions, and characterized visual and molecular phenotypes related to bleaching. bleaching phenotypes were ascertained during natural bleaching events(n=378), under experimental heat stress(n=492), or both(n=211).\r\n\r\nwe sequenced whole genomes from 125 samples to high coverage (>15x after mapping) and imputed genotypes for the remaining 952 low-coverage (~2x) samples; as we show, this produces reliable genotype calls for the combined sample of 1081 individuals. we used these data to infer population structure and demographic history, identify the symbiont composition of each colony, and to examine geographic distributions of host and symbiont genotypes across environmental conditions. we further identified genomic targets of natural selection, in which allele frequency differences are associated with environmental conditions or maintained by balancing selection. we plan to map the genetic basis of variation in bleaching in natural populations and to develop a predictor of bleaching susceptibility based on environmental and genetic data. these analyses provide an unprecedented characterization of demographic and selective pressures shaping genetic diversity in a coral and its symbionts, and establish a foundation for quantifying the species’ adaptive potential.\r\n\r\n+co-supervised this work",
      "country_code": "US",
      "country": "United States",
      "connection_count": 1
