      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Kerry Dunleavy",
          "search_text": "kerry dunleavy bioacoustics and machine learning as key tools in coral reef conservation and restoration coral reef ecosystems are under threat globally from anthropogenic activities, prompting efforts to manage and restore these vital habitats. evaluating the success of such efforts requires rigorous, scalable, and repeatable monitoring methods. advances in acoustic recorders and ai now make passive acoustic surveys a promising tool for large-scale reef monitoring. here, we present results of passive acoustic surveys we conducted to expand monitoring duration at coral research sites and measure effectiveness of reef restoration efforts.  we deployed marine automated recording (aru) units from august 2023 to november 2024 at six sites around mo’orea, with one aru in each site across reef habitat: fore reef, fringing reef, and back reef.  additional arus were placed at a coral outplanting restoration site and a nearby unmanipulated control site.  we developed a convolutional neural network model to detect and classify reef-related sounds.  we present data from four ecologically important signals: ‘parrotfish grazing’ and ‘damselfish calls’,  a ‘knock’ signal produced by unknown fish species, and a metric of phonic richness for signals generated by fish.  we present differences in acoustic detection rates between three coral and three algal dominated sites. preliminary results between coral and algal sites were not significant for any metric.   we also compared the detection rates at the experimental coral restoration and control sites for the four metrics, but with sample size n=1, these results are anecdotal.  we found all four metrics to be different between reef types. this study shows the potential for passive acoustic monitoring of coral reef communities, highlights some of the challenges that still need to be addressed to improve on species detection and classification, and provides practical examples of ways to efficiently measure outcomes of coral reef restoration efforts.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Bioacoustics and machine learning as key tools in coral reef conservation and restoration",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "b40aea0b-dc4f-40c5-aa39-c2e1c2a08ced"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00505",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
