          "name": "Nicte-Ha Munoz Arroyo",
          "search_text": "andrea nicte-ha munoz coral bleaching in american samoa american samoa is a remote pacific island nation where substantial mitigation methods are needed to protect coral reefs from global warming, human impacts, and frequent mass bleaching events. mass coral bleaching events have begun to become more frequent, occurring in the samoan archipelago most recently between 2023 and 2024. these events are increasing in frequency and severity, an alarming sign of the acceleration of climate change. scientists warn that sustained warming could result in the loss of up to 90% of corals globally. the international community, therefore, faces an urgent challenge: to take decisive measures to combat climate change and its effects, such as mass bleaching, to protect these vital ecosystems.\r\nit is expected that mass coral bleaching events will continue to become more frequent and severe, having more regular impacts on coral cover in the coming years. to address this, the american samoa coral reef advisory group (crag) has developed action plans to respond to bleaching events and mitigate their effects. these plans are modified according to the characteristics of each event and involve being attentive to bleaching alerts, recording water temperatures both regionally and locally, and regularly monitoring the territory’s coral ecosystem. this allows us to determine the progression of the event and establish measures to counteract its effects. among these measures is the development of a coral restoration program that involves targeting specific sites and finding thermotolerant corals to address mass bleaching events. these restoration programs are in the early stages of development, so sharing information from the monitoring programs in american samoa, particularly data on the recent bleaching event, will provide important insights and foster collaborative learning. this information exchange will help develop better strategies for coral reef conservation, benefiting american samoa's high marine biodiversity and its communities that heavily rely on marine resources for subsistence.",
