      "search_text": "coral reef alliance julio san martin julio san martin fishapp as a near real-time tool to strengthen the sustainability and resilience of coral reef fisheries in the western caribbean coral reef fisheries across the caribbean are essential for food security, livelihoods, and local economies, yet they face growing pressures from climate change, overfishing, and limited access to timely, standardized fisheries information. in many small-scale coastal communities, monitoring programs operate with limited resources, and while data are often collected with great local effort, constraints in time, tools, or analytical capacity can make it difficult to process and use this information for decision-making. to address these challenges, the coral reef alliance and its co-management partners co-developed fishapp, a bilingual, near real-time fisheries monitoring platform designed to integrate biological, ecological, and socioeconomic data into a single decision-support system. \r\nfishapp compiles catch and effort data, size-based indicators, maturation proportions, biomass spectra, multispecies indices, and long-term cpue trends to support data-limited stock assessments and ecosystem-based decision-making. the platform enables co-managers, community organizations, and government authorities to detect spatial and temporal trends, identify early warning signs of resource decline, and interpret environmental change alongside fishing dynamics. these analyses are automatically translated into accessible dashboards that facilitate transparent communication and shared understanding among stakeholders.\r\na core feature of fishapp is its strong integration of community participation. local fishers, community scientists, and co-managers contribute directly to data collection, validation, and indicator co-design, ensuring that traditional knowledge and field expertise inform management recommendations. fishapp represents a scalable model for how digital tools, developed collaboratively with coastal communities, can enhance fisheries assessment, environmental resilience, and inclusive governance. by expanding across the caribbean the platform aims to support sustainable coral reef fisheries that balance ecological integrity with the livelihood needs of coastal populations.",
