      "search_text": "coral reef alliance helen fox helen fox building a regional evidence base for action: the mesoamerican reef water quality monitoring program satellites to symbionts: developing estimates of genomic diversity using remotely sensed data water quality pollution is one of the most pervasive and solvable threats to coral reefs in the mesoamerican reef (mar), yet efforts to address it have been hindered by fragmented monitoring, inconsistent data standards, and limited tools to guide decision-making. to bridge these gaps, coral and regional partners launched the mar water quality monitoring program in 2021, the first coordinated, standardized, trinational effort to assess water quality drivers across the mar region .\r\nthe project established a network of more than 20 partner organizations across mexico, belize, and honduras, unified monitoring methodologies, and implemented a regionwide sampling initiative covering 90 sites (45 reef, 45 beach). the resulting dataset, over 35,000 measurements, captured spatial and seasonal patterns in key contaminants, including nutrients, pathogens, turbidity, and chlorophyll a. a jointly developed water quality handbook, qa/qc campaigns, a regional database, and policy briefs form the backbone of this new decision-support system.\r\nfindings reveal that the mar is broadly impacted by high nutrient loads, elevated phosphorus, high turbidity and pathogen peaks coinciding with tourism season and seasonal winds. importantly, contaminants were detected directly on the reef, with no dilution effect observed across variables, underscoring the urgency for targeted interventions.\r\nthe project produced the first unified water quality baseline for the mar, enabling cross-country comparisons and pollution profiling through multivariate analysis. these outputs can now be used to guide regional priorities. by transforming dispersed monitoring efforts into a coordinated regional tool, this program provides governments, co-managers, and communities with the evidence needed to advance wastewater solutions and land-based pollution reduction at scale. it lays the scientific foundation for long-term accountability, coordinated management, and future investment in water quality improvements across the mar. increasing climate stressors highlight the urgent need for management that fosters coral adaptation and resilience. although high genomic variance drives evolutionary adaptation, assessing genetic diversity in the field requires significant technical and financial resources – prohibitive barriers for low-income nations with coral reefs. to address this gap, our interdisciplinary collaboration tested possible proxies for coral genomic biodiversity by extracting habitat and environmental variables from remotely sensed datasets including: habitat heterogeneity and “reef gravity” metrics from the allen coral atlas, fine resolution sst from nasa’s ecosystem spaceborne thermal radiometer experiment on space station (ecostress) instrument, and chlorophyll-a microclimate maps from sentinel-2 satellite imagery. reduced-representation and whole genome sequencing were used to estimate intraspecific genetic diversity of a range of coral species (with varying morphologies, reproductive modes, and sensitivities to climate stressors) from major coral reef regions including honduras, mozambique, the great barrier reef, indonesia and the philippines. we assessed the ability of candidate environmental predictors and their combinations to reconstruct observed variation in genomic diversity across our locations and regions using an ensemble-modeling framework, incorporating hierarchical and spatially explicit models including bayesian generalized linear and additive mixed models (glmms and gamms) that allow local estimates to be informed by response variation among species, global estimates to reflect differences across species, locations and ocean basins, and that account for spatial autocorrelation. preliminary results from honduras suggest that habitat characteristics that support multi-species diversity may not sustain high genetic diversity within a single species, as relationships differ between cryptic species and across environmental variables and spatial scales. additional analyses across regions are needed to determine whether these environmental variables can serve as reliable proxies for genomic diversity. developing a freely available global product to estimate coral reef adaptive capacity would significantly advance marine conservation planning and benefit coral reef managers and planners worldwide.",
