      "search_text": "coral reef consulting douglas fenner douglas fenner has the death of the world’s coral reefs been greatly exaggerated?? there are mountains of evidence that humans have caused the deaths of vast numbers of coral colonies; the evidence is undeniable.  graphs of monitoring data for areas around the world are reviewed here.  florida and the caribbean have suffered huge declines of live coral cover.  many areas in the indo-pacific have also suffered huge declines.  in florida and the caribbean, corals have not recovered.  but in the indo-pacific, most (but not all) have recovered.  the world’s reefs (as a whole) have not suffered huge declines in live coral cover because most reefs are in the indo-pacific, and most of those have recovered or not declined. there are several caveats, such as the fact that coral cover does not document coral community change, and the fact that most compilations of regional monitoring data do not extend to this year.  past results do not predict the future, because impacts of all kinds are increasing, and especially, global warming-caused bleaching is a huge, rapidly growing threat.  but statements that imply that the world’s coral reefs are mostly dead are greatly exaggerated.",
