      "search_text": "coral reef watch derek manzello morgan pomeroy derek manzello heat-driven functional extinction of caribbean acropora corals from florida’s coral reef the frequency and severity of marine heatwaves are increasing, resulting in widespread degradation of the function, structure, resilience, and adaptive capacity of ecosystems. coral reefs are one of the most heat-vulnerable marine ecosystems; heat-driven mass bleaching events have caused mortality and reef degradation for > 40 years, with impacts continually increasing in scale and severity. half the atlantic ocean’s reef-building coral species face a risk of extinction due to ocean warming and other threats in the coming two decades, with elkhorn (acropora palmata) and staghorn coral (acropora cervicornis) among the most critically endangered. in 2023, a record-setting marine heatwave triggered the ninth mass coral bleaching event on florida’s coral reef (fcr) since 1987.  degree heating weeks, a satellite-derived metric of heat exposure, were 2.2 to 4-fold higher in 2023 than all prior years in the satellite record.  in-water surveys (n = 525) tracked the fate of 52,356 wild and restored colonies across 391 distinct sites and found that 97.8–100% of the a. palmata and a. cervicornis colonies died in the florida keys and dry tortugas.  mortality was lower offshore southeast florida (37.9%), reflecting cooler temperatures in this region.  since the late 1970s, multiple stressors had already reduced the ecological relevance of acropora in florida, but the 2023 heatwave marks their extirpation from nearly all of the florida keys reef track and functional extinction for the entirety of fcr. on caribbean reefs, a. palmata has dominated the reef crest zone, while a. cervicornis has dominated the mid-slope zone for at least the past 250,000 – 500,000 years.  in florida, a. palmata was disproportionately responsible for reef construction over the holocene.  thus, the 2023 bleaching and mortality event in florida is not only ecologically but also geologically significant. morgan pomeroy mass caribbean-wide acropora coral mortality overshadows emergent thermal tolerance during the fourth global coral bleaching event (gcbe4), coral reefs across the caribbean faced unprecedented marine heatwaves and coral bleaching in 2023 and 2024. impacts to critically endangered acropora corals, in peril for over half a century, are of particular concern following these repeated events. here, we investigate mortality of a. cervicornis and a. palmata from 2023–24 throughout the caribbean, spanning 1,107 sites. heat stress on most reefs was more than twice the threshold recognized for mortality risk. accounting for unequal sampling, region-wide estimated median mortality at this level of heat stress was 75.2% for a. cervicornis and 87.6% for a. palmata. survival varied significantly by geographic subregion; median estimated mortality for both taxa was greatest in the greater antilles and floridian subregions, exceeding 90%. median mortality was lowest in the western and southern caribbean subregions, at roughly 7% for both species. coral inhabiting areas with historical heat stress ≥ 8°c-weeks and depths > 6 m experienced significantly lower (two-to-five fold) mortality, indicating these acroporids have some adaptive capacity and may find refuge in deeper waters. while remnant populations may be better suited to survive future heat stress events, the ongoing loss of genetic diversity, reproductive capacity, and shrinking windows for recovery suggests acropora caribbean-wide remain at risk of a functional extinction in the coming decades.",
