      "search_text": "coral vivo institute flávia guebert flávia guebert art, affection, and oceans: the role of the atlantic literature program in education for conservation the atlantic literature program, developed by the coral vivo institute, integrates artistic creation, literary narratives, and ocean literacy to strengthen emotional and educational engagement with marine conservation in brazilian public schools. the program’s main publications combine poetry and storytelling to address climate change, marine biodiversity, and human–ocean relationships. this approach aligns with studies that recognize poetry and artistic expression as powerful tools for shaping environmental awareness and fostering affective connections that motivate ecological action. through these creative pieces of work, the program seeks not only to inform but to cultivate new ways of sensing, imagining, and caring for the sea.\r\nthe program operates through partnerships with county offices of education along the brazilian coast, donating thousands of printed books to public schools for use in classrooms and interdisciplinary projects. these materials support the inclusion of ocean themes in curricula and provide teachers with accessible resources to explore marine conservation through art, literature, and community engagement. complementarily, the program promotes educator training through coral vivo education network meetings, a national platform for exchanging successful practices that combine creativity, science, and environmental responsibility.\r\nbetween 2024 and 2025, qualitative evidence revealed strong connections between students, teachers, and the unesco–ioc ocean literacy framework (brazil has stood out at the national level). schools formally incorporated ocean content and organized artistic exhibitions, field activities, and community events inspired by the program’s materials. during this period, dialogues were held with 27 county offices of education, and 35.712 books were distributed, potentially reaching 230.000 students.\r\nby positioning art and literature as epistemologies for environmental learning, the atlantic literature program demonstrates how creative practice can catalyze educational engagement in marine conservation. its experience reinforces the potential of artistic approaches to inspire care for coral ecosystems and contribute to the movement of reimagining reefs through interdisciplinary collaboration.",
