      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Laura Cotrino Salcedo",
          "search_text": "laura cotrino salcedo diving into stewardship: capacity building for community-led restoration at varadero reef building capacity within local communities is increasingly recognized as essential for effective coral reef conservation, particularly in regions where institutional support is limited or inconsistent. in 2025, corales de paz, in collaboration with local and international partners, strengthened this approach with the community of bocachica, an island in cartagena bay (colombian caribbean). the initiative developed diving, monitoring, and restoration skills among five community members, beginning with scuba diving and buoyancy control training, followed by reef check ecodiver certification and instruction in the marrs restoration method. participants subsequently joined the active restoration program at varadero reef, near tierra bomba island.\r\n\r\nvaradero reef, discovered in 2014 and designated as a mission blue hope spot in 2018, persists despite intense sedimentation, pollution, and its location within a major navigation channel. its resilience is challenged by fishing pressures and historical destructive practices, highlighting the need for active intervention. using the marrs method, corales de paz and the newly trained community members deployed 400 reef stars, restoring 400 m² of degraded reef. six-month monitoring shows approximately 90% coral survival, demonstrating the ecological resilience of varadero and the effectiveness of locally grounded restoration stewardship. \r\n\r\nbeyond technical capacity building, the program also fostered broader community engagement. an educational workshop reached 200 children in bocachica, promoting early awareness of coral reef ecology and cultivating future local custodians. recognizing the importance of diversifying sustainable livelihoods, the project is also piloting a guided diving experience led by the trained community members, creating opportunities in ecotourism and positioning the restoration site as an attractive location for aquatic activities. \r\n\r\ntogether, these efforts demonstrate how community-led capacities, ecological restoration, and livelihood diversification can intersect to support long-term, just, and locally grounded coral reef stewardship. this model highlights the potential for place-based initiatives to strengthen socio-ecological resilience and transformative reef conservation at multiple scales.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Diving into stewardship: Capacity building for Community-Led Restoration at Varadero Reef",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "db42af14-075b-44fe-ad84-0c647727fc57"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00379",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
