          "name": "Mathieu Reynaud",
          "search_text": "mathieu reynaud from populations to individuals: how environmental and biological variation shape planktivorous fish communities and performance planktivorous fishes play a crucial role in coral reef ecosystems by converting pelagic planktonic resources into reef biomass. they serve as key vectors of energy transfer from the pelagic zone to the reef, thereby enhancing overall productivity. however, the habitats supporting planktivorous fishes are under unprecedented stress global changes, foremost of which widespread warming-induced coral mortality. recent study develops the growing evidence that planktivorous fishes present a relatively stability on coral reefs subjected to major coral loss triggered by a series of disturbance events such as heatwaves, cyclones, or crown-of-thorns starfish outbreaks. investigating biological and environmental drivers that shape ecological performance at the community and intra-specific levels to gain a deeper understanding of how anthropogenic threats shape this essential ecological role that sustains reef functioning. we analyzed 20 years (2004–2024) of benthic and fish monitoring data around mo’orea island (french polynesia, south pacific) across three reef types (costal reef, backreef, and forereef). our results thus suggest that planktivorous fishes communities can persist despite temporal oscillations in coral cover that yield some, albeit not very strong, compositional turnover. oscillations in coral cover driven by these disturbances have long-term cascading effects on associated fish assemblages, as evidenced by the gradual decline of pycnochromis vanderbilti.  using chromis viridis as target species we investigated intra-specific variations in functional traits such as visual acuity, swimming capacity, resources assimilations or gonadosomatic index due to changes in biological and environmental factors. collectively, our findings indicate a relative resilience of planktivorous fishes in the face of human-induced environmental change.",
