          "name": "David Ory",
          "search_text": "david ory genetic connectivity among euphotic and mesophotic hydroids (cnidaria, hydrozoa) supports the deep reef refuges hypothesis in the southwestern indian ocean field genomics of extreme-environment and bleaching-resistant corals to accelerate reef restoration in réunion island as coral reefs face accelerating impacts from global warming and human pressures, mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces) have emerged as a key frontier for understanding reef resilience. situated between 30- and 150-meters depth, these deep reef communities remain poorly characterized, particularly regarding their biodiversity and their potential demographic links with shallow reefs. central to the deep reef refugia hypothesis (drrh) is the assumption that mces can replenish degraded shallow reefs, yet this relies on the existence of effective vertical connectivity.\r\nhere, we present one of the most extensive assessments of vertical and horizontal genetic connectivity in two islands of the southwestern indian ocean, using 9 hydroid target species as a model system. across 57 mesophotic dives together with 50 shallow water dives around réunion and mayotte islands, we collected and genotyped over 2,200 specimens using microsatellite libraries, seven of which were newly developed for this study.\r\nusing a comparative population genetics framework combining differentiation indices, structure, amova, mantel tests, and bayesian estimates of contemporary gene flow (bayesass 3), we identified a greater genetic diversity in mayotte than in réunion, a lack of connectivity between the islands and different patterns of vertical and horizontal connectivity within each island. \r\nwhile only two species in mayotte exhibited strong vertical differentiation between euphotic and mesophotic depths, three markedly contrasting connectivity patterns emerged in réunion: (1) horizontal stepping-stone dispersal, (2) vertical stepping-stone connectivity across adjacent depth zones, and (3) near-panmictic population structure.\r\ndespite these overarching patterns, each species displayed distinct connectivity signatures that differed between islands, highlighting the complexity of mesophotic biodiversity and the need for further investigation. critically, at réunion island, four species showed bottom-up gene flow consistent with the drrh, providing strong evidence that mces can contribute to the replenishment and long-term resilience of shallow reefs. in 2025, witnessing the most severe mass coral bleaching event in decades in réunion island, we tagged and monitored 60 of the few acropora colonies that survived in the lagoon of l’étang-salé - either by resisting bleaching entirely or by bleaching and later recovering. these rare survivors became our natural laboratory. building on this foundation, we initiated supercor-ai, an ambitious project that combines both cutting-edge genomics and ai-assisted biodiversity monitoring to uncover the mechanisms of coral resistance and resilience, and to design new strategies for reef rehabilitation under climate change. \r\nin parallel, we investigated a second natural laboratory: the corals inhabiting the island’s rock pools, environments characterized by severe daily variations in temperature, salinity, and light. studying these two complementary systems provided a deeper and more integrated understanding of the biological traits and ecological contexts that drive coral survival in an era of accelerating global warming and predicted coral collapse.\r\nfield-based minion sequencing coupled with target gene capture of ~2500 loci enabled us to disentangle the respective contributions of the coral host and its microbiome in bleaching resistance. corals inhabiting the most isolated pool hosted durusdinium symbionts and showed complete resistance to bleaching. in contrast, corals in open pools and lagoon habitats were dominated by cladocopium and experienced the most severe bleaching. corals from semi-enclosed pools displayed a mixture of species associated with either durusdinium or cladocopium, resulting in both bleached and non-bleached colonies.\r\ntogether, these genomic approaches uncovered key host genotypes, hybrid lineages, and their associated symbionts. this integrated perspective provides essential insights for identifying, selecting, and propagating bleaching-resilient coral stocks to support future reef restoration efforts.",
