        {
          "name": "Sayeri Paul",
          "search_text": "sayeri paul molecular traits of dom released by cyanobacteria and corals reveal divergent impacts on reef doc dynamics dissolved organic carbon (doc) released by benthic primary producers plays a central role in coral-reef biogeochemistry by influencing microbial metabolism, oxygen availability, and carbon cycling. yet the molecular-level contrasts between dom exuded by reef communities remain poorly resolved. here, we approached untargeted high-resolution lc–ms/ms to understand the heterogeneity of the doc pool of the benthic cyanobacterium lyngbya sp. and the thermal resilient coral pavona sp.. to characterise and compare the molecular constituents along with the reactivity of the distinct exometabolites, we applied mscc stoichiometric classification, cram categorization, and aromaticity indexing. redox-based indicators confirmed that lyngbya sp. doc was more reduced, with a more negative area-weighted oxidation state (−0.74) compared to pavona (−0.54). despite being more reduced, lyngbya sp. doc exhibited higher microbial oxidation potential, with a greater weighted δg°cₒₓ (81.32 kj mol⁻¹ c) relative to pavona (75.74 kj mol⁻¹ c), indicating that lyngbya exudates provide a more energetically favorable substrate for heterotrophs which could be justified by higher nitrogen to carbon ratio(n/c) indicating enhanced bacterial growth efficiency. higher abundance (~2 folds than lyngbia sp.) of cram (30.1 %) like compounds and aromatic compounds (17.6%) in exometabolite released by pavona sp. indicate slower degradation and contribution to long term recalcitrant doc pool; whereas highly unsaturated aliphatics and phytochemicals dominance in exometabolites released by lyngbia sp. reflect a chemically aggressive, bioreactive, allelopathic dom pool capable of stimulating microbialisation and oxygen depletion at coral reefs. overall our findings show that cyanobacterial mats release reactive, energy-rich dom that fuels ddam (doc, disease, algae, microbialization) positive feedback loop, while resilient corals produce reduced, recalcitrant dom that stabilizes reef health. these contrasting chemistries reveal probable mechanisms linking organism identity to reef stability under climate stress. further analysis on bacterial abundance and expanded metabolite database exploration are under process that may deepen these insights further.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Molecular traits of DOM released by Cyanobacteria and Corals reveal divergent impacts on reef DOC dynamics",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "9c3a8d72-b62f-4510-9399-a640ca15a222"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01327",
          "attended": true
        }
