          "name": "Darla White",
          "search_text": "darla white pathogenesis of acropora growth anomalies in american samoa managers in american samoa have prioritized coral bleaching and disease for research and management but effective strategies requires understanding disease processes.  in american samoa, acroporid corals are especially vulnerable to diseases including acropora growth anomalies (agas).  although the cause of agas are unknown, they are associated with reduced colony growth and reproduction and can lower population fitness.  we surveyed fagatele bay national marine sanctuary in 2011 and 2012 to: (1) quantify aga prevalence and incidence, (2) assess impacts on colony growth, mortality, and reproduction, (3) measure disease progression, and (4) test whether lesion removal is a viable management tool.  aga prevalence was 0.55% (se+0.21) in 2011 and 0.79% (se+0.17) in 2012.  between 2011 and 2012, aga-affected a. hyacinthus colonies decreased in size and most loss appeared due to breakage rather than tissue death. ga colonies developed partial mortality on prior healthy sections as well as within growth anomalies, and the aga case fatality rate was 8.3%.  reproductive output (eggs per polyp) did not differ between healthy control colonies and healthy tissue on affected colonies but egg numbers were sharply reduced within growth anomalies. aga is a progressive disease; 10 of 12 tagged colonies (83%) developed between 1 to 8 new growth anomalies between 2011 and 2012. in 2011, we removed gas from five a. hyacinthus colonies to test removal as a treatment.  all removal lesions healed well; however, agas regrew in 4 of 5 colonies, both at the removal margins and at new sites elsewhere. treated colonies appeared more resilient, showing less breakage and partial mortality than untreated colonies.  the small sample size warrants further study.",
