          "name": "Osmar Luiz",
          "search_text": "osmar luiz ocean-basin geometry shapes geographic range limits in reef fishes: a global analysis of longitudinal and latitudinal range asymmetries understanding the determinants of the shape of geographic ranges is central to macroecology, yet marine systems remain understudied relative to terrestrial ones. marine organisms, particularly coral reef fishes, inhabit highly anisotropic seascapes shaped by basin geometry, climatic gradients, and historical barriers. here, we analyse range shapes–comparing latitudinal-longitudinal relationships to neutral isotropic expectations for over 3,100 reef fish species globally. analyses revealed that all ocean regions deviate strongly from neutral expectations, with deviations mirroring regional geometry. in the atlantic, a narrow basin with north–south thermal gradients, species exhibited restricted longitudinal expansion compared to latitude. the tropical eastern pacific, a linear coastal corridor, displayed the steepest coupling (approaching 1:1 slopes). conversely, the indo-pacific, the world’s widest tropical region, showed the shallowest slope, where species expand thousands of kilometres east–west while remaining restricted latitudinally. consequently, oblate (east–west elongated) ranges dominate the indo-pacific, while the atlantic and eastern pacific host more balanced or prolate shapes. we further investigated how species traits relate to range shape and contribute to regional patterns of species richness. our findings demonstrate that ocean-basin geometry imposes fundamental limits on maximum range size, often overriding dispersal ability. two species with identical traits may achieve vastly different realised ranges simply due to the geometry of their inhabited basin. integrating seascape geometry into macroecological models is therefore essential for interpreting current distributions and forecasting shifts under climate change.",
