      "search_text": "durrell institute of conservation and ecology jessica c. savage jessica c. savage title: taking a deeper dive into the local ecological knowledge of aquarium fishers in kepulauan seribu, indonesia the marine aquarium trade remains dependent on small-scale fishers harvesting live fish and non-coral invertebrates from coral reefs, yet these fisheries are data-limited and the ecological impacts of harvesting remain poorly understood. as concerns over the sustainability of aquarium fisheries intensify, improved understanding of the impacts on target species and associated ecosystems is essential. through their direct and frequent observations of wild populations and reefs while free diving, aquarium fishers provide a unique yet underutilised source of local ecological knowledge. however, few studies have examined the local ecological knowledge of marine aquarium fishers. in our preliminary study on the contribution of aquarium fish collection to livelihoods in source communities in indonesia, fishers described challenges to pursuing this livelihood such as declines in target species, impacts of cyanide fishing and stewardship actions such as shifting between fishing grounds. in this study, we are examining aquarium fisher’s local ecological knowledge to understand the observed changes in wild fish populations and coral reef conditions, perceived causes of change and, what stewardship actions or customary rules aquarium fishers use to manage the collection of target species and protect coral reefs. in kepulauan seribu, indonesia, we are using semi-structured interviews and participant observations with both active fishers and retired fishers who were engaged in the fishery from the beginning. we expect to find experience-based insights into long term changes in species abundance and coral reef conditions and to explore what stewardship practices or customary rules, if any, that have supported the persistence of the aquarium fishery since its establishment in the 1960s. this study will reveal the wealth of ecological knowledge possessed by aquarium fishers and, in the absence of long-term monitoring of the impacts of collection, offering a valuable source of data to fill these knowledge gaps.",
