      "search_text": "east carolina university dr cindy grace-mccaskey cynthia grace-mccaskey coral restoration as a nature-based solution: interdisciplinary and inclusive approaches in the u.s. virgin islands in the caribbean, coral restoration is increasingly explored as a nature-based solution (nbs) that can support biodiversity, protect shorelines, and bolster livelihoods tied to tourism and fisheries.\r\nnbs are actions that protect, sustainably manage, and restore natural or modified ecosystems while addressing societal challenges, simultaneously providing human well-being and ecological benefits. while this definition captures the core premise of nbs, recent scholarship encourages us to move beyond a technocratic or ecological framing. instead, nbs should be understood as transformative, collaborative, and inclusive processes that require meaningful engagement with local communities and intentional efforts to integrate local knowledge and scientific practice. when designed and implemented through this lens, nbs have the potential not only to enhance ecosystem resilience but also to redress historical exclusions and promote more fair environmental futures. in this presentation, we describe ongoing efforts with communities in the u.s. virgin islands to co-develop equitable and locally appropriate coral restoration plans as nbs through the strong coasts project. we will present results from community-based workshops, surveys, interviews, and other engagement activities, reflecting on both our success and the challenges we have faced as an interdisciplinary team working to design and facilitate inclusive community engagement and foster equitable outcomes.",
