      "search_text": "experimental marine ecology lab yan zhi yap yan zhi yap vertical zonation of intertidal corals on singapore’s coastal defense structures intertidal coral habitats, characterized by highly variable and often extreme conditions, are understudied marginal environments. while the distribution of intertidal corals has been documented across natural reef flats, zonation patterns are typically described in relation to the horizontal arrangement of species assemblages. how these patterns are shaped by the duration and frequency of emersion stress across vertical elevations is often overlooked and remains poorly understood. further, to our knowledge, coral vertical distribution has not yet been examined on artificial coastal defences– which represent a rapidly expanding but ecologically distinct habitat. compared to natural habitats, these man-made structures typically exhibit steeper profiles, limiting area for colonization and intensifying competition. this makes them an interesting system for examining how coral communities are shaped across an environmental gradient in habitats characterized by high competition. in this study, we conducted 3 × 20 m transect surveys across eight seawall sites along singapore’s mainland and southern islands. real-time gps measurements paired with theodolite readings enabled precise elevation data to be recorded for every coral colony. results will allow species-specific vertical zonation patterns to be identified, offering insight into which taxa can colonise and persist in the most stressful portions of the intertidal zone. these findings will deepen our understanding on how intertidal coral communities are structured in these novel habitats characterised by both high physiological stress and strong competitive interactions.",
