      "search_text": "fisheries infrastructure development center toru kanyama toru kanyama fisheries agency's efforts for coral reef restoration using sexual reproduction in japan sexual reproduction in coral propagation offers significant advantages in terms of genetic diversity and the mass juvenile coral production. we have focused on the advantages and have been continuously working on coral reef restoration using sexual reproduction since 2006. the series of initiatives is divided into three phases, and we will introduce the results achieved thus far. during the first phase from 2006 to 2008, we focused on developing mass production techniques using sexual reproduction methods. we have developed a technique to induce spawning of adult corals collected from sea in land-based tanks, enabling the mass production at once. we continue to utilize this technology as a highly important one even beyond the second phase. during the second phase from 2009 to 2017, we worked on developing propagation technology using sexual reproduction, which consists of three stages: mass production, farming in nurseries, and outplanting. in mass production, we have developed a method that can be completed entirely in the sea by using a larval cradle. in the second phase, the issue was that it was difficult to expand the scale of coral reef restoration because people transplanted juvenile corals onto natural substrates. therefore, in third phase from 2018 to 2025, the scheme aimed to restore coral reefs by creating large adult coral colonies and continuously supplying large quantities of coral larvae to surrounding waters from them. in this scheme, the creation of large-scale adult coral colonies, termed coral spawning farms, is of utmost importance. to efficiently create coral spawning farms, it is crucial to develop artificial base suitable for coral larvae settlement and growth, along with techniques for efficiently releasing coral larvae. we developed these techniques through multiple tests in the sea. we will continue to advance coral reef restoration utilizing coral spawning farms.",
