      "search_text": "fisheries technology institute go suzuki go suzuki mass production of sexually reproduced acropora coral seedlings using “larval cradle” and “cube tube” accelerating degradation of coral reefs globally due to ocean environmental changes makes the development of large-scale coral restoration technologies an urgent priority. we have focused on developing large-scale restoration techniques utilizing sexual reproduction, targeting acropora corals, the most dominant genus in the indo-pacific reefs. we consider establishing larval supply sources, termed coral spawning farms, to be particularly crucial. this necessitates the efficient mass production of juvenile corals. we developed a coral sand-based square tube settlement device (cube tube) as an artificial substrate and established a “larval cradle” system capable of producing seedlings consistently in the sea, from egg collection to larval settlement. however, the yield rate of seedlings (the percentage of cube tubes with at least one surviving juvenile coral) is unstable, varying significantly depending on the nursery location and year. therefore, to establish a stable mass production method for coral seedlings, we compared the results of various juvenile coral rearing results conducted to date and made improvements. the results showed that: ① during the first six months after settlement (summer), utilizing algae-grazing gastropods reduces competition with small algae (cage mesh size 5–25 mm), ② from six months to one year, enlarge the cage mesh size to 90 mm to allow herbivorous fish to remove algae, thereby stably increasing yield. the future goal is to establish methods for spreading mass-produced coral seedlings over wide areas to realize large-scale coral restoration.",
