          "name": "Sharla Sugierski",
          "search_text": "sharla sugierski genetic lineage drives variation in resilience of outplanted acropora palmata sexual recruits acroporid branching corals, once dominant reef-builders across florida’s coral reef (fcr), have suffered dramatic declines in recent decades. following the 2023 marine heatwave, severe losses of these branching species have rendered them functionally extinct in florida. coral restoration efforts in florida have historically focused on asexual fragmentation of acroporids. however, the recent incorporation of ex situ sexual propagation into restoration pipelines has led to innovative methods intended to increase coral survival and resilience, including selective breeding, stress hardening, and genetic rescue. because research is still needed to inform genetic management of these new practices, this project experimentally tested the influence of maternal genetic lineage on outplanted acropora palmata sexual recruit fitness. recruits from three batch crosses with single, unique egg donors were outplanted at three locations representing distinct ecological regions along fcr: broward county, the upper florida keys, and the middle florida keys. outplant survivorship, condition, and growth were monitored for one year and compared across locations and genetic lineages to assess differential fitness. tissue samples were taken before outplanting and at one month and six months post-outplanting to determine if symbiont shuffling occurred after acclimating to in situ conditions or in response to thermal stress and if these shifts were influenced by genetic lineage or outplant location. initial outplant survival was high, with both outplant location and genetic lineage influencing survival. genetic lineage also appeared to influence resistance to and recovery from thermal stress. therefore, genetic factors may be important components of breeding strategies and outplanting designs aimed at increasing the survivorship and resilience of sexual recruits.",
