      "search_text": "florida fish and wildlife research institute sharla sugierski stephanie schopmeyer sharla sugierski genetic lineage drives variation in resilience of outplanted acropora palmata sexual recruits acroporid branching corals, once dominant reef-builders across florida’s coral reef (fcr), have suffered dramatic declines in recent decades. following the 2023 marine heatwave, severe losses of these branching species have rendered them functionally extinct in florida. coral restoration efforts in florida have historically focused on asexual fragmentation of acroporids. however, the recent incorporation of ex situ sexual propagation into restoration pipelines has led to innovative methods intended to increase coral survival and resilience, including selective breeding, stress hardening, and genetic rescue. because research is still needed to inform genetic management of these new practices, this project experimentally tested the influence of maternal genetic lineage on outplanted acropora palmata sexual recruit fitness. recruits from three batch crosses with single, unique egg donors were outplanted at three locations representing distinct ecological regions along fcr: broward county, the upper florida keys, and the middle florida keys. outplant survivorship, condition, and growth were monitored for one year and compared across locations and genetic lineages to assess differential fitness. tissue samples were taken before outplanting and at one month and six months post-outplanting to determine if symbiont shuffling occurred after acclimating to in situ conditions or in response to thermal stress and if these shifts were influenced by genetic lineage or outplant location. initial outplant survival was high, with both outplant location and genetic lineage influencing survival. genetic lineage also appeared to influence resistance to and recovery from thermal stress. therefore, genetic factors may be important components of breeding strategies and outplanting designs aimed at increasing the survivorship and resilience of sexual recruits. stephanie schopmeyer adaptive strategies to improve outplant success of land-reared sexual coral recruits large-scale coral restoration projects in florida have been active since the early 2000’s but the recovery of coral populations has been slow due in part to high predation and mortality of juvenile coral outplants. therefore, the development of outplanting strategies to increase initial survival of newly outplanted coral species is critical to improving outplant survival and increasing outplanting success. land-reared sexual recruits created from broodstock rescued from stony coral tissue loss disease were acclimated within an offshore coral nursery prior to outplanting and biodegradable predator exclusion devices (peds) were deployed around outplants to evaluate potential reductions in predation and increases outplant survival. corals acclimated prior to outplanting suffered less tissue loss and resulting mortality from fish predation while the addition of peds further increased survival. however, findings from this study indicate that outplant size plays the largest role in survival during the first year on the reef with larger outplants successfully recovering from predation impacts better than smaller outplants. based on a cost-benefit analysis, additional time and resources spent growing corals to a larger size prior to outplanting provides corals that are better adapted for outplanting while including additional efforts to acclimate land-reared corals to in-situ conditions further increase outplant survival and restoration success.",
