          "name": "Alain Duran",
          "search_text": "alain duran functional diversity of cryptic macroalgal assemblages of florida’s coral reefs coral reefs are among the most diverse ecosystems where macroalgae are commonly abundant (up to 100% benthic cover) and diverse (over 40 orders with rhodophyta presenting the highest richness). while larger and upright seaweed taxa (e.g., sargassum, dictyota) are relativity easy to identify/quantify, others such as crustose coralline algae (cca) and turf-forming species (e.g., digenea, ceramium) and regularly clumped together as a single group. the oversimplification of these cryptic species groups limits our ability to understand fully their ecological functions. we conducted high-taxonomic resolution field surveys paired with morphological and molecular identifications to assess the functional diversity of crustose and turf-forming algae of florida’s coral reefs. benthic surveys also included sessile animals (e.g., corals) and multiple abiotic factors (e.g., sediment accumulation, slope, rugosity) to evaluate florida's coral reefs' overall composition and functioning. we found low cca taxonomic diversity (less than five species dominated most corals), high turf-forming species, and evidence of reef habitat-specific distribution. light intensity, substrate slope, and herbivory shape macroalgal distribution at a small scale (within centimeters). additionally, we reported low coral cover (~1%) and low density (~ 1 coral m2) of small adult corals, which likely reflects the cumulative effects of years of disturbances. the presence and density of juvenile corals were negatively correlated with the depth of the sediment layer trapped within long, sediment-laden algal turfs, which is particularly abundant (>50% cover) in low complexity reef habitats (low-relief groove, low-relief spur top, and high-relief groove). our results indicate that current unsuitable habitat conditions (high sediment load) for early-life-stage corals may be an essential factor preventing coral recovery.",
