      "search_text": "florida keys national marine sanctuary alexandra fine katey lesneski alexandra fine from regulation to reef: developing a comprehensive monitoring plan to evaluate management effectiveness in the florida keys national marine sanctuary the florida keys national marine sanctuary (fknms) encompasses the largest coral reef system in the continental united states along with extensive seagrass and mangrove habitats, forming a complex ecosystem of immense ecological and socioeconomic value. since its designation in 1990, the sanctuary has aimed to protect biodiversity while supporting sustainable uses such as commercial fishing, recreation and tourism. early management plans introduced regulations and zoning to safeguard critical habitats and reduce user conflicts. however, the first sanctuary “report card” released in 2010 documented accelerating declines in coral cover, reef fish populations and habitat quality due to local, regional and global stressors. these findings made clear that the existing management framework was insufficient to reverse ecosystem degradation. in response, fknms and its partners spent over a decade identifying lessons learned and using them to develop more targeted conservation measures. the resulting restoration blueprint, released in 2025, establishes updated boundaries, new and expanded managed zones, revised regulations and a coordinated restoration and management strategy designed to rebuild degraded habitats, enhance resilience and mitigate ongoing stressors. evaluating the effectiveness of this new management plan is essential for guiding adaptive management and ensuring long-term ecosystem recovery. this comprehensive monitoring and assessment framework will integrate 1) ecological fish and benthic surveys; 2) passive acoustic monitoring to characterize human use and biological activity; 3) in-situ experiments on primary productivity and community composition; 4) restoration-based monitoring, including mission: iconic reefs; 5) assessment of habitat-support structures; and 6) social and socioeconomic indicators. this integrated approach will assess compliance with spatial protections, the efficacy of new zone configurations, changes in biodiversity and habitat condition, restoration outcomes and community impacts. together, these multidisciplinary tools provide a scientifically rigorous basis for evaluating the restoration blueprint, informing future management decisions, and offering a transferable model for assessing marine protected area effectiveness globally. katey lesneski comprehensive physical and biological monitoring informs restoration progress and adaptive management for mission: iconic reefs mission: iconic reefs (m:ir) is a long-term and large-scale restoration program that aims to holistically restore seven of the florida keys national marine sanctuary's ecologically and culturally significant reefs. beyond increasing coral cover, this national oceanic and atmospheric administration (noaa)-led and partner-driven effort will enhance species and genetic diversity, reef structure, and ecosystem function. stocking of key herbivorous invertebrates and a local stewardship program are additional components that will contribute to the success of this ambitious project. considering the spatial and temporal scope of these efforts and inclusion of novel approaches, the ability to apply adaptive management and appropriate course corrections as needed is essential. such changes are made upon evaluation of program progress and success, which in turn is dependent on consistent and comprehensive monitoring of both restoration and control areas. we utilize a variety of instruments and approaches to monitor key physical parameters on the reefs, including pressure sensors to examine wave attenuation, benthic temperature via live-transmitting smart buoys, and periodic water samples to track nutrients, chlorophyll, and carbonate chemistry. to understand changes in biological communities over time, we employ structure-from-motion photogrammetry, nighttime invertebrate surveys, and reef visual census surveys in permanent 10x10m restoration and control area plots. benthic cover and composition and fish communities are also measured biennially through the national coral reef monitoring program in stratified random sites, which allows for comparison of m:ir sites with similar habitats in other florida keys reefs to evaluate the impact of restoration efforts. in this presentation, i will review current monitoring methods, provide highlights of recent results across multiple timepoints, and discuss interactions between physical parameters, biological community metrics, and restoration outcomes. examining such interactions over time will aid in evaluating program effectiveness and success, and will inform needs for timely adaptive management interventions.",
