          "search_text": "janie wulff sponge apocalypse on coral reefs moderated by data as living coral cover decreases on caribbean coral reefs, other benthic organisms may be/seem relatively more abundant. to know if they are actually becoming more abundant, possibly at the expense of corals, requires time-series census data. non-coral taxa vanish after dying, so particular individuals must be followed over time. on 3 shallow coral reefs in belize and panama, we’ve monitored all sessile animals (sponge volume, coral and zoanthid surface area, and gorgonian upright planar area) within permanent plots for 17, 23, and 15 years. sites were chosen that are minimally harmed by humans, except by inescapable warming and acidifying seawater. in every case, mass mortalities and steady attrition have substantially decreased sponge live tissue volume and species diversity. reports of increases elsewhere, and surmises of overall sponge increases, raise questions about how and why sponge dynamics might differ among reefs, and if some apparent differences reflect method choices. methods are key. ecosystem roles scale with volume, as sponges are living 3d tissue. counts of individuals or area fail in meaning when sizes that range over orders of magnitude combine with propensity for fragmentation and partial mortality. sponge species differ in ecosystem roles and vulnerability to challenges, mandating inclusion of all individuals, identified to species. long-term monitoring is required, as short-term data give undue credence to blips. conclusions, from long-term monitoring using appropriate metrics, that sponges are diminishing, do not align with a popular narrative of sponges overwhelming reefs. clarity requires time-series, volume data on all species at more sites, soon: sponges are the only organisms that significantly glue living corals to the reef, feed hawksbill turtles and angelfishes, facilitate rubble consolidation, and clear water of picoplankton, including pathogens and bloom-generating cyanobacteria; and sponges also orchestrate a variety of water column nutrient transformations and protect solid carbonate from erosion.",
