          "name": "Shania Schull",
          "search_text": "shania schull direction, magnitude, and behaviors of coral-sponge interactions coral reef sponges remain underrepresented in restoration and management efforts despite their ecological importance and substantial species richness on caribbean reefs. although frequently portrayed as competitors of corals, in situ manipulations reveal that coral mortality increases by an order of magnitude when sponge neighbors are removed. long-term census data also show that sponges are declining in both species number and volume, contradicting assertions that they will inevitably overwhelm corals under anthropocene pressures. much of the controversy stems from the fact that some studies emphasize the deleterious effects of excavating sponges on corals, while others highlight the beneficial roles non-excavating species provide, such as reducing bioerosion of coral skeletons and stabilizing corals with compromised bases. interaction dynamics are further shaped by coral behaviors, including the deployment of sweeper tentacles and digestive filaments that can damage neighboring organisms and maintain space around coral tissue.\r\nto better characterize these interactions, i conducted a series of in situ experimental manipulations that quantified how sponges interact with corals versus unoccupied reef substrate and how corals respond to direct sponge contact. across experiments, sponges occupied vacant reef space more frequently than space occupied by coral tissue, suggesting that many interactions described as competitive overgrowth may instead reflect opportunistic colonization of available substrate. corals exhibited species-specific relationships with different sponge neighbors, patterns consistent with behavioral differences observed in the field. i also found evidence that corals may digest neighboring sponges using extruded digestive filaments, creating localized space between living coral tissue and adjacent sponge surfaces. an analysis of a year-long observational dataset reinforced patterns seen in time-series studies: few sponges actively overgrow corals, and both taxa are experiencing rapid decline. these findings emphasize the urgency of incorporating both corals and sponges into management strategies, as they share important beneficial associations and each provides distinct ecosystem services.",
