          "name": "Anya Salih",
          "search_text": "anya salih from colour to function: imaging-based evidence for energy-transfer pathways mediated by coral fluorescent proteins corals as kin: art–science collaborations, public emotion and emerging tools for reef conservation fluorescent proteins (fps) in reef-building corals represent one of the most sophisticated biological photonic systems known, yet their functional roles and the specific pathways through which they redistribute energy across tissues remain unresolved. while early studies identified nanometre-scale förster resonance energy transfer (fret) among coral fps, the extent to which both short- and longer-range mechanisms operate in vivo has remained unclear. fps belong to green fluorescent protein (gfp-type) homologues naturally expressed by corals, spanning cyan to red emissions that are naturally produced by corals. this study used high-resolution confocal microscopy, fluorescence micro-spectral analyses and fluorescence lifetime imaging microscopy (flim) to characterise the spatial distribution and energy-transfer behaviour of gfp-type fps in corals from the great barrier reef. imaging revealed structured networks of spectrally complementary fps across epidermal and gastrodermal layers, forming micro-domains closely associated with symbiodiniaceae-rich tissues. flim measurements showed donor-lifetime shortening in the presence of intra-cellularly adjacent red-shifted acceptor pigments, demonstrating non-radiative fret occurring at nanometre separations within intact tissues. in parallel, spectral analyses revealed radiative re-emission and re-absorption events consistent with energy transfer operating over longer distances, both within cells and extending across neighboring cells and tissues, where photons emitted by one fp population were subsequently absorbed by another. these combined findings indicate that coral fps facilitate a dual energy-transfer system, with fret enabling fine-scale spectral coupling and radiative pathways mediating broader redistribution of light through scattering tissues. thus, in addition to their recognised roles in scattering and spectral modulation, fp assemblies also mediate energy transfer that further shapes the light environment experienced by symbiont chloroplasts. by providing in vivo, tissue-level evidence for both radiative and non-radiative energy-transfer pathways, this work expands current understanding of how coral hosts use fp-based photonic systems to fine-tune internal optical conditions within the coral–algal symbiosis. for more than two decades, dr anya salih has collaborated with leading artists, filmmakers and musicians to bring the unseen cellular life and fluorescence of corals into public consciousness. her fluorescence imaging, confocal 3d animations and scientific advising have been central to internationally exhibited projects, including lynette wallworth’s hidden/depths, hold, coral: rekindling venus, awavena, and large-scale immersive installations such as at the world economic forum. these works have consistently elicited strong emotional and intellectual responses; at wef their impact was such that political leaders called spontaneous round-table discussions to address reef decline and the urgency of climate action. as scientific producer for mourning the great barrier reef with oscar-nominated musician anohni, salih contributed scientific content, underwater fieldwork and interviews with coral experts to the 2025 sydney opera house performances, later extended through anohni and the johnsons’ european tour. pre- and post-performance surveys (n = 264 pre; n = 143 post) demonstrated significant increases in ecological concern, emotional connection to the reef and belief in the value of art–science collaboration. during the performances, her message “find the place in nature that you love. go to it and think of it as your kin”, became a defining articulation of the work’s ethical resonance. building on this trajectory, salih has begun a new collaboration with university of sydney’s sydney environment institute to develop coral as kin: a project connecting coral biology with multispecies justice, ethics of attunement and public engagement. this initiative incorporates augmented-reality visualisations of coral fluorescence and stress signatures as future tools for policy communication and environmental decision-making. this presentation examines how immersive art–science collaborations grounded in rigorous scientific expertise can transform public understanding of coral reefs, cultivate kinship-based ethical relationships with marine life and provide powerful pathways for strengthening conservation values and action.",
