          "name": "Swaleh Aboud",
          "search_text": "swaleh aboud from stress to survival: what the 2024 mass bleaching event reveals about red sea coral reef resilience the red sea, long considered a climate refugium for corals, experienced its fourth mass bleaching event in 2024, providing an opportunity to assess reef resilience under extreme thermal stress. shams conducted three consecutive surveys (pre-, peak-, and post-bleaching) across 130 reef sites, including 40 repeatedly monitored sites, quantifying bleaching extent with photo quadrats and colony-level severity along belt transects. results revealed marked regional variability when expressed as a proportion of hard coral (hc). during peak surveys in september, the central red sea (crs) recorded the highest bleaching intensity, with ~69.5% of hc bleached, compared to ~47.8% in the northern red sea (nrs). post-bleaching surveys in november at common sites showed live coral cover and recent mortality of 50.5% and 30.1% in the crs, versus 75.4% and 10.5% in the nrs. the southern red sea (srs) exhibited lower bleaching (~20%) early in the season, with earlier warming (may–june) and slower cooling, suggesting impacts began before monitoring. colony-level analyses indicated that pocillopora and goniastrea were most affected in the crs, while montipora and porites were heavily impacted in the nrs. despite substantial bleaching in the nrs (~40% of colonies at peak), ~41% of colonies remained unbleached, and 46.8% had recovered by the final survey, while 21.5%, predominantly pocillopora, porites, and montipora, experienced the highest mortality. these patterns highlight the vulnerability of key genera, reveal strong regional differences in bleaching susceptibility and recovery, and indicate that even historically resilient refugia may be challenged under prolonged marine heatwaves. the high recovery and low mortality in the nrs support its resilience potential but demonstrate that refugia mechanisms were partially breached, suggesting that future, longer heatwaves may further jeopardize reef condition. these findings underscore the need for region-specific monitoring and proactive management to sustain red sea coral reefs under increasing thermal stress.",
