      "search_text": "general organization for conservation of coral reef in the red sea silvia vimercati swaleh aboud silvia vimercati spatial patterns of coral biodiversity and conservation status across the saudi red sea: establishing a national baseline the red sea supports coral assemblages that tolerate some of the highest seawater temperatures recorded for reef systems, yet species-level biodiversity data remain limited at the national scale. this study presents initial outcomes from a saudi national coral conservation program led by shams, which conducted the first comprehensive species-level coral assessment along the eastern red sea coast. standardized in situ surveys were combined with iucn red list categories and endemicity records to establish a national baseline for evaluating coral diversity patterns and conservation status. coral communities displayed clear spatial variation related to depth, shelf morphology, and reef geomorphology. across reef sites, areas with high species richness often coincided with the presence of globally threatened taxa and were typically dominated by acroporidae. these sites represent important reservoirs of coral diversity but also show elevated susceptibility to thermal stress and physical disturbance. in contrast, other sites supported more simplified assemblages that appear adapted to local environmental conditions, reflecting variable ecological responses across the region. endemic species were recorded throughout the study area, highlighting the biogeographic distinctiveness of the red sea and providing useful indicators for conservation planning. the resulting dataset consolidates previously fragmented information into a coherent national framework that captures spatial patterns of diversity and vulnerability. identifying locations where biodiversity value overlaps with ecological sensitivity enables clearer prioritization for management, protected area design, and long-term monitoring. this baseline provides foundational information for saudi arabia’s marine spatial planning and contributes to national 30×30 targets. it also offers a reference for assessing coral resilience and biodiversity trends across the wider red sea. swaleh aboud from stress to survival: what the 2024 mass bleaching event reveals about red sea coral reef resilience the red sea, long considered a climate refugium for corals, experienced its fourth mass bleaching event in 2024, providing an opportunity to assess reef resilience under extreme thermal stress. shams conducted three consecutive surveys (pre-, peak-, and post-bleaching) across 130 reef sites, including 40 repeatedly monitored sites, quantifying bleaching extent with photo quadrats and colony-level severity along belt transects. results revealed marked regional variability when expressed as a proportion of hard coral (hc). during peak surveys in september, the central red sea (crs) recorded the highest bleaching intensity, with ~69.5% of hc bleached, compared to ~47.8% in the northern red sea (nrs). post-bleaching surveys in november at common sites showed live coral cover and recent mortality of 50.5% and 30.1% in the crs, versus 75.4% and 10.5% in the nrs. the southern red sea (srs) exhibited lower bleaching (~20%) early in the season, with earlier warming (may–june) and slower cooling, suggesting impacts began before monitoring. colony-level analyses indicated that pocillopora and goniastrea were most affected in the crs, while montipora and porites were heavily impacted in the nrs. despite substantial bleaching in the nrs (~40% of colonies at peak), ~41% of colonies remained unbleached, and 46.8% had recovered by the final survey, while 21.5%, predominantly pocillopora, porites, and montipora, experienced the highest mortality. these patterns highlight the vulnerability of key genera, reveal strong regional differences in bleaching susceptibility and recovery, and indicate that even historically resilient refugia may be challenged under prolonged marine heatwaves. the high recovery and low mortality in the nrs support its resilience potential but demonstrate that refugia mechanisms were partially breached, suggesting that future, longer heatwaves may further jeopardize reef condition. these findings underscore the need for region-specific monitoring and proactive management to sustain red sea coral reefs under increasing thermal stress.",
