      "search_text": "global reef gavin miller gavin miller evaluating the responses of coral reef fish assemblages to local variation in crown-of-thorns seastar (acanthaster sp.) densities in koh tao, thailand. beyond attraction: temporal dynamics reveal shifting ecological functionality of two deployed shipwrecks on reef fish assemblages. shipwrecks can mirror predator assemblages of pelagic pinnacles but may lack the trophic balance of natural reef environments outbreaks of crown-of-thorns seastars are a major contributor to coral reef degradation in the indo-pacific, but their localised impacts on fish assemblages remain poorly understood. the current study examined the relationship between crown of thorns seastar densities and reef fish assemblages in koh tao, situated in the gulf of thailand. data was analysed using bayesian generalised additive models. seastar population densities exceeded regional outbreak thresholds across three sites over a five-year period, indicating sustained outbreaks, but effects on fish communities were generally subtle and context dependent. total fish abundance showed a weak positive association with seastar density, though with high uncertainty. at the functional group level, herbivores, particularly scarus spp. showed a moderate positive response, potentially indicating changes to substrate composition associated with acanthaster sp. predation and subsequent increased food availability such as with macroalgal abundance following coral mortality. contradictory to previous research, coral-associated chaetodon spp. showed a weak positive trend with increasing seastar density, providing insights into the relative impact of acanthaster sp. on coral reef ecosystems in the region. overall, outbreaks of crown of thorns seastar may influence specific fish taxa but are unlikely to cause wholescale restructuring of reef fish assemblages at the observed outbreak densities. these findings enhance the understanding of ecological dynamics driven by acanthaster sp. outbreaks in the gulf of thailand. artificial reefs in the form of shipwrecks are frequently adopted around the world to enhance reef associated fish populations and recreational opportunities to various stakeholders in tropical marine ecosystems. despite widespread employment, the monitoring of their post-deployment ecological performance is often insufficient. the role of shipwrecks acting as fish producers (increasing the overall abundance and biomass of species) or as fish attractors (pulling fish from surrounding natural environments and fragmenting populations) is highly debated; largely due to a lack of experimental matched controls and temporal datasets. the current study investigated the attraction vs production value of two newly deployed shipwrecks in the gulf of thailand by comparing fish assemblages between paired artificial and natural reef sites pre and post deployment. recent deployments demonstrated increased fish densities and concurrent declines at paired natural reefs, indicating an attraction effect. high-trophic level predators and planktivorous species were shown to be more strongly attracted to shipwrecks than other functional groups and likely act as first colonizers, supporting previous literature. in contrast, long established, ‘mature’ shipwrecks supported stable fish assemblages while adjacent natural reef environments displayed significant increases in fish abundances, suggesting a degree of biomass spillover. size estimates of fish assemblages highlighted signatures of enhanced production on shipwrecks, acting as elevated nursery habitats for some juvenile fish taxa compared to nearby nr environments, however this finding was limited to newly deployed ars, and was dissolved in mature shipwrecks. these findings suggest a nuanced perspective to the attractor-producer debate positing that shipwrecks may first act as attractors during periods of initial colonization, as well as stimulating early pulses of enhanced production, later maturing into established ecosystems with assemblages more consistent with natural reef environments. site specific differences emphasize the need for careful planning of future wreck deployments to maximize the ecological benefits of artificial reef deployments and reduce the risk of over-exploitation of commercially targeted fish taxa. artificial reefs are widely deployed to enhance fish populations, yet their ecological equivalence to natural reefs remains uncertain, partly because comparisons are typically limited to fringing coral reefs. such assessments may overlook how structural features and varying reef morphologies influence ecological outcomes. fish assemblages were compared across shipwrecks, pelagic pinnacles, and fringing reefs around an island in the gulf of thailand to evaluate how artificial structures of differing relief align with natural community and trophic patterns. bayesian multivariate models applied to 350 surveys from 14 sites showed that shipwrecks resembled pelagic pinnacles in mesopredators and higher trophic level predators, but had consistently lower representation of grazers and invertivores relative to both natural reef types. this functional divergence likely reflects reduced benthic complexity and forage availability; shipwrecks may support predator biomass but not fully function as ecological analogues of natural reefs. findings highlight the importance of aligning artificial reef design with ecological objectives and considering structural context in management strategies across tropical reef systems worldwide.",
