          "name": "Erick Geiger",
          "search_text": "erick geiger comparison of satellite sea surface and in situ temperatures across the caribbean during record-setting marine heatwaves in 2023 and 2024, coral reefs in the caribbean experienced consecutive, record-setting marine heatwaves. the spatial extent and severity of marine heatwaves on coral reefs are commonly assessed using satellite sea surface temperatures (sst) and heat stress accumulation metrics provided by noaa’s coral reef watch (crw) program. while critical for understanding the nature and evolution of marine heatwaves at synoptic scales, sst-only algorithms consist of smoothed and gap-filled satellite data over relatively large areas (usually much larger than the data grid size of 5km x 5km) and provide one temperature estimate per day at the surface sub-skin layer of the ocean (~20μm). therefore, sst-based algorithms do not capture small-scale temperature dynamics over complex reef environments, especially at coral-relevant depths, where wind stress, surface stratification, internal waves, tides, upwelling, bathymetry, and reef composition and structure can all influence the severity and extent of coral’s response to heat stress (i.e., bleaching and mortality). here, we compile in situ temperature data at the surface (~1m) and at depth (1–15m) from several monitoring programs across the caribbean to compare with crw’s sst and degree heating week (dhw) metric during the 2023 and 2024 marine heatwaves. further, we explore patterns in coral bleaching and mortality to identify areas where dhw algorithms over or under predict heat stress, and compare these with in situ temperatures to better understand regions of high diurnal temperature variability. incorporating in situ temperatures at depth to offset heat stress accumulation derived from satellites at the ocean’s surface will ultimately improve crw algorithm interpretation and performance.\r\n\r\ndisclaimer: the scientific results and conclusions, as well as any views or opinions expressed herein, are those of the author(s) and do not necessarily reflect the views of noaa or the department of commerce.",
