      "search_text": "great barrier reef marine park authority david williamson roger beeden david williamson coral protection outcomes of systematic crown-of-thorns starfish control on the great barrier reef resilience-based management is essential to protect coral reef ecosystems facing the increasing and cumulative impacts of climate change and human use. crown-of-thorns starfish (cots, acanthaster cf. solaris) are coral predators that periodically reach outbreak densities and cause extensive coral loss and degradation of reef habitats. here we overview the operations and outcomes of the cots control program in the great barrier reef marine park, australia. drawing upon long-term reef monitoring data, we assessed the degree to which targeted surveillance and manual culling suppressed starfish densities and protected coral across entire reefs and regions (sectors). cots outbreaks were effectively suppressed, and live hard coral cover was maintained across sectors that received timely and sufficient cots control effort, while sectors that received limited to no control effort experienced sustained outbreaks and significant coral losses. outbreaking reefs that received higher levels of culling effort had net increases in coral cover, while the rate of coral loss was more than halved on reefs with lower levels of cull effort. our findings demonstrate that proactive, targeted, and sustained cots control is an effective and scalable action that augments the benefits of marine park zoning management to protect coral and support the reef’s resilience. roger beeden strengthening the resilience of the great barrier reef under a rapidly changing climate. the great barrier reef faces escalating climate change impacts, necessitating a transition from proven ecosystem-based to resilience-based management strategies. the reef authority’s 2024 outlook report, climate change position statement, and reef blueprint 2030 collectively highlight the vulnerability of the reef’s values to the consequences of mounting greenhouse gas emissions and the urgent need for integrated mitigation and local adaptation actions. collaborative efforts involving scientists, traditional owners, policymakers, industries and the community are needed to inform and deliver adaptive management actions that protect reef resilience. the reef 2050 long-term sustainability plan serves as the multi-jurisdictional framework for actions aimed at protecting the biodiversity and cultural values across the reef and its catchment. the reef authority’s climate resilience and adaptation strategy (blueprint 2030) focuses on the reef’s marine ecosystems, identifying high priority intervention options. new measures build on proven actions including marine park zoning and the long-running crown-of-thorns starfish control program, that have demonstrated their capacity to protect ecosystem resilience. the reef authority remains committed to advancing its 50-year adaptive management journey using the latest scientific guidance, climate scenarios and stakeholder engagement to safeguard the reef’s ecosystems and values as the climate changes, consistent with the goals of the great barrier reef marine park act, for the benefit of all.",
