          "name": "Ashlyn Ford",
          "search_text": "ashlyn ford novel co-occurrence of barnacles and helminths within multifocal pink spots on porites spp. across scales in mo'orea, french polynesia coral reefs are a critical example of diverse and highly productive marine ecosystems, yet they are increasingly affected by diseases that contribute to declines in coral populations. human impacts such as overfishing and pollution can accelerate disease prevalence, making it essential to improve our understanding of their distribution and causative agents. examining the diversity of organisms associated with coral diseases can provide insight into the interactions that drive disease dynamics. our objective was to identify agents causing multifocal pink spots (mps) on porites, reported in 2021 and 2022 in mo’orea, french polynesia. mps are a distinctive immune response of porites, characterized by single swollen pink polyps. causative agents such as digenetic trematodes and endosymbiotic barnacles have been documented to trigger this response, which can reduce colony growth by up to 50% due to predation. \r\nsurveys along the north shore were conducted in june–july 2021, april–may 2022, and august–september 2022. to determine causative agents, we employed photography, histology, and dissections. photography targeted visible external agents, histology enabled cellular-level identification, and dissections examined internal agents. across these methods, endosymbiotic barnacles were consistently detected, while nematodes and potential digenetic trematode cysts were primarily identified through dissections. notably, barnacles and various helminths frequently co-occurred within individual polyps, revealing complex multi-organism associations. dna analyses are needed to further confirm trematode cysts, highlighting the importance of multiple approaches for assessing causative agent diversity in coral diseases such as mps. overall, this study documents, for the first time in this region, the occurrence of multiple agents associated with mps on porites, advancing our understanding of coral disease distribution and multi-organism interactions across the indo-pacific.",
