          "name": "Olivia Faris",
          "search_text": "olivia faris establishing thermal bleaching thresholds for montastraea cavernosa on florida’s coral reef marine heatwaves are increasing in frequency and duration, with widespread bleaching events and mortality observed on florida’s coral reef. however, thermal bleaching thresholds for corals in southeast florida are not well established, limiting the ability to forecast or manage thermal stress events in the region. this study was designed to establish bleaching thresholds for montastraea cavernosa, a reef-building stony coral, in southeast florida. ex-situ experiments were conducted where triplicate m. cavernosa fragments of 12 different genotypes were exposed to acute thermal stress as well as held at intermediate temperatures to simulate chronic thermal stress. photosynthetic capacity was assessed using pulse-amplitude modulated fluorometry (pam). these measurements were used to create thermal performance curves and model thermal stress responses according to experimental degree heating weeks (edhw) and temperature. bleaching thresholds were extremely consistent between both acute and chronic thermal stress experiments. overall, m. cavernosa reached an effective dose of 30%, representing a 30% decline in photosynthetic efficiency, at 3.83 edhw which corresponded to a temperature of 33.35°c. there were no significant differences in ed30 values across genotypes. m. cavernosa has been observed to bleach at 30°c in the florida keys, suggesting that colonies in southeast florida may have enhanced thermal tolerance due to limited previous thermal stress. future studies should evaluate thermal thresholds of other species in southeast florida to determine if similar patterns of enhanced thermal tolerance occur. this research has implications for predicting future bleaching events, determining viability of m. cavernosa for restoration, and informing key recommendations in future management plans.",
