      "search_text": "harvey mudd college dr cathy mcfadden catherine mcfadden the rise of invasive soft corals and their impacts on tropical reefs as shallow-water scleractinian populations decline in the face of ongoing environmental change, octocoral-dominated communities have emerged as an alternative state, exemplified by the gorgonian forests flourishing on caribbean reefs. in addition to these resilient native octocorals, invasive soft corals introduced from the indo-pacific have the potential to restructure shallow-water coral communities in the atlantic. to date, eleven non-native or cryptogenic octocoral species have been reported in the caribbean and sw atlantic. the most widely introduced and ecologically destructive of these species belong to the family xeniidae, with four species now recorded from five widely separated locations in tropical se brazil, venezuela, cuba and puerto rico. in addition, 2-3 species of nephtheidae have been introduced throughout brazil and have recently been recorded in the caribbean. the primary vector of introduction of xeniids appears to be accidental or deliberate release from the aquarium trade, while nephtheids have likely been transported on oil industry infrastructure. species from both families exhibit high growth rates and have extensive powers of regeneration and asexual reproduction, allowing them to rapidly monopolize substrate, expand their distribution, and making eradication difficult. both groups compete with native scleractinians and other sessile fauna and flora via allelopathy and direct overgrowth, impacting species richness and in some cases transforming diverse benthic communities into monocultures of the invasive species. frequent reef monitoring and rapid response to the detection of alien soft corals is necessary to control invasions, while increased regulation of the aquarium trade and education of hobbyists may help prevent introductions of invasive ornamental species such as xeniids.",
