          "name": "Zack Craig",
          "search_text": "zachary craig restoring kahalu'u bay corals through collaborative science and community action the west hawai'i division of aquatic resources (dar) has launched a community-based coral restoration initiative in kahalu'u bay in partnership with reef teach staff and volunteers. this project aims to conserve and restore the bay’s natural ecosystem through collaborative science, ultimately identifying best practices for community-led restoration. success of reattachment methods applied in the bay will be evaluated by monitoring the health and distribution of corals through monthly surveys that track the number, size, species, and health conditions of both loose and attached corals. these surveys are conducted within two distinct zones: a restoration zone where active reattachment occurs, and a control zone for comparison.\r\n\r\nthe reattachment process, which occurs, on average, over 3 days every three months, employs cement and two-part epoxy adhesives to reattach detached corals greater than 5 cm in size. snorkelers collect detached corals, cache them at a protected central location, and then replant them onto suitable substrates, ensuring that each coral is securely attached and photographed for documentation. to track progress, 20-30 corals are tagged each quarter for long-term monitoring. the ongoing surveys (20-40 per quarter) assess the effectiveness of reattachment methods and determine if these interventions are outpacing natural detachment rates.\r\n\r\nafter the first year, survey data will be analyzed to identify the optimal timing and conditions for reattachment and the most suitable sub-habitats for restoration based on environmental factors like wave action, sedimentation, and substrate type. the second year will focus on empowering reef teach to lead the project independently, including assisting with their permitting process, training staff and volunteers, and facilitating their transition into full project ownership. this initiative will set a precedent for community-led restoration projects for the conservation of west hawai'i’s coral reefs.",
