          "name": "Iliana Baums",
          "search_text": "iliana baums genome-wide identification of heat resilience markers and prediction of genomic risk in acropora palmata how demographic history and genetic diversity inform species-specific restoration approaches for millions of years, acropora palmata dominated caribbean reefs but is now critically endangered. heat-induced mortality has exerted strong selective pressure on the species. we applied novel genomic tools to reconstruct population history, detect signatures of selection, and generate polygenic scores for heat tolerance. samples from 100 florida and 81 wider-caribbean colonies were sequenced using pacbio hifi and illumina platforms, enabling demographic analyses that revealed an effective population size of ~10,000 in florida and two pronounced historical bottlenecks. using a novel haplotype reference panel, we imputed snp and indel data for 1,285 a. palmata colonies from the stagdb microarray database. population structure analyses confirmed a west–east caribbean division and indicated further substructure within each region; florida was uniquely admixed. effective migration surface analyses (feems) identified four major barriers to gene flow: the mona passage, yucatán channel, florida strait, and colombia–panama gyre. short-term heat stress phenotypes were measured for 196 samples from 98 florida genets using cbass (coral bleaching automated stress system) assays. genome-wide association study, combining whole-genome and imputed microarray data, revealed highly polygenic thermal tolerance. these associations informed polygenic score estimation. a caribbean-wide comparison indicated regional variation in heat tolerance potential. survival data from the 2023 florida mass bleaching event showed median polygenic scores shifted toward more thermotolerant phenotypes, consistent with directional selection. our results demonstrate that genomic tools can resolve fine-scale population structure, identify barriers to gene flow, and quantify polygenic variation in thermal tolerance. the observed post-bleaching score shift suggests that polygenic scores have practical utility in coral conservation, breeding, and recovery programs under climate change. acropora and dendrogyra corals are now functionally extinct along the florida reef tract – a pattern that may play out across the caribbean. conservation genomic approaches can guide interventions required to achieve recovery of these populations by addressing key  questions such as: how many founders do we need? from which source populations should the founders come from? which founders should be bred? how can we monitor genetic diversity, mutation load and inbreeding over time in nurseries and restored sites? and, when and how should we transfer genets between reefs? answers to these questions require data on present day genetic diversity and demographic history. this is because past population bottlenecks influence the drift load and segregation load in contemporary populations. historically larger populations have a higher segregation load that can lead to inbreeding depression when large populations decline. some previously recessive mutations are purged during the initial inbreeding but others become fixed, and are added to the drift load. populations with high drift load face elevated extinction risk. demographic reconstructions from genomic and geological data show that caribbean acroporids have experienced repeated bottlenecks over the past 150 generations, yet maintained larger population sizes compared to the less variable but rarer dendrogyra cylindrus. past variability in effective population size can influence genetic diversity. indeed, the acroporids have an order of magnitude lower genome-wide heterozygosity compared to dendrogyra. further, the length and frequency of runs of homozygosity indicate inbreeding avoidance in dendrogyra while mating incompatibilities in acropora are consistent with ongoing inbreeding depression. standardized short-term heat stress assays from four sites further reveal substantial geographic variability in heat stress responses.  from these results, recommendations for conservation interventions, including managed breeding, assisted geneflow and genomic-based selection designs are developed to support the recovery of iconic caribbean corals.",
