          "name": "Joseane Marques",
          "search_text": "joseane marques coral thermal tolerance after transplantation to fijian nurseries from a phenotypic and microbial perspective coral nurseries are central to conservation and restoration efforts, providing platforms to accelerate reef recovery. however, they remain exposed to heatwaves, which compromise coral survival and fitness. maintaining broad genetic and phenotypical diversity is essential for the long-term management of nursery-grown corals. despite extensive research, mechanisms underlying differential thermotolerance among coral populations remain unresolved, in part due to the complexity of holobiont responses, which result from the integrated contributions of host, symbionts, and microbial communities shaped by environmental history. to investigate these dynamics, we used a standardized heat-stress assay (cbass) to quantify thermal tolerance and holobiont responses in acropora spp. from two fijian reef sites with contrasting bleaching histories, serua and beqa. fifty colonies per site were transplanted for six months to a common-garden nursery, after which 6–8 from each source underwent an 18-hour cbass assay (ambient, +3°c, +6°c, +9°c). photochemical efficiency (fv/fm) and reflectance (r) were measured with a diving-pam-ii+mini-spectrometer. fv/fm was used to fit dose-response curves (drc), while reflectance was used to estimate pigment profiles and tissue optical properties. thermotolerance, expressed as ed50 from drc, was similar between populations, yet several phenotypic traits showed significant source×temperature interactions. beqa corals had higher initial pigment content and a linear decline in pigment and absorbance with heat-stress, suggesting strong baseline investment but limited flexibility. serua corals began with lower pigment levels but displayed a hormetic response, with a modest increase at moderate temperatures before inhibition, consistent with inducible pigment-based protection and greater short-term acclimatory capacity. these contrasting strategies indicate that both populations achieve comparable thermal tolerance through different trait combinations, shaped by environmental history. microbial community and host taxonomy analyses are underway to further contextualize these responses. incorporating corals with contrasting trait signatures in restoration nurseries could enhance overall fitness and phenotypic diversity, supporting long-term reef resilience.",
