      "search_text": "hi-lex corporation nanako kosaka nanako kosaka upcycling titanium swarf into porous scaffolds for efficient coral polyp propagation global coral reefs face catastrophic decline due to climate change and anthropogenic stressors. while restoration efforts are expanding, conventional fragmentation techniques remain labour-intensive and limited in scalability. this study introduces a novel, scalable approach: regenerating coral colonies from isolated individual polyps anchored on upcycled industrial waste. drawing on the remarkable similarities between coral calcification and vertebrate bone formation, we hypothesised that titanium - widely recognised in biomedical implants for its osteoconductivity and biocompatibility - could serve as an ideal substrate for coral growth. to explore this potential while promoting circular resource use, we investigated the utility of titanium swarf (machining waste) as a scaffold.\r\ntitanium swarf, characterised by its ribbon-like geometry, high surface roughness, and corrosion resistance, was compacted into disks. this process created a complex, porous structure mimicking natural reef crevices, ideal for initial polyp settlement. coral polyps were isolated using a salinity-adjustment method to induce polyp bailout without chemical agents and subsequently seeded onto these scaffolds.\r\nobservations revealed that polyps adhered firmly to the titanium surface and remained attached for over 30 days, demonstrating strong substrate compatibility. tissue expansion across the irregular metallic surface was evident, followed by the rapid deposition of a calcium carbonate skeleton. microscopic analysis confirmed the formation of skeletal ridges and growth lines physically integrated with the titanium micro-texture, comparable to osteointegration. furthermore, isolated polyps exhibited clear signs of asexual reproduction, including budding and tissue spreading. these results indicate that even minimal units can initiate viable colony formation when provided with a structurally and chemically favourable substrate. consequently, upcycled titanium swarf scaffolds offer a dual solution: a robust platform for high-efficiency coral regeneration and a sustainable strategy for industrial waste management.",
