      "search_text": "hiroshima university nurhikmah tenripada nurhikmah tenripada mantle color variation and depth-associated photosynthetic patterns in the giant clam tridacna crocea marine photosynthesis underpins coral reef productivity and is sustained not only by corals but also by photosymbiotic invertebrates such as giant clams. tridacna crocea is a giant clam species that inhabits very shallow reef habitats, where symbiotic dinoflagellates (symbiodiniaceae) experience intense and spectrally variable light over small depth gradients. efficient regulation of the internal light environment is therefore essential for maintaining photosynthetic performance while avoiding photodamage.\r\nin giant clams, this regulation is mediated in part by mantle iridocytes–specialized reflective cells that generate structural coloration and modulate light delivery to symbionts. in t. crocea, blue mantles predominantly reflect shorter wavelengths (purple to blue), whereas brown mantles reflect longer wavelengths (yellow to red), indicating differences in optical filtering within the mantle. we hypothesized that these optical differences influence symbiont photosynthetic performance and contribute to fine-scale depth-related distribution patterns, even across meter-scale changes in shallow reefs.\r\nto address this, we combined ultrastructural and optical analyses with field surveys across shallow depth strata (1–4 m). transmission electron microscopy revealed multilayered guanine lamellae within iridocytes, with lamella thickness varying among mantle color morphs. field observations showed that the relative abundance of blue- and brown-mantled individuals differed with depth, and effective quantum yield of photosystem ii (y(ii)) also varied between color morphs across depths. these results provide an overview of how mantle structural coloration may shape internal light environments, photosynthetic performance, and depth distribution in t. crocea, and establish a field-based framework for future studies of marine photosynthesis in reef ecosystems.",
