          "name": "Yuk Kwan Wong",
          "search_text": "yuk kwan wong ai depth estimation of structural complexity delivers photogrammetry-level accuracy from one image structural complexity is a key ecological indicator that informs coral reef functions. photogrammetry is the most standardised approach to estimating structural complexity by reconstructing 3d models from large image dataset sequences. despite offering high-quality visualisations of its reconstructions, photogrammetry techniques demand extensive computational resources (i.e., long processing times and substantial data storage), which constrain their applicability at large spatial scales, and, most importantly, users with limited resources and hardware capacity. to address these limitations, we developed an open-access, computationally efficient ai-based alternative for rapid estimation of structural complexity that requires only a single top-down vertical image as input (e.g., photoquadrats, hr-landscape plots). it employs a deep learning depth-estimation model to infer relative distances between captured objects and the camera at each pixel, generating a 2.5d model representation that can measure key structural complexity metrics, such as rugosity, fractal dimension, and height range. we evaluated the performance by comparing it with photogrammetry on both simulated coral reefs (i.e., to establish ground truth) and real reef data from moorea's outer reef. results show that our approach delivers high accuracy, with mean errors of ~15% for rugosity, ~3% for fractal dimension, and ~25% for height range, values comparable to medium- to ultra-high-quality photogrammetric reconstructions in metashape. from natural reef field data, our method strongly correlates with photogrammetry-derived metrics (r² = 0.84). importantly, our approach reduced processing time from ~10 min for medium-quality and ~80 min for ultra-high-quality photogrammetry to just a few seconds (<5 sec) and data storage from gb to mb (one image). overall, the tool will offer a rapid, resource-efficient alternative for estimating structural complexity and its associated metrics at large spatial scales, complementing photogrammetry workflows that remain preferable for comprehensive 3d reconstructions and habitat visualisation.",
