      "search_text": "hopkins marine station of stanford university brendan cornwell brendan cornwell measuring local adaptation and acclimation to improve restoration success using a large reciprocal transplant experiment in palau conservation practitioners are contemplating ambitious restoration projects to restore reef ecosystems, including by futureproofing for larger heatwaves using corals that are more bleaching resistant. bleaching resistance arises from the underlying genetics of the host, its symbiont, and their interactions, and they can acclimate to local conditions. thus, infusing a reef with currently heat-resistant colonies might not have a durable effect if those colonies switch partners upon transplantation or the successful interaction between partners is environmentally dependent. when transplanting corals across reefs, local adaptation on the native reef may in fact lead to reduced success. here we present the results of a large reciprocal transplant experiment using three species of acropora to measure how local adaptation and acclimation in hosts and symbionts contribute to bleaching resistance. we collected nubbins from 240 individuals on three environmentally divergent reefs across the palauan archipelago (total n=720) and reciprocally deployed them to transplant racks after measuring their bleaching resistance in acute heat stress tests. after one year, we re-assessed transplanted colonies for survival and growth. overall we found little evidence for local adaptation for these three species on these three reefs. however, we found clear site-level effects. for example, growth was highest on the most wave exposed, shallow reef, where transplant success also trended higher.  across all species and locations, higher symbiont density in heated nubbins of our experimental stress test was associated with higher transplant survival: the probability of survival increased if a colony exhibited a larger symbiont density in heated nubbins at the end of our heat stress experiment. in ongoing work, we will combine these phenotypic results with population-genomic datasets for both the hosts and their symbionts to begin isolating the role that host and symbiont genetic identity plays in determining phenotypic responses to warming conditions.",
