      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Bobbie Renfro-Price",
          "search_text": "bobbie renfro-price the roles of changing oceanic conditions and restoration in altering coral reef sponge populations in the florida keys national marine sanctuary, florida, usa oceanic conditions are changing locally due to factors influencing water quality and globally due to shifts in climatic patterns; these abiotic changes are altering biological communities. with estimates of a quarter of marine species inhabiting shallow coral reef ecosystems, reefs represent a relatively achievable area for sustainable management with a high yield for biodiversity preservation. poriferans are integral members of coral reefs and are experiencing declines and mass mortalities on caribbean reefs. this study elucidates the roles of changing oceanic conditions and restoration in altering coral reef sponge populations. three research objectives were used to address this aim: (1) the effects of localized water quality on sponge growth were tested using reciprocal transplants, (2) the effects of a hyperthermal stress event on the relationship between sponges and their photosynthetic endosymbionts were assessed via changes in the chlorophyll a concentrations in sponge tissue, and (3) the ability to restore depauperate populations of reef sponges was explored through the development of outplanting and husbandry methods. reciprocal transplants revealed that greater temperature and lower picoplanktonic food availability negatively affected sponge growth across transplants. chlorophyll a concentrations in sponge tissue throughout a hyperthermal stress event revealed sublethal bleaching effects. pilot studies to design the first-ever nursery and outplanting methods specific to coral reef sponge species successfully restored >2400 sponge fragments with the aid of >300 volunteer recreational divers over the course of 5 years with up to 72% survival at one-year post-outplanting. these data further our understanding of the need for and approach to reef sponge population management under changing oceanic conditions.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "The roles of changing oceanic conditions and restoration in altering coral reef sponge populations in the Florida Keys National Marine Sanctuary, Florida, USA",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "a5f39530-e880-486c-bc38-4dbe167baafb"
            },
            {
              "title": "Trophic Dynamics of Coral Reef Herbivores and Detritivores Across Shifting Resource Landscapes: Integrating Behavioral, Molecular, and Isotopic Insights",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "09fa81cd-b1db-40e5-b30f-d12c7e84e318"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01439",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Reanna Jeanes",
          "search_text": "reanna jeanes introducing i.care: restoring florida’s coral reefs using recreational scuba divers with long-term survival solutions coral restoration is a growing field, focusing on scientific innovation and genetic diversity. plans for restoration projects are typically driven by monitoring data in the first year post-outplant, but long-term outplant survival and ecosystem-wide data is rarely collected. biologists are usually the hands involved in outplanting to meet specific deliverables, leaving recreational divers unable to assist. i.care is a reef restoration organization based in the florida keys that provides recreational divers with opportunities to rebuild coral reef habitats. by partnering with local recreational dive shops, our team of dive professional staff and interns educate divers in coral reef ecology and supervise them during i.care activities. participants actively outplant corals and conduct maintenance activities such as scrubbing competitive macroalgae, removing benthic predators, and reattaching corals post-outplant. at the cluster level, 100% of outplants are monitored at one-week, one-month, and three-month post-outplant, as well as annually for three years. since 2021, i.care has educated over 4,800 recreational divers and outplanted over 25,000 corals from 18 reef-building species of three major groups: acropora cervicornis (acer), acropora palmata (apal), and bouldering (massive) corals. survival of corals was highest for acer, apal, and massives at one-week and declined through time until three years post-outplant, with each group having specific challenges. the thermal stress event of 2023 caused severe declines in acer and apal, identifying and prioritizing thermally resilient survivors as focal genets for future outplanting efforts. massives were minimally impacted by thermal stress but suffered intense initial predation, triggering the development of “cages” as predation exclusion devices to reduce finfish access to outplants. in response to these challenges, i.care evolves outplanting methods to increase both initial and long-term survival, while including citizen science divers to intersect community with effective science, instilling stewardship and active participation in protecting and restoring florida’s coral reef.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Introducing I.CARE: Restoring Florida’s Coral Reefs using Recreational SCUBA Divers with Long-Term Survival Solutions",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "66f6a836-74ca-44c3-957a-b9b47e05695c"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00843",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 2,
      "talk_count": 2,
      "geocode_level": "country",
