      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Célia Caillibot",
          "search_text": "célia caillibot thermal variability and ciguatoxin production: understanding the dynamics of gambierdiscus in the new caledonian lagoon located in the heart of the south west pacific, new caledonia hosts the world’s largest lagoon, a coral reef ecosystem increasingly exposed to anthropogenic pressures and climate change. the rise in sea surface temperature, the increasing frequency of marine heatwaves, widespread coral bleaching, and the proliferation of dead substrates may promote the colonization of macroalgae, which in turn provide a favorable habitat for benthic and potentially toxic microalgae. among these, the genus gambierdiscus is known to produce ciguatoxins responsible for ciguatera. this seafood poisoning is the most widespread non-infectious disease in the tropics. \r\nin the context of climate change, gambierdiscus may alter their dynamics, distribution, and toxicity, representing a growing threat to human health, marine ecosystems, and the local community. this study aims to better understand how thermal variations, whether acute or gradual, influence the genetic diversity, chemodiversity, and toxin production of gambierdiscus in new caledonia.\r\nto this end, monthly in situ sampling across several sites in the lagoon were carried out using artificial substrates (window screen (ws)) and passive samplers (solid phase adsorption toxin tracking (spatt)) to collect benthic dinoflagellates and their dissolved toxins, respectively. artificial substrates coupled with molecular analyses (qpcr, edna) allow us to assess genetic diversity and dynamics, and high-performance chemical analyses (lc-hrms/ms) enable us to identify and quantify toxins, including potential biomarkers. \r\nexpected outcomes include a better understanding of ciguatera dynamics in the lagoon across seasons and during marine heat wawes, the establishment of a reference culture collection of benthic microalgae from new caledonia, and explore the ability to predict risk areas for coral reefs in new caledonia. this study aligns with the theme “harmful cyanobacteria and microalgae on coral reefs: drivers, dynamics, and ecological impacts” theme of the symposium, by proposing a multidisciplinary approach combining ecology, molecular biology, and analytical chemistry.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Thermal variability and ciguatoxin production: understanding the dynamics of Gambierdiscus in the New Caledonian lagoon",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "28536ec3-5afd-4af8-8e94-d80f85c51da6"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00263",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
