      "search_text": "imperial college london margaux steyaert oliver kippax-chui margaux steyaert a global assessment of anthropogenic, climate and biogeographical drivers of eukaryotic diversity in coral reef cryptobenthic communities coral reef ecosystems host rich and diverse communities of marine species, providing a range of important ecosystem services which are now being lost at an accelerating rate due to multiple local and global stressors. the majority of reef biodiversity research has historically focussed on well-known and conspicuous taxa, often overlooking hidden cryptobenthic communities, which reside within the 3d structure of reef matrix. this limited scope severely hinders our ability to understand reef-wide responses to global change, in turn leaving us unable to predict the effects of reef degradation on biodiversity and ecosystem functioning.\r\n\r\nin a global collaboration between 12 international project partners, we have combined 370 sequencing libraries of standardised genetic data from autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms), across 143 reef sites spanning five oceanic regions, to determine drivers of the cryptobiome. across a highly diverse dataset (>90,000 otus), we show that reef connectivity, habitat area, fishing and cyclone activity best predict global eukaryotic richness on coral reefs. at a regional (red sea, coral triangle and pacific islands) scale, thermal history, nitrogen pollution and tourism are key predictors of diversity. we will highlight region-specific trends of phyla-level richness along gradients of anthropogenic stress, and show possible trajectories for key sessile and motile reef groups such as rhodophytes, sponges, annelids and arthropods. with over 130,000 metadata points collated across 115 fields, we will discuss the importance of standardised metadata collection to ensure the continued use and comparability of large-scale arms datasets. to date, this project constitutes the largest analysis of cryptobiome eukaryotic genetic diversity on coral reefs, offering new insight into the hidden biodiversity that underpins coral reef resilience, with broad implications for conservation and reef management worldwide. oliver kippax-chui investigation of coral larvae behavioural responses to settlement cues in still-water conditions using 3d tracking informing artificial reef design through hydrodynamic and particle-driven settlement insights understanding how coral larvae navigate their environment and respond to settlement cues is central to predicting dispersal outcomes and recruitment success under changing ocean conditions. while settlement preferences are increasingly well documented, the fine-scale swimming behaviours that underlie larval–substrate interactions remain poorly resolved, particularly in three dimensions. here, i present a novel still-water experimental approach for quantifying three-dimensional motility patterns of platygyra crosslandii larvae during the onset of settlement competency.\r\nlarvae were recorded at four and five days post-spawning, when active exploration behaviours typically emerge. for each trial, 100 larvae were gently transferred into an experimental tank and given a 5 minute acclimation period followed by 20 minutes of high-resolution behavioural recording. two synchronised cameras captured the tank from orthogonal front and side views, enabling the extraction of paired 2d trajectories that were subsequently matched to reconstruct full 3d swimming paths.\r\nlarvae were observed under four settlement environments: (1) a crustose coralline algae (cca)-encrusted settlement tile, (2) a naturally biofouled tile, (3) a freshly soaked clean tile, and (4) a no-tile control. tiles were suspended at mid-depth to distinguish vertical descent behaviours from directed swimming toward a potential settlement surface. this design allows us to quantify the distances at which larvae detect and begin oriented movement toward substrates, as well as changes in swimming speed, directional persistence, and exploratory patterns across treatments.\r\nby integrating 3d behavioural reconstruction with ecologically realistic settlement cues, this work provides new insight into how larvae modulate their motility in response to different substrate conditions. these results contribute to a mechanistic understanding of larval behaviour during the critical transition from dispersal to settlement, improving predictions of recruitment dynamics and resilience in a changing ocean. artificial reefs (ars) are increasingly deployed to support coral restoration, providing hard substrate in areas degraded by sedimentation, mechanical damage from coastal development, or destructive fishing practices. the 2025 global tipping point report emphasizes that reducing local and regional stressors, alongside climate action, is essential for maintaining coral reef resilience. manual propagation of corals onto ars remains labour-intensive and difficult to scale, making strategies that enhance natural coral settlement a critical pathway for scalable restoration. this work investigates how ar design interacts with hydrodynamics to influence larval settlement.\r\n\r\nan integrated modelling framework was developed, coupling computational fluid dynamics with lagrangian passive particle tracer simulations to examine how ar geometry shapes near-surface flow and larval transport. thirteen ars and two reference geometries were analysed using geometric and complexity metrics, such as curvature, surface area, and packing, alongside hydrodynamic features including flow velocity, wake zones, and vorticity. these outputs were incorporated into a particle simulator to visualise three-dimensional larval trajectories and quantify interactions with reef surfaces under hydrodynamically informed flow conditions.\r\n\r\nresults show that while ar geometry can significantly influence modelled larval settlement, the patterns are not always strong or linear and vary between structures. identifying which specific structural features promote larval settlement and how they alter local hydrodynamics provides an evidence-based foundation for informed ar design. beyond guiding the creation of new structures, these insights can support strategic propagation of corals on existing ars to locally modify flow and enhance recruitment.\r\n\r\nthis approach provides a visually interactive platform for assessing larval trajectories and settlement, bridging engineering, ecology, and design. the tools developed also offer avenues for visualising conservation work on screens or in vr, serving as a powerful medium for science communication to engage both local and global communities in reef restoration and protection.",
