      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Catherine Sabinot",
          "search_text": "catherine sabinot from totems to ancestor species to engage shifts in views, practices, and structures recent studies in new caledonia, including questionnaire surveys of over 500 people and semi-structured interviews with almost 200 individuals, have demonstrated the significance that the population of the country attributes to marine species. a total of 132 marine animals were spontaneously described as emblematic, with many of these being recognised as totems for kanak clans, making them key species from an oceanian perspective!\r\nthe term 'totem' is familiar to everyone in oceania. this polysemous term originated in the americas several centuries ago and has since been adopted by many oceanic language speakers. however, its multiple meanings mean it is sometimes misunderstood, particularly in the field of ocean management, producing an essentialising and simplified view of the world. after describing the diversity of the country's emblematic animals and the reasons why inhabitants define them as such, this paper will analyse the term 'totem' and its recent developments in new caledonia, with a particular focus on the role of a group of customary oceanic referents in the emergence of the term 'ancestral species', offering a way to achieve transformative change in environmental management. we will analyse its gradual appropriation in the management policy of the coral sea natural park, thereby reinforcing socially and culturally informed conservation.we will examine how this semantic change reflects a progressive shift in views, structures and practices aimed at achieving a just and sustainable world.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "From totems to ancestor species to engage shifts in views, practices, and structures",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "4db80b41-2ed7-4dac-9e35-349f64ccee66"
            },
            {
              "title": "Elasmobranchs as Biocultural Keystone Species in Coral Reef Social-Ecological Systems",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "ee62b70b-2c9a-420f-bf81-6a47fb86fb6f"
            },
            {
              "title": "Integrating Indigenous wisdom to shape institutional practices and marine governance.",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "5318fe45-e1b8-4639-9e05-3c6b83c2c273"
            },
            {
              "title": "Valuing Reef Passages as Social-Ecological Keystone Places: transdisciplinary research for their use and management",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "b57f94c8-4a1a-479d-b5b6-419d0ec91195"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01523",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Sebastian Guillermin",
          "search_text": "sebastian guillermin assessing physiological drivers of coral demographic success across reef environments coral reefs are highly vulnerable to environmental change, and their complex dynamics pose major challenges for conservation. understanding the mechanisms underlying coral decline is essential for identifying resilient versus vulnerable populations and the environmental conditions that promote persistence. to assess how environmental variability influences coral physiological performance and population dynamics, we tracked the fate of more than 4,900 colonies from four genera (acropora, stylophora, porites, galaxea) over one year (2023–2024) across 26 sites spanning multiple habitats and remote reef systems in new caledonia, covering 4° of latitude. we additionally quantified physiological traits–including chlorophyll content, symbiont density, photosynthetic efficiency–and characterized symbiodiniaceae communities in a subset of 455 colonies identified to species level. we found temporally consistent latitudinal and spatial differences in symbiodiniaceae assemblages, along with pronounced variation in physiological response, survival, growth, and recruitment among reef systems. by confronting these datasets, we examined how physiological attributes relate to demographic responses, a relationship that remains poorly understood in natural reef environments. overall, our study evaluates the physiological performance and demographic vulnerability of major coral taxa across new caledonia’s latitudinal gradient and advances understanding of the mechanisms shaping coral response to environmental variability.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Assessing physiological drivers of coral demographic success across reef environments",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "33e3c6d5-e89f-4698-ae8d-a283e4ea6965"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00702",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 2,
      "talk_count": 2,
      "geocode_level": "institute",
      "distance_km": 1804.6,
      "search_text": "institut de recherche pour le développement catherine sabinot sebastian guillermin catherine sabinot from totems to ancestor species to engage shifts in views, practices, and structures recent studies in new caledonia, including questionnaire surveys of over 500 people and semi-structured interviews with almost 200 individuals, have demonstrated the significance that the population of the country attributes to marine species. a total of 132 marine animals were spontaneously described as emblematic, with many of these being recognised as totems for kanak clans, making them key species from an oceanian perspective!\r\nthe term 'totem' is familiar to everyone in oceania. this polysemous term originated in the americas several centuries ago and has since been adopted by many oceanic language speakers. however, its multiple meanings mean it is sometimes misunderstood, particularly in the field of ocean management, producing an essentialising and simplified view of the world. after describing the diversity of the country's emblematic animals and the reasons why inhabitants define them as such, this paper will analyse the term 'totem' and its recent developments in new caledonia, with a particular focus on the role of a group of customary oceanic referents in the emergence of the term 'ancestral species', offering a way to achieve transformative change in environmental management. we will analyse its gradual appropriation in the management policy of the coral sea natural park, thereby reinforcing socially and culturally informed conservation.we will examine how this semantic change reflects a progressive shift in views, structures and practices aimed at achieving a just and sustainable world. sebastian guillermin assessing physiological drivers of coral demographic success across reef environments coral reefs are highly vulnerable to environmental change, and their complex dynamics pose major challenges for conservation. understanding the mechanisms underlying coral decline is essential for identifying resilient versus vulnerable populations and the environmental conditions that promote persistence. to assess how environmental variability influences coral physiological performance and population dynamics, we tracked the fate of more than 4,900 colonies from four genera (acropora, stylophora, porites, galaxea) over one year (2023–2024) across 26 sites spanning multiple habitats and remote reef systems in new caledonia, covering 4° of latitude. we additionally quantified physiological traits–including chlorophyll content, symbiont density, photosynthetic efficiency–and characterized symbiodiniaceae communities in a subset of 455 colonies identified to species level. we found temporally consistent latitudinal and spatial differences in symbiodiniaceae assemblages, along with pronounced variation in physiological response, survival, growth, and recruitment among reef systems. by confronting these datasets, we examined how physiological attributes relate to demographic responses, a relationship that remains poorly understood in natural reef environments. overall, our study evaluates the physiological performance and demographic vulnerability of major coral taxa across new caledonia’s latitudinal gradient and advances understanding of the mechanisms shaping coral response to environmental variability.",
