      "search_text": "institut teknologi bandung ken dedes ken dedes sea surface temperature (sst) trends and their effect on annual growth of porites coral in banten bay, indonesia sea surface temperature (sst) is a key parameter for understanding marine ecosystem dynamics, including the health of coral reefs. this study investigates sst trends in banten bay and their relationship with annual linear growth rates of porites corals, as well as links to enso and iod phenomena. six porites cores were collected from three locations–panjang island (ppsbp1), kalih island (pks1p1, pks1p2, pks2p1), and tunda island (pts1p1, pts2p2) using grab sampling to preserve growth structures. coral growth was analyzed with ct-scan imaging (processed in imagej) and x-ray imaging (analyzed in coralxds). sst data combined in situ measurements (hobo u24 and tidbit v2 loggers near panjang island) and daily oisst satellite data (2009–2024), from which annual means were derived. results show a warming trend of 0.0017 °c per year over 2009–2024. annual porites growth ranged from 0.826–1.236 cm/yr with colony ages between 3–16 years. statistical tests revealed no significant differences between transects or analysis methods; thus, transects with lowest deviation were selected as representative. the highest average annual growth rate occurred in ppsbp1 (1.120 cm/yr), followed by pks1p2 (0.865 cm/yr) and pts2p2 (0.771 cm/yr). all three colonies showed declining growth, with the steepest decline in pks1p2 (0.0194 cm/yr). correlations between sst and coral growth varied by site: panjang island showed a weak but significant positive relationship (r = 0.179, p = 0.031), kalih island a weak nonsignificant negative relationship (r = −0.178, p = 0.561), and tunda island a moderate, near-significant negative correlation (r = −0.598, p = 0.056). correlations between sst and climate indices (oni, dmi) were weak and nonsignificant, suggesting local sst variability is not strongly driven by enso or iod. overall, porites growth responses in banten bay appear location-dependent and exhibit non-uniform responses to climate variability.",
