          "name": "Keivan Kabiri",
          "search_text": "keivan kabiri multi-sensor integration for coral reef mapping: coupling drone-based mapping, satellite imagery, and diver-based observations in kish island, persian gulf effective coral reef monitoring in dynamic shallow-water environments requires approaches that simultaneously capture fine-scale structural detail and broader spatial patterns. in this contribution, we present an integrated, multi-sensor workflow developed for the coral reefs of kish island, persian gulf, combining uav-derived photogrammetry, satellite-based remote sensing, and diver-based ecological surveys. ultra-high-resolution drone imagery (~2 cm ground sampling distance) enabled precise mapping of individual coral colonies, bleaching signatures, and benthic classes, validated by comprehensive in situ observations and underwater photo quadrats. building on this, we incorporated bathymetric and geomorphometric variables extracted from field-measured depth data and tidal-corrected profiles collected during the same period, allowing us to link benthic patterns to local topography and reef structural complexity. integration with high spatial resolution multispectral satellite imagery (quickbird and worldview-2) further contextualized colony-level uav outputs within broader reef geomorphic zones, enabling cross-scale comparisons and improving classification consistency. the merged workflow demonstrates substantial benefits: enhanced accuracy from multi-layer validation, improved detection of bleaching and degradation, and clearer interpretation of depth-dependent habitat distributions. we also highlight practical limitations, including water clarity, scale mismatches, and positional uncertainties, and outline solutions for standardized acquisition and processing in data-limited regions. this study provides a scalable model for merging “eyes above and below,” supporting innovation in coral reef monitoring while strengthening regional capacity for conservation-focused mapping, particularly for understudied reef systems such as those of the persian gulf.",
