          "name": "Peter Doll",
          "search_text": "peter doll specific coralline algae regulate the recruitment success of crown-of-thorns starfish population outbreaks of crown-of-thorns starfish (cots, acanthaster spp.) remain one of the foremost causes of coral loss on indo-pacific reefs. for outbreaks to initiate and spread, large numbers of planktonic larvae have to navigate settlement, grow and survive as herbivorous juveniles, and complete an ontogenetic diet shift to corallivory. throughout this early life-history, coralline algae play essential roles as settlement cues and juvenile food and may therefore shape spatial recruitment patterns. yet, these inherently vulnerable early-life stages and transitions remain a black box in our understanding of this notorious coral predator. in a series of experimental studies, we demonstrate that specific coralline algae, especially melyvonnea cf. madagascariensis, disproportionally shape the success, fitness and survival of pacific cots (acanthaster cf. solaris). larval settlement rates for 15 coralline algae from australia’s great barrier reef ranged from >90% in melyvonnea cf. madagascariensis to less than 2% in some other species. this contrasting capacity of cues associated with different species to facilitate settlement may be linked to their surface-associated microbiomes and/or tissue-associated metabolomes. notably, we identified strong correlations between settlement and specific metabolites, some of which were particularly enriched in m. cf. madagascariensis. moreover, daily consumption rates of juvenile starfish were up to 11 times higher for m. cf. madagascariensis compared to other species, resulting in up to 38 times higher growth rates of individuals consuming this alga. another experiment corroborated that starfish feeding on this alga not only grow considerably faster and larger but are also more likely to survive and transition to feeding on acropora coral. together, these new insights suggest that the prevalence of specific coralline algae on reefs not only enhances individual fitness and recruitment success of this starfish but may also explain some of the marked spatial heterogeneity in the incidence of their population outbreaks and corresponding reef degradation.",
