          "search_text": "bernhard riegl jr. stability and decline: regional contrasts in coral size structure across a decade of extreme thermal stress the persian/arabian gulf (pag) contains some of the world’s most heat-tolerant corals, yet also some of the most environmentally extreme reef habitats. to assess how chronic stress and repeated disturbances shape coral demography, we compared colony-size distributions of four dominant species (acropora downingi, porites spp., dipsastraea pallida, platygyra daedalea) across two contrasting regions–the eastern gulf (western musandam) and the southern gulf (abu dhabi). the same reefs were surveyed in 2010 and 2022 using identical measurement protocols. across all taxa, southern pag reefs exhibited pronounced declines in colony size over the decade, and a. downingi was completely absent by 2022. in contrast, eastern pag reefs retained larger, more stable size structures, with porites spp. and a. downingi increasing in size. long-term sst records show that abu dhabi is ~2 °c warmer in summer and exceeds bleaching thresholds more frequently, while seasonal shamal wind events can influence thermal conditions differently across the gulf, aligning with the observed demographic divergence. these regional contrasts suggest that musandam may function as a climate-moderated refuge, whereas southern pag reefs are increasingly shaped by cumulative disturbance. identifying and protecting thermally buffered reef systems is therefore critical for sustaining coral populations in one of the world’s most extreme marine environments.",
