          "search_text": "reshma thachara kunnath exploring the basis of thermotolerance in pocillopora acuta around phuket island, andaman sea, thailand marine heatwaves are threatening coral reefs globally, yet certain coral populations exhibit remarkable thermal resilience. here we investigated the intraspecific variation in thermal tolerance among pocillopora acuta genotypes from thailand’s andaman sea to determine the key drivers of thermotolerance. employing standardized coral bleaching automated stress system (cbass) assays, differences in thermal tolerance were determined, supplemented with ecophysiological assessment, high-throughput its2 and 16s rrna gene metabarcoding approaches to characterize symbiodiniaceae and bacterial communities, and host transcriptomic profiling before and after the heat stress. we observed significant differences in thermal tolerance within the population. notably, tolerant phenotypes were characterized by high retention of algal symbionts, elevated host energy reserves, and stable microbiomes, while sensitive phenotypes displayed symbiont loss, energy depletion, and shifts towards opportunistic bacteria. importantly, bleaching susceptibility was not reflected in algal symbiont communities, which were dominated by the heat-resistant durusdinium in both phenotypes, pointing to the likely influence of the host in holobiont thermal tolerance. changes in bacterial communities, like core and indicator taxa, underscores the variability of the p. acuta bacterial community and clearly reflect the two coral stress phenotypes. host transcriptomic profiles of corals before the heat stress clearly separated into distinct thermotolerant and -sensitive clusters. tolerant p. acuta phenotypes were enriched in transcripts for energy metabolism and cellular maintenance processes. in contrast, sensitive phenotypes were functionally enriched in immune and stress signaling, including tlr, g-protein and apoptosis pathways. this pattern reflects a clear baseline difference in host transcriptional state, with tolerant genotypes expressing pathways reflecting intact symbiosis, whereas sensitive genotypes show higher baseline activation of immune and stress responses suggestive of symbiotic breakdown. these findings emphasize the importance of differential intraspecific responses of p. acuta to heat stress, shaped by intrinsic genetic host factors determining which corals emerge as winners or losers in a changing world.",
