      "search_text": "kalanihale uʻilani naipo uilani naipo community co-management: preserving the integrity of native hawaiian traditional ecological knowledge by honoring those that perpetuate the practices of place traditional ecological knowledge in hawaiʻi is not a discrete dataset but an ongoing practice of reciprocity that binds people, place, and marine resources. rooted in the practices of place, native hawaiian ʻike (knowledge) is carried through multiple bodies, including ʻōlelo (language) and ʻōlelo noʻeau (proverbs), moʻolelo (stories and historical accountings), mele (chants and songs), hula (dance), and inoa ʻāina (place names), as well as place-based fisheries management that foregrounded the health of resources above all other considerations.\r\n\r\ntoday, a western-modeled state of hawaiʻi government selectively “sees” this ʻike through legal and policy lenses such as constitutional protections for traditional and customary practices, the public trust doctrine, and statutory provisions.\r\n\r\nthis presentation will take the audience on a journey through time showcasing how communities, like miloliʻi, south kona (kapalilua), hawaiʻi, have kept the knowledge of their ancestors alive through perpetuating the practices that are the embodiment of the inter-generational place-based knowledge of their coastal resources while also examining the various legal frameworks developed over the years to bridge the divide between a western governance style and a native hawaiian world view. the journey will lead us to present-day, where government grapples with the need to reasonably protect traditional and customary rights, the desire to make management decisions informed by native hawaiian traditional ecological knowledge, and the growing public sentiment for community autonomy in marine resource management, while not appropriating the knowledge and practices of which the communities are the traditional stewards. we will conclude our presentation with an introduction to current efforts to define co-management in hawaiʻi and formalize co-management as a tool to preserve the integrity of native hawaiian traditional ecological knowledge by honoring the important role that community-stewards, like kalanihale in miloliʻi, play by perpetuating the traditional fishing and resource management practices of their place.",
