      "search_text": "kansai university masato ueda masato ueda minimally invasive polyp harvesting via metal clips: a regenerative medicine-inspired \"clip-and-grow\" platform conventional coral restoration heavily depends on fragmentation, which inherently injures donor colonies and limits scalability. to address this critical bottleneck, we present a regenerative medicine-inspired, minimally invasive propagation strategy that enables polyp harvesting without breaking coral branches. building on mechanistic parallels between vertebrate osteogenesis and coral calcification, we leveraged titanium (ti) - a material known for its osteoconductive properties - as a functional substrate to induce spontaneous polyp migration.\r\nin our approach, small ti rods (“clips”) were placed in direct contact with the surface tissue of acropora branches. polyps consistently extended onto the ti within several days, and by approximately four weeks, new skeletal deposition at the coral-titanium interface was confirmed. detaching the clips allowed the selective harvesting of viable polyps anchored to a thin layer of newly secreted skeleton. importantly, this procedure was minimally invasive: although a small mark remained on the donor colony, complete tissue regeneration occurred within two weeks.\r\nharvested polyps on the ti clips survived and expanded in ex situ culture, forming enlarged tissue patches. to demonstrate applicability to reef restoration, the polyp-bearing clips were mounted onto calcium-carbonate 3d-printed scaffolds. polyps successfully migrated from the clips to the scaffolds and initiated de novo colony formation, confirming that the harvested units function as effective propagules.\r\nthis “clip-and-grow” platform represents a significant advance towards sustainable, non-destructive coral propagation. it enables the generation of propagation units without physical fragmentation and provides a modular interface for integrating living polyps with artificial reef substrates, offering a scalable pathway for next-generation restoration strategies.",
