      "search_text": "kaust coral restoration initiative erika santoro erika santoro long-term treatment with coral probiotics mediates intergenerational microbial and epigenomic effects microbiome stewardship framework for science-informed and integrated reef restoration: a case study from the kaust coral restoration initiative coral probiotics have become a key microbial therapy that improves coral physiology, reshapes microbial and metabolic profiles, reduces mortality, and modulates the epigenome, making them valuable for science-based reef restoration. however, whether the application of coral probiotics can also induce intergenerational effects remains unexplored. to address this gap, acropora sp. 1 (sensu tawaha et al. 2019) colonies were inoculated in-situ (al fahal reef, central red sea) with either a placebo treatment (3.5% nacl solution) or a probiotic consortium consisting of four beneficial microorganisms for corals (bmcs), applied three days per week for eight months prior to their natural reproductive season. fragments from coral parents, surrounding seawater, gametes, and larvae resulting from selective fertilizations were collected to investigate whether microbiome manipulation via probiotics induced heritable changes in both the coral epigenome and microbiome. probiotic application resulted in hypomethylation of genes involved in pathways related to dna recombination and apoptosis, specifically to a regulatory two-protein, transposon-derived system in both adult and sperm samples compared with placebo-treated samples, indicating pronounced effects of microbiome modulation on host epigenetics. although no differences were observed between placebo and bmc-treated gametes, 96-hour planulae exhibited significant microbial composition shifts depending on their egg-donor treatment. additionally, bacterial strains were detected in coral larvae via fluorescence in-situ hybridization (fish), and customized probes targeting the probiotics strains will be designed to assess whether these strains were inherited from parental colonies. these collective outcomes suggest that microbial differences are driven by female gametes, and ongoing metabolomics analysis will determine whether microbial differences in the planulae are driven by microbial or metabolic heritability. our results provide the first evidence that long-term probiotic treatment can generate intergenerational microbial and epigenomic effects in corals, highlighting a promising strategy for integrating microbial therapies into restoration projects to enhance and upscale the positive impacts of probiotics. the kaust coral restoration initiative (kcri) at shushah island, one of the largest and most innovative reef restoration projects, is transitioning to its scale-up phase, where high-capacity coral propagation systems offer a unique platform for testing science-based interventions to enhance reef resilience at scale. to support this effort, we developed a microbiome stewardship strategy to guide the responsible use of microbial interventions. our strategy builds on the inherent flexibility of coral microbiomes, which enables targeted manipulation, or bioaugmentation, of microbial partners to rehabilitate disrupted coral health. current strategies at different stages of implementation, through in-situ and/or land-based trials, include: (1) identifying ecological or physiological bottlenecks where microbial therapies can meaningfully improve coral performance; (2) isolating and characterizing native, stress-resistant microbiomes to serve as stocks for tailored-designed probiotics; (3) administrating validated probiotics to enhance the fitness and survivorship of outplanted corals, which we also (4) monitor off-target organisms to support risk assessments; (5)  application of postbiotics (heat-killed cells), which demonstrate thermal-stress protection comparable to their probiotic counterparts while offering extended shelf life and potentially safer application; (6) applying probiotics to coral parents prior to the reproductive season, resulting in enhanced intergenerational benefits; and (7) extensive coral disease monitoring to (8) identify potential pathogens and (9) develop targeted probiotic solutions. our microbiome stewardship framework also incorporates the assessment of genetic and metabolic interactions between introduced microbes and their coral hosts, supported by previous in-situ and land-based trials showing that probiotics can induce restructuring of the coral microbiome and metabolome. while microbial interventions have mostly focused on adult corals, this strategy extends to additional life stages, including larval settlement and recruitment . together, these efforts underpin an integrated, evidence-based approach for incorporating microbial tools into large-scale restoration, illustrating kcri’s role as a model for advancing safe and effective microbiome-based solutions for reef recovery.",
