          "name": "Nicole Burt",
          "search_text": "nicole burt upwelling shapes coral trophic strategies and survival across depths during a marine heatwave pelagic resources fuel coral reef food webs and can shape reef trajectories under climate change. upwelling regularly supplies cool, plankton-rich water to coral communities, yet how marine heatwaves impact upwelling and resource supply is poorly understood. to quantify how a common coral modulates its trophic strategy in response to upwelling before, during, and after a heatwave, we conducted paired coral and oceanographic sampling on an outer reef slope in the central red sea. fifteen tagged colonies of pocillopora favosa at 5, 15, and 25 m depth were sampled bi-monthly from february 2024 to february 2025. upwelling was continuously monitored with an in-situ temperature sensor array, and we conducted bi-weekly sampling of particulate organic matter (pom), zooplankton, and inorganic nutrients on the reef and 2 km offshore to quantify biogeochemical variability in the pelagic resource pool. bulk stable isotopes (δ¹³c, δ¹⁵n) and fatty-acid profiles of coral hosts, endosymbionts, pom, and two zooplankton size classes were used to quantify autotrophic and heterotrophic nutrition in p. favosa. seasonal patterns in coral trophic strategies across depth were driven by water column structure. winter mixing homogenized heterotrophic resources across depths, leading to similar trophic strategies among colonies. as temperatures increased during summer, stratification reduced upwelling to shallower corals, leading to greater coral heterotrophy with depth.  by august, upwelling to 5 and 15 m ceased entirely, coinciding with bleaching and mortality of all colonies. in contrast, upwelling persisted below 20 m, and all deeper colonies remained unbleached. by integrating colony-level trophic strategies with oceanographic sampling, this study provides new insights into how corals shift their trophic strategy in response to resource availability and thermal stress. our results demonstrate that strong stratification can restrict upwelling and exacerbate the impacts of heatwaves on shallow coral communities by concurrently reducing food supply and the opportunity for temperature relief.",
