          "search_text": "sam kahng the trophic ecology of photosymbiotic corals in the lower photic zone using bulk tissue carbon and nitrogen stable isotope analysis to infer the trophic ecology of photosymbiotic organisms photosymbiotic corals create the structural foundation of coral reef ecosystems, which support high biodiversity and productivity in oligotrophic waters throughout tropical and subtropical oceans. these light-dependent corals can exhibit high abundance at depths >100 m and their distribution extends to extreme depths (>190 m). however, knowledge about coral reef ecosystems is primarily gleaned from shallow water studies using coral taxa restricted to the upper half of the photic zone (<80 m). in oligotrophic waters, the lower half of the vertical habitat for photosymbiotic corals receives <3.2% of surface par, and the photosymbiotic coral community is dominated by depth-specialist species typically within the family agariicidae. for example, species of the genus leptoseris are consistently the deepest taxa found throughout indo-pacific locations where the full depth range has been explored. with few exceptions, very little reliable information exists about the physiological function and ecology of photosymbiotic corals in the lower half of their depth range. despite ample contradictory evidence, photosymbiotic corals have long been assumed to increase their reliance on heterotrophy with depths as light exponentially attenuates and the potential for photosynthesis declines. to definitively address the knowledge gap on the trophic ecology of photosymbiotic corals in the lower photic zone, deep-water leptoseris spp. (≥100 m) were sampled from three disparate locations (red sea, east china sea, and south pacific) and analyzed via δ15n csia-aa to determine their trophic position. results of this unprecedented analysis will be presented at the conference and placed within the context of the known physiology and ecology of leptoseris spp. important implications of these results will be discussed, including a long-overdue paradigm shift in the role of heterotrophy in photosymbiotic corals. while carbon and nitrogen bulk tissue stable isotope analysis (sia) has revolutionized food web studies, their specific application to photosymbiotic organisms in coral reef ecosystems involves significant complexities which are not present in nonsymbiotic organisms or most terrestrial ecosystems. the use of bulk tissue carbon and nitrogen sia metrics to infer relative contribution of autotrophy versus heterotrophy in photosymbiotic organisms (i.e., δ13c(host), δ13c(host-symbiont), δ15n(host), δ15n(host-symbiont), siber, mixsiar) has proliferated in recent years due in part to their convenience and low cost. however, a comprehensive review of the literature reveals that the theoretical foundations and assumptions underlying their validity can be quite tenuous, especially for natural studies spanning significant spatial scales. to date, the available empirical evidence does not support the efficacy of these bulk tissue sia trophic metrics as several factors can exert a dominant influence on them independent of an organism’s trophic status. a review of previously published datasets reveals a lack of correlation between these metrics and with higher resolution trophic analyses (i.e., compound specific stable isotope analysis and lipid analysis) confirming that they are primarily influenced by distinct factors. these findings have important implications for a large body of literature on photosymbiotic organisms, their previous conclusions, and the prevailing generalizations based on these studies. specifically, historical assumptions about the energetics of photosymbiotic corals have long assumed that they must increase their reliance on heterotrophic feeding as light attenuates due to increasing depth or turbidity. in the absence of a commensurate reduction in metabolic demand, this assumption of increased reliance on heterotrophy appears reasonable. based on context-limited interpretations of select bulk tissue sia data, this generalization has become widely accepted as dogma and is routinely repeated in contemporary primary literature. however, a holistic reassessment of available data, including data from higher-resolution tools, now unambiguously refutes this generalization.",
