          "name": "Seonock Woo",
          "search_text": "seonock woo coral holobiont stress response to environmental change and the genetic resources marine environmental monitoring: benthic biodiversity and habitat mapping, monitoring technology for marine protected area corals play a crucial role for sustainable marine ecosystems and global carbon cycle. thus coral restoration is important in ocean engineering. the function of coral microbiome is more critical in non-symbiotic, azooxanthellate corals and the complex of community leads to healthy coral life and also the pattern of abundance and diversity shapes coral resilience and adaptation to environmental change. the high latitude corals are facing a decline or a distribution change by increased seawater temperature like as coral reefs around tropical area encountering a tremendous decline and bleaching event. in this study, we investigated the microbiome composition in azooxanthellate coral, dendronephthya gigantea and its larvae and compared the microbiome composition change after heat stress. the majority of otus significantly shifted in corals under reproduction period and in coral habitat seawater indicated distinction in the relative abundance of bacteria compartment/site‐wise. richness and diversity were higher, and more taxa were enriched in the corals under reproduction period and coral habitat seawater in summer. flavobacteria and alphaproteobacteria dominated corals under reproduction period and coral habitat seawater in summer. flavobacteriaceae and oceanospirillaceae showed the most dramatic difference between corals in reproduction period and non-reproduction period. flavobacteriaceae and rhodobacteriaceae showed the biggest composition difference between coral habitat seawater and non-habitat seawater. in the larvae settlement experiment, coral larvae settled on the microbiome coated surface 70% higher than non-coated surface and the normal polyp development were enhanced in group on the microbiome coated surface. we suggest that coral restoration through their microbiome could be a self-sustaining tool in worldwide coral decline. this work was supported by marine biotics project (20210469) funded by ministry of ocean and fisheries, korea coral reefs occur in over 100 countries and territories and are one of the most threatened ecosystems in the world. the global coverage of living coral has declined by 50% since the 1950s, whilst at least 63% of coral-reef-associated biodiversity has declined. the global coral reef monitoring network presented that between 2009 to 2022, a progressive loss of live coral cover on the world’s coral reefs amounting to 14% was recorded. the great barrier reef suffered its worst summer on record enduring cyclones, flooding and mass coral bleaching in 23/24. the fourth global bleaching event in april 2024, marks a critical moment for global environmental action, highlighting the threats facing coral reef ecosystems. urgent, collective action is essential to address this crisis and safeguard the ecological and economic benefits coral reefs provide for future generations. a marine protected area (mpa) is a legally designated and managed marine area as where human activities like fishing and development etc., are restricted to protect the habitats and spawning grounds of marine life. it aims to maintatin biodiversity, restore fisheries resources, respond to climate change by protecting carbon sinks and ficiliatate marine research and education. the international movement to designate 30% of the world's oceans as mpa by 2030 is accelerating with protecting the high seas with the agreement on biological diversity beyond national jurisdiction but currently, approximately 9.6% of the world's oceans are protected areas, but only about 2-3% are 'no-take' areas where fishing is completely prohibited. reflecting the current status, the priotising of placement of mpa for biodiversity protection and managing technology will make the goals possible and can maximise its benefits. by using a holistic set of biodiversity measures, from species to ecosystems including habitats, and environmental data, the monitoring technology can quantify and present the effect of management in future.",
