      "search_text": "kochi university yuji namura yuji namura enhanced branch formation in temperate acropora muricata under electric field exposure the recovery of reef building corals requires the development of effective transplantation techniques. this study examined the growth enhancement effect of exposing corals to an electric field. \r\na table-shaped iron frame with a 10 cm grid was constructed using 10 mm steel bars. in the experimental group, aluminum alloy anode plates were attached to generate a weak current, exposing corals to an electric field (10–50 ma/m²). in june 2025, fragments of acropora muricata approximately 8 cm in length were collected at nishidomari, otsuki town, kochi prefecture, japan, and fixed to the intersections of the frame. lateral photographs were taken at 0, 3, 12, and 24 weeks. branches were categorized as primary, secondary, and tertiary, and their lengths and numbers were measured.\r\nboth groups showed marked growth between weeks 3 and 12, and growth enhancement in the experimental group was observed at week 12. while the number of secondary branches did not differ between groups, the experimental group exhibited a clearly higher number of tertiary branches. “secondary branches bearing multiple tertiary branches” which were rarely observed in the control group, were frequently recorded in the experimental group. no differences were detected in linear extension per branch. comparison of fragments with higher branching showed that the experimental group retained a greater number of branches at week 24. these results suggest that exposure to an electric field promotes branching patterns not previously reported in a. muricata, which may contribute to overall growth enhancement.",
      "country_code": "JP",
      "country": "Japan",
      "connection_count": 1
