      "search_text": "kyushu university dr may jandang suppakarn jandang seasonal variation in microplastic retention among coral components in five tropical reef species microplastic (mp) pollution is increasingly documented across aquatic environments, yet seasonal variation in mp accumulation within coral reef ecosystems remains insufficiently understood. this study examined the influence of monsoonal seasonality on mp abundance and characteristics in five reef-building coral species–pocillopora cf. damicornis, porites lutea, lobophyllia sp., platygyra sinensis, and turbinaria peltata–together with surrounding sediment and surface water at sichang island in the gulf of thailand.\r\nsamples were collected during both wet and dry seasons to evaluate seasonal dynamics across environmental matrices and coral components. surface waters contained considerably higher mp concentrations during the wet season (17.58 ± 21.68 particles m⁻³) than the dry season (0.19 ± 0.25 particles m⁻³), driven by increased terrestrial runoff and enhanced mixing of the upper water column. in contrast, sediment mp abundance was greater in the dry season (380 ± 1.00 particles kg⁻¹ d.w.), likely reflecting reduced hydrodynamic resuspension during calmer conditions. among the corals, pocillopora cf. damicornis showed the highest mp load (2.14 ± 0.03 particles g⁻¹ w.w.; p < 0.05). mp profiles remained consistent across seasons, dominated by black fragments (101–200 µm) and nylon.\r\nprincipal component analysis revealed three distinct clusters linked to water-, coral-, and sediment-associated mp profiles. corals also exhibited the highest polymer diversity (29 types), indicating prolonged mp integration from multiple sources. despite pronounced monsoon-driven variability in environmental mp concentrations, coral mp loads for each species remained comparatively stable across mucus, tissue, and skeleton. although fluctuations in waterborne mps might be expected to alter mucus-associated mps, periodic mucus shedding likely buffers short-term changes. collectively, these results reveal that corals are chronically exposed to mps and reinforce the need to address plastic contamination to sustain coral reef ecosystems under increasing environmental pressure.",
