          "name": "Elana Kysil",
          "search_text": "elana kysil integrative omics of acute heat stress in stylophora pistillata and heliopora coerulea reveal divergent cellular responses and their decoupling from bleaching phenotypes closed-system exposures reveal species-specific sensitivity of reef-building corals to polymeric nanoparticles and carbon nanomaterials widespread coral bleaching driven by ocean warming continues to degrade reefs. it drives the need to better understand the cellular events initiating this process across the holobiont to support the development of effective mitigation strategies. to examine how different corals cope with acute heat stress, we compared responses of the expectedly more heat-tolerant heliopora coerulea and the well-studied model species stylophora pistillata. both were exposed to a ramp from 25.5 °c to 32.5 °c over four days, followed by a three-day heat hold, and their photophysiology, holobiont proteomes and methanol-extractable metabolomes were analysed. despite almost identical reductions in symbiont density and photochemical efficiency, the two species showed distinct molecular responses. core thermal stress elements were conserved, including heat shock and unfolded protein responses, and both corals exhibited markers of increased host amino acid catabolism, β-oxidation, membrane and organelle lipid remodelling, and activation of immune pathways. however, s. pistillata showed signs of host mitochondrial dysfunction and energy deficit on multiple levels (potential mitochondria fragmentation, impairment of autophagy and proteolysis), and its symbionts displayed signatures of chloroplast damage (an increase in chlorophyll-related metabolites and more frequent chlorotic dinoflagellate cells), whereas h. coerulea largely maintained metabolic stability. these differences suggest that although both holobionts activated a shared stress response module, increasing photodamage and energy imbalance put s. pistillata at greater risk under prolonged warming. one possible explanation for h. coerulea’s paling without notable symbiont’s chlorosis may stem from a more effective regulation of its symbiont population. in contrast, s. pistillata’s response may reflect a later-stage symbiont-expulsion process triggered after algal impairment disrupts host homeostasis. these interpretations might offer a new route to enhance coral heat tolerance by strengthening host-driven symbiont regulation. additionally, macroscopic bleaching readouts can mask divergent cellular responses, highlighting the need for diagnostic tools beyond visual paling and pulse-amplitude modulated fluorometry. plastics are now established as widespread pollutants, and their degradation into micro- and nanoparticles poses risks to coastal ecosystems such as coral reefs. emerging advanced materials may follow comparable environmental pathways, but their engineered nanoscale and surface features could lead to different biological interactions and ecological outcomes. anticipating the risks associated to such pollutants requires evaluating their effects for corals across organismal levels. we developed a mid- to long term closed system exposure protocol to analyse the impact of common polymeric nanoparticles and advanced carbon nanomaterials, and tested their impacts on tropical coral physiology and metabolism. clonal fragments of four reef building corals (stylophora pistillata, heliopora coerulea, montipora foliosa, acropora valida) were exposed for eight weeks to fluorescent polystyrene, polypropylene, polyethylene, and silica beads, green-emitting graphene quantum dots, or multi walled carbon nanotubes with weekly water and treatment renewal. the coral holobiont photophysiology responses were monitored regularly by pulse-amplitude modulated fluorometry, and final outcomes were assessed through liquid chromatography coupled to mass spectrometry-based metabolomics and proteomics. graphene quantum dots caused the most pronounced visual responses, removing the white tip coloration indicating growth in s. pistillata, and inducing tissue recession in s. pistillata and m. foliosa. polymeric nanoparticles (polyethylene, polypropylene) did not induce visible tissue damage, but significantly elevated photosynthetic efficiency from week three, while symbiont density was not altered. a. valida and h. coerulea showed minimal responses across all treatments. these preliminary findings demonstrate that certain advanced nanomaterials, such as the graphene quantum dots in our study, can affect coral health, underscoring the urgency of incorporating advanced materials into coral-focused ecotoxicology. ongoing complementary metabolomic and proteomic analyses will resolve pathway-level alterations and host-symbiont stress signatures, enabling mechanistic interpretation of the observed phenotypes.",
