      "search_text": "lingnan university emily chei novia kayfetz-vuong yingxin li emily chei coral trophic plasticity and acclimatization across a water quality gradient as mixotrophic organisms, scleractinian corals rely on autotrophy through their symbiosis with dinoflagellates, but they are also able to use heterotrophy to supplement their energetic and metabolic needs. in particular, the ability to upregulate feeding has been shown to aid coral survival during instances such as bleaching, low light, or ocean acidification. nutritional flexibility across taxa, however, is not well understood, and it is unclear how nutritional dynamics within the symbiosis change under decreased water quality, an increasingly prevalent concern with the rise of urbanization in coastal regions. here, three genera of corals (acropora, porites, and platygyra) were transplanted to sites spanning a water quality gradient to assess coral trophic plasticity and acclimatization. after six months, transplants were collected and processed for bulk tissue δ13c and δ15n stable isotope values, and stable isotope bayesian ellipses in r (siber) was used to determine trophic strategy via separation of paired host and symbiont ellipses. platygyra had the largest overall host and symbiont isotopic niche separation and, along with porites, showed greater heterotrophy correlated with decreased water quality as a likely response to higher turbidity and light attenuation. conversely, acropora exhibited high autotrophic reliance that did not differ consistently across sites. compound specific stable isotope analysis of amino acids (csia-aa) was performed on a subset of samples and further confirmed genus-specific capacities for trophic plasticity, as well as higher coral trophic positions at lower water quality sites. the results of this study emphasize the importance of nutritional flexibility in acclimatization to environmental stressors and provide a framework for understanding coral resilience in an urbanized marine environment. novia kayfetz-vuong calibrating the mixotrophy spectrum: quantifying nutrient inputs in three corals with different trophic strategies reef-building corals are adapted to a stable, low-nutrient environment through mutualism with endosymbiotic symbiodiniaceae algae. this relationship enables mixotrophy, combining heterotrophic feeding and autotrophic photosynthesis. however, coral holobionts are highly sensitive to disturbances such as eutrophication and warming. eutrophication can reduce coral bleaching resistance, and corals more reliant on autotrophy are generally more susceptible to warming, whereas corals that increase heterotrophic feeding during bleaching show greater resilience. in hong kong, the most comprehensive dataset supporting the link between trophic strategy and resilience identified trophic strategies for nine genera by overlapping the coral and algal isotopic niches. corals with greater overlap between partner niches exhibited range contraction near the declining water quality of the pearl river outflow and faster bleaching under experimental warming. however, the mechanism underpinning variation in isotopic niche overlap remains unclear. higher overlap could result from reduced heterotrophy, enhanced nutrient recycling within the holobiont, or greater autotrophic dependence, while greater niche separation may reflect the opposite traits. we hypothesize that variation in isotopic niche overlap reflects trophic diversification along the mixotrophy spectrum: strongly heterotrophic corals rely more on feeding, while strongly autotrophic corals depend more on their symbionts. we measured photosynthesis, respiration, and feeding rates with isotope tracer experiments to quantify heterotrophic and autotrophic nutrients used by three coral species housed in an in situ common garden nursery: 1) platygya carnosa, a heterotroph with minimal overlap, 2) pavona decussata, mixotroph with incomplete overlap, and 3) acropora samoensis, an autotroph with almost complete niche overlap. preliminary data indicate that a. samoensis has the highest photosynthesis-to-respiration ratio, suggesting that variation in autotrophic capacity underpins differences in isotopic niche overlap. our results elucidate the physiological mechanism underlying the mixotrophy spectrum observed across coral genera and improve the use and understanding of isotopic analysis as an efficient method for determining trophic strategy. yingxin li bleaching resilience and recovery of hong kong corals: the role of zooxanthellae and microbiota the coral holobiont is vital for coral health and resilience to bleaching events. in subtropical hong kong, considered a safe haven for corals with 84 reef-building species, five significant bleaching events have occurred in the two decades, affecting over 90% of corals at some sites. this situation underscores the urgent need to understand how local corals respond to stress and recover. to address these questions, we conducted a multi-omics study using three main approaches. first, from 2023 to 2024, we performed a quarterly baseline survey focusing on two bleaching-susceptible species, acropora solitaryensis and montipora peltiformis, and two resilient species, pavona decussata and platygyra carnosa, across three contrasting environmental conditions. we measured zooxanthellae density, chlorophyll-a content, and microbial communities using next-generation sequencing (ngs) techniques. second, we compared these corals during the 2022 bleaching event to identify microbial contributors to resilience. third, we monitored bleached acropora corals in an 11-week recovery experiment, integrating physiological metrics, ngs sequencing, and gene expression studies. preliminary findings reveal that acropora, pavona, and platygyra adjust their zooxanthellae types, mainly cladocopium c1 subclades, while montipora associates with c21. notably, durusdinium was found in bleached corals, indicating its temporary presence during stress. non-bleached corals exhibited beneficial microbial enrichment, whereas bleaching disrupted key metabolic processes. during recovery, significant increases in host genes related to stress response were observed. this research provides essential insights into the factors influencing coral resilience in a changing climate.",
