          "name": "Farhan Pasolong",
          "search_text": "farhan pasolong locally sourced substrates for larvae-based coral restoration: insights from recruit performance on lava rock multi-stakeholder monitoring and management of the invasive coral-killing sponge terpios hoshinota in the banda islands larvae-propagated coral restoration is emerging as a promising strategy to accelerate reef recovery, yet optimizing settlement substrates remains a key challenge, especially since there is an urgent need to move away from artificial substrates. the 1988 lava flow at gunung api, banda islands, is widely known for its remarkable natural coral regeneration, offering an opportunity to better understand how substrate characteristics can support larvae-based restoration. within a capacity building project, we evaluated how lava rock influences the settlement success and early development of coral larvae. gametes collected during a mass-spawning event at the lava flow reef in november 2023 were cultured to planulae and deployed in a damaged reef area ~2 km from the donor site, where natural recruitment is typically low. lava rocks and smoother non-lava rocks were arranged in paired settlement plots, each covered with fine nets to retain larvae during the first seven days (n = 17 plots). in parallel, additional lava and non-lava rock plots (n = 15) were deployed to quantify natural recruitment, allowing direct comparison between larvae-propagated and non-propagated restoration treatments. recruit abundance, survival, growth, and genus-level composition were monitored over a two-year period. our results demonstrate clear differences in early coral performance linked to substrate type and highlight the strong potential of volcanic lava rock microstructures to enhance larvae-propagated restoration outcomes. these findings provide practical guidance for substrate selection in larval-based reef rehabilitation and underscore the broader value of integrating locally available natural materials into restoration strategies across indonesia. the coral-killing sponge terpios hoshinota was first recorded in the banda islands (mollucas, indonesia) in 2023, and has since increased in spatial coverage and number of affected sites. terpios hoshinota is a fast-growing, highly encrusting sponge. we found 48% of the sponge to grow on hard corals and 52% on non-living hard substrates. based on local observations and emerging research, several factors are hypothesised to drive outbreaks. the most likely cause is elevated nutrient input from land-based sources, including residential wastewater discharge, and poorly managed nearshore aquaculture. addressing these causes is essential to prevent further loss of coral cover, maintain ecosystem services, and protect the tourism and fisheries economy of banda. given the ecological and socioeconomic importance of these reefs, timely and collaborative action is required to prevent long-term shifts to degraded, sponge-dominated reefs. here, we report monitoring, impact and innovative pilot-research results obtained from two years of work aimed at combating t. hoshinota. we also discuss our recent collaborative initiative with the ‘banda sea marine park’ management on prevention, local capacity building, restoration, and community engagement to combat t. hoshinota outbreaks. multi-stakeholder collaborative approaches strengthen long-term management effectiveness. innovative rehabilitation of affected reefs using coral larvae seeding supports ecosystem recovery, enhances coral genetic diversity, and provides long-term improvements to habitat structure. the techniques developed are expected to be integrated into the marine park’s standard operating procedures and shared with other mpas through our networks in indonesia.",
