      "search_text": "malama maunalua dan arencibia daniel arencibia insights from a community-led ridge-to-reef restoration project in maunalua bay, o’ahu, hawaiʻi in maunalua bay, o’ahu, hawai’i, a unique initiative led by malama maunalua (mm) is demonstrating how a small non-profit can leverage and integrate government, tourists, community, corporations, ngos, and researchers into protecting and restoring the region’s coral reefs. the maunalua region is heavily urbanized and houses over 50,000 residents. over 90% of the coastal strip has been paved with impervious surfaces, causing significant stormwater runoff and pollution to enter the bay. this means that to adequately restore these reefs, mm must address a wide range of threats throughout the watersheds that feed into maunalua bay. with its partners, mm is undertaking a holistic ridge-to-reef approach that draws inspiration from the native hawaiian ahupua’a management concept. this talk will address the various components of the effort, the lessons learned, and its replicability.\r\nmm is working with community partners to fragment and outplant thermally resilient corals. thus far, over 4,000 fragments have been planted with monitoring being undertaken by expert staff and regenerative tourism. complementing these efforts, mm has established a fisheries management area in the region to protect herbivores and incorporate fishers in restoration. further, mm’s huki project has removed over 4 million pounds of invasive alien algae and welcomed over 46,000 volunteers since 2006. terrestrially, mm is leading efforts to improve coastal water quality by managing and reducing stormwater runoff and pollution. mm is working with a range of partners to capture runoff by installing green infrastructure and to restore the ecological functions of the forest and stream ecosystems that feed into maunalua bay. the success of this program can largely be attributed to the collaborative efforts of mm’s partners, mentored volunteers, and a robust internship program that helps undertake critical science. through this talk, mm offers its insights with communities interested in replicating and scaling similar approaches.",
