      "search_text": "marine environment and resources foundation camille de castro ethel baquiran rouenne camille de castro mainstreaming coral reef management in philippine local governance: an evaluation of its integration in comprehensive land and water use plans mainstreaming coral reef management in philippine local governance: an evaluation of its integration in comprehensive land and water use plans lessons and recommendations from integrating coral restoration into local government management plans in the philippines coral reefs provide essential ecological, economic, and climate-resilience benefits to coastal communities in the philippines. effective coral reef management requires integration into long-term spatial and development planning, yet the degree to which this is realized at the local government level in the philippines remains unclear. this study assesses the extent to which coral reef management is integrated into comprehensive land and water use plans (clwups) across selected coastal municipalities in the philippines and in the suite of national-level planning guidelines issued by sectoral and interagency institutions. using a structured content analysis framework, key informant interviews, and alignment scoring against established coastal management, biodiversity conservation, and climate-resilience criteria, the research evaluates how coral reef management provisions are reflected in land and water use zoning, environmental management strategies, and development priorities. results highlight varying levels of mainstreaming influenced by institutional capacity, data availability, interagency coordination, and local prioritization of coastal issues. findings identify gaps, good practices, institutional challenges, and enabling conditions that influence the quality of integration. the study provides evidence-based recommendations to strengthen policy coherence, improve local planning capacities, and enhance the long-term sustainability of coral reef ecosystems within local governance processes. coral reefs provide critical ecological, economic, and climate-resilience benefits to coastal communities in the philippines.  integrating coral reef management into long-term spatial and development planning is essential, yet its implementation at the local government level remains poorly understood. this study evaluates the extent to which coral reef management is integrated into comprehensive land and water use plans (clwups) across selected coastal municipalities in the philippines and within national planning guidelines. using a structured content analysis framework, key informant interviews, and alignment scoring against established coastal management, biodiversity conservation, and climate-resilience criteria, this research evaluates how coral reef-related management provisions are reflected in land and water use zoning, environmental management strategies, and development priorities. results reveal different levels of mainstreaming for the local governments assessed, which are influenced by institutional capacity, data availability, interagency coordination, and local prioritization of coastal issues. analysis of national guidelines shows that while coastal management principles are present, reef-specific provisions were often fragmented or lack operational detail, limiting effective mainstreaming at the local level. we recommend revising these guidelines to include clearer management standards, stronger linkages to climate adaptation and practical tools and trainings for local implementation. findings from this study inform recommendations to strengthen policy coherence and improve local planning capacities to support long-term sustainability of coral reef ecosystems and scaling it up from local practices to national governance frameworks and processes. the science and practice of coral reef restoration is rapidly developing, yet guidelines on governance and long-term management of restored reefs are limited, particularly in developing countries like the philippines. current guidelines for managing restored reefs often overlook governance structures and policies, risking inefficiencies and wasted financial resources. thus, ensuring effective management is crucial for sustaining restoration efforts. the philippines has a decentralized governance system that grants local governments the autonomy and responsibility to manage coastal areas within 15 km of the coastline. while various stakeholders have implemented coral reef restoration projects, information on how the restored reefs are managed and sustained is limited, and no national policy provides clear governance guidelines. this study documents lessons from three municipalities, lobo (batangas), san vicente (palawan), and cordova (cebu), where we facilitated the integrated management of restored reef areas into local management plans. based on our experiences, we identified key governance challenges, such as unclear permitting processes, limited resources, and weak enforcement mechanisms. to address these issues, we propose a management framework to regulate human activities in restored reefs by integrating these into local government management structures and plans. the framework emphasizes stakeholder engagement, adaptive management, long-term monitoring, funding strategies, and policy recommendations to streamline permitting and enhance coordination between national agencies and local governments. strengthening governance mechanisms is essential for sustaining restoration success and improving reef resilience. our findings offer insights for enhancing coral reef restoration management in the philippines and similar contexts, underscoring the need for stronger policy support, stakeholder engagement, and sustainable governance strategies. ethel ruth baquiran applying coral restoration without reporting and evaluating outcomes hinders adaptive management adaptive management and resilience-based design in coral restoration requires monitoring, reporting, and evaluating coral restoration outcomes. globally, the philippines is among the leading countries in coral restoration research but evaluation of restored sites is seldom done due to lack of standard protocols in the past. so we investigated aspects of monitoring, reporting, and evaluation of coral restoration outcomes in the philippines at national and local scales by 1) synthesizing extracted data from peer-reviewed and non-peer-reviewed reports, and 2) evaluating select publications using an evaluation tool developed by the coral restoration consortium in 2020. we collected data in 2023 on the scale of restoration, coral survival, and live tissue cover for both review and evaluation. then we used empirical and published data accompanied by long-term temperature in evaluating select publications. a total of 128 reports from primary, grey, and online sources published from 1982 to 2023 were included. coral growth and survival dominated among 27 monitored outcomes and the median percent survival was calculated at 63%. however, during evaluation of select publications, all four coral species having different life histories experienced partial mortality and decreased survival >6 years post-outplanting. this may be linked to increased frequency of thermal anomalies recorded from 2005 to 2023 in select restoration sites, as heat-stress was also among the most reported causes of coral mortality in our review. furthermore, results showed that long-term monitoring with community stakeholders, reporting of live tissue cover, and documentation of changes in restoration scale are seldom conducted. moreover, 41% of the total reports did not monitor outcomes at all. the identified causes of restored coral mortality from published and empirical data can help guide future coral restoration planning. this will enable restoration interventions to adopt measurable goals and improve effectiveness  through adaptive management strategies like selecting optimal outplanting sites and season.",
