      "search_text": "maritime and marine science center of excellence, pattimura university james charlie rupiasa janet rande rindah talitha vida james charlie rupiasa exploring reef fish species, phylogenetic and functional diversity in indonesia and the philippines coral reef biodiversity is shaped by multiple interacting factors, including environmental gradients and variability, ecological interactions, evolutionary history, and human pressures. understanding how these drivers influence biodiversity is essential for supporting effective and informed reef management. most studies rely on species diversity to describe biodiversity, but species counts alone cannot capture evolutionary differences nor the ecological functions that communities contribute to the ecosystem. the wealth of functional traits and phylogenetic data available now addresses this limitation, offering a more comprehensive view on community structure, its relationship with environmental variation, and might satisfy other benchmarks of conservation prioritisation. the high species diversity of reef fish in the coral triangle presents a suitable case study to examine the relationship between species, phylogenetic, and functional diversity, as well as how they relate to the environment.  we used extensive underwater visual census (uvc) datasets across 60 reefs in indonesia and the philippines. for each site and all recorded species, we extracted and calculated species phylogenetic and functional diversity indices from the global fish tree of life and fishbase. we then tested the relationships between the diversity indices and habitat heterogeneity. our preliminary results indicate weak positive relationships between the diversity indices and some indication that increasing habitat heterogeneity can increase diversity. by integrating community data, phylogenetic and trait information, our study highlights how environmental variation shapes evolutionary patterns in reef fish communities. the findings provide a deeper understanding of biodiversity–environment relationships and strengthen the scientific foundation for conservation efforts across the coral triangle. janet gusti putri rande assessing the potential of habitat heterogeneity and species diversity as proxies for genetic diversity coral reefs are highly diverse and productive ecosystems that are continuously impacted by human-driven changes. to address these threats, conservation efforts are being implemented, including strengthening reef adaptive potential. understanding reef adaptation requires information not only on species diversity, but also genetic diversity that can indicate a population’s ability to adapt to environmental stress. however, measuring genetic diversity is expensive and technical, so finding good proxies for genetic diversity and thus adaptative potential is important. one potential proxy for genetic diversity is habitat heterogeneity, because of its influence on population size, connectivity, and community stability. additionally, because analogous processes shape both species and genetic diversity, species diversity –which is easier to assess than genetic diversity– might serve as proxy for genetic diversity. here, we evaluate the influence of habitat heterogeneity on genetic diversity and assess whether habitat heterogeneity and species diversity can be used as proxy indicators for genetic diversity. using genomic data, we estimated genetic diversity of pachyseris speciosa and diploastrea heliopora from 60 different reefs in indonesia and the philippines. we derived habitat heterogeneity using fine‐resolution (5 m × 5 m) benthic and geomorphic reef maps, and estimated fish diversity from underwater visual census data. using multilevel modeling, we tested whether habitat heterogeneity and fish species diversity can predict genetic diversity for the coral species that we examined. our findings show that habitat heterogeneity and species diversity showed weak correlation with nucleotide diversity and tajima’s d. however, summarizing the genetic data using site-frequency-spectrum (i.e., quantiles) further improved the variance explained by both predictors. this work highlights how habitat heterogeneity shapes both the “visible” diversity in the field and the “hidden” diversity at the genomic level, offering potential proxies for genetic diversity in support of coral reef conservation. rindah talitha vida reef fish nutrient content across indonesia and the philippines: assessing nutrient dynamics under climate change many coastal communities are heavily reliant on coral reef fishes as an important dietary source of bioavailable essential micronutrients and fatty acids. climate change-induced coral reef degradation can alter total fish biomass and species composition. these changes in fish assemblages may influence the nutrient content of reef-derived food available to humans. understanding how climate change affects the nutritional content of reef fish is critical for supporting adaptative management strategies that are anticipatory and based on local nutritional requirements. using underwater visual census (uvc) survey data, we estimated the nutrient content (calcium, iron, omega-3, protein, selenium, vitamin a, and zinc) of reef fish communities across 60 reef sites in indonesia and the philippines based on species estimates from fishbase. we evaluated how spatial variation in environmental drivers affects nutrient availability to fishing communities using glmms. overall, we found lower total reef fish biomass from sites in the philippines, compared to reef sites in indonesia, resulting in lower overall fishable biomass. reefs in the philippines were dominated by macroalgae and a higher proportion of herbivores and omnivores, while sites in indonesia were dominated by coral and had higher proportions of piscivores. we explore how these spatial patterns and different environmental and human pressures can shape nutrient availability across reef systems. while climate change effects are global, local activities, including high-fishing pressure may have a strong influence on the nutritional availability of local reefs. given almost 120 million people depend on the coral triangle for food and livelihoods, stakeholders and resource managers should use nutritional information to inform climate-smart and nutrition sensitive fisheries policies in the coral triangle region.",
