          "name": "Alan Foreman",
          "search_text": "alan foreman expansion of the indian ocean subtropical gyre over the 20th century decadal oscillations in the ocean’s largest oxygen-deficient zone subtropical gyres are wind-driven, strongly stratified ocean regions that cover 40% of earth’s surface. these so-called ‘ocean deserts’ are characterized by nutrient impoverishment and low biological productivity, and their perennial warmth has profound impacts on atmospheric circulation and climate. satellite observations suggest that subtropical gyres have been expanding and migrating polewards since the late 1990s in response to anthropogenic global warming. however, significant uncertainty remains as to the sign and magnitude of such changes because they are easily obscured by interannual and decadal climate variability and because of the relatively short length of the satellite record. \r\n\r\nhere we present records of the 15n-to-14n isotopic ratio of organic matter preserved in the skeleton of shallow-water scleractinian corals from the mozambique channel, extending back 145 years. toward the present, these corals increasingly record the nitrogen isotopic signature of the indian ocean subtropical gyre (iosg), suggesting that the iosg has been expanding since early in the 20th century. while the observed global expansion of the atmospheric hadley cells may have contributed, we find evidence that trade wind enhancement of the south equatorial current acted as a significant control on iosg expansion over the 20th century. such a driver would likely have enhanced the leakage of water from the agulhas current into the atlantic basin over 20th century, which may have worked to stabilize the atlantic meridional overturning circulation in the face of global warming. the impact of global warming on the ocean’s oxygen-deficient zones (odzs) is uncertain, partly because of a lack of data on past changes. we report monthly resolved records of coral skeleton–bound nitrogen isotopes (cs-d15n) to reconstruct denitrification in the eastern tropical north pacific (etnp) odz over the last 80 years. the data indicate strong decadal variation in etnp denitrification, with maxima during the cool north pacific phase of pacific decadal variability. the maxima in denitrification (and thus oxygen deficiency) were likely due to stronger upwelling that enhanced productivity leading to greater oxygen demand in the thermocline. prior findings of multidecadal-to-centennial odz trends were likely biased by this variability. odz evolution over the next century will depend on how global warming interacts with the decadal oscillations.",
