      "search_text": "meio university yoshikatsu nakano nakano yoshikatsu facultative mutualism between seagrass and coral from a microbiome perspective seagrass thalassia hemprichii and coral montipora digitata inhabiting reef flats form large communities through mutualism, depending on the spatial structure of their habitat. the branching coral species m.digitata frequently disperses by fragmentation, but many die due to bottom currents. the dense seagrass traps branches, not only enhancing coral survival rates by reducing this burden but also creating favorable growth conditions for its own rhizomes. these thrive in the aerobic substrate formed by trapped and accumulated branch fragments, regardless of whether they are alive or dead. it has been confirmed that various materials are exchanged between the two species within the community. on a biogeochemical scale, the relationship between coral and seagrass has been shown to benefit seagrass in terms of co2 and nox availability, while benefiting coral through mitigation of carbonate dissolution, fragmentation, and further habitat development.\r\nthe benefits derived from this symbiotic relationship in coral reef shallows are not only advantageous to both species but are also expected to significantly contribute to maintaining and enhancing the biomass and diversity of the communities formed with both species as their skeletal framework. in this study, we evaluated this by comparing microbial composition and associated functional gene composition through comparative analysis of microbiome data in both laboratory and field studies. we report these results and discuss the interaction between coral reefs and seagrass beds.",
