      "search_text": "memorial university of newfoundland and labrador emmeline broad emmeline broad using benthic imagery to validate regional model projections of temperate mesophotic ecosystems in atlantic canada distribution models are widely used in marine habitat mapping and to predict and map ecological patterns. knowledge generated from these approaches can inform marine spatial management measures. species occurrence data compiled in open-access databases often provide model training data; however, sampling bias can strongly influence predictions. in atlantic canada, for example, species occurrence data relies heavily on bottom trawl surveys conducted across the continental shelf, yet trawl nets cannot be deployed across steep or complex seabed terrains. therefore, taxa associated with complex, high-relief habitats that support temperate mesophotic ecosystems (tmes) are systematically under-sampled, and their contribution to regional seabed biodiversity estimates remains unknown.\r\nhere, we present a hierarchical habitat suitability model (h-hsm) framework designed to predict the distribution of ideal habitat ideal for tmes. tmes characterized by rugged seabed geomorphology and dense aggregations of soft-coral taxa were discovered for the first time in 2024 in the funk island deep (fid) marine refuge off the coast of newfoundland, canada. this discovery provided an opportunity to assess whether broad-scale model projections align with true seabed conditions inside a complex, data-poor environment.\r\nthe h-hsm comprises soft coral occurrence data obtained from bottom trawl surveys and nested random forest classifiers integrating broad-scale ocean-climate (11 km) and fine-scale seabed-terrain predictors (0.2 km). we ground-truthed h-hsm model predictions using drop-camera imagery collected inside fid, and used a binomial glm to define an appropriate suitability threshold. results show that most areas containing low-density soft coral assemblages were projected as highly suitable, while the spatial extent of high-density coral gardens was underestimated. this likely reflects sampling biases inherent in trawl-derived occurrence data. we demonstrate how incorporating imagery from complex seabed habitats can account for bias in regional model predictions, enhance interpretation of suitability projections, and provide a scalable approach for targeting and exploring unmapped potential marine biodiversity hotspots.",
