      "search_text": "memorial university ana-belen yanez-suarez ana-belen yanez-suarez coral garden thriving in anoxic waters on an underexplored seamount in the eastern tropical pacific climate-driven ocean deoxygenation is intensifying in the eastern tropical pacific (etp), expanding oxygen minimum zones (omzs) and altering the environmental conditions experienced by deep-sea benthic communities. yet the physiological limits and environmental tolerances of these communities remain poorly 17 understood. here, we document a dense monospecific octocoral garden on the gemelas ii seamount in isla del coco national park, costa rica. the garden occurred within the anoxic core of the omz, where dissolved oxygen concentrations ranged from 0.5 to 3 μmol kg⁻¹, representing the lowest values yet reported for coral garden presence.\r\nvideo transects and environmental variables were collected using rov subastian, while shipboard bathymetry was obtained from the mapping system on r/v falkor (too). generalized additive models showed that octocoral abundance peaked under extreme hypoxia, moderate temperatures (10 - 11 °c), and areas of elevated local topographic complexity. the garden exhibited minimal associated fauna aside from a dense ophiuroid bed extending across the anoxic zone.\r\nthese findings extend known tolerance thresholds for deep-sea corals and highlight the role of seamount bathymetry in promoting persistence under extreme environmental stress. at the same time, the low richness of associated macrofauna suggests that these conditions constrain broader community complexity and biodiversity. continued investigation of underexplored omz regions will be critical for understanding the environmental drivers that shape cwc distribution, biodiversity, and potential resilience as ocean deoxygenation accelerates under climate change.",
