      "search_text": "micronesian conservation coalition julie hartup julie hartup popan models reveal coral reef habitat resilience constrains reef manta rays in micronesia effective conservation of reef manta rays (modula alfredi) depends on understanding how habitat health influences population stability, particularly on isolated islands where local declines can't be offset through immigration. popan mark-recapture models were created using long-term photo-id databases with 3,093 sightings from guam (u.s. territory [2008–2024]) and the federated states of micronesia (yap [2008–2025] and pohnpei [2010–2025]). we investigated how coral reef habitats impact abundance, recruitment, and demographic structure. popan models consistently indicated high survival rates (φ ≈ 0.92–0.95), low entry rates (pent ≈ 0.05–0.06), and small abundance estimates (95% ci) of 68 for guam (64–71), 79 for yap (73–85), and 139 for pohnpei (122–156). populations seem stable, with detection probabilities varying by site (guam = 0.68; yap = 0.50; pohnpei = 0.24), reflecting differences in habitat accessibility and behavioural predictability. sex and maturity ratios were significantly skewed across all locations (guam p = 0.0124; yap p = 3.80e-09; pohnpei p = 7.97e-18), indicating limited juvenile recruitment typical of a k-selected species. model fit patterns matched habitat structure: yap and pohnpei showed strong model fit, where manta rays utilise lagoonal and channel cleaning stations within well-developed barrier and fringing reef systems. guam demonstrated variability in detection, reflecting its more limited habitat, with overdispersion likely arising from manta use of fish-spawning aggregation feeding sites that fluctuate with lunar-driven shifts, though this mechanism remains to be tested. these differences show that manta population stability, detectability, and recruitment are fundamentally linked to coral reef complexity, cleaning station persistence, and prey availability. micronesian manta rays show high site fidelity; the loss of even a single reef feature can cause population declines with no new individuals moving in to offset these losses. therefore, habitat integrity is a key factor limiting the reef manta ray population across micronesia.",
