      "search_text": "mindanao state university - naawan allyn duvin hinoguin dr anabelle espadero renoir abrea allyn duvin hinoguin reef fish recruitment in planted mangroves: ecological outcomes and lessons for responsible restoration mangrove restoration can enhance coastal biodiversity by creating valuable nursery habitats for reef-associated fishes, yet the ecological outcomes of planting mangroves over existing habitats such as seagrass beds remain debated. this study investigated how 10- and 30-year-old planted mangroves established on former seagrass beds support juvenile reef fish communities and how reef proximity (<100 m, <300 m, <600 m) shapes assemblage structure. fish sampling across tidal stages used a beach seine net (7.5 m × 1 m, 5 mm mesh) with 15 standardized tows per site. a total of 1,035 individuals representing 36 species from 16 families were recorded, the majority of which were juveniles. reef-associated fishes, particularly from lutjanidae and siganidae, were consistently present, demonstrating that planted mangroves provide shelter and feeding opportunities for juvenile reef fishes. species richness and abundance generally increased with distance from coral reefs, indicating that planted mangroves can expand available nursery habitat within the coastal mosaic. however, shifts in habitat use were evident, such as the presence of halichoeres spp. in younger plantations but their absence in older stands, reflecting ecological changes following the conversion of seagrass beds into mangrove monocultures. these findings underscore that while planted mangroves can function as important nursery areas for reef fish, restoration must be implemented responsibly, avoiding seagrass conversion and ensuring that site selection aligns with ecological suitability. such considerations are essential for maximizing restoration benefits for fisheries, reef-mangrove connectivity, and coastal biodiversity conservation. anabelle dece espadero assessing coral benthic status and its influence on reef fish structure in surigao del norte coastal ecosystems this study provides a four-year trend analysis from 2021 – 2024 of coral benthic conditions and associated reef fish structure across monitoring sites in claver and socorro, surigao del norte, offering a multi-year ecological basis for coastal resource management\r\nacross the four-year period, live hard coral cover exhibited a modest but consistent upward trend, resulting in an improvement in overall benthic condition from category d to category c (“poor”). six of the nine monitoring sites recorded increases in hcc, while others showed stable values or minor declines. the coral assemblage maintained exceptionally high genetic diversity, with 48 taxonomic amalgamation units (taus) recorded annually, sustaining a “category a” status. the benthic community remained dominated by resilient, structure-forming genera such as massive and branching porites spp. and acropora sp., which contribute to the habitat’s structural integrity.\r\nreef fish communities showed a corresponding positive ecological response. total species richness increased substantially, reaching 162 species in the most recent assessment with higher than all preceding years. while fish abundance across most sites remained within the “moderate” range, biomass trends revealed localized improvements. two sites shifted from “moderate” to “high” biomass, attributed to the growing presence of ecologically and commercially important families including caesionidae, scaridae, and pomacentridae.\r\nthe findings demonstrate a clear ecological trajectory which is stable and highly diverse coral assemblages with strong structural complexity have supported increasing reef fish richness and enhanced site-specific productivity. these results emphasizes the interdependence between benthic habitat quality and reef fish assemblage resilience, reaffirming the need for sustained habitat protection and adaptive coastal management in surigao del norte. renoir abrea multi-site coral transplantation performance across mindanao: comparative one-year survival outcomes from 2020–2023 coral transplantation remains a central intervention in reef rehabilitation, yet comparative evaluations across distinct biophysical settings and time frames remain limited in the philippines. this study presents a spatio-temporal assessment of coral transplant performance using a uniform methodology applied across three ecologically contrasting sites in mindanao island: kiwalan, iligan city (l1, 2020); calangahan, lugait (l2, 2021); and lawigan, bislig (l3, 2023). coral fragments were extracted from donor colonies using controlled manual detachment and transported underwater to minimize handling stress. each fragment was mounted in plastic-cup bases with concrete adhesive and anchored to the substratum using concrete nails, ensuring standardized stability and comparability across sites. coral taxonomy and survival metrics were assessed using the taxonomic amalgamation units (taus) system following licuanan (2020), enabling consistent, resolution-appropriate comparisons.\r\n\r\none-year post-transplant survival remained consistently high despite environmental variability: 88.72% (38 taus; n=564) at l1, 88.06% (31 taus; n=579) at l2, and 95.64% (36 taus; n=459) at l3. the significant survival at l3 suggests site-specific conditions conducive to enhanced early transplant establishment. this multi-site, multi-year analysis demonstrates the robustness of a standardized transplant protocol across diverse reef environments and provides one of the first tau-resolved comparative datasets for mindanao. the findings emphasize the value of harmonized restoration techniques for scaling regional reef rehabilitation strategies amid accelerating climate and anthropogenic pressures.",
