          "name": "Kate Quigley",
          "search_text": "kate quigley empirical evidence for a climate-resistant reef ecosystems globally are under increasing pressure from warming caused by climate change. however, some locations appear resistant to warming. although rare, these locations, including on coral reefs, might be able to resist damage from thermal stress. one sign of stress on reefs - coral bleaching - is widely viewed as one of the most pervasive threats to coral reef structure and function. yet, despite over four decades of research we still have a poor understanding of the drivers of spatial and temporal variation in the scale and severity of coral bleaching. here we present the first empirical evidence for a resistant reef, where almost no bleaching was observed despite predictions of catastrophic mortality following a prolonged and intense marine heatwave. we further support these observations using experimental data collected during a simulated experimental heatwave. taken together, our results support the capacity of multiple, functionally diverse coral species to resist bleaching to very high heating stress levels. although hopeful, historical data from this region suggests these reefs are neither a refuge from nor resilient to bleaching particularly if ocean warming continues to increase.",
