          "name": "Sara Williams",
          "search_text": "sara williams resilience of five caribbean coral species as determined by six phenotypic responses and seven years of ocean warming and acidification experiments losses in living coral cover due to a multitude of stressors have produced reefs on the brink of functional extinction, but some individuals have persisted. seven experiments over seven years utilized similar methodological approaches to characterize resistance to ocean acidification and warming of five coral species critical for ecosystem recovery and prioritized for restoration: acropora cervicornis, a. palmata, montastraea cavernosa, orbicella faveolata, and pseudodiploria clivosa. during each two month long experiment, several genotypes within each species were exposed to four environmental conditions: control, high temperature, low ph, and combined high temperature & low ph. phenotypic metrics to assess resilience included growth, photochemical efficiency, and metabolism. using a meta-analysis approach, we combined experiments’ results to characterize responses of multiple species, genotypes, and replicate fragments to ocean warming and acidification in isolation and in concert after one month and two months of exposure. the combination of ocean warming and acidification had an additive, negative effect on the corals’ phenotypic responses, but the magnitude varied among species. we identified a. palmata and o. faveolata as more heat tolerant for most of the traits measured, but they were still susceptible to ocean acidification and the combined treatment. our results support consideration of exposure duration in experimental designs, as there were differences in phenotypic responses after one month and two months exposure, both between and within species. some species, particularly a. cervicornis, had more variation in phenotypic response among genets compared to others. those genets that performed well under ocean warming experiments also had higher outplant survival after the 2023 severe bleaching on florida’s coral reef. species with larger genotype-phenotype diversity may have greater potential for more informed restoration plans via resilience screening. our results support the need to prioritize immediate intervention responses for ocean warming in concert with ocean acidification for long-term restoration success.",
