          "name": "Michel Pichon",
          "search_text": "michel pichon towards a framework for scleractinian coral zonation in the indo-pacific mesophotic environment : the broad picture due to their difficulty of access, studies of mesophotic coral ecosystems remain relatively recent and largely conditioned by technological progress and improvements in man’s ability to observe and collect specimens and environmental data. although large areas of the indo-pacific remain unexplored, some locations including, but not limited to, french polynesia, hawaii, japan, australia, mayotte, eilat, now provide reliable data on depth diversity and distribution of zooxanthellate scleractinian corals. hence a broad scale picture of community structure and zonation is starting to emerge, keeping in mind that light penetration, for which data remain scarce, is for practical reasons replaced by depth as a proxy.\r\nin terms of diversity and abundance, the general trend is a decrease with depth, notwithstanding the “mid-domain effect” in some areas of the upper mesophotic zone (ca 40 m). such a trend is maintained at lower depths but is accompanied by significant shifts in the species composition of scleractinian assemblages, across a broad geographic scale. examples arising from the comparison of three depths zones with approximately similar light irradiance conditions over a large geographic scale: (1) 61-90 m, (2) 91-120 m and (3) >120 m, show for instance that the number of acroporidae decreases from 15 in zone (1) t0 9 in zone (2), and to none in zone (3). further, a parallel decrease is observed for the genus acropora (with 6 and 3 species for zones (1) and (2) respectively) which is largely replaced by alveopora. a major change in species composition occurs around 120 m (depth corresponding approximately to 0.1-o.01% of surface light irradiance) beyond which less than a dozen zooxanthellate scleractinians persists, mainly leptoseris. at these depths, the benthic assemblages become dominated by sponges, octocorals, antipatharians, stylasterids and this raises the question of whether there could be a case for an “oligophotic zone”?",
