          "name": "Marlena Joppien",
          "search_text": "marlena joppien rethinking tropical carbonate factories for reef environments in tropical asia coral growth histories under terrestrial sediment input carbonate sediment production worldwide occurs through distinct ecological and environmental systems classified as carbonate factories. current global classifications of these factories, however, derive predominantly from clear-water environments. despite being a global hotspot of coral reef biodiversity, quantitative assessments of modern carbonate sedimentology in tropical asia remain underrepresented within these frameworks. this is particularly problematic given that many reefs in the region exist under turbid or marginal conditions that are distinct from the clear-water systems upon which global models are based. here we present the physical grain properties and constituent composition of benthic coral reef sediment (n =150) collected from >20 sites across five regions in tropical asia to evaluate whether turbid reefs represent a functionally distinct carbonate factory subtype or are degraded versions of existing tropical systems. living benthic reef communities were assessed using ecological survey methods, and environments were determined through measured environmental parameters on reefs. our findings show strong compositional divergence of coral reefs in tropical asia from existing tropical factory models. rather than displaying impaired carbonate sediment production, reefs exhibit high-diversity producer assemblages in which large benthic foraminifera (e.g., calcarina spp.), mollusks, and calcareous red algae are important contributors to detrital sediments on reefs. on turbid reefs, where sediment production by corals and calcareous green algae is reduced, carbonate producers that typically provide a minor contribution to local sediment production, increase their relative dominance within the sediment pool. our results demonstrate that turbid reefs warrant recognition as distinct carbonate factory subtypes, with implications for paleoenvironmental reconstructions and predictions of future sediment production trajectories under climate change. fringing reefs and their associated reef flats provide critical ecosystem services by attenuating waves and reducing shoreline erosion. despite their importance, the long-term accretionary processes and carbonate-producing community dynamics of fringing reef flats remain poorly understood compared to other reef zones. this is particularly true for mainland-attached fringing reefs in southeast asia, where significant terrestrial sediment input creates mixed carbonate-siliciclastic environments. this knowledge gap limits our ability to predict how reef flats will respond to future environmental and ecological change. here we investigate holocene reef flat accretion histories using coral cores collected from an exposed intertidal fossil fringing reef in inopacan, leyte, philippines. we applied radiometric dating (u/th and ¹⁴c), luminescence band analysis, and micro-ct scanning to reconstruct reef growth chronology, past coral communities, and sediment composition. our results show that the fringing reef was dominated by massive coral colonies, primarily porites and favites. siliciclastic sediment was present on the initiating surface of corals, and terrestrial particles were incorporated within coral skeletons throughout their growth history, indicating persistent turbid conditions. the cores also revealed varying degrees of bioerosion. these findings demonstrate how fringing reef flat formation and accretion dynamics respond to sustained terrestrial sediment input, with implications for predicting reef flat development under future environmental change.",
