          "name": "Yun Scholten",
          "search_text": "yun scholten the overlooked architects of reefs: patterns of secondary carbonate production from clear to turbid waters in southeast asia tropical coral reefs are undergoing rapid declines in coral cover, leading to reduced carbonate production and diminished reef accretion capacity. while the role of primary reef-building corals in reef bioconstruction is well established, the ecological dynamics and calcification contributions of secondary reef builders (e.g., calcifying algae, bryozoans, serpulid polychaetes and vermetid gastropods) remain comparatively understudied. these organisms can significantly influence reef framework development and generate substantial quantities of detrital carbonate sediment, especially in the absence of corals. however, spatial variability in net calcification among secondary reef builders and across environmental gradients remains poorly understood. here we use calcification accretion units (caus; n = 62) deployed at 13 reefs across three southeast asian countries (the philippines, singapore and thailand) for a one-year period to quantify carbonate production by secondary reef builders across clear-water and turbid settings. results indicate that secondary reef builders on clear-water reefs exhibited higher carbonate production rates and carbonate density compared to their turbid reef counterparts, suggesting that in turbid settings volumetric extension may be prioritized over carbonate deposition as an adaptive response to high sedimentation. such changes in carbonate deposition may alter the overall reef consolidation and growth, adversely affecting reef health. our findings provide the first baseline data rates of secondary calcification for reefs in southeast asia, highlighting cryptic biodiversity associated with coral habitats across a range of settings. integration of such region-specific data into carbonate budget assessments is crucial for understanding reef resilience, especially in marginal reef environments facing climate change.",
