      "search_text": "national center for wildlife afrah alothman afrah alothman a large-scale assessment of coral reefs along the saudi red sea coast: diversity, abundance, threats, and implications for conservation abstract\r\ncoral reefs are among the most biodiverse and ecologically significant ecosystems, supporting the livelihoods of millions globally. however, they face mounting threats from both local and global stressors. effective conservation strategies require robust baseline data to assess long-term ecological changes and inform adaptive management. this study presents the most recent and thorough large-scale assessment of coral reefs in the saudi red sea, providing critical baseline information to support national conservation efforts. between february 2023 and april 2024, surveys were conducted at 589 sites spanning from haql to the farasan islands using standardized underwater transect methods. a custom reef health index (rhi), integrating coral health index (chi) and fish health index (fhi) metrics on a scale from 0 to 1, was developed to assess reef condition. the rhi indicated “healthy” reefs in the central region (yanbu, 0.66) and predominantly “fair” reefs in the south (al lith, 0.41). hard coral cover was highest in the north (48%) and center (45%), while macroalgae cover peaked in the south (11%). coral communities were dominated by branching, massive, and encrusting forms (76.9–95.9%), with acropora spp. prevalent in the north and center, and porites spp. dominant in the south. key stressors included anchor damage (center, south), discarded fishing gear (north), and widespread coral bleaching (>40% of sites), especially near the farasan islands and banks. these findings highlight the need for region-specific conservation actions to address ongoing pressures and support saudi arabia’s vision 2030 goals through science-based reef management and restoration.",
