          "name": "Brian Walker",
          "search_text": "brian walker losing the most susceptible corals in a disease epidemic obfuscates statistical model associations and shifts our understanding of the baseline conditions that cause disease disentangling causation of coral diseases requires detailed information on the occurrence of novel disease cases in the population and their associations with environmental conditions. however, temporal disease observations are confounded by susceptibility differentials because the initial onset of virulent tissue loss diseases kills the most susceptible colonies, leaving more resistant ones for observation. this loss of the most susceptible colonies may obfuscate the baseline conditions associated with disease and shift our understanding of the optimal conditions for coral reef persistence. this paper identifies a novel method to obtain the number and frequency of infections on individual reef corals while maintaining their health and classifying them by susceptibility. we show that warming ocean temperatures are driving increased incidence and prevalence of stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) and that this is exacerbated by nutrient enriched terrestrial water sources. total disease on these colonies revealed seasonal patterns, being significantly higher in the rainy summer season (may – october). a model simulation of losing the most susceptible colonies from the pool of corals revealed a 36% decrease in explanatory power of total treatments to environmental predictors and a reduction in the number of significant predictors. the model using all corals found five predictors explaining 63.1% of the variation whereas the model with the most susceptible corals removed found only three predictors explaining 40.4% of the variation. further the number of treatments and treated colonies were reduced by 529% and 294% respectively. this result suggests that the loss of the most susceptible corals inhibits the ability to use field observations to determine the initial baseline conditions that cause disease. proactively saving the most susceptible individuals through disease interventions at the onset of an epidemic will allow for a better understanding of the conditions related to a disease outbreak.",
