          "name": "Amana Garrido",
          "search_text": "amana garrido restructuring of the symbiodiniaceae assemblage associated with the hydrocoral millepora alcicornis after the 2019 southwestern atlantic thermal anomaly frequent bleaching events, triggered by temperature anomalies of 1-2°c, have been threatening coral reefs worldwide, with prolonged events leading to the collapse of entire reefs. in this context, symbiodiniaceae associated with corals have been extensively studied, as they may influence corals’ adaptability to global climate change. following noaa’s warning for a high-intensity thermal anomaly in the southwestern atlantic during the summer of 2019, we tagged and monitored weekly colonies of the hydrocoral millepora alcicornis for bleaching and mortality for five months at three subtropical sites in rio de janeiro state, brazil. tissue samples were used to assess symbiont identity through metabarcoding. water temperature reached 29.6°c, 4.5°c above the local bleaching threshold, reaching a dhw of 15°c/week. as a result, a severe bleaching event was recorded, where all monitored colonies bleached, but only 15% had partial mortalities greater than 80%. breviolum b1, b19, and b41, cladocopium c3, and symbiodinium a4 dominated the symbiont assemblages. however, colonies displayed different photosymbiont dynamics: 1) stability throughout the bleaching event and recovery, or 2) changes in relative abundances during recovery, suggesting some adaptive or acclimatory capability. colonies that showed changes in assemblage structure and, additionally, greater symbiont diversity exhibited milder bleaching responses and, consequently, lower mortality rates. corals in the region were susceptible to bleaching, but had low mortality rates compared to other regions in the southwestern atlantic during the same period. moreover, the large temperature fluctuations in the region, caused by frequent upwelling events, expose these corals to substantial thermal variability, which may confer greater resilience. finally, colonies with high diversity and a specific composition of a dynamic symbiodiniaceae assemblage had higher survival rates, highlighting the relevance of the assemblage structure in improving and predicting host response to environmental impacts such as increased water temperature.",
