          "name": "Jamison Gove",
          "search_text": "jamison gove mapping productivity hotspots reveals hidden pelagic–reef connections across an entire island chain coral reef ecosystems depend on exchanges of energy and material with surrounding oceanic waters, especially around islands and atolls in the tropical oligotrophic ocean. however, satellites cannot resolve shallow nearshore waters, and ship-based surveys are costly, sparse, and typically avoid coastal waters and near-island slopes. as a result, the magnitude, variability, and pathways of pelagic subsidies to reefs remain poorly quantified. to address this knowledge gap, we deployed wave-propelled autonomous surface vehicles (wave gliders) to conduct repeated, island-scale surveys of ocean conditions across the hawaiian islands. over three missions from late 2023 to mid-2025, three vehicles operated in tandem at fixed distances from shore (1.5, 3, and 6 km), completing a full circumnavigation of all eight main hawaiian islands. the surveys covered 31,083 km of trackline (equivalent to four-fifths of the earth’s circumference) and collected over 3,000 vertical profiles to 150 m depth, spanning contrasting seasonal cycles and enso phases. our surveys provide the first high-resolution, three-dimensional observations of near-island ocean conditions across an entire archipelago, closing a critical data gap in the very areas that link pelagic processes to nearshore ecology. in particular, they quantified the depth, strength, and vertical structure of chlorophyll (a proxy for phytoplankton biomass), revealing both surface and subsurface productivity hotspots and the oceanographic processes that create them. this presentation will highlight emergent patterns in ocean-reef connections from these surveys and demonstrate how these data can improve our predictions of reef ecosystem responses to changing ocean conditions. we will also discuss their integration into ecosystem and fisheries models to support ecosystem-based management across tropical archipelagos.",
