          "name": "Thomas Oliver",
          "search_text": "thomas oliver species distribution models of esa-listed corals highlight niche differences among taxa that may guide recovery planning decadal reef-scale data reveal mosaic of coral resilience understanding the biogeography of living marine resources is essential for informed resource management, particularly in data-limited environments. here, we demonstrate how ecological niche models, paired with open-access environmental data, can enhance the utility of existing survey programs and support monitoring of species listed under the u.s. endangered species act (esa). we developed ensemble models for two esa-listed indo-pacific corals, acropora globiceps and isopora crateriformis using a maximum entropy (maxent) framework and multi-source occurrence records collected around tutuila, american samoa. environmental predictors were derived from temporally summarized, high-resolution, open-source satellite and other gridded datasets capturing heat stress, turbidity, and seafloor complexity. the models revealed distinct ecological preferences: a. globiceps was associated with intermediate depths and structural complexity, and sites with longer recovery intervals since major thermal events, while i. crateriformis showed broader environmental tolerance but greater sensitivity to recent heat stress. to evaluate real-world applicability, we designed a retrospective forecasting experiment, training models on data through 2024 and testing predictions against independent 2025 observations. ensemble models achieved reliable discrimination (auc = 0.71-0.74), with true skill statistics of 0.47 for a. globiceps and 0.28 for i. crateriformis, indicating useful forecast skill for both species under independent conditions. this approach provides a scalable, reproducible framework for forecasting habitat suitability, guiding targeted monitoring, and informing adaptive management of esa-listed corals under changing reef conditions. here we synthesize large-scale, long-term monitoring data to highlight patterns of differential resilience in the wild and to explore associations that set resilient coral reefs apart. we synthesize two specific datasets from noaa’s national coral reef monitoring program - (1): live coral cover trends from 8,745 surveys spanning 2010-2024 from 37 central and western pacific islands, and (2) demographic models including growth, survival and recruitment of over 10,000 coral colonies (over 15,000 unique colony-level transitions) for four dominant genera at 41 sites spanning 2013 to 2024. for the cover data (1), we first minimize the size of survey reporting zones by applying a novel hierarchical contiguous clustering technique, resulting in 181 sub-island zones. this analysis shows a mosaic of resilient and declining reefs, in which 38% of zones show favorable coral cover trends (i.e. either increasing or maintaining high cover) and 35% show adverse trends (declining or maintaining low cover). given these fine-scale differential reef outcomes, we applied boosted regression tree models (brt) to assess potential associations of zones favorable to coral cover with 20 environmental and ecological variables. for the demographic models (2), we developed site-specific growth, mortality and recruitment models into integral projection models capable of predicting rates of coral population growth. we then repeated our brt modeling to assess associations with 20 potential correlates, highlighting environmental and ecological patterns associated with robust population growth. in a decade and region characterized by severe marine heat wave disturbances, these models show robust associations with drivers more relevant to recovery than to those relevant to refuge from or resistance to disturbance. these associations suggest that a management focus on recovery processes may be critical to maintain these resilient remnants.",
