          "search_text": "de-sing ding gene identification and specific parasitism assessment of coral ciliates at the non-specific infection of the coral triangle this submitter has specifically asked this abstract to be considered as a poster only.\r\n\r\ncorals are facing environmental stress and recovery, leading to serious coral disease problems. coral ciliate disease is a serious coral disease that is highly contagious and has a high mortality rate. infected corals may bleach, rot, or die. this experiment infected two different corals to assess the presence of specific parasitism. for the food, 5 and 10 polyps were used; and three microalgae: tetraselmis chui, isochrysis galbana tml and nannochloropsis oculata, at concentrations of 1.5×106 cell ml-1 and 3.0×106 cell ml-1, respectively. a 5-day infection test was conducted to assess the impact on ciliate growth and development, as well as on survival. the results showed that the ciliates identified in this experiment were scuticociliatia sp. the group fed with b. violacea (10 polyps) had the best proliferation rate, which was significantly different from the proliferation rates of other control groups (p < 0.05). all ciliates died within 48 h of being fed microalgae, indicating that microalgae cannot serve as food for ciliates, and their diet or infection may be specific.",
