          "name": "Mujiyanto Mujiyanto",
          "search_text": "mujiyanto mujiyanto megabenthos indicators of coral reef health in a tropical marine protected area spatial trophic dynamics of reef fish in a small-island marine protected area, northern maluku megabenthos, such as mussels, sea cucumbers, and large benthic invertebrates, play a crucial role in maintaining the ecological function of coral reef ecosystems. they contribute to environmental balance through nutrient cycling and the maintenance of habitat structure. in recent years, megabenthos have become recognised as sensitive indicators of reef health, particularly in tropical mpas in the indo-pacific region, which are vulnerable to climate change and human pressure. based on this understanding, this study examines the diversity and structure of megabenthos communities as a practical approach to assessing the ecological condition of tropical coral reef mpas. field surveys were conducted on 12 transects around mare island, north maluku (indonesia), using a standardised belt transect method (70 m × 2 m). the analysis revealed 32 species from 20 families, dominated by molluscs and echinoderms. diversity values were low to moderate (h' = 0.5-2.4), evenness was relatively high (e = 0.65-0.95), and dominance varied among stations (c = 0.1-0.65), indicating a simple community structure and vulnerability to anthropogenic pressure. economically valuable species such as mussels, sea cucumbers, trochus snails, and lobsters dominated the target population, indicating their crucial ecological role and potential for overexploitation. a higher abundance of megabenthos was found in areas with healthier coral conditions, confirming the role of habitat complexity in maintaining the resilience of benthic communities. these results confirm that megabenthos are effective ecological indicators for monitoring the health of coral reefs and supporting adaptive management of marine protected areas in tropical regions. abstract: mare island’s coral reefs in tidore kepulauan city, northern maluku, support diverse reef fish communities that contribute to local food security and safeguard coastal ecosystem functions. to better understand their current condition, this study examined how reef fish trophic groups vary across depth zones within the island’s marine protected area. surveys were carried out between 23 and 29 august 2025 using underwater visual census (uvc) along belt transects. the community was primarily composed of planktivorous reef fishes, especially members of pomacentridae and caesionidae, suggesting that the plankton-based food web remains intact. commercially valuable predators (serranidae, lutjanidae, scaridae) occurred at only a few stations, suggesting habitat constraints or fishing pressure. coral-dependent indicators (chaetodontidae, zanclidae) were consistently observed, reflecting live coral support for key trophic processes. diversity peaked at mid-depths (9-10 m), while deeper areas showed reduced habitat complexity. these patterns reinforce the need for adaptive conservation through regulated fishing, protection of critical habitats, and long-term ecological monitoring. continued research linking fish community dynamics with benthic conditions will help ensure the effectiveness of this small-island marine protected area.",
