          "name": "Rita Rachmawati",
          "search_text": "rita rachmawati coral spawning in tunda island: larval source for coral recruitment tunda island is one of small islands in the north-western of java island, indonesia, with diverse corals, that is a potential source of coral larvae for coral recruitments for self-reef passive restorations. this study aims to collect coral spawning data in tunda island and to model their larval dispersal to estimate its contribution to surrounding recruitments. the spawning observations were done visually during night dive around full moon from january to november 2025 and the model were developed using opendrift. the results show that there were two coral spawning seasons, which were february to april and september to october.  november should still be a part of the spawning season, but bad weather made the spawning might be postponed or cancelled out. the most common coral to spawn were porites lobata. the other corals that were observed spawning were favia, favites, goniastrea, galaxea, and porites cylindrica. they were mostly observed to spawn between one day before full moon and five days after full moon. the larval dispersal model suggested the potential contribution of tunda island corals as the source of coral recruitments in surrounding areas up to 150 km south and 700 km east. further study to prove this model includes the genetic analysis to estimate the gene flow along the current direction of larval dispersal.",
