      "search_text": "national sun yat-sen university chinmay somani crystal mcrae mei-fang lin chinmay girish somani coral colonies and nubbins show similar responses to acute heat-stress climate change driven ocean warming and marine heatwaves are detrimentally impacting coral reef ecosystems globally. in response to this degradation, an array of lab experiments over the past several decades has aimed to elucidate mechanisms associated with high thermal tolerance. most of these experiments use coral fragments (i.e., nubbins) rather than whole colonies to determine coral heat-stress responses. yet, it is unclear if these nubbin-based responses are representative of colony-based responses. here, we used an acute heat-stress experiment to compare the physiological responses of coral colonies and nubbins using the common scleractinian coral pocillopora acuta. in brief, six colonies were collected from outlet reef in southern taiwan, and post-collection six nubbins were removed from each colony. then, colonies and nubbins were exposed to an 18h experiment with a peak temperature of 36°c; the experiment followed a similar heat ramping/cooling regime as the standardized coral bleaching automated stress system (cbass). key response metric assessed in this experiment included: photochemical efficiency (fv/fm), symbiont density, chlorophyll-a concentration, and host protein concentration. we found that the responses of colonies and nubbins were quite similar. no significant differences in symbiont density, chlorophyll-a concentration, or host protein concentration were found, but nubbins had marginally higher fv/fm than colonies.  this provides support for the use of coral nubbins as representative proxies of coral colonies in heat-stress experiments. future work comparing nubbin-based responses to colony-based responses in-situ would be a fruitful line of inquiry to explore. ensuring that lab assessments of nubbin-based responses are realistic is relevant to many areas of coral research (e.g., testing efficacy of assisted evolution interventions & identifying resilient genotypes for restoration) and their policy-based applications and implications. crystal mcrae coral research in taiwan: a systematic review of the past to inform priorities in the anthropocene taiwan is uniquely positioned at the intersection of major climatic and oceanographic regions and has some of the highest biodiversity of corals outside of the coral triangle. these ecosystems are critical to ecological function, regional ecosystem connectivity, and human livelihoods. despite decades of research on corals in taiwan, there has not been a comprehensive summary of literature produced, which limits broad-scale dissemination and recognition of associated findings. here, we use the standardized protocols of the preferred reporting items for systematic reviews and meta-analyses (prisma) to collate research undertaken on coral reefs in taiwan from the earliest published peer-reviewed record to the end of 2024. to ensure that we captured the breadth of research undertaken, we have completed our review in three languages: english, chinese, and japanese. for each language our search terms included linguistic-specific variants of coral-related terminology (coral, reef, cnidaria, scleractinia, alcyonacea, gorgonian, holobiont) and location terms (taiwan, formosa, republic of china). our review identified over 4000 papers, of which 1102 papers met our inclusion criteria. of the included papers, approximately half were related to natural product research, followed by studies examining the coral holobiont, reef dynamics, reproduction, and taxonomy. less than 5% of the papers examined were focussed on climate change. research locations spanned reefs all around the main island as well as offshore islands, with sites in southern taiwan and offshore islands collectively accounting for two thirds of all study sites. over 200 species of corals were examined, with octocorals being more broadly studied than scleractinian corals. overall, we identified a general research bias toward studies on single-species, adult life stages, and laboratory-based research. understanding taiwan’s dynamic coral reef ecosystems can provide novel insights and strategies relevant to resilience-based management, conservation, and restoration in the greater indo-pacific. mei-fang lin molecular adaptation of reef-building coral leptoria phrygia to localized thermal anomalies coral reef resilience depends on the ability of corals to withstand and adapt to environmental conditions. as reefs face intensifying climate change and local disturbances, understanding the molecular mechanisms underlying resilience has become essential. in southern taiwan, corals experience both global warming and localized thermal stress from a nuclear power plant (nppol), which elevates seawater temperatures and increases daily variability. to investigate adaptive strategies, we sequenced the genome of the common coral leptoria phrygia and integrated transcriptomic analyses across consecutive seasons with population genetic analyses. as expected, corals at the variable nppol site were exposed to greater thermal fluctuations than those at the adjacent stable reef (wlt). despite high connectivity and low genetic differentiation between sites, l. phrygia exhibited distinct transcriptional responses. gene ontology enrichment revealed seasonal shifts in stress responses, apoptosis, cytoskeletal remodeling, and energy metabolism linked to the tricarboxylic acid (tca) cycle, with stress-related pathways enriched in summer and winter. in contrast, autumn and spring featured upregulation of lipid metabolism and biosynthetic pathways, suggesting seasonal reallocation of resources toward energy storage, growth, and reproduction. comparative analysis of field and complementary aquarium tank experimental data identified a core set of differentially expressed genes, including vitellogenins, as potential biomarkers of reproductive status and stress resilience, as well as genes linked to ca2+ homeostasis and tca cycle, suggesting a symbiosis-mediated adaptation strategy. these findings indicate that l. phrygia maintains resilience through transcriptional plasticity rather than genetic differentiation, a strategy that may enhance the coral persistence under ongoing climate change.",
