      "search_text": "national taiwan ocean university pei wen tang tang wen stress tolerance of scleractinian coral tubastraea aurea (dendrophlliidae) adapted in acidic hydrothermal vent ecosystem off kueishan island environmental stressors and/or climate change strongly influence the physiological performance of marine organisms, including scleractinian corals. the azooxanthellate coral tubastraea aurea (dendrophylliidae) that dominantes in the natural high acidic hydrothermal vent (hv) ecosystem off kueishan island, northeast taiwan providing an excellent study model for coral stress tolerance assessment. in this study, we examined oxidative stress and antioxidant responses in t. aurea exposed to multiple environmental stressors at hv site. we quantidied the activities of key antioxidant enzymes, including superoxide dismutase (sod), catalase (cat) and glutathione peroxidase (gsh-px), and characterized the tissue-specific expression and regulation of heat shock proteins (hsps: tahsp25, tahsp60, tahsp70, and tahsp90). the results revealed that hv corals exhibited significantly (p < 0.05) elevated sod and cat activities compared with both non-vent (nhv, control) and hv corals maintained under control aquarium conditions, whereas gsh-px activity did not differ among groups. all four tahsps were expressed across multiple tissues, including whole tissue, tentacles, oral and aboral regions, theca, mesentery filaments, and gonads. notably, tahsp60 and tahsp70 were upregulated markedly in hv corals at 4.53- and 10.69-folds higher than the nhv population, while tahsp25 and tahsp90 showed no significant difference. under the natural acidic hydrothermal vent extreme environment, the t. aurea remains abundance off the vent site highlighting its exceptional physiological plasticity adapt to adverse environment. these findings provide valuable molecular insights of the coordinated roles of antioxidant defenses and hsp mediated stress responses in coral tolerance, enhance our understanding of coral resilience and adaptation under future climate change scenarios.\r\n\r\nkey words: scleractinian coral, hydrothermal vent, acidified environment, oxidative stress, hypoxia, climate change",
