      "geocode_level": "country",
      "distance_km": 10955.5,
      "search_text": "national technological institute of mexico dr carmen villegas carmen villegas sánchez indigenous women’s perceptions and emotional connections to coral reefs in cozumel, mexico coral reefs in cozumel support diving and reef-based tourism, generating an estimated annual revenue of $762 million usd and sustaining local livelihoods. this economic reliance highlights the importance of integrating community perspectives, particularly those of women, into reef conservation efforts. this study focuses exclusively on 27 indigenous women in cozumel, most of whom are of maya cultural background  (25 maya, 2 totonaca), and whose local identity is closely tied to island life. the women demonstrated strong community roots: 13 out of 14 who were born in quintana roo have lived their entire lives in cozumel, reflecting a profound connection to the island. through participatory interviews, the women expressed strong emotional ties to the reef, predominantly with feelings of disappointment, sadness, and fear about its decline, as well as hope and confidence in its recovery. temporal perception analysis revealed a decline in perceived reef health: “good” ten years ago, “regular” at present, and expected to be “bad” in ten years if current pressures persist. there was 100% agreement that women should have greater leadership opportunities, and that both men and women are equally capable of participating in conservation activities. the women identified tourism impacts, pollution, and climate-driven stress as primary threats, while highlighting persistent sociocultural barriers, such as gender norms and limited inclusion, that restrict their participation. these findings underscore the value of indigenous women’s environmental awareness, emotional investment, and culturally rooted socioecological perspectives to reef stewardship, reinforcing the urgent need to integrate gender and indigenous voices into coral reef management practices.",
