      "search_text": "national university of singapore allison miller allison miller what’s in the weeds? epibiotic diversity of sargassum on tropical reefs coral reef ecosystems face increasing pressure from phase shifts dominated by fleshy macroalgae. these shifts alter the habitat structure for reef-associated species, yet the baseline composition of the communities thriving within these algal areas remains poorly characterized. this study addresses the need to understand the overlooked inhabitants of sargassum, a common fleshy brown macroalgae group. sargassum species often demonstrate seasonality, can form harmful algal blooms, and are key players in coral reef phase shifts. we employ a multiplexed dna metabarcoding approach using nanopore sequencing to investigate epiphytic communities associated with specific organs (holdfast and fronds) and seasons (december high abundance and june low abundance) of different sargassum species (e.g., s. ilicifolium). by amplifying coi, 28s, and 18s molecular markers from samples collected across singapore’s tropical reefs, we aim to establish spatio-temporal patterns of epibiotic diversity hosted by sargassum macroalgae. this genomic surveillance allows us to better assess how community structures shift as sargassum populations bloom or shift on coral reefs. this information is needed to better understand the general diversity across reef-associated organisms and to interpret how these communities may be affected by disturbances such as storms and reef restoration actions such as sea-weeding.",
