          "name": "SHEAN-JEN Chen",
          "search_text": "shean-jen chen a yolov12–bytetrack framework for automated fish species annotation in complex coral reef environments achieving accurate underwater fish recognition requires large amounts of precisely annotated imagery, yet manual annotation is slow, labor-intensive, and highly challenging in complex coral reef environments. factors such as irregular reef structures, variable water turbidity, and shifting illumination further complicate annotation. to overcome this bottleneck, this study presents an automated annotation framework that integrates a lightweight yolov12 fish detection model with bytetrack multi-object tracking. underwater video was captured at 3-meter depth in the nearshore waters of southern taiwan at 30 fps. using a small set of manually annotated images for ten common local fish species, a preliminary yolov12 model was trained. the automated system then selects the three most suitable target fish every ten minutes–those with larger bounding boxes and simpler backgrounds–from the first frame of each interval. bytetrack subsequently locks onto and continuously tracks these targets. during the next three minutes, the system automatically extracts and annotates five images at 30-second intervals. this mechanism efficiently produced thousands of annotated images featuring diverse fish poses and coral reef conditions, substantially supporting iterative model refinement. experimental results show that the combined yolov12–bytetrack workflow rapidly generates high-quality annotated datasets and significantly improves detection performance. after incorporating the automatically generated imagery into training, yolov12 achieved over 90% recognition accuracy for the ten target species under complex coral reef environments. the proposed framework greatly reduces dependence on manual annotation while preserving high precision and consistency in the generated labels. it accelerates dataset expansion and offers a scalable solution for automated ecological monitoring, contributing to advanced marine biodiversity assessment.",
