      "search_text": "neom nature reserve wajd alaidrous wajd alaidrous field evaluation of a mechanical water lifting and mixing system to mitigate heat and hypoxia stress on northern red sea corals coral assemblages in the northern red sea, including neom’s reefs, are among the most thermally tolerant globally. however, during the fourth global bleaching event in summer 2024, even these heat-resistant corals exhibited severe stress, with bleaching affecting up to 60% of colonies and mortality reaching up to 30% at some sites. this underscores the urgency for localized adaptation strategies. we developed a renewable-energy-powered water lifting and mixing system (wlms) to entrain cooler, oxygen-rich water from depth to reef crests, simulating natural upwelling. initial trials in open-water conditions validated operational performance, achieving flow rates up to 3,400 m³·h⁻¹ and confirming modeled jet dynamics for optimal cooling (angle 60–80°, shallow outlet depth). the next phase will deploy wlms at coral reef sites during peak thermal stress to quantify ecological benefits. experimental design includes three sites (one intervention, two controls), with continuous monitoring of temperature profiles, dissolved oxygen, and coral health metrics (bleaching prevalence, survival). we hypothesize that wlms can reduce surface temperatures by 0.5–2°c and mitigate hypoxia, thereby improving coral resilience under marine heatwave conditions. outcomes will inform feasibility, ecological trade-offs, and scalability across neom’s marine protected area network, contributing to global strategies for engineering-assisted reef adaptation.",
