          "name": "Trent Haydon",
          "search_text": "trent haydon steroid hormone production by coral-associated endozoicomonas during thermal stress coral-associated bacteria are increasingly recognised as key players in holobiont function, yet the mechanistic bases of their contributions remain poorly understood. members of the genus endozoicomonas are globally abundant, frequently dominating the microbiomes of healthy corals, yet despite their prevalence and intimate association within host tissues, their contributions to coral physiology and stress responses remain largely unknown. here, we provide evidence that coral-associated endozoicomonas possess the capacity to biosynthesise and transform steroid-like compounds, including under thermal stress conditions. using a combination of genomic prediction, culture-based assays, and metabolomic profiling, we show that multiple endozoicomonas strains encode key enzymes involved in steroid biogenesis and modification pathways. in vitro incubations under both ambient and elevated temperatures revealed the production of steroid derivatives, with thermal stress enhancing the abundance of several putative hormone-like metabolites. notably, endozoicomonas was the only microbe capable of metabolising coral-derived testosterone, and under thermal stress converted ubiquitous cholesterol into testosterone and progesterone. these metabolites accumulated within the coral during heat stress, suggesting active microbial involvement in immune priming and stress mitigation. this work provides the first direct evidence linking endozoicomonas metabolism to steroid production in the coral holobiont and establishes a new framework for understanding how bacterial symbionts may chemically mediate coral responses to environmental change. further research into the signalling, immunological, and physiological consequences of these metabolites will help clarify the functional significance of endozoicomonas during coral thermal stress.",
