          "name": "Shanna Davidson",
          "search_text": "shanna davidson coralchromstem: deciphering the nanoscale chromatin 3d organization of transcriptional plasticity in the reef-building corals montipora digitata and pocillopora damicornis coral resilience depends on transcriptional plasticity, the capacity to rapidly alter gene expression in response to environmental stress. while the 3d genome organization is a known determinant of transcriptional plasticity by governing the accessibility of rna polymerase, transcription factors, and regulatory complexes to target genes in model organisms, the structural landscape of the coral genome remains a ‘black box’.  in mammalian models, chromatin folds into heterogeneous, nanoscale structures, termed packing domains (pd), containing dense heterochromatic cores and euchromatic peripheries that physically regulate transcriptional kinetics. the dynamic remodeling of these domains directly regulates transcriptional plasticity and cellular adaptation. however, this critical layer of 3d genomic regulation has never been visualized in reef-building corals. here, we introduce coralchromstem, adapting chromatin scanning transmission electron tomography to corals. utilizing dna-specific mass-contrast staining, we have reconstructed the chromatin architecture of two scleractinia corals, montipora digitata and pocillopora damicornis, with <5 nm resolution. we show that coral chromatin is not a random distribution but self-assembles into spatially distinct packing domains analogous to those found in higher eukaryotes. establishing this technique allows us to test the hypothesis that corals exhibiting higher thermal tolerance possess more dynamic chromatin remodeling capabilities resulting in higher physiological plasticity and increased survival to heat stress. specifically, the ability to rapidly modulate pd density allows resilient corals to optimize the \"goldilocks zone\" of macromolecular crowding, facilitating rapid access for rna polymerase and transcription regulatory factors to stress-response loci.  by defining the structural rules of chromatin geometry, this work provides a novel biophysical framework to understand the limits of coral plasticity and provide insights to inform conservation strategies to enhance reef survival in rapidly warming ocean.",
