          "name": "Michelle Baptist",
          "search_text": "michelle baptist evaluating rapid fish colonization and recreational fishery opportunities generated by nature-based artificial reefs off hollywood, florida natural coral reefs provide invaluable societal benefits, including economic value and livelihoods, and food security associated with thriving sustainable coral reef fisheries. however, accelerated habitat degradation from global and local stressors threatens not only biodiversity but also coastal communities that depend on reef fisheries for income, sustenance, and cultural identity. as natural reef degradation continues unabated, artificial reefs offer one potential complementary targeted strategy to enhance habitat, restore fish populations, and support fishery resilience. despite artificial reefs historically supporting local fisheries and adjacent economies worldwide, critical knowledge gaps persist in understanding how rapid fish colonization of artificial reefs intersects with socioeconomic factors such as stakeholder usage, fisheries management, and community engagement. specifically, we seek to understand how these factors may optimize productivity and economic benefits in urban coastal communities. ocean rescue alliance international’s (orai) “guardians of the reef” project was used as a case study to integrate participatory reef monitoring and socioeconomic analyses into understanding associated reef fishery resilience. in may 2025, 50 nearshore artificial reef structures were deployed at four locations (76.2-106.7 meters) off the hollywood, florida coastline. monitoring results identified 15 fish species and 212 fish counted at the natural reef one month after deployment, indicating rapid colonization. socioeconomic surveys distributed to the recreational fishery community indicated declining fish populations and specific plans to engage with the reefs for fishery enhancement. survey results, coupled with rapid recruitment trajectories, suggest the benefits that similar larger-scale and well-designed projects have for facilitating localized resilience of urban reef fisheries. along with developing research workshops for junior scientists in the sea (jsis), a florida organization designed for marginalized youth to have access to scuba diving and citizen science, this collaborative study focuses on restoring critical coral reef fisheries while simultaneously supporting local community engagement.",
