      "search_text": "ocean university of sri lanka dr fairoz mohamed mohamed farook mohamed fairoz ecological status of naturally recruited corals on the inner wall of the artificial breakwater at hambantota international port microbial taxonomic and functional profiles of sri lankan coral reefs: a metagenomic assessment artificial reefs are increasingly implemented to enhance marine biodiversity and ecosystem services, though their long-term ecological performance remains context-dependent. this study presents a baseline ecological assessment of coral communities and habitat condition along the inner wall of the artificial breakwater at hambantota international port , southern sri lanka. using underwater belt transects, photo quadrats, visual fish and invertebrate surveys, and microbial water-quality analyses, we evaluated community structure, trophic composition, and indicators of ecosystem health. substrate assessments revealed that approximately 65% of the surveyed area (zone 1) supported healthy benthic communities, characterized by an average of 35% live scleractinian coral cover under favourable water-quality conditions. coral assemblages displayed high structural diversity (shannon index h′ = 2.5), comprising a range of growth forms including branching, encrusting, massive, sub-massive, digitate, tabular, and foliose across genera such as acropora, montipora, porites, pocillopora, goniastrea, and platygyra. soft corals, sponges, ascidians, and gorgonians contributed additional three-dimensional complexity. reef fish diversity was similarly high (h′ = 2.3), with representatives from 15 families dominated by herbivorous and carnivorous guilds, reflecting a well-defined functional trophic structure. invertebrate richness included long and double spined sea urchins (diadema setosum, echinothrix calamaris), feather stars (comaster schlegelii), giant clams (tridacna spp.), oysters (saccostrea spp.), and spiny lobsters (panulirus versicolor). coral predation was minimal, with only one acanthaster planci observed. in contrast, zone 2 demonstrated early signs of degradation, including coral rubble, crustose coralline algal overgrowth, and localized bleaching, suggestive of mild thermal or nutrient stress.  recorded bacterial abundance at 2.3 ± 0.1 × 10⁵ cells ml⁻¹, with vibrio cholerae and shigella spp. prevalent in degraded areas, consistent with early stages of microbialization. sediments contained 2% organic matter trends of active bioturbation. these findings highlight ecological value of the hambantota artificial reef while emphasizing the need for continued monitoring to ensure long-term ecosystem sustainability. sri lanka’s coral reefs have experienced substantial degradation over recent decades due to the combined effects of natural disturbances and intensifying human pressures. major bleaching events in 1998, 2016, 2020, and 2024 caused extensive coral mortality, while unsustainable fishing, sedimentation, coastal development, and land-based pollution continue to degrade reef structure and resilience. despite these threats, relatively little is known about the diversity, functional capacity, and ecological roles of reef-associated microbial communities across the island’s coastal regions. this study provides the first integrated assessment of taxonomic and functional metagenomic variation in water-column microbial assemblages across seven coral reef sites in northern (coral island, small island, pigeon island, kuchchaweli) and southern (weligama, polhena, paravewella) sri lanka. reef condition differed between these regions, with northern sites exhibiting higher coral cover and lower algae cover (37.2 ± 3.4% and 23.5 ± 5.7%, respectively) than southern sites (24.3 ± 3.2% and 21.3 ± 3.2%). samples collected from the water column were subjected to whole-metagenome sequencing, functional gene profiling, gini inequality metrics, and average copy-number contribution (acc) analysis. results showed strong biogeographic partitioning linked to reef condition. northern reefs were dominated by oligotrophic taxa such as candidatus pelagibacter, synechococcaceae, and rhodobacteraceae, reflecting stable, low-nutrient conditions. in contrast, southern reefs showed elevated abundances of copiotrophic and disturbance-associated genera, including alteromonas, vibrio, and pseudoalteromonas. functional pathways enriched at southern sites included organic matter degradation, peptide transport, stress responses, and traits associated with rapid heterotrophic growth. acc values, highest at polhena and lowest at paravewella, suggested reduced functional evenness under disturbance. elevated gini coefficients across all sites indicated conserved inequality in dominant pathways such as peptidoglycan biosynthesis and dna competence systems. overall, these findings reveal a distinct microbial gradient from oligotrophic northern reefs to more disturbed southern systems. this study establishes  baseline for microbial monitoring and offers new insights.",
