      "search_text": "ohio state university andrea grottoli isabelle basden nick wilson shannon dixon andrea grottoli underwater zooplankton light enhancement array (uzela) technology as a coral restoration enhancement intervention coral feeding on zooplankton increases as zooplankton concentrations increase. in addition, resilience is higher in corals that eat more zooplankton. to leverage the advantage that zooplankton feeding has on coral resilience, we developed the underwater zooplankton light enhancement array (uzela) to locally concentrate natural zooplankton and give corals more to eat. uzela is programmable, autonomous, waterproof, and deployable for six months on a single battery. in the field, uzela increases local zooplankton concentrations by 7-fold and increases coral feeding rates by 10 to 50-fold. field tests on hawaiian and floridian corals shows that uzela can enhance coral growth, survivorship, and health metrics (see also abstracts presented by shannon dixon, nick wilson, and ann marie hulver). new advances in uzela include a self-cleaning wiper, rechargeable batteries, and an easy-to-use software interface that gives end-users options in the frequency and timing of the illumination and wiper functions. technological interventions like uzela can be part of the solution for enhancing restoration success. isabelle basden coralreef3d – low-cost and accurate underwater 3d ecosystem modeling as oceans continue to change, it is increasingly important to understand how coral health and growth vary over time and across broad spatial scales. traditionally, coral reef metrics have been monitored by trained divers in situ, but this approach is time consuming and spatially limited. advances in structure from motion photogrammetry (sfm) offer a new approach for more efficient reef monitoring. however, this approach requires two cameras to correct for water refraction, rigorous evaluations of its accuracy are insufficient, and accessibility is limited. we are developing a software toolkit, coralreef3d, that can generate high quality refraction-corrected sfm models with a single gopro camera. to assess the geometric accuracy of coralreef3d surface area and volume measurements, underwater gopro imagery was collected of tank-cultured corals at multiple time points and models were generated by the current standard software, agisoft metashape pro, and by the refraction-corrected coralreef3d software. in-air turn-table methods for each time point were used as ground truth measurements. the coralreef3d reconstructions produced denser and more complete point clouds than non-refraction-corrected metashape and achieved a 0.5mm mean residual error relative to the in-air ground truthing. to evaluate coralreef3d in situ, imagery was collected in oahu, hi at sites with varying levels of reef complexity. models will be generated using both metashape and the coralreef3d toolkit. we will compare the accuracy of reconstruction and surface area measurements for three different coral genera of differing morphologies; porites mounding (low complexity), pocillopora branching (intermediate complexity), and porites thicket-forming (high complexity). we expect the reconstruction error to be lower with coralreef3d for all species and that the reconstruction error will increase with species complexity. the coralreef3d toolkit will provide the needed advancements in accuracy and accessibility to use sfm for coral monitoring. nicholas wilson effect of uzela-enhanced feeding on the key reef-building caribbean coral orbicella faveolata global coral reef decline has accelerated under climate change, with florida’s coral reef (florida keys, usa) experiencing substantial losses in coral cover and ecosystem function. ensuring the future of coral reefs will require technological interventions that bolster coral resilience amid rapidly intensifying environmental stress. uzela (underwater zooplankton enhancement light array) is a technology that could help address this need. uzela is an underwater light device that locally concentrates zooplankton, increasing coral feeding rates by 10- to 50-fold. here, we tested the effect of uzela on the mounding reef building coral orbicella faveolata. eighty ramets were placed in the coral restoration foundation’s nursery in tavernier, florida, with and without a uzela from january to october 2025. coral planar surface area was quantified from photographs taken at six and ten months. at ten months, all ramets were collected for physiological analyses of chlorophyll concentration, symbiont density, stable isotopes, and lipid content. preliminary results from the first six months show that the planar area of o. faveolata ramets exposed to uzela increased by 11%, while control colonies decreased by 4.3%. we anticipate that this pattern will continue throughout the ten-month timepoint. additionally, ongoing laboratory analyses will allow us to evaluate other health metrics in response to uzela. we hypothesize that mounding corals exposed to uzela will have higher biomass and symbiont density, and isotopic values corresponding to increased feeding, which are traits that correlate with coral resilience. technological interventions like uzela have the potential to aid in reef restoration efforts in the florida keys and beyond, by giving corals the boost they need to grow faster and be more resilient in the face of climate change stressors. shannon dixon effect of uzela-enhanced feeding on the physiology of bleached and non-bleached montipora capitata and porites compressa corals the global decline of coral reefs has accelerated the need for technological interventions that enhance coral resilience and reef recovery. coral recovery from bleaching can be aided by increased rates of feeding on zooplankton. however, this strategy is limited in nature by natural zooplankton concentrations on the reef. the underwater zooplankton enhancement light array (uzela) is a device that capitalizes on the phototactic behavior of zooplankton and locally concentrates zooplankton. we hypothesized that heat-stressed corals exposed to uzela would have increased feeding and accelerated recovery following heat stress. to test this hypothesis, bleached and non-bleached montipora capitata and porites compressa ramets were deployed on the reef with uzela for six months. feeding rates, survivorship, and physiological parameters including calcification, branch extension, biomass, lipids, algal endosymbiont density, and pigmentation were measured throughout the experiment. twenty-eight ramets were sampled after 10 days, six weeks, and six months for m. capitata. p. compressa was only collected after 10 days as the remaining ramets died shortly thereafter of a parasitic infection. at all timepoints, bleached and non-bleached m. capitata and p. compressa corals demonstrated higher feeding rates with uzela than controls. bleached m. capitata and p. compressa  corals with uzela did not differ physiologically from bleached corals without uzela. non-bleached m. capitata corals with uzela had higher calcification and branch extension at the expense of their lipid concentrations than non-bleached corals without uzela.  these findings demonstrate that uzela can be used to increase growth of healthy m. capitata corals in nurseries or on reefs post-disturbance. with the continuing decline of coral reefs, technologies that can enhance coral feeding could play a critical role in coral resilience for coral restoration and conservation efforts.",
