      "search_text": "oregon state university erick white jun cai olivia burleigh virginia weis erick white symbiotic state and life stage alter the lipidomic profiles of exaiptasia diaphana in reef-building corals, symbiosis with dinoflagellate algae is the driver of host metabolism. algae transfer photosynthetically-fixed nutrients to the host and host metabolism is restructured to support and sustain the intracellular symbionts. lipids in particular are an important nutrient source during early stages of offspring development in corals. however, it is uncertain how the diversity and abundance of these compounds differs in offspring based on symbiosis and different modes of reproduction. therefore, we aimed to characterize the effect of symbiosis and host life stage on the lipidomic profiles of the sea anemone exaiptasia diaphana (commonly referred to as aiptasia), a model system for the study of coral symbiosis. we sampled symbiotic and aposymbiotic aiptasia adults, asexually-derived pedal lacerates, and sexually-derived larvae and isolated lipid fractions for liquid-chromatography mass-spectrometry (lc-ms) analysis. over 200 lipid compounds were identified across all sample groups. at every life stage, there were more compounds in symbiotic compared to aposymbiotic samples. lipidomic profiles of samples separated primarily based on symbiotic state and formed distinct clusters within life stages, except for symbiotic larvae which overlapped with aposymbiotic larvae and lacerates. triglycerides, ceramides, and phosphatidylethanolamines (pes) were the major lipid classes identified among test samples, and all of them were more abundant in symbiotic test groups. our results highlight the major influence of symbiotic state on lipid profiles in aiptasia at different life stages and provide insight into how the lipidome differs between sexual and asexual offspring. jun cai manipulation of sphingosine kinase reveals its functional role in cnidarian–algal symbiosis climate change has accelerated coral bleaching and the destruction of coral reefs, threatening the survival of these crucial hotspots of ecological, cultural, and economic services for millions worldwide. despite the threat of increased bleaching events, many aspects of the cellular mechanisms of coral-algal symbiosis remain under-studied. a greater understanding of these fundamental aspects of coral biology is essential for predicting how corals respond to ongoing environmental change and developing successful conservation strategies. \r\nsphingolipids are important lipid signalers in many cellular processes related to host-microbe interactions. we investigated the functional role of the sphingolipid pathway during symbiosis establishment in exaiptasia diaphana (commonly known as aiptasia), a model system for coral-algal symbiosis studies. we combined rnai-mediated gene knockdown and chemical inhibition of sphingosine kinase (sphk) to study sphingolipid regulation during symbiont uptake. we tested effects in symbiotic animals and those aposymbiotic animals inoculated with their endogenous symbiont breviolum minutum. symbiont density was quantified in living host tissues with epifluorescent microscopy and tissue homogenate with an automatic cell counter. our results showed that rnai successfully knocked down gene expression in symbiotic animals and sphk inhibition significantly reduced algal colonization in re-colonizing animals. these results indicate that sphk activity and sphingolipid signaling are essential for successful symbiont establishment, highlighting their important role in regulating coral-algal symbiosis. olivia burleigh smad3 regulation of cnidarian–algal symbiosis revealed by chip-seq: symbiotic state drives tgf-β signaling dynamics in aiptasia reef-building corals and other cnidarians rely on a crucial symbiosis with dinoflagellates (symbiodiniaceae), which can supply the host with up to 90% of its energy. with climate change driving more frequent and severe bleaching events, host stress and starvation result under elevated temperatures due to the expulsion or degradation of its symbionts. immune modulation is increasingly recognized as a key regulator of symbiosis establishment, maintenance, and breakdown. transforming growth factor beta (tgf-β) is a conserved cytokine involved in innate immune modulation, and prior work shows that tgf-β signaling promotes symbiont tolerance, whereas its inhibition can exacerbate bleaching. however, how tgf-β signaling integrates the diverse cellular and immune processes involved in symbiosis remains unclear. to address this, i identified binding sites of smad3, the canonical transcription factor downstream of the tgf-β receptor, during early heat stress in symbiotic and aposymbiotic exaiptasia diaphana. i paired chromatin immunoprecipitation sequencing (chip-seq) for smad3 with rna-seq to provide evidence of direct regulation of transcription by smad3. smad3 showed greater differential binding between symbiotic and aposymbiotic anemones than between temperature treatments, suggesting tgf-β/smad3 signaling is central to host–symbiont regulation under both ambient and heat-stress conditions. additionally, all differentially bound smad3 peaks were more enriched in symbiotic versus aposymbiotic anemones. go enrichment analysis was performed for genes with enriched smad3 binding in their promoter-proximal regions. between symbiotic states, smad3-associated genes showed enrichment for metabolic, signaling, and stress response pathways consistent with known mechanisms of cnidarian–algal symbiosis. this represents the first application of transcription factor chip-seq in a symbiotic cnidarian, providing a novel framework for directly investigating host transcriptional regulation. virginia weis in sickness and in health: cellular regulation of cnidarian-algal symbioses corals engage in a mutualistic symbiosis with intracellular photosynthetic dinoflagellates. this intimate partnership forms the trophic and structural foundation of coral reef ecosystems. this presentation will examine the cellular and molecular mechanisms underlying the establishment, maintenance and breakdown of the symbiosis in coral- and sea anemone-dinoflagellate partnerships. the talk will over two principal areas of inter-partner regulation. first, host innate immunity and symbiont strategies for modulating this immune response are central to the stability of the symbiosis. these innate immune mechanisms have been a key focus of the weis group and will be summarized during the presentation. second will be inter-partner regulation of biomass: how the partners keep pace with one another as the host grows and the algal population increases, will be presented as another focus of the weis lab. finally the presentation will cover the development of the model organism aiptasia as key tool for discovery in coral biology, aimed at accelerating solutions for assisting in the coral reef crisis.",
