      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Léonore Page",
          "search_text": "léonore page digital innovations for life history data in coral reef fisheries. knowledge of species biological characteristics is foundational to effective fisheries management. key parameters such as size at maturity, growth and reproductive status underpin data-limited assessment and inform measures such as size limits and seasonal closures. yet in many coral-reef fisheries, these data remain scarce because of resources and logistical constraints. most pacific island countries and territories (picts) still lack country-specific life-history information and often borrow parameters from other countries, despite growing evidence that size at maturity and growth vary considerably among countries. \r\n\r\nto bridge that gap, the pacific community (spc) coastal fisheries science team developed a bio-sampling module into ikasavea, a digital monitoring application used by national agencies across 16 picts. this module streamlines standardised collection of key biological data for reef species by recording length, weight, sex, maturity stage and gonad weight, essential for life-history analysis. spc complements the tool with trainings on biological sampling and on using the app, enabling officers to collect consistent data.\r\n\r\nto further support data use, a companion r shiny application was developed to analyse ikasavea-formatted bio-sampling data. after uploading data, users can explore dynamic maturity curves with sizes at 50% and 95% maturity, along with a simple index of spawning activity over time. this tool enhances accessibility to analytical outputs without requiring advanced coding skills.\r\n\r\ntogether, these illustrate how digital innovations can empower local actors to contribute to science and management. by embedding life-history data collection and analysis in accessible platforms, ikasavea helps bridge critical data gaps and supports more informed, locally grounded decision-making for pacific coral-reef fisheries.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Digital innovations for life history data in coral reef fisheries.",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "08f1e5ba-e0cd-45fa-8a4f-ad03f1fb8b79"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01307",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
      "geocode_level": "country",
      "distance_km": 1809.0,
      "search_text": "pacific community léonore page léonore page digital innovations for life history data in coral reef fisheries. knowledge of species biological characteristics is foundational to effective fisheries management. key parameters such as size at maturity, growth and reproductive status underpin data-limited assessment and inform measures such as size limits and seasonal closures. yet in many coral-reef fisheries, these data remain scarce because of resources and logistical constraints. most pacific island countries and territories (picts) still lack country-specific life-history information and often borrow parameters from other countries, despite growing evidence that size at maturity and growth vary considerably among countries. \r\n\r\nto bridge that gap, the pacific community (spc) coastal fisheries science team developed a bio-sampling module into ikasavea, a digital monitoring application used by national agencies across 16 picts. this module streamlines standardised collection of key biological data for reef species by recording length, weight, sex, maturity stage and gonad weight, essential for life-history analysis. spc complements the tool with trainings on biological sampling and on using the app, enabling officers to collect consistent data.\r\n\r\nto further support data use, a companion r shiny application was developed to analyse ikasavea-formatted bio-sampling data. after uploading data, users can explore dynamic maturity curves with sizes at 50% and 95% maturity, along with a simple index of spawning activity over time. this tool enhances accessibility to analytical outputs without requiring advanced coding skills.\r\n\r\ntogether, these illustrate how digital innovations can empower local actors to contribute to science and management. by embedding life-history data collection and analysis in accessible platforms, ikasavea helps bridge critical data gaps and supports more informed, locally grounded decision-making for pacific coral-reef fisheries.",
