      "search_text": "pacific islands fisheries science center hannah barkley jennifer samson hannah barkley revisiting the darwin point 40 years later: spatial patterns and temporal change in coral reef carbonate production across the hawaiian archipelago the critical ecosystem services that coral reefs provide rely on the production and persistence of complex, three-dimensional habitat. reef framework accumulates when calcification by marine organisms exceeds erosion, and thresholds of habitat persistence–termed darwin points–mark the geographical limits beyond which net carbonate production can no longer keep pace with sea level rise. as coral reef growth rates decline globally, reefs will increasingly transition toward net erosional states and the location of darwin points may consequently shift. in the 1980s, strong latitudinal gradients in coral cover, calcification, and carbonate production rates were documented across the hawaiian archipelago, with the northernmost atoll, kure, identified as the darwin point. forty years later, we reassessed spatial patterns and drivers of hawaiian coral reef carbonate production and investigated the present day location of the darwin point threshold. using in-water and image-based reefbudget assessment techniques refined for the u.s. pacific islands, we evaluated benthic cover and estimated carbonate production and biological erosion rates across twelve islands and atolls in the main and northwestern hawaiian islands. we observed significant declines in coral cover and carbonate production at most islands over the past four decades, likely driven by repeated bleaching events, ocean acidification, invasive species outbreaks, and other localized environmental stressors. while most reef sites and islands were net producing, several locations were already at or approaching net erosional states. in addition, although methods differ between studies, our findings suggest a more southerly darwin point for the hawaiian archipelago than previous estimates. our study demonstrates the importance of these functional metrics in resilience-based management and highlights the increasing spatial constraints on reef growth in the face of ongoing climate change. jennifer samson maximizing impact: enhancing noaa's coral reef monitoring for pacific islands management long-term, large-scale coral reef monitoring is essential for detecting ecosystem change, evaluating management actions, and supporting ecological assessments. since 2000, noaa coral reef conservation program’s (crcp) national coral reef monitoring program (ncrmp) has surveyed more than 25,000 individual sites, recording over 20 different benthic, fish, and climate indicators, including coral cover and demography, fish biomass and abundance, temperature and carbonate chemistry. in recent years, ncrmp has worked on streamlining data collection, improving data pipelines, standardizing qa/qc, adopting advanced technologies, and integrating multi-disciplinary datasets. these advances have increased both the accessibility of data to managers and communities, and the suitability of these data to support local and regional management actions, fisheries management plans, coral restoration siting, and ecosystem condition reports. ncrmp surveys also allow for comprehensive ecological assessments of uninhabited, remote islands under different management regimes, offering a unique opportunity to evaluate the impacts of human activities and management effectiveness.\r\n\r\nncrmp's \"wide but thin\" approach of surveying each u.s. pacific archipelago every three years with island-scale data delivery, still has limitations for local management needs. to address this, noaa’s crcp provides project-based funding to support direct collaboration with local managers and communities to co-develop monitoring efforts that leverage the wider ncrmp datasets, while providing training and support for data collection, management, and analyses, and the development of local and regional technical products, including a restoration guidance framework, a u.s. pacific-wide resilience assessment, and an environmental data summary tool that links survey data with environmental information to better understand drivers of ecological observations. future plans to increase the impact of ncrmp survey data include improved analytical workflows, creation of interoperable data products, increased data visualization tools, and a focus on developing ecological analyses and indicators tailored to meet priority coral reef management needs.",
