          "name": "E. Ikelau Otto",
          "search_text": "evelyn otto palau edna project: integrating accessible and innovative technologies to understand biodiversity across large ocean space and the interconnection between coral reef and pelagic ecosystems the republic of palau is a small island, large ocean country with strong conservation practices instilled in its traditions. today, palau has over 34 marine protected areas (mpas) nearshore. in 2015, palau took a bold conservation action by creating the palau national marine sanctuary (pnms)–closing off 80% of palau’s exclusive economic zone (eez)– leaving 20% open for fishing activities and went into full effect in 2020. a biodiversity assessment of the 475,077 km2 large-scale marine protected area (mpa) and a better understanding of the interconnectedness of the coral reefs with the pnms was determined crucial to fill knowledge gaps and guide management decisions. the palau environmental dna (edna) project aims to establish a baseline for biodiversity monitoring. since 2021, edna samples have been collected across 32 stations – 12 stations within the coral reefs of palau and 20 stations beyond, providing insights on occurrence and distribution of culturally important marine vertebrates and filling the biodiversity knowledge gaps. environmental sensors were incorporated alongside cost-effective and accessible sampling devices, which provided additional environmental context of the physical conditions and further insight into the taxa identified. the palau edna project is the first edna timeseries in palau and its novel cross-agency collaboration allows research and conservation to be streamlined to enable increased data collection for protected areas that can inform future decision-making and connect local communities to offshore ecosystems. with a strong record of conservation, palau offers an example for other nations striving to protect their oceanscapes. as the palau edna project continues, it serves as a framework for observing efforts to scale to other large ocean states within the region and internationally.",
