          "name": "Greta Sartori",
          "search_text": "greta sartori morphologically driven sedimentation patterns on a coral reef the functional geomorphology of coral reefs as living landforms sediment dynamics are a critical but underexplored component of reef function, influencing coral settlement, growth, and recovery. traditional monitoring often overlooks how reef morphology regulates sediment fate, yet these linkages can determine how reefs recover from disturbance. in 2012, typhoon bopha caused substantial damage to palau’s eastern barrier reef, where a 4 km tract has since exhibited contrasting recovery across adjacent sections. this recovery pattern provided an opportunity to assess how geomorphic differences drive post-disturbance trajectories.\r\nour recent study (sartori et al. 2025, coral reefs), demonstrated how spur and groove (sag) morphology shapes sediment transport pathways and stabilizes substrate conditions. we used lidar-derived bathymetry to characterize reef geomorphology and identify major sediment deposits. we combined these observations with targeted hydrodynamic measurements and sediment analysis to quantify sediment suspension thresholds. spurs consistently exceeded thresholds for sediment motion, remaining free of deposits and offering coral settlement niches, while grooves exhibited tidal asymmetry with net seaward transport of sediment during ebb flows. this exported sediment and rubble downslope into deeper zones less prone to resuspension, promoting substrate stabilization. in contrast, adjacent low-relief areas lacking sags were dominated by tidal channel-driven sediment accumulation, which inhibited benthic colonization and delayed recovery.\r\nthese findings establish reef geomorphology as a first-order driver of sediment stress and recovery potential, bridging hydrodynamic and ecological perspectives. by demonstrating how morphologically driven sedimentation dynamics underpin substrate availability and stability, this work advances the use of geomorphology as a functional indicator of reef resilience. more broadly, it emphasizes the need for a holistic approach that treats coral reefs as biogenic landforms, where geomorphology and ecological function are inseparable. recognizing reef form as an active driver of ecosystem dynamics provides a pathway toward process-based indicators of resilience that operate at scales relevant to management and restoration. monitoring coral reefs has long relied on ecological metrics, yet recent work (sartori et al. 2025, coral reefs) highlights the pivotal role of reef form in regulating ecological outcomes. reef geomorphology governs hydrodynamics and substrate stability, yet no unifying framework links morphology-flow interactions to reef function.\r\nwe propose a functional classification of reef morphology grounded in hydrodynamic processes that govern ecological function. the framework is hierarchical, accounting for the scale dependence and nesting of morphological units, boundary layer physics, and ecological interactions:\r\n1. planform units (>1 km) – large-scale reef bends that regulate oceanographic patterns, connectivity and larval supply.\r\n2. classic reef zones (km–100s m) – within planform units, positions along the reef profile that define gradients in light, exposure, and community structure (e.g., crest, flat, slope, lagoon).\r\n3. nested units (100s–10s m) – within classic reef zones, regulate mass transfer, sediment transport, substrate stabilization and can be:\r\na) repetitive: self-organizing in regular flow regimes (e.g., spur-and-groove systems, patch reefs).\r\nb) localized: transient phases of flow acceleration, directional change or gravity driven (e.g., tidal deltas, boulder ridges, sediment chutes, aprons).\r\n4. embedded features (10s m–cm) – fine-scale elements mediating benthic community responses (e.g., single spurs or grooves, delta lobes).\r\ntogether, these nested morphologies define the physical and ecological template of the reef, from broad-scale energy exposure and connectivity to fine-scale processes of settlement and recovery. this framework enables resilience to be evaluated through flow regimes, sediment dynamics, and substrate stability - key processes defining the context for coral recovery at management-relevant scales. palau high resolution lidar bathymetry data is now being used to trial this classification scheme, coupled with in situ hydrodynamic measurements and ecological surveys. this moves us closer to a future where high-resolution maps could diagnose reef resilience in real time, optimizing monitoring efforts and empowering resource-limited regions.",
