          "name": "Clancy McIntyre",
          "search_text": "clancy mcintyre fine scale resolution of zooxanthellae cell wall molecular composition and its potential role in regulating endosymbiosis the intra-cellular mutualisms between animals and dinoflagellate endosymbionts (family: symbiodiniaceae) are widespread and, most significantly, the foundation of earth’s coral reef ecosystems. symbiodiniacean dinoflagellates are diverse and associate with a broad diversity of invertebrate and single-celled hosts that require them for survival. the molecular communication between both eukaryotic partners involving signaling, recognition, and nutrient transfer is complex and poorly understood. the interface for this exchange occurs at the symbiont’s cell wall, which functions as both a barrier and filter that modulates the flux of molecules necessary to maintain this mutualism. dinoflagellate cell walls are made primarily of cellulose, proteins and glycoproteins and bound in vesicles (called amphiesma) found beneath the plasma membrane, and are thus internal. symbionts capable of living within and outside a host undergo a visible reduction in cell wall thickness for intracellular life, but it is unknown whether a change in cell wall molecular composition is responsible for this adaptation. glycome profiling, an approach often used to study plant cells, was used to characterize pectin and hemicellulose related moieties in symbiodinium microadriaticum and breviolum minutum living in vitro and in hospite. additionally, bioinformatic analyses of cell wall modification genes were obtained from available symbiodiniaceae transcriptomes and compared against the cazy (carbohydrate-active enzymes) database to infer the evolution and origin of discovered carbohydrates. this data has revealed that previously uncharacterized carbohydrates in dinoflagellate cell walls such as xylans and mixed-linkage glucans make up a significant component of symbiodiniaceae thecae and may be varying in abundance between symbiotic states.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Fine scale resolution of zooxanthellae cell wall molecular composition and its potential role in regulating endosymbiosis",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "a15d4f44-e90e-44dc-b7ca-f0a950416208"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01142",
          "attended": true
