      "search_text": "perry institute for marine science aaron hartmann aaron hartmann demonstration of coral reef arks in vieques, puerto rico: a modular, whole-ecosystem restoration innovation. rebuilding complexity: community seeding as a framework for holistic coral reef restoration coral reef arks are a new type of artificial reef that aim to provide ideal environmental conditions for corals reef communities by elevating them off degraded benthos. arks are geodesic spheres that can be seeded with both translocated corals, and key cryptic reef biodiversity passively accumulated on autonomous reef monitoring structures (arms) units. two 8-foot geodesic coral arks were deployed in vieques, puerto rico in the midwater several miles from shore. as hypothesized, this location indeed provided improved water quality conditions that led to significant improvements in reef health metrics compared to benthic control sites: higher coral survival, growth, and recruitment, less fleshy macroalgae and turf algae, more biodiversity, and more piscivorous fish. arms units were added to the arks partway through the deployment, allowing separate testing of how that intervention affected various aspects of reef resilience. most notably, arms seeding led to rapid increases in coral growth and fish biomass. after 2.5 years in the midwater, the arks were then relocated, intact, to benthic sites closer to shore, keeping the arks at the same depths but securing them directly to the seafloor, to test whether the improvements achieved in the midwater could be retained.   \r\nefforts to reverse the decline of coral reefs around the world are hampered by degraded benthic conditions that caused much of the decline in the first place. coral reef arks provide a useful approach to provide healthy conditions to regenerate small reef units that are more ecologically functional than degraded natural reefs. once established communities develop on the arks, they can re-seed adjacent natural reefs with larvae or the arks can be translocated back to adjacent degraded locations to support re-establishment of reefs. results from the vieques coral arks demonstration project will be presented, identifying how this intervention influences various aspects of reef resilience. ecosystems are complex assemblages of micro- and macroscopic organisms interacting with non-living components. the interaction of this milieu generates biological and ecological functions commonly called ecosystem services, such as water purification, atmospheric gas exchange, and biomass generation for human consumption. ecosystem services are being lost due to the ongoing decline of earth’s ecosystems at the hands of humanity, giving rise to ecosystem restoration–an active and theoretically holistic process so essential that the un declared the 2020s the decade on ecosystem restoration.  but most ecosystem restoration efforts focus on one or a handful of foundation-forming species, not the greater community. implicit in this approach is the assumption that restocking organisms like corals and trees will produce reefs and forests because everything else will follow. existing data do not support this assumption on coral reefs. therefore, there remains a need for reef restoration approaches that reconstruct the biological and non-biological complexity of these ecosystems. here, i describe aspects of ecosystem restoration that are currently underutilized within the area of “community seeding” on coral reef systems and highlight case studies for: (1) restocking communities/assemblages rather than species, (2) using passive organism collection approaches rather than intensive nursery rearing, (3) conferring beneficial viral and microbial communities as reservoirs of raw material for rapid adaptation and acclimatization, and (4) building food webs in addition to rebuilding habitat structure. these approaches and strategies demonstrate a path toward next-generation reef restoration by emulating natural ecosystem complexity and are thus more likely to produce resilient, self-sustaining reefs in the face of ongoing environmental change.",
