      "search_text": "posgrado en ciencias del mar y limnología daniela rojas daniela rojas-cano gene expression of heat-stress biomarkers in corals from remote reef in the gulf of mexico during the 2023–2024 warming event. gene expression modulation is a key acclimation mechanism in reef-building corals, as it allows rapid responses to environmental stressors. consequently, molecular biomarkers provide powerful tools for the early detection of physiological stress and for linking cellular responses to local environmental conditions. during 2023-2024, identified as one of the most extreme and spatially widespread thermal stress events ever recorded on coral reefs. we analyzed the expression of heat shock protein (hsp70) in orbicella faveolata and montastraea cavernosa from the remote cayo arenas reef system on the campeche bank in the southern gulf of mexico. thermal conditions prior to sampling differed markedly between years: in 2023, the historical annual maximum temperature for the site (29.56 °c) was exceeded for 28 consecutive days, while in 2024 only 12 days of thermal stress were recorded, likely mitigated by hurricane beryl.\r\nin july 2023 and 2024, 1 cm² tissue fragments were collected from ten colonies per species on six reefs at depths between 7 and 9 m. hsp70 expression was quantified using qpcr. high-resolution orthomosaics were generated using underwater photogrammetry to relate gene expression to local habitat-scale variables, enabling the analysis of seascape metrics such as colony connectivity, colony area, patch complexity, coral cover at the species level, and the depth of individual colonies.\r\nin 2023, o. faveolata showed positive expression of hsp70 in 30 colonies and m. cavernosa in 22, with significant differences between reefs but no apparent relation to the variables analyzed. samples from 2024 are still being analyzed. the spatial variability observed in 2023 may reflect localized acclimation processes and the thermal resilience of corals in this remote reef system, underscoring the importance of integrating molecular, ecological, and seascape data to better understand coral responses to increased thermal stress.",
