      "search_text": "positive ripple consulting marie-céline piednoir marie-céline piednoir coral reef commitments are largely absent from national biodiversity and climate frameworks. global agreements under the convention on biological diversity (cbd) and the un framework convention on climate change (unfccc) increasingly emphasize integrated, multi-scalar action that links community-led stewardship with national and global policy commitments. yet how countries translate these frameworks into national strategies remains poorly understood, even for ecosystems–and communities–highly vulnerable to warming, such as tropical coral reefs. bleaching-level heat stress has affected over 85% of global reefs and likely impacted at least 97 of 101 reef-holding countries, threatening the social, cultural, and livelihood benefits that reef-dependent communities rely upon.\r\n\r\nwe assessed how the 25 countries with the largest reef areas (“high-coral countries”) are operationalizing global commitments by analyzing national biodiversity strategies and action plans (nbsaps) and kunming–montreal global biodiversity framework (km-gbf) targets, as well as nationally determined contributions (ndcs) and national adaptation plans (naps) under the paris agreement. as of august 2025, 71% of high-coral countries had submitted km-gbf–aligned targets, yet none included measurable commitments for coral reefs. only one country linked climate threats to quantitative target 3 commitments, and eight referenced marine ecosystem action under target 8. under the paris agreement, fewer than half (48%) mentioned coral reefs in ndcs, and only three included measurable reef-related commitments; seven countries referenced reefs in naps, none with quantitative targets.\r\n\r\nthese findings reveal a significant disconnect between global ambition and national implementation, leaving reef managers and local communities without the enabling policy environment needed to scale effective, bottom-up conservation. we propose three priorities to close this gap: (i) co-develop national coral reef strategies that integrate local knowledge and stewardship; (ii) elevate coral reefs within nbsaps as biodiversity and climate-resilience priorities; and (iii) include measurable reef commitments within future ndcs and naps. strengthening coherence across biodiversity and climate frameworks–and building multi-scalar pathways that amplify community-led stewardship–is essential to securing climate-resilient coral reefs.",
