      "search_text": "rare - indonesia irlan assidiq irlan assidiq livelihoods at risk: how climate vulnerability links fishing families and reef fish populations small-scale fishing communities are critically dependent on coral reef ecosystems for both livelihoods and food security. this reliance renders them acutely vulnerable to climate-induced pressures on fisheries, including coral bleaching, rising sea surface temperatures, sea level rise and ocean acidification. this study investigates the socioecological linkages between small-scale fishing households and climate-vulnerable reef fish populations. interdisciplinary data from household-level socioeconomic surveys and underwater reef fish assessments was examined across more than 1500 fishing households and 60 reef locations in indonesia and the philippines.\r\nwe examined relationships between household-level livelihood diversity, income dependence on fishing, fishing gear use, and fish consumption, against the vulnerability of reef fish populations to climate impacts. fishing households were categorised along a gradient of dependence on fisheries for livelihoods, fishing income dependence, gear use and fish consumption frequency. similarly, we classified reef sites as having low, medium, or high vulnerability of reef fish assemblages, using published species-level climate vulnerability indices. \r\npreliminary findings indicate that households with more jobs, more fishing gears and with the highest fish consumption, are associated with reefs containing a lower proportion of highly climate-vulnerable reef fish species. we explore these identified relationships between reef fish vulnerability and multiple interacting socioeconomic domains, to advance our understanding of the benefits of climate-resilient reefs on coastal people’s livelihoods and food security. the research underlines the need for context-specific, community-based coastal management strategies that strengthen ecological integrity and enhance socioeconomic resilience to climate change.",
