      "search_text": "rebuilding with nature chitra ramphul chitra ramphul an innovative, low-cost substrate for boosting coral conservation a highly promising, innovative, and environmentally friendly substrate for enhancing both sexual and asexual coral propagation in marine restoration initiatives  derived from recycled glass, fg. fg offers a low-cost and sustainable alternative to conventional materials, addressing a major barrier in large-scale coral restoration: substrate availability and affordability. its application in coral conservation has gained increasing interest due to its durability, light weight, and capacity to support biological processes essential for coral growth.\r\n\r\nfield evaluations conducted in rodrigues island, mauritius, demonstrated that fg significantly outperformed traditional terracotta plates during coral spawning events. higher larval settlement rates were consistently observed on fg surfaces, indicating its strong potential to support natural recruitment. complementary laboratory experiments further confirmed that coral fragments attached and stabilized more rapidly on fg compared to other tested substrates. this accelerated fixation reduces handling time and improves overall restoration efficiency.\r\n\r\nthe effectiveness of fg is closely linked to its physical and chemical characteristics. its porous structure, combined with a composition enriched with silica and calcium carbonate, creates an optimal environment for coral settlement and attachment. the pores also facilitate the colonization of beneficial microorganisms such as bacteria, plankton, crustose coralline algae, and macroalgae. these organisms play a critical role in producing settlement cues that stimulate coral larval attachment. additionally, fg with varied pore sizes provides microhabitats that shelter early-stage coral larvae, enhancing juvenile survival by offering protection from predators and environmental stressors.\r\n\r\nbeyond its biological advantages, fg is easy to transport, shape, and deploy in different reef environments, making it a practical option for restoration practitioners. overall, the study highlights foamed glass as an ideal substrate for coral conservation, significantly boosting settlement success, attachment rates, juvenile survival, and long-term restoration outcomes, all while remaining cost-effective and time-efficient.",
