      "search_text": "reef and rainforest research centre sheriden morris suzanne long sheriden morris managing for reef resilience as the climate changes: key factors, innovations, interventions and tools from the great barrier reef effective management for reef resilience under climate change requires a clear understanding of the processes that sustain and enable adaptation in coral communities, as well as targeted actions that support those processes during periods of stress and recovery. drawing on recent experience from the great barrier reef (gbr), this presentation outlines four key aspects that underpin resilience-focused management and highlights how they interact to influence ecological outcomes in a rapidly warming climate.\r\nfirst, maintaining the survival and condition of adult corals remains foundational. adult colonies not only contribute directly to reef structure and ecosystem function, but also provide the reproductive capacity that fuels recovery following disturbances. management actions that reduce local stressors (including high water temperatures), support water quality improvements, and mitigate cumulative impacts help preserve this critical biological capital.\r\nsecond, understanding and enhancing connectivity and larval recruitment is essential for supporting long-term reef replenishment. recent advances in larval dispersal modelling, genetic approaches, and field-based seeding interventions are providing new insights into the sources, sinks, and pathways that shape reef recovery potential across the gbr.\r\nthird, we need the capacity for stewardship and active intervention. this includes community-led monitoring, traditional owner leadership, targeted protection of high-value sites, as well as scalable restoration and adaptation technologies. adaptive governance and strong partnerships are increasingly central to this capacity.\r\nfinally, targeted research that directly informs management decisions is crucial as climate risks accelerate. driven research–management co-design, rapid applied trials, and clear decision pathways are necessary to ensure that emerging knowledge translates quickly into on-ground action.\r\nthese are the key features of a practical, interlinked framework for supporting reef resilience. insights from the gbr illustrate both the challenges and opportunities of managing coral ecosystems in this era of unprecedented change. suzanne long working faster together: a trans-hemisphere collaboration enabling accelerated development of a “toolkit” of coral bleaching interventions (red sea + coral sea) the global nature of the bleaching crisis is (sadly) an opportunity to accelerate research and development of cost-effective management interventions that aim to reduce water temperatures and increase water circulation on vulnerable coral reefs. to date, individual organisations’ efforts to develop such tools and improve outcomes for corals during mass bleaching events have been hampered by only being able to field-test interventions at one time of year (i.e. their peak & late summer). coordinated collaboration under a single programme covering both the northern and southern hemispheres could at least double the rate at which we develop, trial, and implement effective interventions; with two-way sharing of results and key learnings between partners.\r\n\r\nhere we report on how our “red sea + coral sea” collaboration between the cairns-based reef and rainforest research centre and neom nature reserve in the northern red sea has accelerated progress towards an effective water mixing intervention that could improve outcomes for corals at small-scale sites of high biological, economic, and cultural value during mass bleaching events.",
