          "name": "Victor Bonito",
          "search_text": "victor bonito integrating climate-smart coral restoration into community-based coral reef management: a case study from fiji’s korolevu-i-wai district. coral reef resilience: outcomes and lessons learned from 23 years of community-based marine management from fiji’s korolevu-i-wai district. rapidly intensifying impacts from climate change and local stressors are beginning to outpace the capacity of traditional passive management practices alone to sustain coral communities on some reef areas creating a need to integrate climate-smart, ecologically relevant active interventions to help bolster coral community resilience. tailoring active coral restoration to local contexts and management practices can be challenging in fiji and other developing nations, particularly with limited access to finance and other resources; however, when implemented in conjunction with other marine management tools and using locally available materials and cost-effective methods, such approaches can provide scalable opportunities for ecological gains while strengthening local stewardship.\r\n\r\nhere we share lessons learned and strategies utilized from a 19-year collaboration with the vanua davutukia in fiji’s korolevu-i-wai district where active coral restoration has been integrated into ongoing community-based marine management efforts to achieve a suite of ecological and socio-economic objectives. scaled up in 2015, this effort strategically propagates and transplants 9000-10,000+ corals a year to complement the outcome from other ongoing marine management efforts.  key approaches include using low-cost, locally sourced materials to implement locally led coral-gardening programs, the integration of traditional knowledge with applied science into restoration planning, using resilience-based methods that prioritize genotypes likely to withstand future climate impacts, and incorporating coral predator control efforts. by presenting both successes and challenges from nearly two decades of restoration efforts, we provide practical insights for other pacific island communities seeking to bolster coral community health and resilience, climate adaptation, and local stewardship under resource-limited conditions. the lessons shared underscore the value of context-specific, culturally informed yet scientifically designed, scalable approaches for supporting coral reef resilience in a rapidly changing climate. as coral reefs undergo accelerating climate-driven and local stressors, long-term evaluation of management strategies is essential to understand their capacity to sustain ecosystem function and services. the establishment of no-take protected areas (mpas), where extractive activities are prohibited, has been a primary strategy for managing and enhancing coral reef fisheries, conserving biodiversity, and building resilience to climate impacts in fiji and the south pacific, where mpas are primarily community-based and relatively small (<1 km²). however, the temporal ecological trajectories of these mpas and the extent to which they influence adjacent fished areas remain poorly quantified.\r\n\r\nusing over two decades of ecological monitoring in the korolevu-i-wai district of fiji’s coral coast, we assessed how small mpas influence temporal shifts in benthic communities and fish assemblages, and whether protection fosters system-wide resilience. in 2004, both mpas and adjacent fished areas had <10% coral cover, >35% macroalgal cover, and depressed food-fish populations. protection enabled herbivore recovery within mpas and by 2012, mpas supported >50% coral cover, 50% more coral species, 50% more food-fish species, and 500% more food fish biomass compared to adjacent fished areas, which remained macroalgae-dominated.\r\n\r\nin 2016, a marine heat wave caused a 30–50% decline in coral cover within mpas, yet corals rebounded to pre-bleaching levels by 2020 and coral and fish species richness continue to increase, demonstrating strong resilience. in contrast, adjacent fished reefs showed no comparable recovery; instead, they experienced continued declines in coral and fish species richness and remain locked in a persistent macroalgal state despite proximity to mpas. these divergent trajectories highlight that mpas alone are insufficient to restore adjacent fished areas or maintain herbivory at scales necessary to support system-wide coral recovery. maintaining adequate herbivory across entire reef systems is critical for promoting coral resilience and enabling replenishment benefits from mpas to adjacent areas.",
