          "name": "Ofri Johan",
          "search_text": "ofri johan managing large polyp corals as a trading target from indonesia by decreasing the quota, and efforts to culture both in situ and ex-situ of asexual and sexual reproduction indonesia is the world's leading exporter of wild and cultured corals. large polyp corals (lps), especially those with a slow growth rate, have not yet been successfully cultured by the fragmentation method. the limited population of 6 provinces, which the government allows to be collected every year, is a source of collection areas. stock assessment carried out at 9 sites from 3 provinces of the collection area found the abundance of lps across four different habitats: hard substrate, sandy, reef flat/slope, and lagoon. the survey found the catalaphyllia geoffroyi in lagoon (8.96 col/m2) and hard substrate (4.48 col/m2), cynarina lacrymalis on hard substrate (0.22 col/m2), sandy (0.05 col/m2), reef flat/slope (0.23 col/m2), and lagoon (0.04 col/m2). then the coral trachyphyllia geoffroyi is found in sandy and lagoon with the same abundance amount (0.55 col/m2).\r\nfragmentation of some species of lps have already been done at three different depths in the wild, 5 m, 10m, and 15 m for plerogyra sp, physogyra sp, and nemenzophyllia sp that changed the color during the research, which using rgb analyse of time series of underwater photos, and the survival rate was 71.4%, 50.0% and 0%. unlike the ex-situ site, all fragments were successful until 8 months into the research. it means the possibility sign to doing in the control area (ex situ) in the future. but the slow growth is not guaranteed to supply the high demand for this coral trading. the last effort is to study the potential reproduction by sexual in situ and ex situ. currently, we are preparing a collection of broodstock to keep in ex-situ with a controlled water system and try to observe the spawning during and after a full month for the next several months.\r\nkeywords: abundance, asexual- sexual reproduction, fragmentation, large polyp corals, stock assessment",
