          "name": "Lisa Schellenberg",
          "search_text": "lisa schellenberg untargeted metabolomics reveals distinct regional chemical dom signatures across the caribbean coral reefs in the caribbean, among the world’s most biodiverse ecosystems, are increasingly threatened by climate change, but also by coastal development, and pollution. untargeted environmental metabolomics offers a powerful way to track anthropogenic impact, besides natural processes, by providing molecular fingerprints of dissolved organic matter (dom). metabolites are the resultant of reef exudates, microbial processes, and terrestrial inputs, capturing subtle chemical signals often overlooked in traditional monitoring. we applied untargeted metabolomics for comparative characterization of dom in nearshore waters of bonaire, curaçao, saba, and st. eustatius. this high-resolution analyses revealed a pronounced regional divide in the overall dom composition, abundances and chemical class distribution of northern–southern islands, also reflected in n:c ratios, gibbs free energy, and doc concentrations. northern islands consistently exhibit higher n:c ratios and lower nominal carbon oxidation states, suggesting a biogeochemical regime enriched in nitrogenous and energy rich dom. chemical characterization showed that northern islands contained substantially higher proportions of organic nitrogen and organosulfur compounds, while the southern islands were enriched in lipids and lipid-like molecules. more specifically, anthropogenic pollutants (i.e., xenobiotics) accounted for 12% to 34% of the dom fraction, with higher contributions in the northern islands (st. eustatius 28%, saba 25%) than in the southern islands (curaçao 22%, bonaire 17%). the xenobiotics categories human metabolites, food-contact chemicals, and plastic-additives were particularly elevated in the northern islands. these xenobiotics introduce anthropogenic molecular features to the dom pool and may alter microbial and chemical processes, adding a layer of human-driven complexity with potential ecotoxicological effects on the ecosystem. we will relate our metabolomics findings with ecotoxicological data to explore this potential.",
