      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Ben Darby",
          "search_text": "benjamin darby who knew? viruses are good for coral reef little-known viruses, bacteriophages or phages, kill bacteria, which is good for coral reefs. this simple biological truth, known as viralization, is the conceptual anchor of my artistic practice. viralization describes the dynamics when viruses restrain runaway bacterial growth, allowing reef ecosystems to recover from microbial stress. \r\n\r\nfor over a decade, my work has unfolded through ongoing collaboration with the rohwer lab, where i explore how artistic methods can illuminate and generate new scientific research lines. i first created dozens of bacteriophage illustrations for life in our phage world. those images emerged through long, recursive conversation; looking closely so art and science became inseparable. what began as representation became speculation and a shared attempt to understand how viruses shape the future of coral reefs.\r\n\r\nthis inquiry expanded into the development of coral reef arks, midwater reef-building structures designed to study and restore coral ecosystems. sculpture became a research instrument. i helped design, engineer, and fabricate structures, including the dnark, a limestone and travertine underwater sculpture modeled on the icosahedral geometry of a bacteriophage capsid entwined with a dna helix. its form served as a test of how internal geometries influence water flow, organism settlement, and cryptic biodiversity.\r\n\r\nthrough these works, i approach art not as an illustrative tool but as a mode of ecological thinking. drawing and sculpting rehearse scientific hypotheses by creating physical analogs for conceptual frameworks like viralization. the process generates forms that scientists can adapt and test: art catalyzing new questions in coral reef biogeochemistry. by embedding scientific ideas into structures that live underwater and accumulate life, i aim to expand the vocabulary we use to talk about coral reef conservation and restoration. my works invite people to recognize ecological change hinges on microscopic actors and like a phage, art can enter a system quietly and transform it.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Who Knew? Viruses Are Good for Coral Reef",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "596efd44-b6ef-4282-bfdb-812ed21042cd"
            },
            {
              "title": "Demonstration of Coral Reef Arks in Vieques, Puerto Rico: a modular, whole-ecosystem restoration innovation.",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "056614f1-f290-408a-9816-e88e6498b5ae"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00416",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Catherine Lawrence",
          "search_text": "catherine lawrence evaluating the virus-to-microbe ratio (vmr) as an indicator of coral reef health coral reef health is increasingly understood to transition from viralized (i.e., pristine, coral-dominated) to microbialized (i.e., degraded, algal-dominated) states. the viralized state is characterized by high viral predation that constrains microbial abundances, whereas the microbialized state has elevated pathogenic microbial abundances and lower oxygen concentrations. the virus-to-microbe ratio (vmr), defined as the virus-like particles abundance (vlps ml⁻¹) divided by the microbial cell abundance (cells ml⁻¹), tracks the viralized-to-microbial transition, and we hypothesize that it is the best indicator of relative reef health identified so far. here we use vmr to access the potential for guiding coral reef restoration efforts. our study sites include an established microbialized benthic reef system serving as a control, three deployed midwater reef mesocosms (‘coral reef arks’) spanning a gradient of reef health, and 15 deployed autonomous reef monitoring systems (arms) distributed across the arks and control site. from each site we sampled vmr, microbial biomass (independent of cell counts), dissolved oxygen, total nitrogen, and dissolved organic carbon (doc). by randomly subsampling the benthic control site, we will estimate the mean vmr and its natural range of variation to create a site-specific null distribution. we will then calculate the kullback–leibler (kl) divergence to quantify how strongly each experimental ark departs from this baseline. values outside of the null distribution range indicate significant divergence in reef state, supporting vmr as an indicator of reef health. to interpret the direction of change, we will examine the components of vmr by plotting cells ml⁻¹ versus vlps ml⁻¹ to determine whether systems are shifting toward viralized or microbialized conditions. these results, paired with principal component analysis (pca) and random forest models across the whole dataset will identify the strongest predictors of reef health and evaluate the utility of vmr for guiding reef restoration.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Evaluating the Virus-to-Microbe Ratio (VMR) as an Indicator of Coral Reef Health",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "593a4bd2-8a1b-4da2-ac4b-818b11afb8ed"
            },
            {
              "title": "Demonstration of Coral Reef Arks in Vieques, Puerto Rico: a modular, whole-ecosystem restoration innovation.",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "056614f1-f290-408a-9816-e88e6498b5ae"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00979",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 2,
