          "name": "Marisa Agarwal",
          "search_text": "marisa agarwal assessing biodiversity-biomass relationships in pacific coral reef fish assemblages understanding patterns in biodiversity is a fundamental component of ecosystem function assessments across large spatial scales. biogeography is an important driver of marine biodiversity, with gradients in latitude predicting higher species richness at towards the equator and gradients in longitude predicting increasing richness from east to west towards the indo-pacific coral triangle. further, increases in biodiversity are hypothesized to affect ecosystem function, with areas of higher biodiversity related to higher biomass. here, we examine reef fish assemblages spanning longitudinal and latitudinal gradients to evaluate relationships between biodiversity patterns, biogeography, and biomass. estimated fish biomass and relative, abundance-based biodiversity metrics were quantified from 50 islands in the western, central, and southern pacific, spanning 47 degrees of latitude and 33 degrees of longitude. we found that while reef fish diversity metrics did not follow the predicted latitudinal diversity gradient, they did match longitudinal expectations with higher richness and evenness values closer to the coral triangle. fish biomass was highly variable across the islands surveyed (ranging from 17.7 g m² to 469.2 g m²) and was often strongly influenced by the prevalence of top consumer guilds within the assemblage (e.g., top-predators and sharks), likely linked to the intensity or history of fishing pressure. correlations between relative diversity metrics and biomass estimates were identified, but the strength of these correlations varied based upon the definition of diversity and the trophic group considered. biomass was negatively correlated with components of beta diversity, indicating that islands with more compositionally homogeneous fish assemblages supported greater, and less variable, biomass. by characterizing reef fish biodiversity–biomass relationships across an expansive latitudinal and longitudinal gradient, we demonstrate the variability in associations between biodiversity, biogeography, and biomass for pacific coral reef fish assemblages.",
