          "name": "Heather Page",
          "search_text": "heather page monitoring caribbean coral reefs through undergraduate study abroad programs at sea education association a combination of local, regional, and global stressors have resulted in decreased coral cover on tropical reef ecosystems worldwide including the caribbean. additionally, caribbean coral reefs have low resilience to environmental stressors. protecting these reefs requires multiple approaches across local to global scales including monitoring and research to understand how environmental changes are impacting coral reefs. in sea’s coral reef conservation: caribbean program, undergraduate students monitor the condition of regional oceanography and nearshore coral reefs during annual six-week research cruises. while underway, we continuously measure physical and chemical properties of the surface ocean. discrete measurements of physical and chemical properties through the water column are taken once a day by deploying a carousel equipped with niskin bottles and scientific sensors. additionally, we tow plankton nets twice a day to examine abundance, geographic distribution, and diversity of these important primary and secondary producers. during the cruise, we anchor at caribbean islands to conduct environmental and ecological surveys of nearshore reefs (n = 2 per island) using snorkel-based techniques. we deploy scientific sensors for 24-72 hours to capture high-frequency environmental data, allowing us to investigate short-term variability in water quality. acoustic recorders are deployed alongside these sensors to capture reef soundscapes. these data can inform potential climate change refugia for reefs and provide recordings for attracting coral larvae to reefs to promote recruitment. while sensors and recorders are deployed on the reef, students conduct surveys along 3-4 twenty meter transects. benthic community composition, including coral and macroalgae cover and coral health, is measured using standard photoquadrat sampling methodologies. multiple survey methods are used to assess the abundance and diversity of reef invertebrates and fishes, too. thus, we are building a comprehensive dataset that allows us to investigate how caribbean coral reefs are changing alongside environmental changes at local and regional scales.",
