      "search_text": "senckenberg research institute alexander knorrn alexander knorrn deep-water ecosystems of fiordland: novel observations from the 2025 coralnewz rov surveys during the latest scientific collaboration between new zealand and germany, marine researchers joined the coralnewz expedition aboard the german research vessel sonne between january and february 2025. a central aim of this joint mission was the detailed documentation of cold-water corals within the fiordland region. parasound mapping and rov surveys were carried out across the thompson, milford, and dusky sound systems, as well as along the acheron passage. the resulting high-resolution bathymetry and video imagery provide a new perspective on the distribution, structure, and ecological significance of fiordland’s deep-water coral communities, revealing several previously unrecorded occurrences and associations.\r\nacross four rov dives, coral habitats and associated fauna were systematically documented and sampled whenever feasible. specimens were photodocumented on board, with tissue material preserved for subsequent dna barcoding. among the most notable observations were extensive frameworks of the zig-zag coral (madrepora oculata), alongside diverse assemblages of stylasterids, octocorals, and solitary scleractinians. newly recorded coral–fish associations were observed between m. oculata and multiple perch species, including the wavyline perch (lepidoperca tasmanica) and the orange perch (l. aurantia), underscoring the role of corals as key habitat formers in fiordland’s steep-walled fjords.\r\na further remarkable discovery was the presence of the deep-sea oyster pulvinites exempla between 315 and 226 m depth, an extant representative of a lineage otherwise known almost exclusively from fossil deposits, indicating overlooked hard-substrate refugia within the region.\r\nthe collected material highlights the scientific importance of these underexplored fjord systems and advances our understanding of the structure, biodiversity, and ecological complexity of fiordland’s deep-water coral habitats.",
