      "search_text": "shahid beheshti university of tehran sara asadi gharabaghi sara asadi gharabaghi microbial plasticity of coral holobionts across depth and season under extreme environmental conditions in the persian gulf coral holobionts rely on microbial partners to withstand environmental stress, yet the structure and drivers of these communities in marginal, extreme reefs remain insufficiently resolved. we profiled bacterial and archaeal assemblages associated with five scleractinian corals–porites harrisoni, platygyra daedalea, pavona decussata, acropora downingi, and a. microphthalma–at northern (kish, iran) and southern (sheraoh, qatar) gulf reefs across seasons (winter vs. summer) and depths (shallow vs. deep). using full-length 16s rrna sequencing with reference-based classification (emu, silva v138.1), we captured community structure spanning both dominant and rare lineages.\r\nacross hosts and locations, microbial richness was consistently higher at deep sites than at shallow sites, with seasonal declines evident at both locations. bacterial communities were typically dominated by pseudomonadota, while archaea formed a substantial proportion of the holobiont microbiome, including members of halobacteriota and thermoproteota. assemblages were host-specific and strongly structured by depth and season. notably, a. microphthalma remained archaea-skewed year-round, suggesting functional stability under extreme conditions, whereas a. downingi exhibited a pronounced summer shift toward pseudomonadota, indicative of community restructuring under heat stress. for p. harrisoni and p. daedalea, summer was associated with reduced pseudomonadota and increased archaeal contributions. multivariate analyses (permanova/manova) confirmed significant effects of location and season for most hosts, while deep p. decussata remained comparatively stable.\r\ncollectively, these results highlight (i) depth-mediated buffering of microbial richness; (ii) host-specific seasonal plasticity; and (iii) elevated archaeal roles in select taxa–pointing to alternative metabolic pathways that may underpin resilience in hypersaline, thermally variable, and hydrocarbon-influenced reefs. we propose archaeal lineages and hydrocarbon-degrading bacteria as candidate indicators for monitoring in marginal seas and identify deep-reef porites and acropora as promising targets for conservation and assisted-resilience strategies.",
