      "search_text": "shenzhen msu-bit university viacheslav ivanenko viacheslav ivanenko macrofauna vs meiofauna in pocilloporid corals: current database status, diversity patterns, and indo-pacific bias knowledge gaps and biogeographic patterns in reef-building coral-associated copepod crustaceans abstract\r\npocilloporid corals host diverse animal associates, but knowledge is uneven across size classes and regions. here we present the current status of a developing literature-based database that is being continuously expanded, with a particular focus on animals living in association with the living tissue of coral colonies. we synthesize available occurrence records to compare macrofaunal assemblages with meiofaunal symbioses (mostly copepods). macrofauna dominates the evidence base: 2,167 occurrences from 83 sources document 946 species (291 families, 47 orders, 10 phyla) associated with 7 of 60 known pocilloporidae species. notably, 537 associations were reported only once. most records come from living colonies, while 71 occurrences (67 species) were reported from dead coral substrates. localities span 7/12 ocean ecoregions and are strongly concentrated in the indo-pacific, whereas the atlantic remains poorly documented. decapoda account for 49% of occurrences, and pocillopora hosts the most diverse assemblage. in contrast, meiofaunal copepods are far less documented: 29 papers report 273 occurrences of copepods (cyclopoida, harpacticoida, siphonostomatoida) on pocilloporidae. records are geographically biased, indicating substantial hidden diversity. overall, the compiled evidence still covers only a limited fraction of potential pocilloporid host diversity, highlighting priorities for standardized reporting, broader geographic sampling, and integrative taxonomy for tissue-associated symbionts on living corals. this presentation synthesizes current knowledge of coral-associated copepod diversity based on 160 published taxonomic studies covering all copepods associated with scleractinian corals, together with molecular datasets from samples collected across multiple regions of the indo-pacific and the caribbean. special emphasis is placed on the coral families pocilloporidae and euphylliidae (including galaxea) and on the highly diverse copepod family anchimolgidae.  \r\nthe integrated taxonomic and molecular evidence reveals hidden diversity, previously unrecognized host associations, and a remarkable zoogeographic fragmentation among copepod assemblages distributed across distinct coral reef ecoregions, a unique pattern not previously documented for other coral-associated invertebrate symbionts or for coral algal symbionts. these findings indicate strong regional structuring and a wide spectrum of host specificity across taxa.\r\nthe presentation identifies major knowledge gaps and emerging biogeographic and ecological patterns essential for understanding coral–copepod symbioses across tropical reef systems.",
