      "search_text": "smithsonian institution noam altman-kurosaki valerie paul noam altman-kurosaki biodiversity mitigates human impacts on fish biomass–but not climate vulnerability–across a degraded reef tract coral reef fishes are both culturally and economically valuable. however, they are vulnerable to a multitude of stressors including ocean warming, overfishing, and habitat degradation. the florida reef tract (frt) in particular represents a region that 1) is rapidly warming,  2) experiences substantial pressure from both recreational and commercial fisheries, 3) is subject to high amounts of terrigenous inputs, and 4) has suffered numerous disturbance events, including hurricanes, bleaching events, and disease outbreaks. the florida fish and wildlife research institute (fwri) has been monitoring reefs throughout the frt since 1996 and has documented precipitous declines in living coral cover. however, the effects of these declines in reef quality on fish communities remains unexamined. we surveyed fishes following the reef life survey protocol at 108 sites spanning the frt to assess environmental and anthropogenic drivers of fish biomass and community thermal index (cti), a metric of thermal affinity. we hypothesized that fishing pressure, degraded benthic communities, nutrient pollution, and increased temperatures would be associated with reduced species richness and biomass, favoring communities with lower cti and thus increasing the vulnerability of fish communities to warming. however, we found that species richness – not fishing pressure, habitat degradation, nutrient pollution, or temperature – was the strongest predictor of fish biomass. in contrast, species richness had little effect on cti, whereas fishing pressure and temperature variability had direct negative effects. further, the majority of reef fish communities we surveyed had a cti lower than the mean annual temperature at the sites in which they were surveyed, suggesting elevated vulnerability to warming. our results suggest that biodiversity may benefit reef fishes by enhancing biomass in the face of intensifying anthropogenic stressors, but it may not buffer these communities against the broader effects of ocean warming and climate change. valerie paul chemical ecology of benthic marine cyanobacteria on coral reefs changing coral disease dynamics with the emergence of stony coral tissue loss disease on reefs at carrie bow cay, belize benthic marine cyanobacteria are becoming increasingly abundant in tropical and subtropical habitats worldwide, and many species produce diverse, sometimes toxic, natural products. blooms of cyanobacteria occur regularly throughout florida and on coral reefs worldwide. over the past 20 years, we have made progress in characterizing the species composition and natural products chemistry of benthic cyanobacteria on coral reefs. crude extracts and pure compounds from some cyanobacteria deter feeding by generalist herbivores such as fishes and sea urchins. cyanobacterial metabolites may serve multiple ecological functions, including feeding deterrence, inhibition of microorganisms and competition for space. the role of allelopathy (chemical inhibition) in mediating interactions between cyanobacteria and different life history stages of corals has been tested. extracts and isolated compounds from multiple cyanobacteria negatively influence the settlement of coral larvae. on reefs experiencing increased abundance of chemically rich benthic cyanobacteria, the restocking of adult coral populations may be slowed due to recruitment inhibition caused by cyanobacterial metabolites. one species of interest in coral disease ecology is roseofilum reptotaenium, a filamentous marine cyanobacterium that is a major component of black band disease (bbd), a lethal disease that occurs on many species of corals worldwide. bbd is abundant on florida’s coral reef and throughout the caribbean, especially in summer months. to decipher chemical signaling among microorganisms in bbd, we investigated the natural products chemistry of bbd and nonaxenic cultures of r. reptotaenium and identified lyngbic acid, which inhibits quorum sensing (qs) in vibrio harveyi qs reporters, and macrocyclic depsipeptides, which we named looekeyolides. lookeyolides are not toxic, but may protect the cyanobacteria from reactive oxygen species, which are coral’s frontline defense against pathogens. stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) is the most devasting coral disease ever recorded. sctld has now spread from florida, where it emerged in 2014, to most of the caribbean. in 2019, anticipating expansion of sctld onto the belize barrier reef (bbr), we conducted baseline disease belt surveys at nine permanent sites near the smithsonian’s carrie bow cay (cbc) field station. belt surveys were repeated in 2022, following the report of sctld within the region, and again in 2024. additional one-hour roving diver surveys along the bbr were conducted in 2022 and 2023. in 2019, we documented 18 diseases in eight coral species but no signs of sctld. average disease prevalence was 0.5%. in 2022, average disease prevalence was 6.2%, sctld was found at all nine sites, and roving diver surveys found sctld broadly distributed on nearby reefs. a new chronic tissue loss disease (ctld) with discoloration was found affecting siderastrea siderea and accounted for 57.4% of the disease cases. histology on the new s. siderea lesion was not consistent with sctld. 2brad genotyping showed evidence for two distinct s. siderea lineages on the reefs. analyses are ongoing to determine if lineages differ in disease susceptibility. in 2023, roving diver surveys found sctld was widespread along the bbr and at glover’s reef. repeat belt surveys on cbc reefs in 2024 found that average disease prevalence had dropped to 4.2%, sctld was still in the coral populations but at a lower level, and 90% of the disease cases were the new disease (ctld) affecting s. siderea. within cbc sites, benthic cover also changed through time with a 24.2% reduction in coral cover, 72% reduction in colony densities and 33.3% loss of coral species within transects. benthic changes are attributed to mortality from disease and a bleaching event in 2023.",
