          "name": "NIZAM IBRAHIM",
          "search_text": "nizam ibrahim low-cost, community-led larval-based reef restoration in huvadhu, maldives: insights from multi-night spawning observations and early larval culture trials widespread coral decline in the maldives has reduced natural larval supply and compromised the recovery potential of degraded reefs across the country. larval-based restoration offers a scalable and cost-effective alternative to conventional fragmentation-based methods. here, we present the results of the first coordinated, low-cost, community-implemented larval-restoration effort in huvadhu atoll, supported by a cordap-funded project. in november 2025, eight consecutive nights of spawning surveys were conducted across southern huvadhu reefs, following ongoing monitoring of the reproductive condition of tagged coral colonies during previous months. three nights of multispecies acropora spawning were recorded, including extensive releases from acropora hyacinthus table colonies. field assessments showed heterogeneous reproductive maturity among tagged colonies of acropora c.f. gemmifera, with some colonies spawning over two nights, and other colonies retaining pigmented eggs after the spawning period. longer-term monitoring of gravid a. tenuis colonies showed maturing eggs prior to the november spawning period recorded for other corals, and absence of eggs and sperm after this period, indicating that these colonies also spawned during this period. spawned gametes were collected and transferred into an inflatable larval culture pool moored on sand near the reef, and larvae were reared until competent to settle. dead coral pieces were collected from nearby reef areas and deployed into the larval pool as settlement substrates. after larvae had settled they were transferred to reef locations for ongoing monitoring of survival and growth of recruits. the successful trial of reef-based larval restoration in huvadhu forms the foundation for expanded larval culture and settlement trials during upcoming extended coral spawning periods that characterize reef-coral reproductive patterns in the maldives. this work also demonstrates the feasibility of integrating local divers, community partners, and council teams into an effective larval-restoration team on huvadhu atoll, which will support future larval restoration expansion in the southern maldives.",
