      "search_text": "southern cross university hugo denis hugo denis genetic basis of coral heat adaptation across populations and heat stress exposures regional-scale genetic diversity and connectivity of coral holobionts across the western pacific persistence of reef-building coral populations hinges on adaptation to rising sea temperatures through natural selection acting on heritable variation in heat tolerance. yet, the discovery of genetic variants associated with heat tolerance and genomic predictions of adaptive potential remain in early stages, partly due to limited large-scale genotypic and phenotypic datasets. their application to conservation and restoration is also limited by uncertainties regarding the effect of candidate genetic variants on coral fitness in the wild, the consistency of heat tolerance genomic architecture under varying duration and intensities of thermal stress, and the degree to which heat adaptation is repeatable across populations and species. to address these gaps, we used a comprehensive whole genome sequencing dataset from ~1,000 acropora spathulata colonies across 29 western pacific reefs to conduct genome-wide association analyses of heat tolerance phenotypes and thermal environments. genetic variants associated with long-term climatic conditions indicate largely non-parallel polygenic adaptation across distant populations, but overlapping signals point to key known and novel genes under repeated selection by temperature. several of these variants had measurable effects on coral bleaching and mortality in the wild, and collectively improved predictions of these outcomes, suggesting that genome-environment associations successfully detected adaptive variants. association studies revealed that tolerance to acute heat stress involves distinct genetic variants and genes across populations, but similar molecular pathways. importantly, we found little overlap between genomic regions underlying experimental acute heat tolerance, and in-situ tolerance to marine heatwaves, indicating that the genetic architecture of heat tolerance may vary across thermal exposures. overall, our results emphasize the complex interplay between genetic architectures and types of heat stress in shaping selection under climate warming across populations, and highlight the value of integrating multiple approaches and taxa to identify genomic markers with broad relevance for coral management under climate warming. the genetic diversity, size and connectivity of reef-building coral populations play central roles in their demographic resilience and adaptation to ocean warming. for broadcast spawner species with high dispersal and environmentally acquired microalgal symbionts, genetic diversity can be shaped by the interaction of local adaptation and gene flow across wide geographic ranges. yet, limited datasets exist to jointly examine the spatial genetic variation of coral symbiotic partners across local and broad regional scales. here, we present a comprehensive investigation of both coral hosts and symbiodiniaceae genetic diversity in acropora spathulata , using whole-genome sequencing data from 1,088 colonies across the western pacific. we found considerable dispersal of coral larvae in this taxon, around ~100 km per generation, that results in weak genetic structure across vast spatial scales and large breeding populations. due to geographic isolation, populations separated by thousands of kilometers show stronger genetic differentiation with onset of divergence estimated ~0.27–0.65 million years ago. however, we also provide evidence that coral populations from new caledonia and the great barrier reef have maintained gene flow throughout their evolutionary history due to occasional long-distance dispersal. by contrast, the associated cladocopium symbionts exhibit finer-scale genetic structure driven by environmental gradients, and independent of coral host population boundaries or symbiont phylogeny. our results demonstrate how hologenome data can reveal the interplay between evolutionary processes and environmental factors shaping coral holobiont genetic diversity, as well as aiding in the delineation of conservation units and informing their adaptive potential.",
