          "name": "Kira Hughes",
          "search_text": "kira hughes designing a watershed and water quality program to reveal connectivity between land-based impacts and aquatic resource health healthy watersheds and water quality are critical to aquatic ecosystem wellbeing in hawaiʻi. however, the compartmentalized nature of state agencies in hawaiʻi has made it challenging to address water quality issues that span entire ahupuaʻa (traditional ridge to reef land divisions in hawaiʻi, often compared with watersheds). to remedy this gap in understanding and lack of interagency coordination to improve watershed health and water quality, the division of aquatic resources has developed a watershed program and he wai e ola, a water quality strategy. through partnering with the department of health, the commission on water resource management, the division of forestry and wildlife, and other nongovernmental and community organizations around the state, dar has committed to these interagency efforts to incorporate watersheds and water quality into our management of aquatic resources. to gather feedback and multiple perspectives to guide these programs, dar convened (1) a panel discussion featuring both managers and scientists at the 2024 u.s. coral reef task force meeting in saipan to discuss the merits of co-locating water quality data with marine monitoring data, (2) a workshop gathering dar staff focused on streams, muliwai, and anchialine pools to discuss current watershed-related efforts and future program interests in fall 2025, and (3) a workshop gathering managers, community representatives, and academic scientists from around hawaiʻi to discuss what statewide collaboration surrounding water quality should look like. this talk will focus on the process taken to arrive at these collaborations, share results from these discussions, and present paths forward to continue implementing these important collaborative efforts. through these programs, dar will be able to more directly adjust our management of aquatic resources to incorporate the water and watershed ecosystems that feed into them, improving outcomes for the unique fishes, limu, coral, and invertebrates that call hawaiian watersheds home.",
