          "name": "Genevieve Dallmeyer-Drennen",
          "search_text": "genevieve dallmeyer-drennen how does competition perturb cell crosstalk among coral holobiont associated bacteria? coral holobionts are hotspots of biodiversity encompassing a diverse community of symbiotic dinoflagellates (symbiodiniaceae), bacteria, fungi, archaea, and viruses. bacterial symbionts contribute to holobiont nutrient acquisition, disease resistance, and secondary metabolite production. secondary metabolites, including bacterial volatile compounds (bvcs), are important in mediating holobiont community dynamics. despite the broad benefits conferred by microbes to the holobiont, our understanding of the specific functions, mechanisms, and ecological roles of individual bacterial taxa remains limited. this study has two focal genera: marinobacter, a common symbiont of dinoflagellates across systems, and alteromonas, a dominant coral-associated genus implicated in holobiont disease resistance. pairwise comparisons of bacterial strains isolated from symbiodiniaceae cultures were conducted to quantify colony morphology, pigmentation, and growth over lag, log, and stationary growth phases were made using image and pigment analyses. pairwise proximity assays captured morphological changes associated with exposure to diffusible and bvcs, direct challenge assays demonstrate antimicrobial activity via zone of inhibition measurement, and agar diffusion and headspace analyses assessed antimicrobial activity of diffusible and volatile compounds, respectively. comparisons across normal and partitioned agar plates, which isolate bvc-mediated interactions, demonstrate the bvcs inhibit colony growth and motility. emergent pink coloration observed in specific pairings will be extracted and characterized as lycopene or prodigiosin, compounds with known antimicrobial activity. integrating phenotypic screening with genomic mining will enable links between competition-induced phenotypes and the genetic pathways that underpin microbial survival strategies. understanding microbial functions that confer beneficial advantages to the holobiont, such as disease resistance or other stress resilient phenotypes, is critical for informing microbial driven conservation interventions of coral ecosystems. increasing fundamental knowledge of coral holobiont associated bacteria, linking their genes and competitive phenotypes, will enhance our ability to develop applied interventions for conservation of iconic ecosystems.",
