          "name": "Eleanor TenBrink",
          "search_text": "eleanor tenbrink mitigation to management: evaluating ocean alkalinity enhancement as a tool for coral reef restoration coral reefs are diverse and valuable ecosystems made up of complex calcium carbonate structures created by corals and calcifying algae. unfortunately, the persistence of coral reefs is threatened by elevated atmospheric co₂ levels that alter seawater chemistry. as such, intervention strategies to reduce co₂ are needed to address this threat to coral reefs and conserve them for future generations. ocean alkalinity enhancement (oae) is an emerging strategy that could simultaneously mitigate seawater acidification and enhance long-term oceanic carbon storage. by adding alkaline substances to seawater, oae increases the buffering capacity and availability of carbonate ions, which may promote favorable conditions for coral growth and reef accretion. however, the ecological implications of oae remain unknown, particularly regarding how different alkaline sources and concentrations interact to affect the growth of corals and calcifying algae. therefore, this project evaluates acute response to oae by measuring the impact of different alkaline agents (olivine, calcium oxide, and sodium hydroxide) at varying concentrations (+1000–3000 µmol kg⁻¹) on reef-builder calcification rates. specifically, we aim to identify the optimal level of enhancement of different alkaline agents on three representative reef-building species in hawaiʻi (coralline spp., montipora capitata, and pocillopora acuta). initial findings indicate that elevated alkalinity generally enhances calcification, but with species- and agent- specific responses. m. capitata exhibited maximal calcification rates at +1000 µmol kg⁻¹ across all three agents, whereas p. acuta displayed a lesser, more variable calcification response with maximal rates at +2000 µmol kg⁻¹, particularly under olivine enhancement. these results suggest that oae may be a viable carbon mitigation option for coral reef conservation based on coral physiological response.",
