      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Makeda Mills",
          "search_text": "makeda mills the microbial diversity of deep-sea precious corals on an unexplored seamount octocorals in the genus of corallium are commonly known as precious corals due to their striking red-pink hues and elaborate branching structures. for millennia, these corals have held economic significance across the globe, serving as jewelry and ornaments. while traditionally harvested using coral nets through the 1800’s, the shift towards trawling methods has led to a decline in their mesophotic populations particularly evident in once-thriving habitats in the mediterranean and the seamounts near japan. yet, the increase in trawling efforts is linked to the growing demand for precious coral, primarily for the high-end jewelry industry. given the increasing pressures for precious corals, it is likely that precious coral populations will continue to decline. \r\nthe o’brian seamount, located in the northwest pacific ocean, is an unstudied seamount that shows no visual signs of trawling disruption. it is home to corallium japonicum, a precious coral species that boasts hues of deep and dark reds. this species is commercially harvested from depths of 80 to 300 meters off the coast of japan. although, the species remains in high demand, studies on the health of the coral holobiont are limited, especially for those at greater depths. \r\nby characterizing an apparently unimpacted population, a baseline understanding of associated microbiota could serve as a critical reference point for future comparisons with harvested or trawled seamounts. thus, the goal of this study is to investigate the microbial ecology of c. japonicum inhabiting the undisturbed o’brian seamount at depths ranging from 1398 to 1995 m. fragments of c. japonicum (n=17) were collected in the fall of 2023. ongoing metagenomic analyses will characterize the taxonomy, community structure, and functional composition of associated microbiota. \r\nestablishing a baseline understanding of c. japonicum-associated microbiota is imperative, as it unveils the functional dynamics and ecological structure within these understudied yet overexploited coral ecosystems.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "The Microbial Diversity of Deep-Sea Precious Corals on an Unexplored Seamount",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "57536682-39b6-41f0-a873-4f201200862d"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01184",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Michael Hood",
          "search_text": "michael hood water quality and ecological rebound: a transdisciplinary assessment of coral reef health at lizard island, australia coral reefs worldwide face critical challenges from acute stressors (mass bleaching, cots outbreaks, cyclones). the frequency and intensity of these acute disturbances have led to substantial coral loss, demanding prompt assessment of ecosystem viability. the national science foundation (nsf) international research experiences for students (ires) advanced studies institute in australia provides graduate students with crucial, immersive, and transdisciplinary field experience, focusing on solutions to environmental challenges. this project stems directly from the course's mandate to apply an integrated social-ecological understanding to assess the current status and resilience of the reefs surrounding lizard island following significant decline.\r\n\r\nthis research conducted a comprehensive transdisciplinary assessment of water quality parameters and coral cover. underwater autosamplers were strategically deployed across diverse reef-associated habitats (coral reef, mangrove, rubble shore) to analyze the critical diurnal and weekly shifts in key water chemistry parameters, including alkalinity, dissolved oxygen, and salinity. this continuous temporal sampling strategy aimed to precisely capture natural baseline variability and potential metabolic signals, providing a fine-scale understanding of the environment. these chemical assessments were rigorously coupled with in situ ecological surveys, utilizing the transect line method to assess current coral cover against historical data.\r\n\r\nanalysis of water samples determined that all measured parameters were within optimal ranges conducive to the growth and health of reef-building corals. furthermore, ecological assessments demonstrated a measurable and encouraging increase in coral cover compared to previous post-disturbance assessments.\r\n\r\nthe convergence of chemical evidence (optimal water quality) and visual evidence (increasing coral cover) strongly indicates that the lizard island coral reefs are actively rebounding from previous declines. this finding highlights the potential for high natural resilience within these northern great barrier reef ecosystems, suggesting effective local management and successful ecological processes, and exemplifies the value of ires transdisciplinary training in developing holistic, effective strategies for long-term ecosystem recovery.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Water Quality and Ecological Rebound: A Transdisciplinary Assessment of Coral Reef Health at Lizard Island, Australia",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "d0841fde-80e7-4f61-9756-3d5b436c5e87"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00778",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Sam McClain",
          "search_text": "samantha mcclain short-term improvements in coastal acidification and eutrophication along a maui coral reef during the covid-19 pandemic as global ocean acidification and warming intensify and threaten corals, mitigating local stressors, such as land-based pollution, becomes increasingly important to the future survival of reefs. an example of land-based pollution occurs on the west coast of maui, hi, where the lahaina wastewater reclamation facility (lwrf) injects treated wastewater into the groundwater aquifer. injected wastewater travels to the coast and emerges in acidified, eutrophic submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) onto a fringing reef within the kahekili herbivorous fish management area. here, sgd has been linked to decreased live coral and increased algal coverage. during the covid-19 pandemic, visitation to maui drastically decreased (98%) when hawaiʻi's governor enacted travel restrictions. as a result, significantly less (>50%) wastewater was injected by the lwrf. during this time, we observed reduced acidification and eutrophication of sgd (ph: +0.32; [no₃-]: -104.12 μmol l-1) and reef water (ph:+0.12; [no₃-]: -0.33 μmol l-1), as well as increased reef daytime net ecosystem production. trends reversed after travel restrictions were removed. additionally, daytime net ecosystem calcification did not seem to respond to changes in sgd chemistry, potentially due to the effects of additional stressors (e.g., bleaching). we will discuss how short-term reductions in acidification and eutrophication led to generally improved reef health. this work is especially important and timely considering that the lwrf is redefining injection protocols, including injected wastewater quality targets, following the 2020 supreme court case in which the county of maui was found in violation of the clean water act. therefore, we will provide recommendations for wastewater management and injected water quality targets (e.g., limits to injected total nitrogen), and compare targets to those recently proposed by the hawaiʻi department of health.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Short-term Improvements in Coastal Acidification and Eutrophication along a Maui Coral Reef during the COVID-19 Pandemic",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "06e748a5-6df7-48d6-b956-542bd5cccb16"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01132",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
