      "search_text": "texas state university erin shilling lauren fuess raúl gonzález-pech erin shilling gene expression of corals throughout a stony coral tissue loss disease outbreak indicates varying mechanisms of resistance and immune response the relatively novel stony coral tissue loss disease (sctld) has caused severe declines in scleractinian coral cover in the caribbean since it was first observed in florida in 2014. coral disease ecology has been heavily studied in many aspects, but many questions remain as to how and why some coral individuals and species develop disease while others do not. for this study, we leveraged the outbreak of sctld in carrie bow cay, belize to assess these inter- and intra-specific differences in coral physiology. individual corals were tagged, tracked, and sampled in june 2019, before sctld was present, and again in may 2022, two months after sctld was confirmed in the area. we analyzed gene expression from ten individuals each of the species siderastrea siderea, montastraea cavernosa, and porites astreoides across both time points. these species represent a range of sctld susceptibility- high/intermediate, intermediate, and low, respectively. some individuals in our sample set developed sctld in may 2022 and others, including all p. astreoides colonies, remained apparently healthy. we leveraged these differences to look for pathways or responses indicative of susceptibility and/or resistance to sctld. analyses reveal that species exhibited unique shifts in immune pathways as sctld emerged. however, once diseased, individuals’ overall gene expression appears more similar. additionally, there is evidence of the involvement of a potential viral agent. understanding the mechanisms through which disease affects not only corals that show phenotypic signs, but also those which remain apparently healthy, is crucial to predicting how reefs will fare long term and in the face of subsequent stressors. lauren fuess longitudinal multi-‘omics approaches reveal holobiont interactions indicative of resistance and response to stony coral tissue loss disease scleractinian corals are well known to be intimately dependent on numerous microbial partners, with microbes contributing significantly to diverse physiological processes such as nutrient exchange and cycling. increasing evidence suggests that these microbial partners are also essential contributors to the coral response to both stress and disease. yet how microbes enhance or weaken coral resistance to stressors, and the degree of specificity of beneficial host-microbe associations across species, remains unknown. the recent outbreak of a devastating coral disease (sctld) in the caribbean, which has affected nearly 20 species of corals, has provided unprecedented opportunities to investigate the contributions of holobiont interactions to coral disease dynamics. here we detail a long-term sampling approach, combining ecological monitoring of individual coral colonies with repeated mulit-‘omic sampling through disease phases to identify signatures of both resistance and general host response to disease. a multi-species sampling approach allowed for the investigation of species-specific and species-independent markers of disease resistance and response. integrating transcriptomic data from hosts and photosymbionts with microbial and viral community composition we identify key holobiont interactions contributing to disease resistance. furthermore, we highlight species-specific and species-independent shifts in these associations across disease progression. generally, our results highlight intimate associations between host immune gene expression and microbial community members which are likely essential to defense response. notably, while some of these associations are species-independent, we observe a high degree of species specificity to these patterns, highlighting a need for species-specific management approaches. combined, this powerful dataset significantly advances our understanding coral disease in a holobiont framework. raúl gonzález-pech a paleogenomic approach to reconstruct historical responses of coral holobionts to anthropogenic change coral reefs are declining worldwide due to anthropogenic-driven environmental change. the foundation and health of these ecosystems rely on the harmonious functioning of all members of coral holobionts, i.e., cnidarian host, symbiotic microalgae, and associated microbiome. coral stress responses often involve shifts in the taxonomic makeup of their symbionts and microbiomes. tracing back changes in coral holobiont composition over prolonged time periods can help us reconstruct health history of reefs and gain a better understanding of coral response to current stressors. here, we focused on a major caribbean reef-builder coral, orbicella faveolata, from varadero reef, colombia. this reef has undergone extensive freshwater sediment discharge for decades as result of urbanization and the centuries-long magdalena river watershed man-made modifications. we show, for the first time, that paleogenomic and paleoclimatic approaches can be combined to reconstruct historical coral holobiont dynamics–including associated algal symbionts, bacteria and viruses–potentially associated with anthropogenic disturbances.",
