      "search_text": "the international seakeepers society alessandra vallim alessandra vallim international junior seakeepers: a multinational hybrid educational program increasing environmental literacy in students aged 14-18. please review for a poster only. please consult simon davy on this project.\r\nthe international junior seakeepers program (ijsk) is an educational program designed to increase marine science literacy in high-school aged children. based on the strong foundations laid by the junior seakeepers program (jsk) based out of seakeepers headquarters, this novel international approach utilizes online tools, a shared curriculum, and hyper-localized instruction to run simultaneous, linked programming with individualized impact. in 2025, the first ijsk program was initiated in new zealand, united kingdom, and singapore. in aotearoa new zealand, the first cohort of 21 students embarked on an in-field hands-on learning opportunity, visited goat island marine reserve te hawerea-a-maki’s discovery center to conduct water quality analysis, and analyzed images from an international citizen science program, citizens of the reef. the second ijsk cohort in aotearoa new zealand consists of 25 students and will build on the previous cohort’s experiences by leading their own independent research project on a marine ecosystem of their choosing and creating a scientific poster to share in an annual seakeepers research summit. across all junior seakeepers programs (jsk + ijsk), more than 101 students were impacted in 2025, and an anticipated 99 students in 2026. the aotearoa new zealand cohorts made up 21% and 25% of these students respectively. thanks to the international nature of these combined programs, students in 2025 had access to expert presentations from 3 countries, with 9 presentations from seakeepers staff, and 15 presentations from external experts in marine science (6 of which were specifically offered to students from aotearoa new zealand). as a preparatory tool for students interested in marine science, this program equips the next generation of academics, reef managers, restoration practitioners, research scientists, and icrs presenters.",
