      "search_text": "the nature conservancy - venezuela aldo croquer aldo croquer critical decline of coral reefs in the dominican republic revealed by the national monitoring program monitorea the donor paradox viewed through the lens of coral restoration efforts coral reef monitoring programs guide coral restoration efforts in the dominican republic: a case study from southeast marine sanctuary a comprehensive review of the legal framework for coral reef protection and restoration in the dominican republic coral reefs have undergone rapid change during the anthropocene. declining trends have been documented across multiple bioregions, particularly in the caribbean and the dominican republic, where major reef-building species have been severely affected by coral diseases and bleaching. here, we present results from monitorea, the largest interinstitutional monitoring effort ever conducted in the dr. a total of 64 sites were surveyed nationwide following a spatially nested design that included four regions and 19 localities. we used a modified version of the global coral reef monitoring network (gcrmn) biophysical assessment method based on the analysis of 80–90 cm photo quadrats to determine benthic cover, and the agrra protocol to assess fish biomass. our results show that more than 70% of surveyed reefs exhibited live coral cover values below 5%, with benthic communities dominated by macroalgae. coral recruitment was four times lower (<5 recruits/m²) than the average reported for the dr between 2015 and 2022. current disease prevalence remains below 5% at the national level, consistent with an endemic or latent phase. biomass of commercial and herbivorous fishes was variable but generally below 500 g/100 m², with size-frequency distributions across all functional fish groups skewed toward small individuals (1–10 cm). invertebrate abundance was generally below 1–5 individuals/100 m². we conclude that coral reefs in the dr are in critical condition and require urgent attention. while the drivers of coral reef decline in the dr are complex, coral diseases (e.g., stony coral tissue loss disease and yellow band disease), the recent diadema antillarum die-off, and the 2023–2024 bleaching events have been major contributors to recent shifts. in the immediate future, the conservation status of coral reefs in the dr will depend on the adoption of a national strategy to preserve what remains and restore what has been lost. coastal systems are in decline, prompting repeated calls for their restoration to rebuild biodiversity and to ensure the continued provision of valuable services. many restoration projects initially require a source of individuals or propagules, generally from an intact donor system. however, very few efforts consider the impact that extraction has on the donor system in assessing overall restoration success. if the structure and function returned by the restoration does not replace or exceed that which was lost from the donor system, the act of restoration may lead to a net negative outcome–a phenomenon we refer to as the “donor paradox” of restorarion. here, we define the restoration donor paradox and consider the ecological and ecosystem consequences of extractive restoration. we then empirically evaluate the donor paradox using iki’s coral carib case study in the dominican republic, central caribbean. finally, we provide recommendations for practitioners to mitigate or reduce the impacts of the donor paradox when planning future restorations efforts. as coral reefs continue to undergo rapid change levels of uncertain about the future of these ecosystems increases. this scenario demands effective actions to preserve what is left and to restore what has been lost. in this context, coral reef monitoring programs can provide insights to advance assertive management actions and robust coral restoration strategies based on quantitative data. the southeast marine sanctuary (samar) is an mpa co-managed by the dominican ministry of environment, local and international ngos, academic institutions and the private sector. samar monitoring program (i.e., monitorea) incorporates different methodologies and protocols successfully implemented across the region to describe spatial and temporal trajectories of samar's coral reefs following a spatially nested design. the program gathers information on benthic cover (from benthic groups to coral species), abundance of recruits, coral disease prevalence, fish biomass and invertebrate abundance. from 2022 to 2024 reefs at samar were severely impacted by sctld, a diadema antillarum die-off, a key herbivore in the reef; and the 2023-2024 massive bleaching event. live coral cover decreased by 60-95%, populations of coral frequently used in restoration programs depleted and macroalgal cover increased by 60-75% likely due to the paucity of herbivory. samar's monitoring program allowed the identification of drivers of coral decline and was pivotal to redefine restoration efforts. specifically, we had to revise/redesign program's objectives, site selection, interventions and reshape restoration plans and metrics of success. based on analysis of trajectories and trends of decline of reefs in samar, our restoration efforts are now focused on maximizing production at lower cost, while minimizing extractive practices of wildlife corals and increasing effective return. coral reefs across the caribbean are among the most biodiverse and economically valuable marine ecosystems, providing essential services such as shoreline protection, fisheries production, and supporting millions of livelihoods through tourism. the dominican republic (dr) depends on healthy coral reefs to sustain coastal economies, safeguard communities, and preserve marine biodiversity. however, these ecosystems in the dr are in critical condition due to climate change, pollution, coastal development, and unsustainable fishing practices, highlighting the need for a robust legal framework to guide their protection and restoration. this work reviews and analyzes the international and national legal instruments relevant to coral reefs in the dr, to identify regulatory gaps and priority needs. the assessment was conducted under the coralcarib project, implemented by the nature conservancy in collaboration with puntacana foundation and fundemar as local partners. the methodology combined an extensive literature review with a participatory workshop held in 2025, involving 16 national institutions. these contributions were complemented by technical reviews from local (idard) and regional (aida) environmental legal associations, as well as the dominican ministry of environment. we found that the dr is part of a wide range of international commitments related to the conservation and management of marine biodiversity. nationally, laws, decrees, and sectoral regulations provide indirect protection for coral reefs; however, the country lacks a dedicated legal instrument focused on these ecosystems. key regulatory gaps include limited enforcement capacity, insufficient institutional coordination, and the need to strengthen environmental assessment and compliance requirements for activities that may impact reef systems. priorities identified include updating threatened species lists, improving wastewater and solid waste management, and advancing integrated ecosystem-based management approaches. recommendations emphasize reducing pollution, strengthening governance, expanding restoration and monitoring efforts, promoting sustainable development practices, increasing environmental education, and community engagement to support the long-term resilience of coral reefs in the country.",
