      "search_text": "tokyo metroplolitan university ryusuke obata ryusuke obata development and performance evaluation of an all-solid-state fet-based carbonate ion sensor for coral reef carbonate budget assessment4 global warming and ocean acidification pose serious threats to the persistence of coral reef ecosystems, which support high biodiversity. the carbonate budget of coral reefs is governed by the balance between carbonate production and carbonate dissolution; however, research on the dissolution of reef sediments–one of the dominant contributors to this budget–has progressed slowly. consequently, the mechanisms controlling sediment dissolution and the quantitative prediction of these processes remain inadequately understood. under these circumstances, there is a strong demand for high-precision, in situ techniques capable of monitoring carbonate ion concentrations. to address the limitations of conventional measurement approaches, we report the development of an all-solid-state carbonate ion sensor based on a field-effect transistor (fet). by combining a carbonate-selective ionophore with an fet-based detection platform, the device enables solid-state detection of carbonate ions (co₃²⁻). unlike traditional potentiometric sensors, which often exhibit signal drift due to contamination or instability of the internal solution, the present sensor eliminates the need for an internal electrolyte entirely. this structural advantage ensures stable measurements even in seawater containing multiple interfering ions. in addition, the sensor demonstrates a rapid response on the order of seconds and offers inherent miniaturization benefits, making it highly suitable for real-time and on-site monitoring applications. in this study, the fundamental performance of the sensor was evaluated. the device was immersed in three standard carbonate ion solutions of different concentrations, in artificial seawater, and in natural seawater, and its response characteristics were examined in detail. reproducibility was assessed by repeatedly using the same sensor under identical conditions. this presentation highlights the linear response to carbonate ions and the high measurement reproducibility achieved. establishing this sensing technology is expected not only to advance carbonate budget assessments in coral reef environments but also to facilitate applications in other scientific fields, including biomedical sensing.",
      "country_code": "JP",
      "country": "Japan",
      "connection_count": 1
