      "search_text": "transformations community bruce goldstein bruce goldstein emerging coral interventions under accelerating ecological stress: readiness, risks, and governance needs coral reef interventions are evolving rapidly in response to accelerating ecological decline, with approaches such as assisted gene flow, thermal-tolerant symbiont inoculation, shading, hybridization, and microbiome manipulation moving toward field deployment. yet intervention science is advancing faster than the governance systems needed to evaluate, authorize, and steward their responsible use. drawing on a synthesis of the 2024 reef futures interdisciplinary workshop (80+ participants), the coral restoration consortium (crc) policy brief, and a cross-case review of emerging interventions in the caribbean and beyond, this study examines how accelerating stress conditions reshape assessments of readiness, risk, and appropriateness.\r\nwe present an updated intervention readiness–risk framework that integrates three dimensions: (1) expected ecological effect under worsening thermal regimes, (2) context-dependent risk profiles, including ecological, genetic, and socio-political risks, and (3) governance requirements for responsible deployment. using expert-derived ratings and qualitative uncertainty assessments, we identify mismatch patterns in which interventions with high potential utility under climate stress face severe governance barriers, while lower-risk interventions often lack the enabling conditions for scaling.\r\nour findings show that:\r\n• ecological urgency compresses timelines for evaluation, heightening the significance of institutional readiness;\r\n• perceived risks vary substantially across regions due to differences in regulatory capacity, community trust, and histories of intervention;\r\n• community-led stewardship and transparent decision-making processes increase intervention acceptability across diverse stakeholder groups.\r\nwe conclude by identifying critical governance gaps–including permitting complexity, coordination challenges across agencies, and limited policy frameworks for interventions beyond traditional restoration. addressing these gaps is essential for ensuring that emerging interventions can be scaled responsibly and equitably as reefs enter a period of heightened instability.",
