      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Heather Bauer Reid",
          "search_text": "heather bauer reid developmental acclimation to local thermal environments shapes physiological and hormonal responses to warming in anemone-associated reef fish tropical reef systems are increasingly threatened by rising ocean temperatures and other anthropogenic stressors. species with limited mobility, such as anemone- or coral-associated species, are particularly at risk because they remain exposed to the local environmental conditions around their host after settlement. in moorea, french polynesia, the threespot damselfish (dascyllus trimaculatus) and polynesian anemonefish (amphiprion maohiensis) are both associated with anemones and occupy habitats that differ markedly in temperature, ranging from warm, shallow lagoons to cooler outer reefs from 10-20 m in depth.\r\n\r\nour objective was to determine whether developmental acclimation to local thermal conditions enhances physiological resilience to warming, measured through metabolic rates and circulating cortisol. juveniles of each species from both habitat types (lagoon and outer reef) were assessed for respirometry as well as baseline and stress-induced cortisol soon after capture under ambient temperature conditions (28˚c). individuals were then split into two groups and either held at 28˚c or acclimated to 32˚c, a predicted end of century temperature which can induce anemone bleaching and is currently considered abnormally high. respirometry was then retested after approximately 10 days, as were cortisol levels.  \r\n\r\nby comparing baseline and thermally-induced metabolic and endocrine responses across origin and temperature treatments, the results of this study will not only elucidate the responses of two vulnerable fish species to future warming, but also how their microhabitat-specific developmental histories influence their vulnerability to future warming. we will further improve our understanding of the physiological consequences of warming on site-attached species through comparison with known effects of anemone bleaching on these species. with limited dispersal capability past the larval phase, these findings will provide insight into the adaptive potential of site-attached reef fishes via developmental acclimation and highlight that resilience to rising temperatures may vary substantially across reef zones, even across small spatial scales.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Developmental acclimation to local thermal environments shapes physiological and hormonal responses to warming in anemone-associated reef fish",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "fa9e6f91-5be9-4449-a4b8-98c7016cb00a"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-02032",
          "attended": false
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
      "geocode_level": "country",
