      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Angela Larson",
          "search_text": "angela larson compiling in situ temperature data to predict reef-water temperatures coral bleaching events are globally monitored by satellite-derived sea surface temperature (sst), which is used to track heat exposure on coral reefs and predict the degree of bleaching without direct in situ observations. however, satellites provide temperatures at resolution coarser than most coral reefs, causing them to miss small-scale temperature variability, leading to spatial and temporal differences in predicted versus observed bleaching severity.  in situ temperature data, collected directly on the reef rather than remotely sensed, are essential for capturing these small-scale dynamics and improving our understanding of reef-water temperature variance. therefore, we developed the coral reef in situ product (crisp), the first global-scale repository of in situ coral reef temperature data. the repository currently contains over 2,500 consistently formatted raw temperature-logger files gathered from peer-reviewed studies, gray literature, and community submissions. crisp is publicly accessible online, where users can access the database, view metadata for each logger, and download the raw temperature data immediately. using crisp, we are developing statistical models to predict fine-scale temperature variability such as daily temperature range calculations on reefs globally. we are also leveraging a variety of reef characteristics and environmental drivers including reef geomorphology, depth, distance to shore, and distance to open water. our aims are to (1) evaluate the accuracy of satellite-derived sst through systematic comparisons between satellite product and in situ data, and (2) develop predictions of reef-water temperatures.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Compiling In Situ Temperature Data to Predict Reef-Water Temperatures",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "3600a4d9-d5b2-4d2e-b3d9-19068aca85e5"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00972",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
      "speaker_count": 1,
      "talk_count": 1,
      "geocode_level": "institute",
      "distance_km": 12361.6,
      "search_text": "tulane university angela larson angela larson compiling in situ temperature data to predict reef-water temperatures coral bleaching events are globally monitored by satellite-derived sea surface temperature (sst), which is used to track heat exposure on coral reefs and predict the degree of bleaching without direct in situ observations. however, satellites provide temperatures at resolution coarser than most coral reefs, causing them to miss small-scale temperature variability, leading to spatial and temporal differences in predicted versus observed bleaching severity.  in situ temperature data, collected directly on the reef rather than remotely sensed, are essential for capturing these small-scale dynamics and improving our understanding of reef-water temperature variance. therefore, we developed the coral reef in situ product (crisp), the first global-scale repository of in situ coral reef temperature data. the repository currently contains over 2,500 consistently formatted raw temperature-logger files gathered from peer-reviewed studies, gray literature, and community submissions. crisp is publicly accessible online, where users can access the database, view metadata for each logger, and download the raw temperature data immediately. using crisp, we are developing statistical models to predict fine-scale temperature variability such as daily temperature range calculations on reefs globally. we are also leveraging a variety of reef characteristics and environmental drivers including reef geomorphology, depth, distance to shore, and distance to open water. our aims are to (1) evaluate the accuracy of satellite-derived sst through systematic comparisons between satellite product and in situ data, and (2) develop predictions of reef-water temperatures.",
