      "search_text": "tuvalu meteorological service faoliu teakau faoliu teakau drivers of coral reef degradation and consequences on shorelines in tuvalu coral reefs surrounding funafuti, tuvalu, play a critical role in coastal stability, ecosystem productivity, and community livelihoods. however, climate-driven stressors are reshaping reef health and shoreline dynamics. we examine how recurrent coral bleaching events–driven primarily by rising sea surface temperatures–have weakened natural coastal protection from significant coral mortality that reduces reef structural complexity. as live coral cover declines, wave energy reaching shorelines increases, contributing to erosion and shoreline change that compound the effects of regional sea-level rise. simultaneously, rapid expansion of the floating seaweed sargassum within the lagoon further hinders reef recovery. dense mats reduce light penetration, inhibiting coral growth and recruitment, while their proliferation may itself be linked to ocean warming and altered nutrient regimes. the combined impacts of bleaching-induced reef degradation, sargassum overgrowth, and rising sea levels create a feedback loop that threatens both ecological resilience and human habitation on low-lying atolls. understanding how these stressors interact is essential for developing effective management strategies to support reef restoration and coastal protection in funafuti and similar pacific atoll systems.",
      "country_code": "TV",
      "country": "Tuvalu",
      "connection_count": 1
