      "search_text": "ucsd or ben-zvi or ben-zvi the benthic underwater microscope imaging pam (bump): a non-invasive tool for in situ assessment of microstructure and photosynthetic efficiency the benthic underwater microscope imaging pam (bump): a non-invasive tool for in situ assessment of microstructure and photosynthetic efficiency\r\n\r\ncoral reefs are undergoing rapid decline due to climate-driven stressors, yet many of the early indicators of coral health deterioration occur at microscopic scales that have remained largely inaccessible in situ. traditional approaches to studying coral photophysiology and microstructure rely on laboratory-based imaging or destructive sampling, leaving a critical gap in our ability to observe corals non-invasively in their natural environment. \r\nthe benthic underwater microscope imaging pam (bump) integrates high-resolution underwater microscopy with pulse amplitude modulated (pam) fluorometry to create a non-invasive platform for in situ visualization of coral microstructure and assessment of photosynthetic performance. this diver-operated system enables researchers to capture polyp-scale morphology, tissue organization, and symbiont distribution while simultaneously quantifying photophysiological processes. through combined structural, color, and fluorescence imaging, the bump resolves spatial heterogeneity in symbiont photosynthetic efficiency at previously inaccessible scales and reveals distinctive patterns of photosymbiont distribution and host fluorescence.\r\nhere we provide examples for the potential applications of the system including morphology-based in situ species identification, non-invasive estimation of in hospite photosymbiont density, and early detection of stress signals, such as subtle declines in photosynthetic performances or shifts in host fluorescence, that may precede visible bleaching. overall, the bump provides a transformative framework for linking microscale structure and function to coral health, enabling unprecedented in situ, non-invasive tracking of symbiosis dynamics and early indicators of stress.",
