      "search_text": "universidad del magdalena rocio garcia rocio garcia acropora: a key genus for the conservation of reef fish fauna in the colombian caribbean coral reefs are crucial habitats for many fish species due to their function as feeding and shelter sites. however, due to their sensitive to environmental changes, reef degradation has led to a drastic decrease in fish and reef assemblages, which has also been reflected in related economic activities. the loss of branching corals acropora palmata and acropora cervicornis reaches up to 90% in some areas, which means a reduction in the abundance and diversity of associated fish populations. in two time periods, between 2011-2012 and 2023-2024 throughout the colombian caribbean, the fish associated with the acropora formations were evaluated in relation to the size and health status of both species. the most important patches of a. palmata were found in the tayrona national natural park and a. cervicornis in the reefs of the gulf of urabá. 113 species from 34 families were associated with a. palmata, with the best-represented families being labridae, haemulidae, pomacentridae, and serranidae. thalassoma bifasciatum, microspathodon chrysurus, and stegastes adustus were the species common to all reef locations, with the highest number of species found in the tayrona national natural park (55). in a. cervicornis, 78 species belonging to 25 families were recorded, with labridae, pomacentridae, and haemulidae being the most abundant. stegastes planifrons was the only species common to all locations, and the highest number of species was found in san bernardo islands (39). although the state is critical throughout the caribbean, and with the bleaching of 2023-2024, the populations of a. palmata practically disappeared from two localities, the loss of structure is not yet reflected in the associated ichthyofauna.",
