      "distance_km": 8703.6,
      "search_text": "universiti kebangsaan malaysia hin boo wee hin boo wee up and down, left and right: discrepancies of east coast peninsular malaysia coral reefs among leeward-windward and north-south by the monsoon and pabuk typhoon (2017-2023). coral reefs at the islands along the east coast of peninsular malaysia are affected by the annual monsoon season, possibly shaping the leeward (westward facing mainland) and windward (eastward facing south china sea) reef compositions. furthermore, a devastating pabuk typhoon in january 2019 hit the northern part of the region and significantly damaged the reefs. this study aimed to examine effects of the acute and chronic weather phenomena on the reef composition. coral reef compositions were recorded, based on reef check malaysia standards, across nine islands (70 sites) along east coast peninsular malaysia from 2017 to 2023. the composition parameters analyzed were sessile benthic cover, and populations of fish and invertebrates.  permanova statistical methods, with data stratified by site were used to reduce variation. the analysis revealed distinct patterns between northern and southern island groups, as well as between leeward and windward sites. northern leeward sites showed significant compositional changes across years, particularly a notable hard coral decline after pabuk typhoon (2019-2020), replaced primarily by nutrient indicator algae. northern islands also exhibited increased populations of parrotfish, sweetlips, and snappers. southern islands displayed different patterns, with 2023 showing the most degraded benthic conditions rather than 2019-2020. fish composition varied more by island than by year in the south, while invertebrates and disease patterns remained inconsistent. the findings suggest geographically distinct reef dynamics, with northern reefs experiencing earlier degradation compared to southern reefs. the global and local increase in human activity following the covid-19 pandemic may have been the primary cause of the degradation in 2023. this study highlighted the importance of region-specific coral reef management strategies, and different incidence affecting the coral reefs.",
