      "search_text": "universiti malaysia terengganu dr james tan najwa zulkifly chun hong tan exploring mesophotic coral diversity at pulau tioman, malaysia: insights from the 2024 oceanx rov survey tracking acropora coral reproduction under extreme 2024 heatwave conditions at pulau bidong, terengganu, malaysia mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces) are increasingly recognized as vital refuges for coral biodiversity but remain poorly explored due to depth constraints. in the south china sea, mesophotic reefs typically occur at depths of 30 to 40 meters, forming the deeper basal reef zone. between 7 and 11 october 2024, we conducted an rov-based survey of hard coral communities at pulau tioman, pahang, malaysia, as part of the oceanx expedition, using the rov chimaera. seven sites were surveyed, with dives ranging from 28 to 65 meters and an average depth of 32.9 meters to target areas with highest expected coral diversity. each dive lasted 120 to 130 minutes, allowing comprehensive video documentation and identification of coral genera.\r\n\r\na total of 36 hard coral genera were recorded across all sites, highlighting the richness of pulau tioman’s mesophotic reefs. the six most abundant genera: leptoseris (13.24%), goniopora (9.85%), pavona (8.71%), dipsastraea (8.33%), fungia, and plesiastrea (each 6.06%), accounted for more than 50 percent of observed diversity. water visibility varied between sites, ranging from less than two meters to around five meters, which influenced survey clarity but not overall genus identification.\r\n\r\nthe dominance of a few genera suggests depth and site-specific structuring of mesophotic coral communities. this study provides an essential baseline for understanding coral distribution patterns in malaysia’s deeper reefs. it also contributes to broader regional knowledge of mesophotic reef ecosystems in this region, offering insights for conservation planning and management. by demonstrating the effectiveness of rov technology for exploring hard-to-access reefs, this research reveals hidden biodiversity and highlights the critical role of mces in maintaining coral diversity under increasing environmental pressures on shallow reefs. seawater temperature anomalies have intensified in recent years, increasingly affecting marine organisms. hard corals are particularly sensitive to elevated temperatures, which can disrupt key reproductive processes. the 2024 marine heatwave, one of the most severe globally, caused widespread bleaching, and mass bleaching was observed at pulau bidong between june and august 2024, suggesting potential impacts on coral reproduction. understanding these responses is critical, as coral reproduction underpins reef recovery and long-term ecosystem resilience.\r\n\r\nthis study examined the effects of extreme seawater temperatures during the 2024 bleaching event on the reproduction of acropora digitifera and acropora paniculata, and compared their reproductive condition in the post-bleaching spawning year of 2025. temperature loggers at the study site recorded a mean of 30.58°c (mean daily maximum=31.66°c) between april and september 2024, and 30.19°c (mean daily maximum = 31.07°c) in 2025. ten colonies per species were sampled, with preserved, decalcified branches dissected to assess reproductive characteristics. both species spawned in september in 2024 and 2025.\r\n\r\nin 2024, a. digitifera showed a mean fecundity of 6.79 ± 0.122 oocytes per polyp and oocyte size of 509.68 ± 2.899 µm, while a. paniculata exhibited higher fecundity (10.74 ± 0.358) with smaller oocytes (471.47 ± 4.821 µm). in 2025, both species increased fecundity: a. digitifera to 9.8 ± 0.152 oocytes per polyp (462.99 ± 8.057 µm), and a. paniculata to 13.43 ± 0.257 oocytes per polyp (510.72 ± 3.333 µm).\r\n\r\nthese results indicate species-specific reproductive responses following the 2024 bleaching event. the findings provide insight into post-stress reproductive trajectories, highlighting that corals at pulau bidong can partially recover fecundity after thermal stress. such resilience, coupled with retained spawning timing, suggests potential for targeted management and restoration strategies to support reef recovery under ongoing climate change. najwa zulkifly comparative studies on coral reef fish diversity in relation to coral cover between turbid water coral reef of pulau langkawi and pulau payar, north of malacca straits, malaysia . coral reefs are the rainforest of the sea, providing critical habitats for many marine species, particularly reef fishes. the diversity and abundance of fish species serve as indicators of the reef's well-being and should be monitored for conservation and management purposes. pulau langkawi, recognized as a unesco global geopark, while pulau payar is one of the marine parks in malaysia, both serve as vital ecological and economic resources. yet the coral reefs around these islands remain vulnerable to climate change, pollution, and unsustainable tourism practices, with both islands being subjected to a heavy load of land-based sedimentation. this comparative study aims to assess the diversity of coral reef fish and the current condition of coral in these two islands. 11 sites on each island have been selected where fish surveys and coral surveys were conducted using the visual census technique during scuba dives. a total of 19 families, 35 genera, and 61 species of coral reef fishes were identified at pulau langkawi. reef sites were categorized as having “poor” to “good” coral conditions, with the percentages of live coral cover ranging from 14.4% to 73.1%. at pulau payar, a total of 25 families, 52 genera, and 115 species of coral reef fishes were identified, while the reef sites were characterized as “poor” and “fair” coral conditions, with the percentages of live coral cover ranging from 2.5% to 42.6%. this study provides useful information on the updated coral reef fish species checklist and the status of corals in both islands, offering a baseline for identifying priority zones that require stricter protection. it also facilitates early detection of degradation to initiate conservation planning. also, the data can help test the effectiveness of policies and improve planning for policy improvements.",
