      "search_text": "universiti sains malaysia nur ain amani abdul mubin nur ain amani abdul mubin predicting bleaching through physiology: bridging coral stress responses with adaptive management4c coral reefs in the northern straits of malacca forms ecologically important reef systems that support fisheries, coastal protection, and tourism. situated in a semi-enclosed and sediment-influenced channel, these reefs are highly sensitive to warming seas and local pressures, yet management responses remain largely reactive and insufficiently guided by scientific indicators. this study integrates physiological, and governance perspectives to support the development of climate-responsive bleaching management strategies. general physiological responses indicate that corals exhibit clear signs of stress well before bleaching becomes visible. changes in coral–symbiont associations further show shifts toward more stress-tolerant partnerships, suggesting some degree of natural adjustment but also revealing limits to resilience under continued warming. together, these findings underscore the importance of early, science-based indicators for anticipating bleaching events and guiding proactive management decisions. governance assessment reveals persistent challenges within malaysia’s marine park system, including fragmented institutional roles, limited integration of scientific data into operational practice, and the absence of defined thermal thresholds to trigger timely interventions. in response, a coral bleaching management action plan (cbmap) is proposed, emphasizing early-warning systems, localized thermal thresholds and adaptive management strategies to enhance reef resilience in a warming world.  by translating scientific insights into actionable management tools, this work provides a scalable pathway for strengthening climate resilience in coral reef governance within malaysia and the wider coral triangle.",
