          "name": "Sahra Greve",
          "search_text": "sahra greve submarine groundwater discharge drives sea-level signatures in coral δ234u submarine groundwater discharge (sgd) exerts a major influence on nutrient supply, carbonate chemistry, and water quality in coral-reef environments, yet it remains difficult to quantify and even harder to reconstruct over time. terrestrial waters typically exhibit δ234u signatures markedly different from seawater, thus making sgd the dominant driver of δ234u variability in reef settings where groundwater fluxes are significant. because sea-level changes modulate sgd flux, we show that δ234u measured in corals also provides a powerful means of reconstructing past sea-level variability. \r\nwe present the first spatially resolved assessment of uranium-isotope variability in sgd within the volcanic island and reef system of tahiti, french polynesia. coastal seawater maintains δ234u values near the open-ocean baseline, indicating conservative behaviour and rapid uranium mixing, whereas sgd samples display pronounced spatial heterogeneity in both δ234u and uranium concentrations. these variations reflect differences in aquifer flow paths, residence times, and redox conditions, and are modulated by tidal forcing, with elevated δ234u at low tide signalling enhanced discharge.\r\na decade-long δ234u record from a coral at maraa, tahiti captures interannual sgd variability that closely tracks enso-driven sea-level fluctuations, demonstrating that sea-level changes are directly influencing sgd flux and recorded in coral skeletons. to test the broader applicability of this proxy, we examine a comparable coral record from the yucatán peninsula, a contrasting hydrogeologic system where sgd dominates coastal hydrology. the yucatán record, spanning the past century, reveals an even stronger correspondence between annual variations in δ234u and centimetre-scale sea-level variations. \r\ntaken together, these results demonstrate that δ234u in corals robustly encodes groundwater–sea-level interactions, enabling quantitative reconstruction of high-resolution sea-level variability in reef environments. this approach provides a new, proxy-based pathway for deciphering past sea-level change in regions where traditional indicators are limited or absent.",
