      "search_text": "university of alabama at birmingham dustin kemp dustin kemp integrating biomass, symbiosis, and energy dynamics to understand coral recovery under thermal stress coral reefs are highly vulnerable to thermal stress, yet the physiological mechanisms driving their recovery remain incompletely understood. recovery is not merely the visible reversal of bleaching; it is a complex, time-intensive process involving multiple interdependent physiological pathways. this study examines the multifaceted nature of coral recovery, with a focus on symbiosis with dinoflagellate partners, heterotrophic feeding, energy acquisition, and biomass accumulation. the interactions among these components are assessed to determine their influence on coral resilience, resistance, and long-term persistence under thermal stress. results indicate that biomass serves as a critical integrative measure, reflecting the energy reserves and overall physiological state of corals. recovery trajectories are closely linked to the capacity of corals to rebuild biomass through coordinated symbiont repopulation and energy acquisition via feeding and photosynthesis. corals with higher initial biomass and effective energy accumulation pathways demonstrate enhanced resistance and faster recovery, whereas those with depleted reserves exhibit prolonged stress and reduced persistence. these findings underscore the importance of monitoring biomass alongside traditional metrics such as symbiont density or chlorophyll content to better predict recovery potential. the results highlight the inherent complexity of coral physiology, demonstrating that successful recovery requires the integration of multiple physiological processes over extended timescales. by elucidating the relationships between biomass, symbiosis, and energy dynamics, this study provides a framework for understanding coral resilience under climate-driven thermal stress and informs management strategies aimed at sustaining coral populations and reef ecosystems.",
