          "name": "Marcellin Tsihoboto",
          "search_text": "roandrianasolo marcellin tsihoboto first insights of both ecological effectiveness and community stewardship of an artificial reef deployment in the loky manambato mpa (north madagascar) artificial reefs are increasingly used in madagascar as a cost-effective strategy to restore degraded coral reefs, enhance marine biodiversity, and support coastal livelihoods. within the loky manambato marine protected area (mpa) in northeastern madagascar a project of artificial reef deployment (ard) was launched in 2025. the first step consisted in the local construction of  449 artificial reef units across two villages (ambavarano and antafiamivony). our study proposes the first insights on the ecological and social impacts of these deployments in the two villages. \r\nbaseline ecological data were collected before ard (march 2025), followed by a six-month monitoring survey (november 2025) over a surface of 500 m2 covered by the ard on each site. our study focuses on data collected for inventories of the fish compartment of the ecosystem carried out by line-intersect transect standard method. results show substantial positive effects on both fish biomass at both sites with respective increases from 178 g to 6033 g in antafiamivony and from 2161 g to 4605 g in ambavarano over 7 months. additionally, 12 and 5 new fish families were respectively recorded in antafiamivony and ambavarano.\r\nfishermen communities were also invited to deliver their opinion on the usefulness of the project. two groups of 20 and 23 participants representing the half of the fishermen communities in each village were asked to participate in antafiamivony and ambavarano, respectively. the survey revealed strong local support, with 100% of participants expressing willingness to protect the artificial reefs and over 85% identifying the project as useful. \r\nour preliminary results suggest an active use of the artificial reefs by fish for feeding, breeding, and shelter. the ard project has fostered positive behavioral change of the fisher community, by reducing fishing pressure around the sites, discouraging the use of of destructive gear, and increasing community participation in monitoring.",
