          "name": "Yusup Jentewo",
          "search_text": "yusup jentewo coral reef health and associated biota in sasi-managed areas of cenderawasih bay national park the cenderawasih bay national park is the largest marine national park in indonesia, located in the northern region between west papua and central papua provinces. several villages within the park have inherited customary practices to protect their surrounding marine ecosystems. the tradition known as sasi in maluku but in cenderawasih bay emerge by various local names, such as kadup and sawora.\r\n\r\nwe surveyed in the areas governed by these sasi practices in 2023 and 2024 to assess the coral reef condition. data collection took place in three sasi villages: menarbu, where sasi is implemented for years; aisandami, where sasi is influenced by tourism; and windesi, where sasi has been initiated. the team collected data on coral lifeforms using the point intercept transect (pit) and timed swimed  methods, as well as reef fish and megabenthos using visual census.  \r\n\r\ncoral cover was mostly moderate to high (20-70%), dominated by hard corals and coral rubble, with bleaching detected in windesi; menarbu showed fluctuating trends (40.9% in 2023). reef fish diversity consisted of three main herbivore and six carnivorous fish. there are four other ecologically important fish species. reef fish abundance varied by site, with biomass estimates ranging from 10 to 120 kg/ha for key fishery families and 80 to 100 kg/ha for key functional families. megabenthos consisted of 4-9 families with low abundances 0.02-0.08 ind/m² in aisandami, 0.936 total individuals in menarbu (declining trend), and presence-based counts in windesi dominated by holothuridae and tridacnidae.\r\n\r\nthe important restrictions in the sasi that directly affect coral reefs are: the first, complete closure of certain areas to harvesting; second, restrictions on destructive fishing gear and protected fish is not catched. this local tradition has strengthened the legal marine protected area, which is governed by the cenderawasih bay national park authority.",
