          "name": "Emma Ryan",
          "search_text": "emma ryan can growth rates of crustose coralline algae be reliably measured in situ with photogrammetry? calcifying algae play a critical role in coral reef flat accretion, sediment production and reef resilience, yet their vertical growth rates and contribution to reef flat accretion processes are challenging to quantify at scale in situ. structure-from-motion (sfm) photogrammetry offers a non-invasive, low-cost method to measure vertical and marginal growth rates of coralline algae by comparing high-resolution (sub-mm scale) digital surface models (dsms). however, applying this technique to reliably quantify growth presents challenges associated with temporal repeatability, co-registration, and spatial scaling. \r\nwe tested whether close-range sfm can be used to measure annual coralline algae growth rates on a fringing reef flat in tongatapu, the kingdom of tonga. we also tested scaling up measurements spatially across-reef flats that are exposed at spring low tides, using drone-based sfm. close-range dsms were generated from handheld camera surveys of 1 m² quadrats at ~1 m elevation, repeated over three years to capture annual growth. aerial dsms were produced from drone imagery captured with dji mavic 3e at 10–15 m elevation, overlapping the quadrat locations to assess detection limits for algal accretion at broader spatial coverage. all models were processed in agisoft, with ground-control and check-points surveyed using a leica total station. we present vertical and marginal growth rate estimates derived from the close-range data and evaluate the sensitivity of drone-based models to detect growth through time. this presentation will discuss the successes and limitations of photogrammetric approaches for monitoring coralline algae growth on sub-aerially exposed reef flats. the importance of validating and maintaining high ground control elevational accuracy and precise co-registration is discussed.",
