          "name": "Therese Miller",
          "search_text": "therese miller follow your gut: a bacterial microbiome survey of guam's rabbitfish foregut and hindgut rabbitfish (genus: siganus) are of cultural significance for island communities around the pacific and are a candidate for aquaculture. rabbitfish have been a food staple and focus of traditional fishing practices in guam for centuries. given their cultural significance, research in guam and micronesia has focused on rabbitfish life histories. for s. spinus, differing growth rates that appeared linked to nutritional availability have been observed across locations. understanding the role that the s. spinus gut microbiome plays in nutritional intake and how it potentially impacts growth may provide important information on the biology of rabbitfish, as well as contribute to the management and development of fishing and farming practices. \r\nto begin addressing this knowledge gap, we targeted 16s rrna for metabarcoding bacterial microbiome communities from the foregut and hindgut of 30 s. spinus specimens from three different locations in guam. alpha and beta diversity were assessed to examine microbial diversity, differences in community composition between fore- and hindguts, and differences between locations. in specimens from all three locations, hindguts possessed greater bacterial evenness and shannon diversity than foreguts. additionally, specimens from the tanguisson reef flat held many bacterial taxa that were differentially abundant from those of other locations. differences in hindgut microbiome of tanguisson specimens were characterised by high abundances of endozoicomonadaceae. endozoicomonadaceae sequences found in this study matched sequences isolated from staghorn coral acropora pulchra living in the same location. we also observed elevated abundances of desulfovibrionaceae in the foregut and hindgut of tanguisson specimens. these results suggest an influence of habitat on shaping s. spinus gut microbiome composition. this study provides a first baseline for guam rabbitfish gut microbiomes and sheds light on the bacteria that may aid rabbitfish in digestion, be beneficial to their host, and bacteria that may represent elements of diet.",
