          "name": "Roman Sieler",
          "search_text": "roman eric sieler the impact of coral bleaching on fisheries, nutrition, and stunting in east africa a critical channel through which climate change affects human welfare and economic development is the degradation of ecosystems that underpin livelihoods and nutrition. coral reefs are a prime example: they are highly vulnerable to climate-induced coral bleaching while simultaneously supporting fisheries that are central to food security in many coastal regions, in particular in developing countries. using a novel large-scale coral bleaching dataset created based on satellite imagery and exploiting long-run sea surface temperature variation as an instrument, this paper provides causal evidence on how coral bleaching translates into socio-economic damages relating to fisheries, incomes, consumption, and health in east africa.\r\n\r\nthe paper documents a clear chain of impacts from ecosystem degradation to human outcomes. coral bleaching significantly reduces fish catch, leading to declines in household protein consumption and increases the prevalence of child stunting, an indicator of early childhood malnutrition. at the same time, it forces households to adapt by selling agricultural assets, while other theorized adaptation channels like migration or increased work effort do not appear to play a role. these findings highlight that coral bleaching is not only an environmental concern but also presents a considerable threat to the economic well-being, health, and long-term human capital formation in coastal communities.\r\n\r\nfrom a policy perspective, the results highlight the importance of considering coral bleaching when developing climate adaptation strategies, social policies, and aid interventions. they suggest that coral reef conservation and restoration can generate substantial co-benefits beyond biodiversity protection, and that communities and entire countries can benefit in terms of improved fishery yields, better health outcomes that matter for long-term wellbeing, incomes - and consequently economic development. more broadly, the findings support the case for policy frameworks that explicitly account for the role of coral reefs in sustaining livelihoods and human well-being.",
