      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Malte Ostendarp",
          "search_text": "malte ostendarp experimental molecular hydrogen addition can support photosynthetic performance of the common hard coral pocillopora favosa following an extreme marine heat wave in the central red sea most coral reefs worldwide are severely affected by recurrent mass bleaching and mortality events induced by ocean warming. this underlines the urgent need for immediate interventions to prevent the widespread loss of coral reefs, while carbon neutrality is achieved. recent findings suggest that molecular hydrogen (h₂) dissolved in seawater benefits coral photophysiology under short-term heat stress, potentially by counteracting oxidative damage. however, long-term effects of h₂ on the coral holobiont remain poorly understood. our study therefore explored whether h₂ exposure could support coral photophysiology following a natural marine heat wave (mhw) thereby enhancing coral recovery. in 2024, the central red sea experienced an extreme mhw that lasted for 202 days, with sea surface temperatures of up to 34.3 °c, triggering widespread mass coral bleaching and mortality. at the end of this event, we collected coral fragments from non-bleached colonies of the widespread hard coral pocillopora favosa. corals were kept in a flow-through aquarium system at ambient seawater temperatures (~ 30.2 °c) below thermal thresholds for three weeks separated into two treatments, with and without h₂, while monitoring key ecophysiological parameters. regardless of treatment, coral fragments experienced a significant loss in coloration, symbiodiniaceae cell density and relative maximum electron transport rate indicating a stress response even after the marine heatwave, potentially linked to the recently described “post-heat stress disorder”. by the end of the experiment, h₂-treated corals retained significantly higher photosynthetic and light-capture efficiency by 40% and 30%, respectively, compared to untreated corals. h₂-treated corals also maintained a stable chlorophyll c2:a ratio in contrast to a significant increase in untreated corals, suggesting a specific loss of chlorophyll a. in conclusion, our results suggest that h₂ can preserve photosynthetic performance of the coral holobiont highlighting the potential of h₂ as a promising tool to maintain and improve coral resilience against ocean warming.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Experimental molecular hydrogen addition can support photosynthetic performance of the common hard coral Pocillopora favosa following an extreme marine heat wave in the Central Red Sea",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "000b947b-3d3c-4de0-af82-9aa6bf440132"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01295",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
