      "search_text": "university of california - davis anya brown dr ben moran kenzie pollard anya brown can coral diversity increase resistance to thermal stress? genotypic diversity leads to increased resistance to disease in coral populations. however, it is unknown how increased genotypic diversity influences coral responses to other important global stressors, such as high temperatures. one underlying mechanism underlying increased resilience under stress is contributions from the coral microbiome. we hypothesize that when corals are in diverse mixtures, their microbiomes shift towards increased bacteria associated with thermal resistance. we tested how genetic diversity influences coral growth, thermal stress, and microbial responses in a coral nursery in rarotonga, cook islands.  we arranged genets of acropora hyacinthus corals from five sites around the island of rarotonga in monocultures (single genotypes) and polycultures (mixtures of genotypes). corals grew in these arrangements for one year, and following one year, they were exposed to an acute heat stress experiment. our results suggest that the net biodiversity effects of polycultures depend on coral sensitivity to thermal stress: sensitive corals grow more in polyculture. for most coral responses (e.g., physiological characteristics, such as lipid content, carbohydrate content), the effects of polyculture depended on genotype. continuing work will assess if mixtures of coral genotypes lead to a) an increase in thermal stress resistance and b) convergent microbial communities associated with lower thermal stress. our results provide insight into whether population diversity can buffer thermally sensitive corals to stress, and if a mechanism underlying this response is due to adaptive shifts in the coral microbiome. we expect this research to inform coral restoration practices by quantifying the individual, population, and ecosystem benefits associated with increasing coral genotypic diversity. benjamin moran pacific-wide population genomics in the acropora hyacinthus complex: speciation, gene flow, and local adaptation growing evidence suggests that many morphologically defined coral species contain multiple genetically distinct groups, or cryptic taxa. these complex patterns of genetic diversity present a challenge to traditional conservation, but also an exciting opportunity to reveal the fundamental processes by which species diverge genomically and ecologically. the table coral, acropora hyacinthus, is one such cryptic complex, with recent population genetic and phylogenetic work describing numerous cryptic sympatric groups, some of which may represent independent species. however, previous studies have been limited in geographic extent, sample size per location, or in the number of genetic markers surveyed. as such, the relative strength of within-location population structure vs. isolation by distance at the oceanic scale, the extent of historical and contemporary gene flow between taxa, and the degree to which cryptic taxa may share adaptive genetic variation, remains unclear. i will present the work of a collaborative network combining 1300 new and existing a. hyacinthus samples from over 40 sites throughout the species’ pacific range. we will subject these sequences to a standardized whole-genome sequencing and genotyping pipeline, and use this unique dataset to create a clearer and broader view of diversity within the a. hyacinthus complex. we will distinguish cryptic population structure from the effects of allopatric divergence, and estimate effective migration rates across the range of each cryptic taxa. we will also identify admixed individuals and infer the history of gene flow across the complex, revealing the degree of long-term reproductive isolation between taxa. finally, we will perform genotype-environment association within each taxa to infer loci involved in local adaptation, and quantify the extent to which adaptive alleles are shared across taxa. together, our work will reveal the evolution of this ubiquitous coral at the pacific scale, and create a rich resource for future a. hyacinthus research and conservation. kenzie pollard lasting effects of bleaching: how prior thermal stress shapes coral physiology and gene expression understanding how organisms respond to repeated environmental stressors is central to predicting resilience in the face of climate change. in reef-building corals, exposure to acute thermal stress can lead to bleaching; however, not all individuals respond in the same way. increasing evidence suggests that prior exposure to stress can influence coral responses to subsequent events, either by enhancing resilience through acclimatization or by compounding damage if recovery is incomplete. in this study, we investigate how prior bleaching history influences coral growth, physiological stress responses, and gene regulation during a simulated heatwave following a natural thermal stress event. corals were grown in a common garden environment and monitored through a natural bleaching event then subjected to a controlled heat stress experiment a year later. corals that bleached and recovered were labeled as “resilient” while corals that did not bleach were considered “resistant”. preliminary results suggest that prior bleaching has lingering effects reflected in both physiological stress responses and long-term growth and energy reserves. notably, resilient corals grew less and were still producing significantly increased antioxidant levels even under control conditions compared to resistant corals. by exploring coral recovery and re-exposure dynamics, this study highlights the potential for stress memory and lingering physiological effects of past bleaching to influence resilience in an era of increasingly frequent marine heatwaves.",
