          "name": "Adelaide Dahl",
          "search_text": "adelaide dahl exploring reef resilience trajectories through community dynamics of rock-boring sea urchin, echinometra spp. art in unexpected places: visualizing community dynamics through sticker design – a collaboration with community members, artists and ecologists across ecosystems macroinvertebrates support key ecological processes on coral reefs, yet many remain understudied despite their roles in shaping reef structure, function and resilience. one such example are sea urchins, which have emerged as possible functional replacements to overfished herbivorous fishes, clearing algal turf and establishing microhabitats to facilitate coral resilience. they are important sources of subsidence and commercial fishing and have been of cultural importance in regions such as the indo-pacific for centuries. widespread die offs have left urchin-grazed systems more vulnerable to state change and benthic community instability, yet, at high densities, urchins can accelerate bioerosion. uncertainty persists over both drivers and patterns of urchin density and the levels at which their grazing may shift from enhancing reef health to accelerating bioerosion. using in situ surveys, population and diet data, and abiotic, we quantified drivers and patterns in density of the boring urchin, echinometra, across a gradient of anthropogenic disturbance on three society islands of french polynesia and palmyra atoll. fishing protection was associated with a decline in urchin density between study locations (lm: f6, 27 = 10.49, p < 0.001), and within-island variability ranged from <1  0.05 m-² to >11  3.68 m-², showing overall decline from historic records. differences in within-location urchin density were significantly predicted by site aspect (and dominant wave direction; lm: f2,15 = 6.179, p = 0.01), with no significant effect of nutrient concentrations and temperature variation. urchin density was associated with an increase in small coral abundance at intermediate densities (gam: f6.74, 7.43 = 39.95, p < 0.001), and an increase in rugosity (lm: f1,21 = 5.848, p = 0.025). overall our results suggest the important role of these underexplored macroinvertebrates in contributing to coral dominance on future reefs, and the possible risk of bioerosion at high densities. with the aid of memorable statistics such as: “coral reefs take up less than one percent of the sea floor yet make up an estimated 25% of global marine life”, these systems are in an ideal position to encourage public engagement. yet given their small footprint, we become challenged to reach a broad audience. we explored how creative practice grounded in scientific research, community passion and inexpensive distribution might expand the reach and conversation around coral reef community ecology and conservation. the project was piloted using our local ecosystem: the rocky intertidal. through community conversation and local outreach, we developed and illustrated a comprehensive list of species. these illustrations were combined into an “infinity sticker” which was designed to wrap around a water bottle continuously, taking the viewer through the zonation of this iconic ecosystem, where benthic ecology was born and fostered. the sticker became a mobile gallery that allowed community members to see their coastal identities reflected in the ecosystem’s complexity, and learn more deeply about how we explore the complex dynamics present. building on this success, we are piloting a parallel project for coral reefs, co-designed with community partners and informed by ecological research from the moorea coral reef long term ecological research project. this coral-focused design highlights culturally significant species and key interactions currently under investigation, translating complex dynamics into shareable artwork. in doing so, it aims to connect people who may never visit a coral reef with the ecological stories unfolding beneath the surface, expanding the research into geographically distant communities. we aim to sell the sticker at various conferences and meetings, with proceeds supporting work of local educational nonprofit, reva atea. through widely distributable, community-rooted artworks with low ecological footprint, we explore how impact can scale socially and geographically, activating a global, living gallery.",
