          "name": "Adrian Stier",
          "search_text": "adrian stier restoration strategy evaluation: a structured framework for adaptive coral restoration marine ecosystem restoration has entered an era of unprecedented ambition, with billions invested globally to recover coral reefs, kelp forests, and other foundation habitats. yet restoration outcomes remain highly variable, and planning often relies on precedent or expert judgment rather than structured evaluation. we propose restoration strategy evaluation (rse), a simulation-based framework adapted from management strategy evaluation in fisheries science, to guide more effective and adaptive marine restoration. rse integrates operating models that simulate ecological dynamics with iterative feedback from field monitoring, enabling practitioners to compare alternative strategies, quantify uncertainty, and refine approaches as new data emerge. the framework comprises seven components: setting restoration targets, building operating models that capture demographic processes and environmental variability, simulating candidate restoration strategies, defining performance metrics, implementing preferred strategies, monitoring outcomes, and updating models through feedback loops. unlike traditional fisheries applications, rse emphasizes place-based socioecological outcomes–from coral cover and ecosystem function to local employment and community engagement–aligned with practitioner and community objectives. we illustrate rse through a coral reef restoration case study where size-structured population models simulate colony growth and survival under climate stress, comparing strategies including thermally tolerant genotype deployment, variable outplanting densities, and herbivore protection zones. performance metrics encompass both ecological indicators and socioeconomic benefits such as restoration-related jobs and tourism revenue. by embedding current scientific knowledge into dynamic decision-support tools, rse creates a structured platform for collaboration among analysts, practitioners, and stakeholders. this approach offers a practical pathway for moving coral reef restoration from experience-based planning toward predictive, evidence-based decision-making capable of addressing the urgent challenges of climate change and biodiversity loss.",
