          "name": "Dr Jenn Caselle",
          "search_text": "jennifer caselle an experimental evaluation of herbivore diversity and size structure in guiding post-disturbance reef recovery at palmyra atoll panellist widespread corallimorph proliferation following the grounding of a fishing vessel at palmyra atoll caused a  phase shift that displaced hard corals across the shallow reef terrace. following wreck removal in 2013, corallimorph cover declined precipitously, leaving a largely bare substrate with limited living coral. palmyra’s intact herbivorous fish community subsequently responded, providing a rare opportunity to evaluate how herbivores of different size classes influence benthic recovery following a major disturbance.  we combined long-term community monitoring with a multi-level herbivore exclusion experiment to track reef recovery across a gradient of fish body sizes. the experimental design included three levels of herbivore exclusion plus open controls, enabling us to quantify how access by large (>50 cm), medium, and small herbivores shapes benthic composition, coral settlement, algal dynamics, and sedimentation.  benthic outcomes diverged strongly across exclusion levels. large-fish exclusions were dominated by corticated algae (e.g., lobophora, peyssonnelia), while mid-level exclusions supported low-lying algal forms and upright macroalgae. the smallest-mesh treatments (excluding most or all herbivores) were characterized by long algal turfs and sediment accumulation, conditions not commonly observed at palmyra. the presence of surgeonfishes and butterfly fishes in mid-level treatments, but not in the smallest mesh treatments, underscored the importance of medium-sized herbivores in suppressing sediment and limiting turf development.  a second experimental phase sequentially relaxed cage treatments to simulate natural herbivore recovery. this “herbivore return” phase reversed many earlier benthic shifts, reduced sediment and algal cover, and accelerated coral regrowth–demonstrating that a robust, size-diverse herbivore community is essential for redirecting disturbed reefs toward coral dominance.  our results show that palmyra’s unfished herbivore assemblage rapidly responded to the removal of the wreck and declining corallimorph cover, exerting strong, size-structured control over benthic  trajectories. these findings provide experimental evidence that the recovery of herbivore communities is a key mechanism facilitating post-disturbance coral reef resilience.",
