          "name": "Julianna Renzi",
          "search_text": "julianna renzi nutrients and overfishing favor corals with low structural complexity on reefs in moorea, french polynesia global change is differentially impacting taxa, indirectly selecting future “winners” and “losers” based on how well species do in human-altered environments. when these taxa are foundation species that provide unique habitats for other organisms, changes in the relative abundance of winners and losers can shift the quality of habitat provided by an ecosystem, even when total cover remains the same. on coral reefs, disturbances like marine heat waves can reduce the structural complexity of surviving corals, given that branching corals–which contribute disproportionately to reef structure–are particularly susceptible to coral bleaching. however, we know far less about the impacts of local stressors (e.g., nutrient pollution, overfishing) on corals with different morphologies. to examine how nutrient pollution and overfishing influence the relative abundance of branching vs encrusting corals in moorea, french polynesia, we: (1) ran a four-year experiment on the forereef of moorea, simulating nutrient pollution and multiple levels of overfishing, and (2) surveyed ~200 sites around moorea to measure how the relative abundance of branching versus encrusting corals varied across natural gradients in nutrient concentration and fish density. experimental results suggest that nutrients and simulated overfishing favored encrusting over branching corals, particularly at the highest levels of simulated overfishing. survey results found similar patterns, with lower corallivorous fish biomass and higher nutrient concentrations favoring encrusting species. however, areas with extremely high nutrient concentrations had little-to-no coral cover, suggesting that there is likely a limit above which nutrient levels also disadvantage encrusting corals. although more work is needed to confirm these patterns in other regions, our results suggest that local human impacts may be selecting for less structurally complex coral species, leading to a flattening of reef structures.",
