      "search_text": "university of dar es salaam ophery ilomo ophery ilomo chromium, zinc and manganese contamination and its ecological impacts in some reefs of the western and eastern part of the zanzibar channel the concentration of manganese (mn), chromium (cr), zinc (zn) and total nitrogen contents (tn) in porites corals from dar es salaam and on the zanzibar coastal areas, located in the western and eastern parts of zanzibar channel respectively, within tanzania continental shelf were determined to assess the spatial and temporal contamination.  after coring, the corals cores were thoroughly cleaned with water and then distilled water. the growth rate or growth bands of the sliced coral slabs, were visually determined with the use of uv-light, and were found to be 7 to 12 mm/year. every fifth growth band was sub-sampled for analysis. the mean maximum concentration (n=3) were found to be 11.4±1.5 μg/g mn, 43.0±3.0 μg/g cr, 8.0±1.1 μg/g zn and 0.29 % tn in the skeleton coral growth bands. the atomic absorption spectrophotometer (aas) graphite furnace and chn elemental analyzer were used for metal and nitrogen analysis respectively. the results showed the level of mn, cr, zn and tn in western part of zanzibar channel were higher than those of eastern part of the channel (p<0.01). this suggest that the western zanzibar channel is highly affected by anthropogenic activities and favorable to cyanobacteria. notably the sewer pipe from dar es salaam city centre, terrestrial runoff from highly polluted kizinga and msimbazi rivers, and anthropogenic activities at dar es salaam port and its vicinity are presumed to be the cause of contamination in these coral reef areas it is therefore, important for the conservation and monitoring processes to start to be in place to avoid pollution in the channel and its ecosystem in general.",
      "country_code": "TZ",
      "country": "Tanzania, United Republic of",
