          "name": "Serena Hackerott",
          "search_text": "serena hackerott combined-stress priming alters physiological and transcriptional dynamics in acropora cervicornis corals and promotes acute thermal resistance evaluating species-specific photobiological responses as predictors of coral thermal resistance and resilience enhanced resilience conferred through pre-exposure to sublethal stress (i.e., priming) may be an important mechanism for reef building corals to cope with variable environments. the effect of stress priming on acropora cervicornis thermotolerance was evaluated in the context of both elevated temperature and ammonium enrichment, 3 °c and/or 6 μm above ambient, respectively. primed corals were pre-exposed to each stressor individually or in combination for eight days, while the non-primed corals remained at ambient conditions. after an eight-day recovery period, primed corals and a subset of non-primed corals (naive) were subjected to an acute 15-hour thermal challenge. coral metabolism, symbiosis, and gene expression were characterized throughout the experiment. thermal tolerance was quantified as the retention of symbionts, chlorophyll, and live tissue, along with the probability of survival through 3 days after acute heat stress. corals in each priming treatment were more likely to retain symbionts and chlorophyll following heat stress and the rate of tissue loss was reduced in all priming treatments relative to the naive corals. moreover, the risk of experiencing tissue loss or predicted mortality was significantly lower in corals pre-exposed to elevated temperature, with or without ammonium enrichment. analysis of differential gene expression and functional enrichment revealed that regulation of the apoptotic process differed between primed and naive corals following the initial priming exposure and again at the onset of the secondary heat stress. while limited differential gene expression persisted between recurrent stress exposures, gene expression dynamics throughout the thermal challenge suggested that primed corals may exhibit higher transcriptional resilience which is likely linked with the lessened heat-induced physiological stress observed in this study. altogether, these results provide support for the capacity of a. cervicornis to gain resilience through pre-exposure to ecologically relevant conditions as well as insights into the molecular mechanisms underpinning this process. the capacity for corals to resist and recover from environmental stress is vital for their survival under a rapidly changing climate. effectively maintaining photosynthetic processes under heat stress is a key factor in determining stability of symbiosis between coral hosts and algal endosymbionts, along with recovery and survival outcomes after disturbance. measured with novel chlorophyll fluorometers, informative photobiological metrics are often linked to coral thermal tolerance. however, photosynthetic resilience will vary across algal genera and coral hosts. therefore, we evaluated if plasticity in five photobiological metrics served as predictors of thermal resistance and resilience across several reef-flat corals in guam. species representing distinct functional groups included acropora pulchra (competitive), pocillopora damicornis (weedy), and pavona decussata (generalist) and encompassed a range of predicted thermal sensitivities. thirty genets of each species were subjected to experimental chronic warming (3c above ambient temperature for 16 days) and the heated and control corals were monitored for an additional 26 days following heating. thermal resistance (the retention of chlorophyll after heating) and resilience (the probability of survival throughout and following heat exposure) both varied across coral species. photobiological metrics, including the maximum quantum yield of photosystem ii (psii), psii functional absorption cross section, connectivity between psii reaction centers, and psii reoxidation rate constants, were measured at five timepoints (i.e. accumulated heat doses) and dose-responses were species-specific. the photophysiological dose-response is predicted to correspond with coral bleaching and mortality risks. further, the most informative photobiological metric is expected to differ between coral species. this study evaluates the link between photosynthetic resilience and thermal tolerance with the aim of identifying effective photobiological predictors to select resilient corals for targeted restoration.",
