      "search_text": "university of florida andrew altieri andrew mcinnis andrew altieri deoxygenation triggers a shift in the microbiome of caribbean corals when combined with ocean warming submitter has requested a 15min oral presentation.\r\n\r\nthe microbiome plays a critical role in the resilience of corals to global change stressors including warming temperatures, and we are just beginning to understand the coral microbiome response to ocean deoxygenation. even less is known about the potential response of the microbiome to the combined stressors of warming and deoxygenation, despite these stressors commonly co-occurring. here, we employed an experimental approach that compared the response of two caribbean reef-building corals, acropora cervicornis and orbicella annularis, to elevated temperatures and lowered oxygen. we exposed both species to high temperature (31.5 degc) and low oxygen (0.5 mg/l) conditions simultaneously in a fully factorial lab experiment that include control conditions (29.0 degc and 6.0 mg/l, respectively). we found that a. cervicornis, the species recognized as more vulnerable to deoxygenation, had its greatest shift in microbiome community composition when exposed to a combination of both stressors – a response that is less apparent in o. annularis. moreover, a. cervicornis had more differentially abundant microbial taxa than o. annularis in the combined stressor treatment, including several potential coral pathogens. we also tested whether sequential exposure to stressors would affect the microbiome response by having conducted the above lab experiment with corals that had been previously outplanted over the summer at either of two reefs in the florida keys that differed in thermal regime. we found that prior exposure to more stressful reef temperatures muted the subsequent response of several microbial taxa to warming and deoxygenation, suggesting a “stress hardening” response that carried over from thermal to deoxygenation stress. our work reveals the importance of testing deoxygenation in a multiple-stressor context, and that differences between coral species can be altered by the history of stress exposure. andrew mcinnis contemporary drivers of pacific atoll coral reef communities in the anthropocene the republic of the marshall islands (rmi) is an atoll nation which spans some of the most remote, pristine coral reefs to some of the most densely populated and heavily used reef systems. this study utilizes data collected sporadically over the past 10 years across 12 atolls in rmi. we evaluated how environmental and geomorphologic gradients interact with emerging anthropogenic stressors, including increasing human population density and climate‐driven thermal indicators, to assess trajectories of coral communities, benthic condition and net carbonate accretion. environmental gradients remained important with low wave exposure reefs exhibiting higher proportions of slow growing heterotrophic corals, with faster growing autotrophic corals representing a consistently higher proportion of communities on high exposure coasts. atolls with minimal direct local human pressure typically retained higher coral cover, greater representation of fast-growing taxa, and more favorable overall calcified benthic composition. atolls that have experience more frequent and severe thermal stress had consistently lower coral cover, selective loss of fast-growing sensitive taxa, and a higher proportion of small weedy corals. in the most extreme cases, when thermal stress was paired with high human pressure, a noticeable a shift in benthic substrates from coral and crustose coralline algae dominated communities toward fleshy macroalgae and other non-calcified substrates became evident. our results show that (1) low human impact can buffer, but not fully prevent, climate-driven degradation, (2) high thermal stress alone can drive reef transitions to slightly diminished calcification states, (3) the combination of intense local use and repeated thermal events produces phase shifts to degraded, net erosional reefs. at the global level this study highlights the urgency of reducing local stressors through effective management while simultaneously advocating strong global action on climate change to sustain coral reef atolls into the future.",
