      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Camilla Nivison",
          "search_text": "camilla nivison coral mortality in a mass bleaching event influenced by proximity to diseased tissue and reef topography coral reefs of the florida keys were hit with the hottest summer on record in 2023, causing mass bleaching and mortality of scleractinian corals. sea surface temperatures remained above 31ºc for 41 days, exceeding all prior records for the region. branching corals fared especially poorly through this heatwave, such that acropora palmata was recently deemed functionally extinct in the region. high temperatures and prolonged thermal stress were the major causes of mortality. however, we show that prior disease increased the onset of death for both the disease lesions and neighboring tissue. using a cox proportional hazard model, we found that, compared to initially healthy tissue on the same colony, disease lesions and neighboring tissue had a 3.8 times and 1.5 times higher risk of death, respectively. we show that multiple stressors, at different spatial scales, acted synergistically, increasing mortality. furthermore, we found that close proximity to the reef edge significantly increased coral survivorship. the reef edge is protective due to potentially faster local velocity, as water courses around the reefs, which would help reduce the boundary layer and enhance the removal of cytotoxic metabolic wastes. these findings reinforce the importance of the interactive effects of disease and reef topography on coral bleaching susceptibility and mortality, and suggest that restoration efforts should be focused on reef edges where the chance of survival is improved relative to the reef interior.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Coral mortality in a mass bleaching event influenced by proximity to diseased tissue and reef topography",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "680c31a6-cf75-4a1e-9578-03cad9078870"
            },
            {
              "title": "CoralReef3D – Low-cost and accurate underwater 3D ecosystem modeling",
              "primary": false,
              "talk_id": "5dc8c3fc-5f55-4157-9da5-baf4fa9c1d09"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01267",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Michelle Diminuco",
          "search_text": "michelle diminuco wound healing and regeneration in porites: effects of the coral microbiome, algae and wound size coral tissue can be damaged by predation, storm-induced fragmentation, or abrasion from anchors. if not healed quickly,  lesions can be colonized by algae or deleterious microbes, which can jeopardize colony growth, reproduction, and survival. alternatively, there could be beneficial microorganisms that promote recovery and tissue regeneration. the coral microbiome likely plays a role in determining the fate of the wounds and consequences for the holobiont. this project investigates the relationship between microbial community composition and diversity and tissue regeneration by asking: 1) how do microbial communities respond after corals are wounded? and 2) how does the size of the wound and presence of macroalgae affect microbial community composition and influence regeneration? in the lagoon of mo’orea, french polynesia, we artificially wounded massive porites sp. by scraping away tissue and skeleton. we created different-sized wound treatments (sixteen 1 cm², four 4 cm², or one 1 cm²) but kept the total wound area fixed and crossed this with the presence or absence of the brown algae, turbinaria ornata. we characterized the regeneration process over 49 days, photographing weekly to measure regeneration rate, and collected a time series of mucus and tissue samples to analyze the microbial community using pacbio full-length 16s rrna sequencing. we predicted that larger wounds would take longer to heal, increasing the potential for putative pathogens (and algae) to invade the wound, resulting in a more diverse microbial community and delaying the healing process. in contrast, we predicted that smaller wounds would heal faster and have higher abundances of endozoicomonas, a coral mutualist. additionally, we predicted that the presence of turbinaria ornata would increase microbial diversity by introducing microbes unique to macroalgae, which would inhibit wound healing. our results (which are currently being analyzed) will enhance our understanding of the role of microbes in coral regeneration and resilience.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Wound healing and regeneration in Porites: effects of the coral microbiome, algae and wound size",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "6de60cc2-4af5-4f8b-bd8b-a2b0431be1b8"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00463",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
