          "search_text": "bastian bentlage causes and impacts of sedimentation on guam’s nearshore reefs coastal development and poor land-use practices are a major cause of erosion and terrestrial runoff that lead to sedimentation and the decline of near-shore coral reefs on small islands such as guam. species of massive porites are among the few corals able to tolerate turbid waters and sedimentation. my lab has studied the impacts of sedimentation on guam’s near-shore reefs using a combination of in situ transcriptomics, symbiont metabarcoding, and microbiome genomics, with the aim of elucidating the mechanisms that porites and its symbionts employ when dealing with sedimentation stress. in addition, microbiome surveys are ongoing to identify potential terrestrial sources of microbial agents and harmful genetic elements that may negatively impact near-shore reef health in guam. our data suggest that porites switch between energy generation pathways to facilitate survival when faced with sedimentation. for example, reduction of aerobic respiration-associated gene expression at sites with high chronic sedimentation suggests acclimation to hypoxic conditions.  chronic sedimentation stress was also associated with upregulation of genes with immune and cell-cell signaling functions, suggesting shifts in immune metabolism for survival. algal (symbiodiniacae) endosymbiont community compositions remained relatively stable across a sedimentation gradient, with caldocopium c15 dominating across sites and seasons. bacterial microbiome communities were dominated by coral-endosymbionts of the genus parendozoicomonas and increasing runoff/sedimentation during the wet-season was associated with increased microbiome diversity that may have its origins in soil microbiomes. interestingly, relative abundance of actinomycetes increased in porites microbiomes during the wet-season. actinomycetes are ubiquitous and a source of well-known source of antimicrobial compounds. our working hypothesis is that some actinomycetes are transported from degraded upstream soils to guam’s near-shore reefs as a result of erosion. degraded soils may promote the proliferation of antimicrobial resistance genes, which could negatively impact guam’s near-shore coral reef ecosystems exposed to such bacteria via runoff.",
