          "search_text": "lily borema comparing species trends and formal stock assessment outcomes using fishery-dependent time series in a micronesian coral-reef fishery over the past two decades, evidence of declining reef-fish stocks has been growing throughout the western pacific region, including micronesia, where commercial landings and subsistence catches have decreased through time and across human gradients. the high diversity of coral-reef fisheries coupled with the limited stock assessment data, make traditional management strategies hard to implement. data-poor fisheries models present a method for insightful stock assessment using limited fishery-dependent (fd) data, but their application to coral reef fisheries appears inconsistent due to the diversity of life histories. while unexpected findings have emerged when looking across species from snapshot data, approaches like length-based spawning potential ratio (lbspr) appear to be strengthened when analyzing the same species through time. this study examined trends in a diverse micronesian coral-reef fishery by evaluating two extensive fd snapshot surveys conducted a decade apart, representing a time period when the development of several fisheries policies began. fd data from pohnpei, micronesia, were used to examine temporal catch trends for 11 target species focusing on total landings, cpue, proportional contribution of species, and size distribution of species. in compliment, lbspr models estimated values of relative fishing mortality as well as spawning potential ratio for each species. results showed the change in relative fishing mortality and spr between years usually, but not always, corresponded to the observational trends. based on the observational trends, the 11 species were grouped according to their response to fishing pressure as persistent dominant, sensitive dominant, or apparently successfully managed species. lbspr results for these groups were mixed, highlighting that differences in life-history traits may constrain the use of lbspr frameworks even when comparing stocks through time. however, integrating fd metrics with lbspr outputs provided the basis for developing species-specific management recommendations for the 11 reef-fish species.",
