          "name": "Maria Andersen",
          "search_text": "maria andersen all strung out: microbial variation between wild populations and nursery-reared culture of the at-risk acropora virgata in guam (micronesia) between 2013 and 2021, a series of thermal anomalies, disease, and extreme tide events decimated 68% of acropora staghorn in guam, micronesia. in response, guam established two ocean nurseries in 2017 as biorepositories from surviving populations. while nursery corals show high success as measured by survival, growth, and fecundity, the transplanting process remains stressful. a rapid transition to a new environment may exceed a colony’s capacity to restructure its symbiotic communities. fragments grown on substrate must overcome different stressors than fragments suspended in nurseries and, in turn, may harbor different microbial communities. we hypothesize that transplanting corals from nurseries to substrate may trigger shifts in the microbiome that may correlate with phenotypic stress responses commonly observed post-outplaning acclimation. as a preliminary effort to document this shift, we examined coral-associated bacterial and symbiodiniaceae diversity, as well as genetic diversity, across wild and nursery-reared colonies of acropora virgata. we predicted colonies would share similar assemblages by site, but found the opposite. wild colonies, regardless of reef-site conditions, showed closer similarities due to high affiliation with endozoicomonas, a bacterium associated with healthy coral, and distinct symbiodiniaceae assemblages dominated by either durusdinium or cladocopium its2 profiles. in contrast, nursery fragments hosted higher symbiodiniaceae and bacterial diversity and had unique profiles specific to each nursery. despite appearing visually healthy, nursery colonies showed lower relative abundance of endozoicomonas across both nurseries. colonies reared in the piti nursery also showed higher affiliation with cladocopium rather than durusdinium compared to their wild sources. we speculate that reducing outplant mortality rates may, in part, rely on minimizing microbial shifts and suggest an intermediate nursery phase to expose colonies to substrate-associated microbes to facilitate stress recovery. these preliminary results show the importance of incorporating the role of microbial assemblages in restoration success to evaluate species-specific acclimation and coral-site compatibility.",
