      "search_text": "university of guam bastian bentlage lily borema maria andersen bastian bentlage causes and impacts of sedimentation on guam’s nearshore reefs coastal development and poor land-use practices are a major cause of erosion and terrestrial runoff that lead to sedimentation and the decline of near-shore coral reefs on small islands such as guam. species of massive porites are among the few corals able to tolerate turbid waters and sedimentation. my lab has studied the impacts of sedimentation on guam’s near-shore reefs using a combination of in situ transcriptomics, symbiont metabarcoding, and microbiome genomics, with the aim of elucidating the mechanisms that porites and its symbionts employ when dealing with sedimentation stress. in addition, microbiome surveys are ongoing to identify potential terrestrial sources of microbial agents and harmful genetic elements that may negatively impact near-shore reef health in guam. our data suggest that porites switch between energy generation pathways to facilitate survival when faced with sedimentation. for example, reduction of aerobic respiration-associated gene expression at sites with high chronic sedimentation suggests acclimation to hypoxic conditions.  chronic sedimentation stress was also associated with upregulation of genes with immune and cell-cell signaling functions, suggesting shifts in immune metabolism for survival. algal (symbiodiniacae) endosymbiont community compositions remained relatively stable across a sedimentation gradient, with caldocopium c15 dominating across sites and seasons. bacterial microbiome communities were dominated by coral-endosymbionts of the genus parendozoicomonas and increasing runoff/sedimentation during the wet-season was associated with increased microbiome diversity that may have its origins in soil microbiomes. interestingly, relative abundance of actinomycetes increased in porites microbiomes during the wet-season. actinomycetes are ubiquitous and a source of well-known source of antimicrobial compounds. our working hypothesis is that some actinomycetes are transported from degraded upstream soils to guam’s near-shore reefs as a result of erosion. degraded soils may promote the proliferation of antimicrobial resistance genes, which could negatively impact guam’s near-shore coral reef ecosystems exposed to such bacteria via runoff. lily borema comparing species trends and formal stock assessment outcomes using fishery-dependent time series in a micronesian coral-reef fishery over the past two decades, evidence of declining reef-fish stocks has been growing throughout the western pacific region, including micronesia, where commercial landings and subsistence catches have decreased through time and across human gradients. the high diversity of coral-reef fisheries coupled with the limited stock assessment data, make traditional management strategies hard to implement. data-poor fisheries models present a method for insightful stock assessment using limited fishery-dependent (fd) data, but their application to coral reef fisheries appears inconsistent due to the diversity of life histories. while unexpected findings have emerged when looking across species from snapshot data, approaches like length-based spawning potential ratio (lbspr) appear to be strengthened when analyzing the same species through time. this study examined trends in a diverse micronesian coral-reef fishery by evaluating two extensive fd snapshot surveys conducted a decade apart, representing a time period when the development of several fisheries policies began. fd data from pohnpei, micronesia, were used to examine temporal catch trends for 11 target species focusing on total landings, cpue, proportional contribution of species, and size distribution of species. in compliment, lbspr models estimated values of relative fishing mortality as well as spawning potential ratio for each species. results showed the change in relative fishing mortality and spr between years usually, but not always, corresponded to the observational trends. based on the observational trends, the 11 species were grouped according to their response to fishing pressure as persistent dominant, sensitive dominant, or apparently successfully managed species. lbspr results for these groups were mixed, highlighting that differences in life-history traits may constrain the use of lbspr frameworks even when comparing stocks through time. however, integrating fd metrics with lbspr outputs provided the basis for developing species-specific management recommendations for the 11 reef-fish species. maria andersen all strung out: microbial variation between wild populations and nursery-reared culture of the at-risk acropora virgata in guam (micronesia) between 2013 and 2021, a series of thermal anomalies, disease, and extreme tide events decimated 68% of acropora staghorn in guam, micronesia. in response, guam established two ocean nurseries in 2017 as biorepositories from surviving populations. while nursery corals show high success as measured by survival, growth, and fecundity, the transplanting process remains stressful. a rapid transition to a new environment may exceed a colony’s capacity to restructure its symbiotic communities. fragments grown on substrate must overcome different stressors than fragments suspended in nurseries and, in turn, may harbor different microbial communities. we hypothesize that transplanting corals from nurseries to substrate may trigger shifts in the microbiome that may correlate with phenotypic stress responses commonly observed post-outplaning acclimation. as a preliminary effort to document this shift, we examined coral-associated bacterial and symbiodiniaceae diversity, as well as genetic diversity, across wild and nursery-reared colonies of acropora virgata. we predicted colonies would share similar assemblages by site, but found the opposite. wild colonies, regardless of reef-site conditions, showed closer similarities due to high affiliation with endozoicomonas, a bacterium associated with healthy coral, and distinct symbiodiniaceae assemblages dominated by either durusdinium or cladocopium its2 profiles. in contrast, nursery fragments hosted higher symbiodiniaceae and bacterial diversity and had unique profiles specific to each nursery. despite appearing visually healthy, nursery colonies showed lower relative abundance of endozoicomonas across both nurseries. colonies reared in the piti nursery also showed higher affiliation with cladocopium rather than durusdinium compared to their wild sources. we speculate that reducing outplant mortality rates may, in part, rely on minimizing microbial shifts and suggest an intermediate nursery phase to expose colonies to substrate-associated microbes to facilitate stress recovery. these preliminary results show the importance of incorporating the role of microbial assemblages in restoration success to evaluate species-specific acclimation and coral-site compatibility.",
