          "name": "Melanie Dörr",
          "search_text": "melanie dörr engineering coral resilience: functional testing of probiotic bacterial isolates in aiptasia under thermal stress host-microbe symbioses form a central part of coral biology since corals rely on their associated symbionts for organismal functioning and survival. in the face of escalating environmental stressors, the targeted manipulation of microbial communities emerges as a potential intervention to enhance coral resilience. while probiotic inoculation has demonstrated potential to improve recovery and survival following coral bleaching, the mechanisms driving coral-bacteria interactions remain poorly understood. this is partly due to the complexity of coral microbiomes and a lack of standardized diagnostics to assess the impact of microbial modifications. here, we present a standardized screening framework using the cnidarian model aiptasia, integrating bacterial isolate libraries, phenotype pre-screening, and cbass acute thermal assays to identify beneficial bacterial strains. through this effort, we could identify bacterial isolates that increased aiptasia heat tolerance by up to 1.4 °c. one candidate, microbacterium ginsengisoli, is a robust probiotic candidate that consistently increased thermal tolerance across aiptasia strains, suggesting that some bacteria may exert beneficial effects across host boundaries. these findings establish a platform to dissect the molecular mechanisms underlying microbiome-mediated thermal tolerance. ultimately, this approach may contribute to developing targeted probiotic therapies aimed at enhancing coral resilience, thereby supporting ongoing efforts in coral reef conservation.",
