          "name": "Fiona Chong",
          "search_text": "fiona chong can we infer adaptive capacity from reef fish community size spectra? climate change is increasingly threatening the livelihoods of coastal communities reliant on subsistence fisheries. predicting reef fish population trajectories is thus central to adaptation actions in sustainable reef fisheries. given signals of thermal stress and fishing pressures are difficult to tease apart, examining reef fish size spectra across a range of conditions, from heavily fished to unfished remote reefs, can highlight reefs that may be capable of sustaining fisheries under a changing climate. we examined reef fish community size spectra from uvc (underwater visual census) data collected from 205 reefs  across indonesia and the philippines, to quantify how fish size spectra are affected by differential environmental factors. the effects of the environment on fish size spectra differ between trophic levels, providing additional insights into energy flow in the entire ecosystem. analyses of abiotic and human drivers of size spectra differences confirmed higher biomass and abudances of large fish in reefs furthest from human population centres, such as the lucipara group. destructive fishing and sedimentation were associated with fewer large-bodied fish and also lower total fish abundance. regions with fisheries co-management strategies, such as in the ambon-lease islands, might have higher adaptive capacities to climate change, serving as hope-spots in an overexploited seascape. through modelling how fish size spectra change under differential fishing and environmental pressures, we show that uvc data-generated size spectra can be a useful management tool to assess reef fishery condition, providing contemporary baselines for resource management where fishery catch data is scarce.",
