          "name": "Vanessa Neo",
          "search_text": "vanessa hui fen neo effectiveness of remote sensing and logger-derived sst in measuring reef scale coral cover changes over 30 years in zanzibar western reefs zanzibar reefs such as chumbe, bawe and changuu reefs are located in two separate marine protected areas (mpas) in the west of the island and have been affected by sea surface temperature (sst) anomalies. since the establishment of these mpas, coral cover monitoring has been consistently conducted by local academic institutes using line intercept transects (lit) method, making it possible to compare coral cover (cc) changes across 30 years. field observation data using lit method was also collected in april and november 2024 during and after the global coral bleaching event and included in this study. despite recognising the role of high sst in causing coral bleaching and mortality, little is known about the relationship between post-events cc changes and sst changes measured from satellites or in-situ loggers. hence, our study utilises statistical methods to compare between 30 years’ time series of cc changes to in-situ daily logger sst data and two similar satellite products, coral reef watch (crw) and climate change initiative (cci) 5km high-resolution daily sst data. similarities were found between time series of satellite sst products in all three reefs and between satellites. however, differences were found between time series of cc changes in all three reefs, regardless of whether they are in the same mpa or in a different mpa in close proximity. in addition, logger sst time series showed differences with satellite sst time series and cc time series changes within the same reef. the results of our study hints at difficulties of remote sensing sst methods to predict changes on a local reef scale. within local reefs, there are differences that appear standardized when relying on remote sensing measurements that are known to impact coral reefs. hence, caution should be taken when using either in-situ logger or remote sensing methods to determine reef changes.",
