          "name": "Nina Schiettekatte",
          "search_text": "nina schiettekatte linking structural complexity with biodiversity and ecosystem function coral reefs increasingly experience events of mass coral mortality, triggering a transition into modern reefs characterized by a decreased diversity and structural complexity. while some reef-associated organisms depend on living coral specifically, most rely on the complex habitat built by corals. reef-building corals are expected to continue declining in the near future and reefs may become less complex over time due to erosion. therefore, it is urgent to understand if and how these transitioning, less complex reefs will sustain diversity and ecosystem functions. we first  developed theory to hypothesize the relationship between biodiversity and rugosity and fractal dimension. rugosity (defined as surface area per planar area) should increase diversity as species richness is known to scale with area. fractal dimension (a measure of space filling) is expected to promote species diversity by dividing the available area into a wide variety of micro-habitats. we next tested these predictions using empirical fish community data and complexity metrics generated from digital elevation models on small (2x2m) to medium (8x8m) scales. finally, using bioenergetic modeling, we investigated the relationship between structural complexity and ecosystem functions. rugosity is expected to increase function and fractal dimension is expected to influence the size distribution of mobile animals, which has consequences on ecosystem functions mediated by fishes. improving the quantification of ecological functions constitutes an important step towards the efficient monitoring and management of coral reefs. rooted in theory, our results could help understand what future, less complex reefs would look like in terms of biodiversity and ecosystem functioning.",
