          "search_text": "viviana brambilla ecological niche construction in coral reefs: linking coral-built structure, adult abundance, and recruitment ecological niche construction occurs when organisms modify environments in ways that enhance their own or other organisms’ fitness. as reef ecosystem engineers, scleractinian corals inherently change the reef with their skeletons, modifying local environmental conditions both directly, defining habitat 3d structure, and indirectly, affecting environmental conditions such as flow and light regimes with their shapes. here, we examine scleractinian corals as ecological niche constructors by testing how coral presence and coral-built structures influence the coral community at early life stages. to estimate complexity in local habitats and reefs, we used structure-from-motion algorithms in both manipulative and observational studies. in a field experiment, artificial tiles placed along a gradient of local coral-built structural complexity showed a 15.7% increase in coral settler presence across the complexity range considered, indicating that greater structural complexity facilitates larval settlement. in an observational study encompassing 9 reef sites (~120 m2 each) where adult and juvenile corals were mapped in the reef through time, juvenile coral occurrence was more strongly influenced by adult coral abundance than by reef structural complexity. together, these findings suggest that while reef structures may promote settlement, they do not necessarily ensure survival into later stages, as juvenile occurrence in the field correlates more closely with adult abundance. nonetheless, corals meet a key criterion of ecological niche construction, as their presence creates conditions that facilitate recruitment, directly and indirectly interacting with other biotic factors that influence local coral populations persistence. these findings show how combining digital imagery with niche construction theory could improve our understanding of coral community dynamics.",
