      "distance_km": 13891.3,
      "search_text": "university of maryland joanne salzer joanne salzer identifying the symbiont’s role in palytoxin production from aquarium zoanthids: implications for human health palytoxin is one of the most potent non-protein marine toxins known, posing significant risks to human health. despite its danger, the sale of palytoxin-producing corals (zoanthids) is completely unregulated. additionally, the biosynthetic origin of palytoxin remains unresolved–whether it is produced by the coral host, a dinoflagellate symbiont, or the holobiont. this study investigated correlations between toxin production and host-symbiont genomics from aquarium-sourced zoanthids, hypothesizing that the symbiotic dinoflagellate from the cladocopium genus has a primary role in palytoxin synthesis. zoanthid colonies (n35) were acquired from commercial aquarium stores and residential aquariums, and polyps were sampled and screened for palytoxin using liquid chromatography-mass spectrometry (lcms). dna was extracted from remaining tissue for sanger sequencing targeting the its and 16s regions to identify the symbiont and host respectively. lcms methods detected palytoxin in 49% of all zoanthid samples, and all but one of the toxin-positive colonies fell within or above the range associated with human poisoning cases (400–7,000 μg/g tissue). genomic analysis revealed that palytoxin is detected in multiple zoanthid clades, all harboring cladocopium symbionts. correlation of palytoxin concentration with cladocopium taxonomy revealed a strain-specificity, providing further evidence for cladocopium’s role in palytoxin production. these findings confirm that aquarium-traded zoanthids can harbor dangerously high levels of palytoxin. understanding the symbiont’s role in palytoxin synthesis can inform the aquarium industry, which currently has no regulation in place for selling these harmful organisms to aquarium hobbyists. gaining insight on the symbiont’s role in toxin synthesis will also further toxin-producing dinoflagellate research, which is an understudied field despite dinoflagellates’ large presence in aquatic ecosystems.",
