          "name": "Guilherme Longo",
          "search_text": "guilherme longo using geographic comparisons to predict the future of reefs global changes are rapidly reshaping reef ecosystems, by eroding coral cover and driving shifts in species distributions. using space-for-time substitution approach and integrating multiple modeling and experimental frameworks we projected how corals, reef fish communities, and their trophic interactions will respond to future warming across the western atlantic. we estimated current and future probabilities of coral occurrence, cover, and bleaching along the brazilian coast and found that current coral occurrence probabilities peak in tropical reefs but is projected to decline sharply in this region while expanding toward subtropical latitudes. these shifts threaten endemic and range-restricted species such as mussismilia braziliensis and m. harttii, whereas more generalist taxa (siderastrea, porites, millepora) are likely to expand southward. we further modeled climate-driven changes in trophic interactions of reef fishes between 34°n and 27°s in the western atlantic. warming is expected to reduce key trophic processes (herbivory and invertivory) in tropical reefs, reinforcing algal dominance and weakening critical trophic pathways. in contrast, tropicalization of feeding intensity is expected primarily in the northern hemisphere (us east coast), with tropical herbivores expanding poleward. a large-scale standardized experiment spanning 115° of latitude in the americas corroborates these projections. predators exert stronger consumption and greater impacts on prey biomass and assemblage structure in warmer tropical waters, providing robust evidence for temperature-dependent interaction strength. finally, ecosystem modeling of a near-pristine atoll food web revealed substantial declines in biomass (1–44%), reduced trophic transfer efficiency, shorter biomass residence times, and increased dependence on pelagic subsidies under future warming. collectively, these geographic comparisons show warming impacts on the population, community and ecosystem levels, with tropical reefs trending toward structural simplification and functional erosion, while subtropical reefs may serve as limited refugia. anticipating these shifts is essential for designing ecological corridors, prioritizing conservation, and guiding adaptive management in a warming ocean.",
