          "name": "Juntong Hu",
          "search_text": "juntong hu metatranscriptomics reveals the dynamics of microbial community composition and functions of porites lutea in response to thermal stress reef-building corals are complex holobionts composed of the cnidarian host and a diverse assemblage of prokaryotic and microeukaryotic symbionts. while most studies of the coral microbiome have focused on the 1–3 mm tissue layer, the largely unexplored coral skeleton harbors a highly structured microbial community shaped by its unique physicochemical environment. beneath the tissue resides the endolithic alga ostreobium, which forms conspicuous green bands within massive corals and functions as a primary producer with the potential to support the coral host during stress. to investigate the composition, function, and interactions of the microbial community within the porites lutea holobiont, we conducted a thermal bleaching–recovery experiment and performed metatranscriptomic analyses on three distinct anatomical compartments: coral tissue, the underlying ostreobium layer, and the deeper skeleton. ribosomal rna analyses revealed that the microbial community of partially recovered corals closely resembled that of healthy colonies. de novo assembly of ostreobium 16s rrna sequences uncovered high phylogenetic diversity and dynamic compositional shifts of this endolithic alga throughout the thermal stress experiment. preliminary rnaseq results indicate compartment-specific transcriptional activity, with coral health condition driving microbiome community diversity in both the tissue and the deeper skeleton. ongoing mrna analyses focus on key metabolic pathways–including carbon fixation, photosynthesis, sugar transport, and nitrogen and sulfur metabolism–to elucidate the functional roles and interactions among holobiont members during bleaching and recovery. this study represents the first metatranscriptomic comparison across coral compartments, providing unprecedented insights into the community and functional dynamics of the coral holobiont under thermal stress. ultimately, our findings will establish a foundation for understanding the metabolic integration that supports coral resilience, with important implications for coral reef preservation in a warming ocean.",
