          "name": "Yohan Louis",
          "search_text": "yohan louis thermal preconditioning modulates the transcriptomic response of pocillopora damicornis to acute heat stress thermal preconditioning has been demonstrated to enhance bleaching tolerance in tropical reef-building corals, offering potential applications for coral restoration and assisted evolution strategies. although experimental evidence supports the efficacy of thermal preconditioning in increasing tolerance in corals, the molecular mechanisms underpinning this process remains poorly understood. limited investigations have been conducted on the molecular basis, with only few coral species studied. therefore, in this study, colonies of pocillopora damicornis were subjected to thermal preconditioning through controlled exposure to sub-lethal temperature (28 °c, for 2 weeks), simulating conditions that induce increase tolerance in previous studies. non-preconditioned colonies were maintained under lower stable conditions (25 °c). all experimental groups were then subjected to a heat stress of 33 °c. rna was extracted from coral samples and rna sequencing was employed to determine the transcriptome-wide changes in gene expression. bioinformatics analyses were conducted to identify differentially expressed genes, pathways, and functional categories. the transcriptomic analysis revealed significant differences in gene expression profiles between preconditioned and non-preconditioned colonies. following preconditioning, preconditioned corals exhibited higher expression of genes related to antioxidant response and dna repair. during heat stress, preconditioned colonies showed delayed bleaching compared to non-preconditioned colonies. preconditioned colonies showed increased expression of mitochondrial genes associated with oxidative phosphorylation, energy production, antioxidant defense, homeostasis, and efferocytosis. overall, these responses suggest that preconditioned corals activate pathways that sustain energy balance and limit oxidative damage during thermal stress. the non-preconditioned colonies displayed a less pronounced stress responsive transcriptomic signature, suggesting reduced preparedness for thermal stress. these findings provide a comprehensive understanding of the molecular dynamics governing thermal preconditioning in coral. overall, this investigation will help bridge the knowledge gap surrounding the molecular mechanism of thermal preconditioning and provide further insights for the practical implementation of this technique in restoration strategies.",
