          "name": "Simone Montano",
          "search_text": "simone montano monumental corals as key of climate resilience: the map the giants initiative coral reefs are under unprecedented global threats from multiple stressors. although most corals cannot survive the climate change-associated conditions, some coral colonies have survived and grown for centuries, reaching remarkable sizes. studying such giant colonies is therefore likely to yield invaluable biological, ecological, and paleo-climatic information. still, there is limited knowledge on their abundance and distribution due to their relative rarity. to fill this gap, we have launched “map the giants”, a citizen science research initiative aimed at engaging the public in reporting coral colonies exceeding five meters in linear length. the project received almost 200 reports from 23 countries. most of these belong to porites and pavona genera including colonies over 30m in their longest 2d linear length. we provide here extensive arguments supporting the importance of preserving these ancient corals, which may hold keys to resilience against global climate change. in particular, focusing on 85 large coral colonies with sizes varying between 5 and >20m in their maximum dimension, we investigated whether their extended survival stems from intrinsic resilience to thermal stress, or from low exposure to heatwaves. for that, we used historical data to assess the exposure of giant coral colonies to bleaching-triggering conditions in the last 4 decades. most colonies never experienced such conditions within the observational time window. however, 13 giant colonies were exposed to severe heat stress multiple times, and six of them significantly more often than in control localities. these results support both hypotheses. on the one hand, environmental protection emerges as a major factor favoring the survival of giant corals. on the other hand, the few giant coral colonies that survived through high thermal stress might have exceptional features worth further, in-depth investigation. the project highlights the potential of citizen science to uncover rare ecological data while fostering public engagement.",
