      "search_text": "university of mississippi tamar goulet tamar goulet can coral chimersim play an ecological engineering role in combatting the effects of climate change? survival of the fittest assumes that, given genetic variability, some individuals of a given species will fare better than other individuals in the face of challenges, be it escaping an enemy, or surviving adverse environmental conditions. chimeric individuals, entities composed of two or more genotypes of one species, offer a twist and a different dimension to who may be the fittest. here we present the current state of chimeric knowledge in cnidarians, where nuances exist in the spatial distribution of the genotypes within a chimera, representing a panorama of ecological and conservation ramifications. we discuss the theoretical framework of five different states of spatial chimerism, alongside current data on spatial distribution of genotypes in coral chimeras.  chimerism can be sectorial, whereby the genotypes co-exist, but each in their own zone.  alternatively, the genotypes within the chimera may be mixed, either as a mosaic, evenly distributed, multi-chimerism or micro-chimerism. with the spatial distribution of genotypes, cnidarian chimerism may affect interpretation of data in cnidarian population studies. likewise, depending on the spatial arrangements of genotypes within a chimeric coral, fragmenting such a coral, either for experimental or conservation efforts, may result in the formation of fragments that are genotypically different, either keeping the within-species genetic variants, or genetic combinations that may be less fitted to current or future environmental conditions. however, whether all partners within a chimera have equal access to the germ line remains unresolved. we conclude by highlighting how chimerism can be harnessed for cnidarian conservation efforts, primarily for coral reef restoration.",
