          "name": "Daigo Kishi",
          "search_text": "daigo kishi categorization and taxonomic study for morphological polymorphism of favites valenciennesii favites valenciennesii belongs to the family merulinidae and is a reef-building coral widely distributed from tropical to temperate regions in the indo-west pacific ocean. the colony shape is massive, and individual characteristics include polygonal corallite ranging from eight to 15 mm in diameter. a distinct feature is the “groove” that forms between individuals, which makes us easy to distinguish from others in the same genus. fukami et al. (2009) reported two morphological types within this species: the “separation type”, which individuals are clearly separated, and the “adhesion type”, which individuals are joined together. however, no further morphological differences were found between these two types, even examination of skeletal microstructures (huang et al. 2014).\r\nin this study, we collected 41 samples of this species from temperate to subtropical regions in japan. we quantified morphological data from their skeletal specimens to clarify species boundaries among these morphological types by integrating quantitative morphological and molecular phylogenetic analyses.\r\nfor morphological analysis, we measured 11 morphological features and performed variable selection using the random forest method prior to conducting principal multivariate analysis. in the molecular phylogenetic analysis, we used nuclear internal transcribed spacers (itss) of ribosomal rna and mitochondrial intergenic region (igr) between coi and trna as markers. \r\nthe morphological analysis revealed four morphologically distinct types overall, with both separation and adhesion types divided into two more types. moreover, the molecular phylogenetic analysis indicated that the two adhesion types formed clades within the genus favites, while the two separation types formed clades within the genus dipsastraea. our results suggest that at least three distinct species are concealed within the polymorphism of f. valenciennesii.",
