          "name": "Michael Lesser",
          "search_text": "michael lesser spatial and temporal studies of mesophotic reef sponges in the caribbean understanding the ecology of mesophotic coral ecosystems (mces; 30-150 m), and their functional relationship within coral reef ecosystems, is an essential component of advancing the field. recently, concerns regarding the limitations of studies on mces, including shortcomings on geographic range, taxa studied, environmental variability, temporal sampling and depth range, have been expressed. for over two decades we have repeatedly sampled mce communities at multiple locations within the caribbean basin from shallow reefs to lower mce depths (~15-100 m) using a range of approaches including biodiversity and ecological assessments, population genetics, environmental measurements and trophic dynamics on both sponges and corals. we argue that sponges are a “keystone” species on mces, especially at lower mce depths (>60 m) below which a consistent faunal break within the caribbean basin, and on mces elsewhere, occurs. we will present a synopsis of our studies on depth-dependent genetic structure in adult coral populations at sites hundreds of kilometers apart, as well as site, depth and seasonal differences in particulate and dissolved organic food that effect mce sponge trophic ecology, growth and densities. we also show significant changes in mce communities subsequent to biological invasions (i.e., lionfish), based on temporal sampling. many of our findings have been generally supported by other investigators in the caribbean, red sea, and indo-pacific. there are also important and interesting exceptions in mce structure and function largely driven by differences in oceanography, or geomorphology and downwelling irradiances, that help inform us on the variability observed on contemporary tropical mces. finally, using the available data, and some hand waving, we will take a large scale, island-wide, perspective on the importance of sponges on the trophic ecology of mces from the cayman islands, and by extension wherever sponges are a dominant component of marine benthic communities.",
