          "search_text": "robert mason reef-scale variation in coral larval supply and settlement significant gains in coral reef health are achievable through setting sustainable limits on boat anchoring larval dispersal in the water column and connectivity between reefs helps to drive benthic assemblage dynamics and recovery in coral reef ecosystems. connectivity modelling is commonly used to understand this phenomenon. we performed field experiments to investigate the spatial patterns and rates of coral larval arrival and settlement at a cluster of reefs in the great barrier reef, and then used these field data to validate a high-resolution hydrodynamic and connectivity model (∼300 m resolution). at the reef cluster level, interannual variations in larval supply predicted by hydrodynamic and connectivity modelling were well-correlated with observed interannual variations in larval settlement. at the within-reef scale, prediction was much more challenging, with connectivity modelling demonstrating only a fair accuracy at replicating observed spatial patterns of larval arrival and settlement. thus, dispersal patterns at scales relevant to reef cluster metapopulation dynamics (annual-to-interannual temporal scale and 1–10’s km spatial scale) appeared to be well resolved by modelling, whilst spatial scales relevant to current restoration interventions (>1/10 ha to <10 ha) were fairly-predicted for settled larvae but poorly-predicted for planktonic larvae. additional sampling highlighted fine temporal resolution variations in larval supply that increased the difficulty of achieving good model-to-observation concordance for planktonic larvae. our findings demonstrate the need for decision support frameworks that appropriately handle potential error in connectivity model predictions when informing marine management and restoration planning. boat anchoring is a common threatening process at coral reefs, but has received comparatively little attention in the field of coral reef science. to address difficulties in quantifying the impact of interventions to alleviate this threat, we developed a model of coral populations that simulated the long-term effects of anchor damage. the model facilitated the estimation of the carrying capacity of anchoring for coral communities, which we define as the amount of anchoring a reef can sustain by virtue of its inherent recovery ability. comparing four hypothetical coral assemblages, each starting at a range of levels of coral cover, the carrying capacity of anchoring from small to medium-sized recreational vessels ranged between 0 and 3.1 anchor strikes per hectare per day. we then used the model, combined with field datasets of anchoring damage, to perform a case study at two groups of islands on the great barrier reef. we modelled the benefits of mitigating anchoring damage under regimes of coral bleaching expected for four climate scenarios. partially mitigating a mild level of anchor damage (1.17 strikes per hectare per day) resulted in median improvements in reef health of 2.6–7.7 % (absolute) coral cover under the rcp2.6 climate scenario. this is a very notable gain, particularly as starting anchoring incidence was relatively low and as full mitigation was not applied. benefits varied over time and were dependent on the choice of atmosphere-ocean general circulation model. our results show that anchoring damage presents an opportunity for designing local interventions that can significant improvements in reef health.",
