          "name": "Kiah Gongaware",
          "search_text": "charles gongaware symbiotic synergy: identifying microbial traits that promote algal growth and coral health the scientific understanding of coral physiology has expanded to encompass the coral holobiont, including the coral-algal symbiosis. this encompasses the coral host and its diverse microbial associates, including the algal symbionts (family symbiodiniaceae), bacteria, archaea, fungi, viruses, and microeukaryotes. within this framework, the role of bacteria in supporting algal growth, survival, and function remains largely speculative. although some studies suggest that these symbionts rely on bacterial partners, experimental evidence confirming whether such relationships are essential or facultative remains limited. microbial partners may influence nutrient cycling, thermal tolerance, or can be leveraged in microbiome engineering, such as probiotic development to enhance coral resilience under disease stress. axenic cultures of coral-associated symbiodiniaceae are rarely available due to the difficulty of removing bacteria without compromising algal viability. cultures of symbiodinium, breviolum, and durusdinium are being developed using multistep antibiotic treatments. pilot trials indicate substantial reductions in bacterial load following a single exposure, with algal cells remaining viable and photosynthetically active. refinements included sequential treatments in liquid and solid media, followed by recovery in antibiotic-free conditions. bacterial presence will be screened for via plating, pcr, and scanning electron microscopy (sem). using axenic cultures, bacterial isolates derived from parent cultures and corals will be introduced individually and in groups to evaluate their impact on algal growth and physiology. functional assays will screen isolates for traits relevant to symbiosis, including nitrogen fixation, phosphate solubilization, phytohormone production, siderophore activity, and vitamin biosynthesis. confocal, sem, and transmission electron microscopy (tem) imaging will visualize bacterial-algal interactions, distinguishing surface associations from potential internal colonization. it is hypothesized that isolates expressing traits that improve nutrient availability or promote algal cell growth will enhance algal performance and form closer associations with host cells. understanding these microbial contributions to symbiodiniaceae function could reshape approaches to coral restoration, holobiont manipulation, and microbial intervention strategies.",
