          "name": "Laura Fiegel",
          "search_text": "laura fiegel advances in standardized ecotoxicological testing with scleractinian coral larvae applications of the bacteria-derived pigment cycloprodigiosin as settlement cue for scleractinian coral larvae coral reefs face substantial ecosystem shifts under increasing anthropogenic threats including global (i.e., climate change) and local stressors. personal care products and (untreated) wastewater, for example, may be released into coastal environments and may harm corals on a local level. standardized test guidelines would be needed to reliably assess these effects. current methods focus on the adult corals; however, coral larvae might be more sensitive to various chemical stressors. larval settlement is a crucial developmental step. if this process is inhibited, the swimming larvae will certainly die, which could have far-reaching consequences for the propagation of corals resulting in limited recruitment of juvenile corals and large-scale reef degradation in the long term. \r\nthis study refines the experimental design for a standardized toxicity test on coral larvae, evaluating the larval endpoints settlement and mortality, first introduced by miller and colleagues in 2022. to improve variabilities of recovery rates, the experiments here were conducted in glass containers, improving recovery rates of benzophenone-3 (bp-3) and diuron (dcmu), which would allow to refer the determined ec50 or lc50 values to the nominal test concentrations. preliminary results already indicated that larval settlement may be a sensitive endpoint. however, implementing a settlement endpoint is challenging, as the application of biological settlement cues tend to (1) trigger variable settlement responses in different coral species and (2) the additional biological components in the test vessels may interact with the test substances. the recently described chemical settlement cue cycloprodigiosin reduces the variability in the settlement endpoint and prevents unintended interactions between the biological cue and the target substance. the latter improvements in the experimental design will enable further testing of the effects on coral larvae and develop holistic bioassays in a standardized way. the ongoing climate change is leading to frequent mass bleaching events, representing the greatest threat to tropical coral reefs today. sexually reproduction in scleractinian corals is a major driver of natural coral reef adaptation towards changing environmental conditions. a crucial and still not fully understood step in the life cycle of corals is the transformation of mobile larvae into sessile recruits, which is referred to coral larval settlement. recently, the cca-associated bacteria-derived pigment cycloprodigiosin (cypro) was identified as potent settlement inducing compound for multiple scleractinian coral species of the genera acropora, leptastrea, pocillopora and favia. this discovery offers the opportunity to further study (1) coral larvae settlement processes from a mechanistical perspective. furthermore, due to its biochemical properties it has (2) a potential application as tool for coral restoration. in addition, a controllable multispecies settlement cue with low variability, such as cypro, (3) could improve coral larvae ecotoxicological studies involving settlement as endpoint. in our icrs contribution, we will highlight the newest developments in these three areas of application for the coral larvae settlement cue cypro.",
