      "search_text": "university of otago kathryn scafidi robin hartley thomas johnstone kathryn scafidi a series of unfortunate events: the sequence of disturbances affects the recovery potential of a south pacific coral reef natural disturbances have long affected coral reefs and favored low coral cover from which recovery often is slow. until recently, the reefs of moorea, french polynesia, have provided an exception to this trend as their coral communities have recovered from sequential disturbances over the last 50 years. early in 2019, the north shore fore reef at 10-m depth had ~ 75% coral cover, but later that year it was decimated by bleaching to leave 17% coral cover by august 2020 and countless dead-in-place colonies of pocillopora spp. by 2025, coral recovery had not begun because of the chronology of decay affecting dead pocillopora skeletons. we combine ecological analyses of dead pocillopora with high-resolution microscopy of aragonite skeletal structure to understand the fate of these dead corals. dead pocillopora initially were resilient to breakage and were colonized by macroalgae and a unique assemblage of invertebrates, although coral recruits were deterred. over 2022–2023, hollowed-out branches of dead pocillopora were detected. and microscopic inspection showed that hollowing occurred through bioerosion of dead branches that were scaffolded by encrusting taxa and entrapped sediments. following the last major disturbance by cots and a cyclone in 2010, corals in moorea rapidly recovered over 2011–2019; in contrast, recovery from bleaching in 2019 has not occurred over 2019–2025 because of the retention of dead corals and a new cots outbreak, that have created the opportunity for erosion to slowly hollow and weaken dead pocillopora branches. this study shows how the temporal structuring of bleaching-related events can create ecological surprises that introduce unpredictable variability in recovery processes. robin hartley reefs of hope: what coral restoration can learn from forest phenotyping. coral reefs are among the most biodiverse ecosystems on earth, yet are experiencing rapid declines driven by anthropogenic climate change. conventional restoration methods, such as propagation in nurseries and outplanting, lack resilience against climate-driven impacts. some coral genotypes, particularly those inhabiting shallow “hot pockets”, display naturally high thermal tolerance, representing a critical genetic resource. however, these environments are becoming lethal, and there are currently no systematic or scalable methods readily available to identify thermally tolerant corals or the habitats that foster their adaptation.\r\n\r\ncoral phenotyping, the identification and measurement of desirable traits across corals and populations to understand their responses to environmental conditions, is an emerging field. forest phenotyping, by comparison, has developed robust in-situ and multi-scale workflows using advanced sensors to quantify how tree genotypes express traits in different environments and silvicultural regimes. parallels between forestry management and coral restoration suggest strong potential for cross-pollination of methods.\r\n\r\nthis study reviews current coral phenotyping approaches, with particular emphasis on remote and proximal sensing, and compares them with state-of-the-art forest phenotyping practices. a substantial gap persists in coral reef management, where remote sensing tools, such as uavs, remain underutilised, and efforts largely focus on satellite products and in-water photogrammetry. this work aims to accelerate the adoption of scalable tools that can improve decision-making and prioritisation in coral restoration under accelerating climate pressures. \r\n\r\nthe review reveals a rich suite of forestry methodologies, from uav-based structural metrics to hyperspectral trait mapping, that could be adapted to coral reefs with minimal modification. these approaches offer pathways toward more efficient, larger-scale reef management by enabling the identification of thermally resilient coral populations. the emerging reefs of hope ocean decade action framework aligns strongly with this potential, integrating advanced sensing, stress-testing and genetic tools to identify resilient coral populations and optimise restoration investments. thomas johnstone temporal variations in the foraging behaviour of the coral reef mesopredator pseudochromis fuscus mesopredators play a vital role in marine food webs and are an important link between trophic levels. on coral reefs, small carnivorous fish are important mesopredators and exert strong, top down pressure regulating prey communities. however, despite this importance, few studies have directly examined the foraging ecology of small, coral reef meso-predators. to better understand this, we examined the foraging behaviour of pseudochromis fuscus (the dusky dottyback) over three ecologically important temporal periods (the full moon, coral spawning, and the new moon) at lizard island on the great barrier reef, australia. we conducted a total of 56 one hour observations, each focusing on the number of strikes and successful predation attempts directed at coral reef fish recruits and invertebrates. contrary to previous research, we did not observe a successful capture of a coral reef fish recruit, despite p. fuscus displaying a similar strike rate to previously published research. we observed that  strikes directed at invertebrates were common, and consistent across all three temporal periods, and conclude that invertebrates therefore constitute a stable food source. interestingly, there were significantly fewer attempted strikes on coral reef fish recruits during the coral spawning period, likely due to the influx of lipid-rich food. we also observed little difference in foraging behaviour between p. fuscus colour morphs, sizes, or at different times of day. these findings reveal that the foraging behaviour of p. fuscus can be highly variable temporally and may play less of a role in the mortality rates of newly, settled coral reef fish than previously thought. quantifying these interactions is crucial for understanding the ecological interactions of coral reef ecosystems, especially in light of significant environmental change.",
