          "search_text": "david e. carrasco rivera landscape changes in coral reef watersheds worldwide and their influence on sediment pollution land-based sediment pollution is one of the primary local stressors contributing to coral reef decline worldwide. sediment pollution alters chemical, physical, biological, and ecological processes in reef systems, and interacts synergistically with other local and global disturbances. managing sediment pollution is therefore essential for enhancing reef health and supporting their recovery ability after disturbance events. recent international initiatives have increasingly highlighted the urgency for managing and reducing local pollution to ensure the long-term persistence and resilience of coral reefs in the changing climate. however, there are major gaps in knowledge and information that hinder the ability to meet these targets, particularly in data-poor regions that host most of the world’s coral reefs. these gaps include limited understanding of how specific land-use land-cover (lulc) changes drive sediment pollution, their trajectories over time, and their spatial relationship with and subsequent implications for coral reefs. these gaps result in managers not having the appropriate spatially and temporally-explicit information, and tools to design and implement the integrated land-sea management required to safeguard coral reefs from land-based sediment pollution.\r\n\r\nthis study presents a global, watershed-level assessment of lulc changes and associated sediment export from over 21,000 watersheds draining into coral reef habitats from 2000 to 2020. using harmonized global datasets and spatially explicit sediment load modelling, we quantify spatial and temporal trends in watershed sediment export and identify regions where landscape transitions (i.e., deforestation, agricultural expansion, and urbanization) pose the greatest threats to these ecosystems. the products of this study provide information on sediment export dynamics and highlight priority areas where targeted land-based management can substantially reduce sediment pollution exposure to coral reefs. additionally, by integrating lulc trajectories with sediment export modelling at global, yet locally applicable scales, this research provides critical spatial and temporal information to guide integrated land-sea management.",
