      "distance_km": 8726.3,
      "search_text": "university of queensland harris wk heng harris wei-khang heng embracing new faces of conservation by integrating cultural and scientific practices in nature storytelling culture is often understood through tradition, ancestry, and place. yet culture evolves continuously, shaped by science, technology, and the lived experiences of people responding to environmental change. however, modern conservation knowledge is often excluded from this cultural narrative. in the wake of the 2024 global mass coral bleaching event, we created is this love, mama?, an illustrated storybook that translates this ecological crisis into an emotionally resonant narrative for children, families, and non-scientists. grounded in real field data from malaysian coral reefs, the story integrates accurate ecological information, local biodiversity, and conservation practices, while weaving in cultural and emotional themes relatable to local audiences. the development process involved iterative feedback from malaysian ngos, coral reef scientists, conservationists, illustrators, and storytellers to ensure biological accuracy while maintaining emotional resonance. through themes of curiosity, loss, and resilience, the story also depicts simple reef-supporting behaviours, such as debris removal and coral monitoring, modelling conservation as something relatable rather than remote. by portraying conservationists as community members rather than distant heroes, the story reframes their work as part of a living cultural identity. the storybook will be made freely available for digital download to extend its reach while minimising material footprint. we propose that integrating scientific practice into nature storytelling allows it to enter cultural memory, shaping how modern communities understand and value their relationship with the natural world. in a time when reef decline is accelerating faster than public awareness, storytelling offers a pathway to reach diverse audiences whose identities extend beyond traditional cultural boundaries. positioning conservation as a shared cultural practice through storytelling may be essential for empowering the next generation of reef guardians in malaysia and beyond, helping them recognise their responsibility at a moment when the future of coral reefs is being shaped in real time.",
      "country_code": "MY",
      "country": "Malaysia",
      "connection_count": 1
