          "name": "Kris Jypson Esturas",
          "search_text": "kris jypson esturas national coral reef restoration database to support monitoring, evaluation and policy implementation in the philippines designing climate-smart protected areas based on climate adaptation hotspots using the coral triangle as case study coral reef restoration has been increasingly used to rehabilitate degraded reefs in the philippines, yet there is no national standardised system to track, evaluate, and learn from these projects. we updated and refined an existing catalog of restoration efforts to make it accessible to different stakeholders and to support governance integration. we then created a set of adaptive management criteria, based on the conservation standards and the environmental governance literature, to evaluate restoration projects in the database. our analysis revealed three key gaps: 1) project objectives for restoration did not fully align with broader threat contexts; 2) permitting frameworks lagged behind the emerging science of restoration; and,  3) database entries showed limited completeness for monitoring, evaluation, and learning (mel) reporting. given these gaps, we recommend pathways for the database and the adaptive management criteria to contribute to the development of a mel framework for coral restoration to strengthen regulation, improve documentation, and guide policy implementation. this initiative aims to support the philippine government in systematically tracking coral restoration projects, ensuring compliance with best practices and advancing national reporting towards target 2 of the kunming-montreal global biodiversity framework (restore 30% of degraded ecosystems by 2030). by bridging data gaps, standardising permitting processes, and linking these to monitoring, this effort will contribute to more effective, transparent and science-driven coral restoration in the country. climate change continues to accelerate biodiversity loss worldwide. conservation planning across ecosystems has largely focused on protecting climate refugia–areas buffered from climate impacts–to ensure that biodiversity persists long-term. by contrast, protecting climate adaptation hotspots–areas more exposed to climate change but potentially harbouring stress-adapted populations–remains limited yet offer a complementary approach to enhancing ecosystem resilience against climate change. using coral reefs in the coral triangle as a case study, we developed an approach to incorporate adaptation hotspots into the design of protected area systems using chronic- and acute-exposure climate metrics from monthly and high-resolution daily sea surface temperature projections. we also devised two ways to combine climate refugia and adaptation hotspots into a single protected areas design to ensure climate representativity. our findings suggest that an approach based on climate representativity could be more cost-effective, requiring less area for protection while meeting representation targets compared to prioritising either adaptation hotspots or climate refugia alone. our framework could be applied to other coastal and marine ecosystems. advancing the approaches developed here may require transdisciplinary research on evolutionary adaptation of different ecosystems to different climate stressors to better integrate climate adaptation hotspots in the design of climate-smart protected area networks.",
