          "name": "Francesca Necci",
          "search_text": "francesca necci coral-sponge joint transplantation enhances restoration of degraded benthic communities the current global rate of biodiversity loss and habitat degradation calls for innovative approaches to ecosystem restoration. despite receiving growing attention, multispecies approaches to habitat restoration remain poorly explored in marine environments. this study was carried out in the porto cesareo marine protected area (italy), where illegal harvesting of endolithic date mussels caused dismantling of shallow rocky bottoms and the development of extensive barren grounds.\r\nthe aim was to improve restoration outcomes by transplanting propagules of two species that naturally co-occur in the same habitat: the mediterranean reef-building coral cladocora caespitosa and the yellow massive sponge aplysina aerophoba. we established three experimental treatments: coral fragments transplanted alongside sponge fragments (multispecies), coral fragments transplanted in pairs (coral, monospecific), and sponge fragments transplanted in pairs (sponge, monospecific). we tested the hypothesis that the joint transplantation of coral fragments with their co-occurring sponge could enhance the survival and growth performance of both species.\r\nfor a. aerophoba, mono-species transplants exhibited an 84% survival rate, while survival in multispecies treatments was 64%. after one year, the mean surface area increased from comparable initial values to 15.8 ± 1.0 cm² in the mono-species treatment and 23.2 ± 3.2 cm² in the multispecies treatment, with trajectories diverging significantly between treatments (p < 0.05). \r\nfor c. caespitosa, survival rates were comparable between mono-species (65%) and multispecies (63%) treatments. after one year, the mean number of living corallites reached 14 ± 2 in mono-species transplants and 19 ± 2 in multispecies assemblages, resulting in significantly different final measurements between treatments.\r\nour findings are consistent with the initial hypothesis that both species benefit from being transplanted together showing growth performance in multispecies assemblages. these results provide new insights into the advantages of incorporating positive ecological interactions into restoration strategies, thereby supporting more effective ecosystem recovery.",
