          "name": "Henry Arbaugh",
          "search_text": "henry arbaugh assessment of sedimentary microplastics in coral reefs of the us virgin islands using sediment traps given the negative impact of microplastics (mp) on corals, understanding the distribution and accumulation rate of mp in coral reef sediments is important. most marine sedimentary mp studies utilize benthic samples, which can only measure microplastic concentrations without reference to the time frame of accumulation. however, sediment collection via sediment traps can provide mp accumulation rates (#mp/cm2/day) as a function of time. to determine how the concentration, type, and accumulation rates of mps varied near the fringing reefs of st. john, us virgin islands, sediment samples were collected using tube sediment traps with roughly monthly deployment lengths from a near shore and reef site in coral bay, st. john from 2007-2016. paired benthic samples were collected adjacent to the traps for some sampling periods. mps were extracted via a density separation method which employed a sequential phased (interstitial water, organic matter treatment, zncl separation) in a centrifuge, developed for small volume, variable lithologies, and low blank (contamination) levels. mps were recovered from each extraction phase, with 15.5±12% and 25±17%. of the mp extracted from the sea-water and hydrogen peroxide phases, respectively. mp concentrations ranged from 153-6,622 mps/100ml (2.8-102 mps/g) and mp accumulation rates ranged from 180-4,800 mps/day/m2, with the variability driven primarily by the location and depth. mp concentrations in the sediment traps were found to be far greater than in paired benthic samples. this difference may be due to the effects of a) bioturbation, b) consumption by organisms, c) resuspension or d) laboratory microplastic extraction approaches.  in addition, the concentration of mp was greater in st. john compared to similar tropical reef environments, including heavily urbanized areas. future research should examine the how field sampling practices affect measurements of mp concentrations/accumulation and how mp vary with sediment composition, sedimentation rates and mps flux through the water column.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Assessment of Sedimentary Microplastics in Coral Reefs of the US Virgin Islands Using Sediment Traps",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "5a011a03-4e6f-4e6b-b005-e8cbe431bc7a"
            }
          ],
