      "search_text": "university of san diego henry arbaugh sarah gray henry arbaugh assessment of sedimentary microplastics in coral reefs of the us virgin islands using sediment traps given the negative impact of microplastics (mp) on corals, understanding the distribution and accumulation rate of mp in coral reef sediments is important. most marine sedimentary mp studies utilize benthic samples, which can only measure microplastic concentrations without reference to the time frame of accumulation. however, sediment collection via sediment traps can provide mp accumulation rates (#mp/cm2/day) as a function of time. to determine how the concentration, type, and accumulation rates of mps varied near the fringing reefs of st. john, us virgin islands, sediment samples were collected using tube sediment traps with roughly monthly deployment lengths from a near shore and reef site in coral bay, st. john from 2007-2016. paired benthic samples were collected adjacent to the traps for some sampling periods. mps were extracted via a density separation method which employed a sequential phased (interstitial water, organic matter treatment, zncl separation) in a centrifuge, developed for small volume, variable lithologies, and low blank (contamination) levels. mps were recovered from each extraction phase, with 15.5±12% and 25±17%. of the mp extracted from the sea-water and hydrogen peroxide phases, respectively. mp concentrations ranged from 153-6,622 mps/100ml (2.8-102 mps/g) and mp accumulation rates ranged from 180-4,800 mps/day/m2, with the variability driven primarily by the location and depth. mp concentrations in the sediment traps were found to be far greater than in paired benthic samples. this difference may be due to the effects of a) bioturbation, b) consumption by organisms, c) resuspension or d) laboratory microplastic extraction approaches.  in addition, the concentration of mp was greater in st. john compared to similar tropical reef environments, including heavily urbanized areas. future research should examine the how field sampling practices affect measurements of mp concentrations/accumulation and how mp vary with sediment composition, sedimentation rates and mps flux through the water column. sarah gray lessons learned: monitoring the impacts of watershed development and restoration on coral reef sedimentation in st. john, us virgin islands foraminifera as an indicator of variable water quality in coral reefs and mangrove areas in st. john, us virgin islands terrigenous sediment derived from unpaved roads is a significant stressor to coral reefs in the us virgin islands. the coral bay, st. john, usvi watershed was the focus of a land-based pollution monitoring program (noaa-arra) from 2009-16. the aims were to evaluate (a) how land use and watershed restoration activities affect the generation and delivery of runoff from terrestrial systems at various temporal and spatial scales, (b) the effectiveness of watershed restoration activities on marine sedimentation and coral stress (indirectly) and (c) the most effective marine sediment-monitoring protocols. the integrated terrestrial-marine monitoring program measured sediment erosion and runoff response in the watershed as well as marine terrigenous sediment accumulation rates, grain size, and sediment composition using marine sediment traps (monthly resolution), and marine turbidity (sub-seconds resolution) at multiple shoreline and coral reef sites.\r\nseveral restoration actions were effective in reducing watershed erosion and sediment yields. factors that explained the magnitude and spatial variability of marine sedimentation included the degree of watershed development, restoration actions, and exposure to sediment resuspension. significant decreases (17-41%) in sediment trap and benthic % clay post-restoration at marine monitoring sites (and potential coral siltation stress measured by silt accumulation rates) below the restored watersheds were consistent with a reduction in delivery of clay from unpaved roads measured by terrestrial monitoring. \r\nwhen attempting to document changes in marine terrigenous sedimentation, we found that marine monitoring approaches that target sediment composition/grain size were more effective sediment metrics than terrigenous sediment accumulation rate alone. baseline studies to understand the nature, pathways and time-scales of connectivity between the watershed and reef as well as long term integrated (several seasons) terrestrial-marine monitoring should be included as an essential component of watershed restoration programs. though poor water quality and land-based sedimentation is contributing to the decline of coral reefs in the caribbean, it is often challenging to monitor water quality regularly in remote areas. the foram index has been developed as a proxy for water quality based on the proportion of symbiont-bearing foraminifera in coral reef sediments. coral reefs in john, us virgin islands are negatively impacted in areas below developed watersheds where runoff from unpaved roads and septic systems reduce water quality. the objectives of this study were to compare how the foram index varied among areas below a developed and minimally developed watershed, over time, and with sedimentation. as part of a long-term sedimentation study, benthic sediment samples were collected over nine sampling periods near coral reefs in three bays below areas of variable watershed development and coastal habitats including (a) below a developed watershed (coral bay), (b) a minimally developed watershed in the vi national park and (c) within a mangrove area with coral reefs (hurricane hole). sediment composition and texture was analyzed and at least 150 foraminifera were picked, classified into three morphological groups and the foram index calculated. a greater proportion of symbiont-bearing foraminifera (greater foram index indicating better water quality) was found at the minimally developed site in the us virgin islands national park compared to the sites below the developed watershed or a mangrove. the foram index was lowest (poorest water quality) when there was a greater percentage of clay in the sediments.  there was not a consistent pattern of variability in the foram index over time. however, data from the foram index generally indicated marginal conditions for coral reef growth at all sites suggesting vulnerability of corals in st. john to disturbance.",
