          "name": "Shawna Foo",
          "search_text": "shawna foo feeding patterns of non-outbreaking crown-of-thorns seastars allow for regenerative capacity on reefs crown-of-thorns seastars (acanthaster spp., cots) are best known for driving coral mortality during population outbreaks. we tracked individual feeding scars, bare coral skeletons cleared of tissue, from a low-density (non-outbreak) population of acanthaster cf. solaris at one tree reef on the great barrier reef. despite variation in coral genera and colony sizes preyed on, average feeding scar size did not differ by season or cots size. most scars (65%) retained living coral tissue around their perimeter and were initially colonised by turf algae, with some crustose coralline algae (cca) present by 10 weeks post-predation. by 22 weeks, 25% of scars showed signs of regenerating coral tissue. montipora exhibited significantly greater regenerative capacity than branching genera. in contrast, acropora scars remained turf or cca covered despite partial predation. these results suggest that low-density a. cf. solaris exerts moderate predation pressure across a range of coral species, often leaving viable tissue remnants. this feeding pattern may help maintain coral community diversity by limiting fast-growing taxa, consistent with the intermediate disturbance hypothesis. consistent scar size and broad coral predation may reflect a feeding strategy of low-density cots that allows some corals to regenerate post-predation, thereby contributing to the recovery of live coral cover.",
