          "name": "Tony Hsu",
          "search_text": "tsai-hsuan tony hsu drivers of butterflyfish corallivory in a coral reef lagoon behavioural, physiological, and biochemical responses of scleractinian corals to butterflyfish predation climate change, particularly recurrent bleaching, is causing biodiversity loss on coral reefs. obligate corallivores feeding exclusively on live coral , are among the most affected groups. while links between corallivore abundance and coral cover are established, the amount of coral tissue they consume and the drivers of this consumption remain poorly quantified. this study quantified daily coral consumption rates of corallivorous butterflyfishes at the reef edge (peripheral) and centre (interior) of the one tree reef lagoon, southern great barrier reef, australia, using stereo-remote underwater videos (stereo-ruvs). we evaluated drivers of consumption, including feeding behaviour (intensity and duration), assemblage structure (richness and biomass), and predation risk (piscivore biomass). at the community level, mean daily coral consumption rates were over five times higher at peripheral (mean: 17.9 g 200 m⁻² day⁻¹, 95% ci: 16.3–19.6) compared to interior sites (mean: 3.5 g 200 m⁻² day⁻¹, 95% ci: 3.2–3.7). this spatial pattern was driven by higher corallivore richness at peripheral sites. at the species level, the most dominant species, chaetodon rainfordi, accounted for ~69% of all bites. c. rainfordi’s consumption rate was ~15 times higher at peripheral (16.5, 95% ci: 15.2–17.8 g 200 m⁻² day⁻¹) than interior sites (1.1, 95% ci: 1.0–1.2 g 200 m⁻² day⁻¹). this difference was best explained by c. rainfordi’s higher biomass and longer feeding duration at peripheral compared to interior sites, while predation risk had no observable effect. this study provides new estimates of coral consumption, which have implications for modelling trophic energy transfer, and reveals that both population dynamics (richness and biomass) and feeding behaviour (duration) are critical drivers of fish corallivory. the negative effects of coral loss on fish populations are well established, particularly for obligate corallivores. in contrast, the impacts of corallivorous fish on corals themselves have received less attention, though chronic predation is expected to impose sublethal costs. this study examined how two scleractinian corals, acropora samoensis and pocillopora damicornis, responded to varying levels of predation by the polyp-feeding butterflyfish, chaetodon rainfordi, under controlled conditions. we assessed the effects of predation on behaviour (polyp withdrawal), physiology (growth, ash-free dry weight, photosynthetic efficiency), and biochemistry (fatty acid content) of coral colonies; with fatty acid profiles further providing insight into energy balance (structural vs. storage lipids), trophic strategy, and coral health. the two coral species showed contrasting responses to predation. a. samoensis withdrew most polyps even at low predation (88% withdrawn at 0.5 bites min-1), leading to reduced growth (likely through suppressed heterotrophy), although other physiological and biochemical traits remained unaffected. in contrast, p. damicornis, a relatively heterotrophic species, kept the majority of its coral polyps (~71%) extended under the same intensity of predation, sustaining feeding but incurring greater tissue loss and declining health condition. nevertheless, growth and ash-free dry weight were maintained over a 17-day period, likely through mobilization of storage fatty acids derived from both autotrophic and heterotrophic sources. these results highlight coral’s tolerance to chronic butterflyfish predation (at least over short timescales), as most colonies maintained positive growth even under relatively high per-colony bite rates. however, the observed energy trade-offs suggest potential consequences for reproduction and susceptibility to environmental stress, indicating that chronic predation by non-skeletal feeding corallivores may subtly shape coral performance and persistence.",
