          "name": "Lorelei Ing",
          "search_text": "lorelei ing integrating transcriptomics and histopathology to understand similarities and differences between the host and symbiont response to bleaching and disease to help understand how the two main drivers of coral decline, bleaching and disease, affect coral and their algal endosymbionts, we collected 15 colonies of orbicella franksi of various health states following a white plague outbreak in st. john’s, usvi. health states included: apparently healthy with no signs of active lesions (hh), diseased colonies with active white plague lesions (dd), the apparently healthy tissue from a diseased colony (hd), and slightly bleached but otherwise healthy colonies (h-bl). in order to investigate the underling mechanisms of these phenotypes, we looked at host and symbiont gene expression and histopathology including symbiont size, vacuole size, and tissue degradation. host gene expression shows dd corals are mounting the strongest response and have the most differentially expressed genes . furthermore, many genes associated with immune and inflammatory responses, apoptosis, and extracellular matrix were identified in dd corals. h-bl corals upregulated known calcification-related mechanisms such as calcium-ion binding receptors and carbonic anhydrases as well as immune mechanisms related to apoptosis and inflammatory response like those seen in the dd corals. overlapping immune related genes and processes between these two stressors will be identified and further explored to identify the most important responses to both bleaching and disease in the host. on the symbiont level, hh colonies were exclusively dominated by durusdinium whereas h-bl and diseased (hd and dd) colonies were largely symbiodinium dominated. symbiont gene expression will determine if dominant symbiont is driving a unique response in the host or within itself. histopathology measurements will be correlated to host and symbiont gene expression to contextualize the genotypic response to each stressor. by integrating cellular and molecular metrics we hope to find health state specific responses to aid in our understanding of how the coral immune system responds to these environmental stressors.",
