          "name": "Daisy Flores",
          "search_text": "daisy flores corals hungry for survival and the cryptic montastraea cavernosa lineages that shape reef resilience genome‑wide segregation distortion reveals candidate viability loci in coral larvae and settlers the cryptic genetic diversity in the reef‑building coral montastraea cavernosa suggests that some lineages may be specialized for distinct environmental niches.  in honduras, m. cavernosa colonies on the turbid, nutrient enriched reefs of tela bay frequently show daytime polyp extension and active heterotrophic feeding, in contrast to colonies on the clear, oligotrophic reefs of nearby roatan where daytime feeding is rare. this study tests whether tela bay m. cavernosa represent a distinct adaptive lineage within this cryptic diversity complex and whether their elevated daytime feeding is primarily genetically determined or environmentally induced.\r\n\r\npopulation genetic structure will be assessed using 2b‑rad sequencing of 175 m. cavernosa colonies collected across eight sites in tela bay and four sites in roatan. these data will be integrated with existing caribbean‑wide 2b‑rad datasets to determine the genetic relationship of tela and roatan populations to previously defined cryptic lineages associated with different environmental regimes. differentiation of tela bay corals from known lineages will support a genetic adaptation hypothesis, whereas genetic similarity combined with site‑dependent feeding behavior will indicate strong behavioral plasticity.\r\n\r\nresults from these analyses will guide a subsequent reciprocal transplant experiment designed to test survival, condition, and feeding behavior of lineages across turbid and clear‑water habitats, representing degraded versus “typical” reef conditions.by explicitly linking cryptic genetic structure to habitat type and feeding behavior, this work will clarify how individual m. cavernosa lineages contribute to reef persistence in marginal environments and provide a framework for incorporating cryptic diversity and behavior into restoration planning. assisted fertilization and larval propagation are now central tools in efforts to rescue caribbean reef‑building corals, highlighting the need to understand how segregating genetic variation influences offspring survival. studies of highly fecund marine invertebrates show that segregating deleterious variants can drive intense mortality during early life stages. to investigate how inherited variation affects early life‑stage survival in corals, this study uses controlled within‑species reciprocal crosses among three caribbean broadcast‑spawning species to generate multiple full‑ and half‑sib families with samples spanning gametes, larval time points, and settled polyps. high‑coverage whole‑genome sequencing of all parents and individual offspring from selected families provides dense variant data for each reciprocal cross. parent–offspring trios are used to phase parental haplotypes and to perform segregation and transmission ratio distortion tests, quantifying where and to what extent surviving larvae and settlers deviate from mendelian inheritance across the genome.\r\n\r\ngenomic regions that show consistent underrepresentation of parental haplotypes among surviving larvae and settlers, relative to mendelian expectations, are identified and mapped as candidates for loci influencing early life‑stage survival. these candidate regions provide a genome‑wide set of targets for future functional and multi‑omic investigation. the viability map generated from these analyses offers a practical starting point for integrating genomic information about early life‑stage survival into coral breeding and larval propagation strategies.",
