          "name": "Hannah Rempel",
          "search_text": "hannah s. rempel trophic dynamics of coral reef herbivores and detritivores across shifting resource landscapes: integrating behavioral, molecular, and isotopic insights bridging scientists, schools, & communities through creative marine science education coral reef herbivores and detritivores–including parrotfishes, surgeonfishes, chubs, and sea cucumbers–play key roles in regulating algal and cyanobacterial community dynamics and are vital sources of food and income. however, overfishing can jeopardize their ecological functions. moreover, uncertainty about whether nominal herbivores target algae or incidentally graze it while seeking protein-rich bacteria and detritus complicates predictions of trophic dynamics under reef change. to evaluate how the foraging roles, diverse array of ingesta, and nutritional targets vary among species on a caribbean reef, we integrated behavioral surveys; gut content dna metabarcoding using coi, 23s, and 16s markers; and both bulk and compound-specific stable isotope analyses. we also assessed intraspecific dietary plasticity among the most locally abundant parrotfish and surgeonfish across five sites spanning coral-dominated reefs to degraded, algae- and cyanobacteria-dominated reefs. we found that while some species have more generalized diets, others are specialists that may provide unique functions in controlling reef benthic cover and utilize distinct nutritional resources. molecular data revealed they ingest a more diverse array of metazoan, algal, and bacterial prey than captured via visual surveys, while isotopic data suggest most species assimilate algae, cyanobacteria, and detritus, but to varying degrees. species differed in their dietary plasticity, where some foraged more on algae and cyanobacteria at sites where they were more abundant. improved understanding of herbivore and detritivore trophic roles is critical as algal and cyanobacterial cover have risen over 3-fold and 20-fold, respectively, on caribbean reefs since the 1970s. this research can help inform species-specific and ecosystem-based management strategies to sustain resilient, coral-dominated reefs while meeting community needs. accelerating coral reef decline calls for conservation approaches that reach beyond academic communities and cultivate partnerships among scientists, educators, and young learners. this talk highlights creative and collaborative approaches to marine science education that strengthen community partnerships, foster scientific literacy and stewardship in early learners, and promote communication and teaching skills in graduate student trainees.\r\n\r\nwe developed a ‘coral reefs & our changing oceans’ 4th–5th grade lesson series integrating ecological concepts with creative storytelling and art. students learn about trophic interactions, co-construct a food web, explore how human impacts alter reef ecosystems and consider management interventions. finally, they design a ‘reef superheroes’ comic linked to careers in science communication. initially focused on texas’ flower garden banks, the lessons were shared in texas schools and with the texas state aquarium. through a collaboration with jamaican educators, we adapted the curriculum to jamaican ecosystems, conservation initiatives, and teaching standards for a scientist-educator stem camp in negril. we published lessons in english and spanish as open educational resources on oer commons for free use and regional adaptation.\r\n\r\nthis educational outreach model helped inspire the implementation of a university of texas marine science institute graduate training program in evidence-based teaching practices. graduate students learn about student-centered pedagogy, develop k–12 lessons, and gain teaching experience in local schools (batterton et al. 2024, jmbe). k–12 students build ecological literacy and a commitment to marine stewardship, while early-career scientists strengthen teaching and community-engagement skills. standardized test trends suggest improved student performance in partner schools and graduate trainees report increased confidence in evidence-based teaching and communication. conserving coral reefs for future generations begins with cultivating genuine partnerships among scientists, educators, and the communities they serve. this collaborative approach–rooted in creative science education and graduate pedagogical training–offers a scalable model to build trust in science and foster marine stewardship.",
