          "name": "Karim Primov",
          "search_text": "karim primov acropora millepora’s microbiome is predicted by host genetics, algal symbiont genus, and the environment, not bleaching status the coral microbiome has been proposed to be critical of coral health and resilience, yet it is unclear what factors drive coral microbiome composition, especially within the context of coral bleaching. here, we use whole genome sequencing data combined with a machine learning approach (rdaforest) to assess various predictors of the microbiome in 208 colonies of acropora millepora from 12 reef sites in the central great barrier reef during a 2016 bleaching event. we find that microbiome variation is primarily predicted by the first principal component of coral host genetic structure, prevalence of durusdinium, and some environmental variables (most prominently chlorphyll seasonal range and maximum degree heating weeks), with negligible predictive power of bleaching score. strong associations of the coral microbiome to symbiont genus has not been reported before and is likely due to the difference in exudate availability for microbiomes from different photosymbiont genera. in turn, the association of the microbiome with the environment suggests that the coral microbiome can serve as a proxy for environmental variation when environment cannot be measured directly, which may be especially effective in ancient dna studies.",
