          "name": "Rowan Martindale",
          "search_text": "rowan martindale lessons from early jurassic reef crises: collapse and recovery dynamics of pliensbachian and toarcian reef ecosystems from the central high atlas of morocco “reef survivor”: a new board game about coral conservation reefs are key marine ecosystems but are currently threatened by anthropogenic and natural threats. ancient reef crises and subsequent recoveries are important analogues for modern conservation, and one of the most severe reef collapses of the phanerozoic occurred during the pliensbachian–toarcian transition (early jurassic). during the pliensbachian, lithiotid bivalves, corals, sponges, and microbialites built bioherms and biostromes; however, these reefs were decimated by environmental changes associated with the pliensbachian/toarcian boundary event and toarcian oceanic anoxic event (t-oae) ~183 million years ago.\r\n\r\ninterdisciplinary research in the central high atlas mountains of morocco has identified a dynamic record of reef proliferation and collapse. during the late pliensbachian, coral-sponge-microbial bioherms and lithiotid-coral biostromes thrived in shallow-water depositional environments. these reefs were home to diverse communities with abundant macro and microfauna. just after the pliensbachian/toarcian boundary, the climate regime shifted, resulting in a pulse of terrigenous clastic material and nutrients that poisoned carbonate ecosystems, leading to a first reef collapse. lithiotid bioherms and biostromes returned to shallow-water settings quite rapidly and were often the first to colonize these settings. conversely, corals, sponges, and foraminifera did not recover quickly, with large benthic foraminifera being absent from early toarcian reefs and corals only occupying minor roles in lithiotid communities. it was not until just before the onset of the t-oae that corals and sponges reappeared as reef builders, producing small patch reefs in several locations just before the onset of the event. reef recovery was short-lived as the t-oae caused the extinction of the lithiotid bivalves and a second reef collapse. despite the severity of the t-oae, coral and microbial reefs were quick to recover, albeit with an extremely depauperate foraminifera and sponge communities. this high-resolution record of multi-phased reef crisis and recovery is a rare and important analogue for modern reef ecosystems. educational games have been increasing in popularity in both classrooms and conservation efforts because they promote learning through amusement and encourage players to engage with both the material and each other. here we describe a new board game, “reef survivor”, which was originally designed as an instructional tool for undergraduate and high school classes. the educational objective is to teach players about ecology, evolution, and environmental perturbations, while the gameplay objective is to build a resilient reef ecosystem. through collaborative and competitive gameplay, students learn about evolution mechanics as well as ecology and how reefs survive natural disasters. the game blends informed decision-making with chance to encourage players to learn and model complex earth systems and evolutionary processes. players choose their environment and reef community, whereas chance influences mutations and disasters. the game was incorporated in undergraduate classroom activities in 2021 and 2022 at 20 colleges and universities, mostly u.s. public institutions. students were enthusiastic about the game, with two-thirds saying they would rather play the game than have a normal lab. the learning gains were positive, with statistically significant positive learning gains by the final semester of assessment. a print-and-play version of the game (doi: 10.18738/t8/s3kwt7), onboarding and follow-up assignments, and suggested extension activities will be presented as well as adjustments for time, course objective, and educational level. in addition, “reef survivor” was modified for use with local community members in jamaica in collaboration with local conservation experts; “reef survivor: jamaica” incorporates elements of place-based education and resembles the marine environment in the east portland special fishery conservation area in portland, jamaica. the team observed that the game facilitated discussions amongst the local community members, which indicates that the game can be a useful tool to promote cooperative learning for reef conservation.",
