          "name": "Tanya Brown",
          "search_text": "tanya brown when bacteria attack: virulence of the opportunistic coral pathogen serratia marcescens in the exaiptasia diaphana model system cnidarians, in general, are long-lived organisms and hence may repeatedly encounter common pathogens during their lifespans. currently, there are many diseases effecting corals, especially in the caribbean basin. previous research has shown that the sea anemone, exaiptasia diaphana possess a primitive form of immune priming in response to the known coral pathogen, vibrio coralliilyticus up to one month after the initial bacterial dose. this short-lived capability is beneficial to the anemone as it provides protection during the warm summer months when pathogens are more prevalent and virulent. currently, experiments are underway to establish if priming may be possible using a second known coral pathogen, serratia marcescens. work conducted with s. marcescens shows strain specific lethality. only strain pdl 100 causes mortality in e. diaphana while a strain that showed mortality in fellow cnidarian, cassiopea xamachana does not affect the anemones (p=0.000). microbial dose dependent mortality (p=0.006) is found with 10⁸ cfu/ml s. marcescens pdl100 depicting the most consistent mortality. furthermore, s. marcescens pdl 100 shows differing mortality rates between symbiotic and aposymbiotic e. diaphana with aposymbiotic individuals dying a day earlier that their symbiotic counterparts (p=0.026). using this data, trials will be run to see if priming may be capable using s. marcescens pdl100. given the very quick complete mortality of e. diaphana (within 3 days of initial challenge) heat killer s. marcescens will be tried. results from this work will have the potential to help vaccinate vulnerable corals during disease outbreaks.",
