          "name": "Ambrocio Melvin Matias",
          "search_text": "ambrocio melvin matias imprints of low sea-level stands on tropical coral diversification glacial cycles led to a series of biodiversity contraction and expansion events, culminating in many known refugia of diversity at high latitudes. in contrast, how these glacial cycles shaped present-day genetic diversity in tropical corals remains uncertain. specifically, the mechanisms by which periods of low sea-level stand contributed to the diversification of widely distributed coral species and their remaining signatures to within species genetic diversity are mostly undescribed. here, we tackle this question by leveraging existing genomic data of widely distributed lineages of pachyseris speciosa across the indo-west pacific and complementing them with new whole genome sequences from the coral triangle (i.e., the philippines and indonesia). the coral triangle is a region of interest because its basins were repeatedly isolated during glacial cycles. we first examined patterns of divergence between lineages (i.e., strict isolation, isolation with migration, or isolation with introgression) while incorporating potential contributions of selection. using approximate bayesian computation, we show that pairwise divergence between lineages follows isolation with introgression, highlighting the roles of isolation and reconnection during low-sea-level stands. importantly, the support on heterogenous genomic effective population size and gene flow indicates potential roles of selection on the divergence of p. speciosa lineages. we further examined the role of introgression focusing on two lineages we sampled within the coral triangle. using site frequency spectrum-based demographic modeling, we show that a model with series of isolation and gene flow is supported in both lineages of p. speciosa. overall, our results reveal the impacts of barriers during low-sea-level stands for both between and within lineages. in diverging lineages, barriers result in isolation, which is likely sustained by selection, leading to divergence between widely distributed lineages. within lineages however, low-sea-level stands resulted in a series of isolation and reconnection, potentially “pumping” genetic diversity into these coral lineages.",
