          "name": "Jan Theodore Miranda",
          "search_text": "jan theodore miranda long-term monitoring demonstrates increased coral and reef fish on larval enhancement sites in magsaysay reef, northwestern philippines coral reefs are increasingly threatened by local anthropogenic stressors and global climate change. to support reef recovery, various coral restoration techniques have been developed, with coral larval enhancement emerging as one of the most promising approaches. over the past decade, this method has demonstrated success by harnessing the natural reproductive output of corals to increase the supply and genetic diversity of competent larvae delivered to degraded reefs. recent studies have shown strong recovery outcomes resulting from larval restoration with acropora species in the bolinao-anda reef complex in northwestern philippines.  however, longer-term monitoring of larval enhancement efforts remain limited. longer-term monitoring of the condition of larval-enhanced reefs is crucial for evaluating the effectiveness of the technique and identifying opportunities for improvement and upscaling. changes in associated species, particularly benthic communities and reef fish assemblages, serve as strong indicators for reef condition. this study aimed to assess the status of larval enhancement sites in magsaysay reef, northwestern philippines, from 2016 to 2023. benthic communities were monitored using photo quadrats within 25 m2 experimental plots and analyzed in coral point count with excel extension (cpce). reef fish communities were surveyed through underwater visual census (uvc) within the established plots. initial results of the long-term monitoring of acropora cf. tenuis experimental plots revealed an increase in hard coral cover from 18.5% to 46% after 8 years. reef fish abundance increased by 56% from 2016 to 2023, with larval-enhanced plots exhibiting 48% higher abundance than control, unrestored plots in 2023. species richness of fish increased by 37% over the eight-year period, with a 19% difference relative to control plots. overall, this study underscores the effectiveness  of larval enhancement in increasing coral cover and reef fish communities in degraded reefs and highlights the importance of long-term monitoring for evaluating restoration effectiveness.",
