          "name": "Mariyam Shidha Afzal",
          "search_text": "mariyam shidha afzal coral bleaching and mortality throughout okinawa island, japan during the 2024 mass bleaching event in this study, we examined the coral cover and bleaching severity across okinawa island, japan during the 2024 mass bleaching event. using the point intercept survey method, we sampled seven reef crests in august- september (peak bleaching period) and december 2024 (post-bleaching). all recorded coral colonies were identified to the genus level, and their degree of bleaching was categorized. our results show that 84.1% of the coral colonies were bleached with 1.9% of them undergoing bleaching induced mortality in august - september. the overall coral cover for okinawa island was 50.5%, however, the results of post bleaching surveys in december indicates a 70.0% decline in overall coral cover for the island. the reef crests of okinawa island were dominated by acropora species, where in august 90.5% of the colonies recorded were undergoing bleaching and 2.0% of the colonies suffering bleaching-induced mortality. post-bleaching surveys revealed 63.0% decline in acropora assemblages across reef crests in okinawa island. moreover, no acropora colonies were observed on reef crests of three sites in the december surveys.  at sites such as yamagawa port (kakinouchi), which were dominated by 86.0% of acropora species in august, the genus was completely absent in december. these findings highlight the severity of the 2024 bleaching event on reef crests of okinawa island. in an era of recurrent bleaching events, it is crucial to monitor their extent and intensity across broad spatial and temporal scales.",
