          "name": "Mikito Murakami",
          "search_text": "mikito murakami assessment of the effects of zno on reef-building corals sunscreen ingredients released into coastal waters at tourist sites are estimated to reach approximately 14,000 tons annually, raising concerns about their impacts on marine organisms, including reef-building corals. although several organic uv filters have recently been regulated due to their ecological risks, the use of so-called “reef-safe” inorganic filters, particularly zinc oxide (zno), has increased. however, zno is also classified as hazardous to aquatic life under the un ghs, and its potential to inhibit photosynthesis in symbiotic algae and induce coral bleaching has been suggested. thus, the environmental safety of zno remains insufficiently evaluated. this study aimed to assess the effects of different zno concentrations on corals and to identify candidate genes associated with zno-induced bleaching.\r\n\r\nfragments of acropora solitaryensis, a common indo-pacific species, were collected by scuba diving in tateyama in september 2024. four colonies were assigned to each treatment (control, 0.3 mg/l, 0.5 mg/l, 1.0 mg/l) and exposed to zno for 48 hours. photosynthetic activity (fv/fm) was measured using pam fluorometry, rna-seq analysis was performed, and a time-lapse camera documented changes in appearance.\r\n\r\nclear differences were observed between the low-concentration groups (0.3 and 0.5 mg/l) and the high-concentration group (1.0 mg/l). corals at 0.3 and 0.5 mg/l showed minimal bleaching, little visible change, and fv/fm values similar to baseline. in contrast, complete bleaching occurred within 48 hours at 1.0 mg/l, accompanied by a marked decline in fv/fm. rna-seq results showed no significant differences in the number of expressed genes among the control, 0.3 mg/l, and 0.5 mg/l groups, whereas overall gene expression decreased at 1.0 mg/l. notably, several genes exhibited more than a 1,500-fold increase in expression under the 1.0 mg/l condition. these highly responsive genes may serve as potential molecular markers for the non-invasive detection of zno-induced stress.",
