        {
          "name": "Sarina Bao",
          "search_text": "sarina bao strategies for social adaptation to ecological range shifts in coastal ecosystems: insights from japan purpose\r\nclimate-driven range shifts in coral reef ecosystems are reshaping local biodiversity and altering the ecological foundations that coastal social communities rely on. while the ecological mechanisms behind these shifts have become increasingly well understood, much less is known about how communities perceive and respond to such rapid socio-ecological change. this study investigates strategies that support social adaptation to range shifts associated with coral reef ecosystem changes, drawing on empirical insights from japan.\r\nmethods\r\nthis study combined a national coastal survey (n = ca. 2,000) with ecological indicators to examine social community responses to biodiversity change in coral reef ecosystems. residents’ perceptions of marine environmental change and ecosystem-related resources were compared with sea surface temperature-based data on the presence of corals, seaweeds, and fish, in order to analyze gaps between perceived and actual ecological change.\r\nresults\r\ncluster analysis identified three groups with distinct levels of engagement and adaptive attitudes. recognition of coastal resources varied clearly across clusters: familiar resources such as fisheries products, ports, and tourism facilities were most widely recognized. the low-engagement group showed consistently low awareness, the conservation-oriented group the highest, and the adaptation-oriented group remained intermediate. overall awareness of changes in coral and seaweed habitats was low, with no significant differences among clusters for coral-related recognition. substantial municipal-level variation in subjective and objective understanding of coral reef ecosystem shifts suggested uneven access to ecological information. differences were also observed across levels of ocean interaction, marine resource use, and demographic attributes.\r\nconclusion \r\nthese findings highlight the uneven recognition of ecosystem shifts among the public and underscore the importance of strengthening locally grounded communication and community-based adaptation efforts.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Strategies for Social Adaptation to Ecological Range Shifts in Coastal Ecosystems: Insights from Japan",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "92f3c521-b371-43cc-a605-ba7a041f4aa6"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00100",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Supisara Pipithkul",
          "search_text": "supisara pipithkul phylogenomic relationships of a highly threatened coral species with a unique distribution, acropora pruinosa : species history and conservation implications climate change is forcing many reef‑building corals at distributional margins to either adapt, shift their ranges, or go locally extinct, yet the evolutionary pathways by which tropical species give rise to temperate specialists remain poorly resolved. the temperate and highly threatened coral acropora pruinosa, restricted to the waters of japan and hong kong, is closely related to the tropical acropora solitaryensis and thus provides a model for understanding such transitions. here we test the hypothesis that a. pruinosa originated from an indo‑pacific a. solitaryensis ancestor in subtropical hong kong, subsequently expanded into the temperate coasts of japan, and then diverged further into eastern and western japanese lineages. we combined comparative skeletal morphology with genome‑wide snp data spanning a. solitaryensis and a. pruinosa across their ranges. colonies identified as a. pruinosa in hong kong display gross morphological characters intermediate between a. solitaryensis and japanese a. pruinosa. species‑tree inference in snapper under the multispecies coalescent places hong kong a. pruinosa as a distinct, basal lineage that diverged after a. solitaryensis and before japanese populations, which form two well‑supported clades corresponding to eastern and western japan. together with its intermediate morphology, this topology suggests that hong kong a. pruinosa retains more ancestral character states relative to japanese populations and may approximate the ancestral lineage from which contemporary japanese a. pruinosa originated. this pattern is consistent with a stepping‑stone scenario in which an a. solitaryensis‑like ancestor first colonized hong kong, evolved temperate‑tolerant traits characteristic of a. pruinosa, and then colonized and diversified within japanese waters under geographic isolation. our results reveal cryptic evolutionary diversification within a. pruinosa and illustrate how marginal environments can promote lineage formation along tropical–temperate transition zones. we further propose that hong kong, western japan, and eastern japan lineages should be treated as separate evolutionarily significant units for future management.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "Phylogenomic Relationships of a Highly Threatened Coral Species with a Unique Distribution, Acropora pruinosa : Species History and Conservation Implications",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "2cc5fbcf-ec3b-4371-ae06-e639c28e980f"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01362",
          "attended": true
        }
