          "name": "Taylor Naaykens",
          "search_text": "taylor naaykens spatial mismatch in dispersal capacity of a caribbean goby and its host sponge symbiotic partners can differ widely in their movement and dispersal capacities, yet this topic remains understudied for reef fishes involved in symbioses. in the caribbean, large demosponges form prominent microhabitats and host a variety of cryptobenthic fishes. one such fish is elacatinus horsti, a goby that occupies two different types of sponges: a stinging massive sponge (neofibularia nolitangere) and nonstinging tube sponges (aplysina spp). although these sponge types offer distinct energetic trade-offs to their residents, they both host persistent groups of fish.  here, we explore the dispersal capacity of the reef fish elacatinus horsti and two of its host sponge species that differ in key larval traits. while fish larvae can actively swim and have a month-long larval duration, sponge larvae are passive and their larval duration is shorter. to estimate genetic connectivity in each partner, we collected tissue samples from each species along the ~70 km leeward coast of curaçao and used genome-wide data to assess spatial genetic patterns and infer mean generational dispersal capacity. results revealed a clear mismatch in population structure between partners. the fish e. horsti showed no detectable genetic structure, suggesting high island-wide connectivity and a per-generational mean dispersal distance that extends beyond the scale of the island. in contrast, the sponge hosts exhibited significant spatial genetic differentiation characterized by strong isolation-by-distance patterns, consistent with limited larval dispersal. together, these findings demonstrate that symbiotic association does not necessarily result in the evolution of similar dispersal capacities for a reef fish and its sponge hosts. this spatial mismatch has important implications for host-symbiont co-distribution and the persistence of sponge-associated fishes on rapidly degrading caribbean reefs, as differing dispersal capacities may increasingly decouple fish from suitable hosts and constrain where these partnerships can be maintained.",
