          "name": "Dr Alex Van Nynatten",
          "search_text": "alexander van nynatten marine heatwaves transform coral symbioses with enduring effects climate change-amplified extreme weather events are reshaping ecological communities globally. on coral reefs, heatwaves typically disrupt the obligate coral–algal symbiosis, with symbiont identity a prime determinant of coral resilience to these thermal extremes. yet, whether heatwaves have long-term effects on coral symbioses remains unclear due to a lack of longitudinal symbiont data. \r\nhere, we report on a decade-long symbiont survey from kiritimati atoll (2013–2023), spanning the most prolonged tropical marine heatwave on record (2015–2016) and its aftermath. using its2 dna metabarcoding data from 856 samples belonging to 551 coral colonies and benthic surveys from 37 forereef sites along a gradient of chronic local human disturbance we document wholesale transformation of the symbiont assemblages in two coral species, montipora aequituberculata and pocillopora grandis, the legacy of which was persistent for seven years post heatwave. the recovery of coral cover and pre-heatwave symbioses was negligible in both species for at least three years after the heatwave (2017-2019) and by 7.5 years after the heatwave (2023) was only observed in p. grandis and not m. aequituberculata, a species driven close to local extinction by the marine heatwave. we also observe significant changes to the incidence and abundance of the heat tolerant generalist symbiont taxa after the marine heatwave, and in p. grandis, higher durusdinium abundance associated with colonies that survived the heatwave and colonies at sites exposed to high human disturbance. \r\nour study demonstrates that marine heatwaves can have long-lasting impacts on symbioses, making the recovery of symbiont communities in some corals increasingly unlikely due to the protracted duration of the recovery of pre-heatwave symbionts, the potential for local extinctions of host-adapted symbiont species, and the additional delays in recovery associated with chronic stressors, raising concern for coral resilience to future heatwaves.",
