          "name": "Kathleen Durkin",
          "search_text": "kathleen durkin multi-omic approach for decoding coral epigenetic regulation corals rely on complex molecular regulation to maintain physiological function under natural environmental variability and anthropogenic perturbations of increasing frequency and severity. epigenetic mechanisms, which dynamically influence expression without directly altering the nucleotide sequence, are central to this regulation and may be key determinants of phenotypic plasticity and acclimatory potential. however, while individual epigenetic mechanisms (e.g., dna methylation or mirna activity) have been described in some coral species, their combined contributions to gene expression and phenotype remain largely unresolved in non-model systems. here, we present a cross-species, multi-omic analysis integrating matched time-series datasets of gene expression, mirna and lncrna expression, and dna methylation from three reef-building coral species (acropora pulchra, porites evermanni, and pocillopora tuahiniensis). using a suite of statistical and machine-learning frameworks, we identify gene networks associated with key physiological processes (e.g., biomineralization), and evaluate the degree to which combined epigenetic feature sets can jointly predict these genes’ expression. this approach allows us to explore putative interactions among regulatory layers, disentangle their relative contributions, and build preliminary quantitative models of epigenetic influence on coral expression and physiology. our results reveal both shared and species-specific strategies for expression regulation, and provide insight into the interactions among layers of epigenetic control. more broadly, this integrative approach advances understanding of the epigenetic architecture shaping environmental response in corals, and offers a foundation for studying how epigenetic mechanisms may work together to facilitate acclimation or resilience in reef organisms facing rapid environmental change.",
