          "name": "Teresa Baptista Nobre",
          "search_text": "teresa baptista nobre selective breeding enhances thermal tolerance in ningaloo reef corals: evidence from reciprocal crosses and genomic analysis ocean warming threatens coral reefs as marine heat waves increase in frequency and magnitude. assisted evolution approaches, including selective breeding, may enhance coral thermal tolerance, yet the genomic basis of heat tolerance and how it can be inherited across generations remains poorly understood. ningaloo reef, a world heritage site in western australia (wa), is one of the world’s longest and most diverse fringing reef systems, hosting about half the coral species in the indian ocean. we conducted a selective breeding experiment using colonies from acropora tenuis and acropora millepora from two sites ~130 km apart, oyster stacks and pelican point, the former experiencing approximately 1°c higher mean sea surface temperature. larvae from 40 families from all possible cross combinations were exposed to control (27°c) or elevated (35.5°c) temperatures, and survival was tracked. survival under heat stress varied significantly with parental origin. in a. tenuis, larvae from warm reef parents (o×o) showed highest survival probability (58.7%), 1.7x greater than cool reef larvae (p×p: 25.3%). critically, interpopulation crosses demonstrated intermediate but higher survival probability compared to cool reef families: o×p crosses had 48.1% survival probability (1.5× higher than p×p), while p×o crosses reached 34.6% survival probability (1.2× higher than p×p). similar patterns were observed in a. millepora, confirming consistency across species. narrow-sense heritability estimates for heat tolerance were higher at 35.5 °c relative to 27 °c, generating baseline information for the area. preliminary genomic data resulting from whole genome sequencing of 45 a. tenuis larvae revealed population-level genomic divergence and genetic variants associated with thermal tolerance. these findings combining phenotypic, pedigree, and genomic marker data, represent the first demonstration that selective breeding can enhance coral heat tolerance within a single generation in indian ocean corals and highlight the potential of assisted gene flow as a tool for reef restoration.",
