          "name": "Teresa Bednarek",
          "search_text": "teresa bednarek acropora species show distinct symbiont and bacterial communities and species-specific responses to resident damselfish symbiotic interactions are central to coral reef ecosystems, spanning the coral holobiont’s internal microbial partnerships to broader associations with reef fish. coral-dwelling damselfish live in close association with coral colonies, using their branches as shelter. these associations can influence the coral's immediate environment through various mechanisms including nutrient inputs, oxygen dynamics, and sediment removal, while fish may also serve as vectors for microorganisms. despite their ecological prevalence, our understanding of how these fish-coral associations influence holobiont composition and ultimately coral health remains largely unexplored. in this study, we investigated how resident damselfish influence the coral holobiont composition of acropora millepora and acropora digitifera along ningaloo reef, western australia. coral samples were collected from colonies with different levels of resident damselfish association, and analysed for symbiont physiology, as well as symbiont and bacterial community composition using its2 and 16s rrna amplicon sequencing. our findings reveal distinct species-specific holobiont signatures between the coral species, with a. millepora exhibiting higher symbiont cell density and bacterial alpha diversity whereas a. digitifera showed greater symbiont alpha diversity. while both highly related species were dominated by symbionts of the genus cladocopium and bacteria of the family endozoicomonadaceae, they hosted different sequence variants. we found that increasing fish abundance was associated with decreased chlorophyll c₂ concentration per symbiont cell and increased chlorophyll a:c₂ ratio. additionally, damselfish presence and abundance correlated with reduced microbial diversity and subtle shifts in bacterial community structure in a. millepora, while a. digitifera remained largely unaffected. our results offer an initial snapshot of coral–fish–microbe interactions and a framework for how ecological associations can influence holobiont dynamics. the species-specific responses we observed highlight that accounting for host identity is essential for predicting coral resilience in a changing climate, with important implications for reef conservation and restoration strategies.",
