      "search_text": "university of york david medio david medio cura (collaborative union for research and advocacy) – reducing anthropogenic impacts to saudi red sea coral reefs the red sea coast of saudi arabia extends over 1,800 km, along most of which are fringing coral reefs. offshore are further well-developed reef complexes, notably around the strait of tiran, through the al wajh lagoon, and between al lith and al shuqaiq, an area including the farasan bank. these reefs were in pristine condition until the 1980s, when over 100 sites were proposed as marine protected areas (mpas), including national parks on the al wajh and farasan banks. although none of these proposals were implemented at the time, in the early 2000s the saudi wildlife commission produced detailed proposals for mpas in the southern gulf of aqaba, on the tiran islands and coast, and covering the al wajh bank.\r\nsince then there have along the saudi coast been numerous coastal and island-based developments, including ports, marinas, global trade and energy hubs, and tourist resorts. these projects have incorporated efforts to protect marine habitats and ecology, but their promoters need to further consider how easily cumulative and synergistic processes can impact coral reefs.\r\nfor example, the red sea has since 1998 become increasingly subject to coral bleaching.  on central and southern saudi reefs this is now a near-annual occurrence, while in the northern red sea mass mortality has only more recently been reported. warming seas will make the region more sensitive to other anthropogenic impacts.\r\nhere we report on the work of a new saudi arabian foundation, cura, launched with the intention of promoting positive, innovative approaches to the conservation of the red sea’s unique marine ecosystem. on the one hand it is promoting means by which the direct and indirect impacts of coastal development can be minimised. on the other it has initiated research to identify genes underlying the greater tolerance of some coral genotypes to elevated sea-surface temperatures.",
