      "distance_km": 12379.3,
      "search_text": "université de mayotte emmanuel corse emmanuel corse lagoon under influence: how human activities shape sponge assemblages and their microbiomes mayotte hosts one of the largest lagoon systems in the indian ocean, forming a major regional biodiversity hotspot. rapid demographic growth over recent decades has intensified land-use change,  sediment and nutrient inputs to the lagoon, underscoring the need for sustained monitoring of marine communities.\r\nsponges are relevant indicators of land–sea interactions due to their sensitivity to water quality and substrate conditions. since 2020, projects have developed sponges and their microbiomes as a model system to quantify how watershed anthropisation influences (i) sponge-associated microbiomes and (ii) sponge community structure across mayotte’s fringing reefs. to do so, microbiome analyses were conducted in 2020 across four campaigns and ecologically contrasted stations using 16s metabarcoding (two markers) on xestospongia testudinaria (hma) and stylissa massa (lma). sponge assemblages were assessed during large-scale campaigns in 2022/2023 and 2025, surveying 30 stations around the island. environmental covariates (anthropisation index, nutrients, substrate type, depth) were measured to identify pathways linking watershed pressures to benthic responses.\r\nmicrobiome analyses showed that watershed anthropisation influences part of the microbial community, with several bacterial lineages shifting along the disturbance gradient despite stable core microbiomes. sponge assemblages displayed strong within-station structuring driven by depth, and anthropisation emerged as a major determinant of community composition. stations adjacent to the most anthropised watersheds exhibited the highest sponge richness and abundance, including species favoured by enriched or degraded conditions. causal modelling confirmed expected effects related to nutrient enrichment and substrate variables, but detailed analyses revealed that anthropisation did not act primarily through siltation; instead, it operated more subtly by altering substrate diversity. despite major disturbances–including the 2024 mass bleaching and cyclone chido–sponge assemblages remained remarkably stable at the island scale.\r\nour results highlight sponges and their microbiomes as powerful indicators of land–sea interactions and provide a robust foundation for long-term ecological monitoring in the mayotte lagoon.",
