      "search_text": "université libre de bruxelles mathilde godefroid mathilde godefroid ciliary flow and morphology as regulators of mass transport in black corals corals are microenvironmental mosaics shaped by ciliary flow, spatial geometry and holobiont activity black corals (antipatharians) are sessile colonial animals ubiquitous across oceans, thriving until great depths (> 8,000 m). particularly abundant in mesophotic zones, they form dense assemblages often referred to as “marine animal forests”, which create biodiversity oases supporting a wide range of marine life. due to the challenges associated with sampling and exploration at depth, our knowledge on this taxa remains very limited, leaving critical gaps in understanding their ecological success in such environments. \r\nthis study explores how morphology and ciliary flow shape mass transport in black corals that were collected down to 80 m depth through tech-diving operations. we compared two co-occurring species with contrasting morphologies: the whip-like stichopathes sp. and the branched antipathella wollastoni. using multi-scale flow visualization (particle image/tracking velocimetry), and metabolic assays (oxygen-sensitive senspiv and respirometry), we explored how morphology influences flow dynamics and ecological performance. \r\nour findings revealed that ciliary flow at the microscale plays a central role in mass transport, with species-specific roles. stichopathes sp. relies strongly on ciliary flow to enhance ventilation and feeding, compensating for its limited exchange surface, whereas a. wollastoni benefits from greater exchange surface, but uses ciliary flow for local redistribution of small food particles. additionally, we observed bidirectional particle movement in gastrovascular systems and tentacles, with flow characteristics shaped by skeletal spine morphology. \r\nour results highlight the critical influence of morphology and the diverse functions of cilia in shaping the ecological success of black corals in mesophotic and deeper habitats. in coral colonies, the combination of highly variable reef conditions and the intense metabolic activity of the host and its symbionts creates steep microscale gradients within a single colony. exposed branch tips experience rapid fluctuations, whereas the sheltered interior remains more stable but weakly ventilated. these intracolonial contrasts likely structure spatial heterogeneity in coral physiology, yet the mechanistic links remain poorly understood.\r\nin this study, we investigate how corals exchange compounds with the surrounding seawater across different microenvironments and flow conditions. these exchanges, collectively referred to as mass transfer, are bidirectional, from the release of oxidative stress molecules to the uptake of oxygen and nutrients. these are shaped by both ambient flow and ciliary activity, which drive active transport. we quantify mass transfer using complementary approaches: particle image velocimetry (piv) is used to resolve flow fields and characterize cilia-driven transport, which, when combined with chemical sensing through senspiv (ahmerkamp et al., 2022), reveals oxygen distributions and quantifies oxygen-related fluxes across microenvironments. we then integrate these experimental data into a 3d numerical model to test how flow conditions and ciliary activity influence mass-transfer efficiency across the colony. we hypothesize that ciliary activity compensates for reduced flow in sheltered intracolonial regions, thereby mitigating limitations in mass transfer relative to more exposed areas.\r\nby resolving these microscale transport processes, our study advances our understanding of how coral colonies function as spatially heterogeneous systems and highlights the importance of considering ciliary flow to understand their performance. \r\n\r\n\r\nahmerkamp, s. et al. (2022). simultaneous visualization of flow fields and oxygen concentrations to unravel transport and metabolic processes in biological systems. cell reports methods, 2(5).",
