      "search_text": "vavaʻu environmental protection association karen stone karen stone informing recovery: pre- and post-hthh coral reef assessments to support tonga’s marine management in 2017, the government of tonga, the vava’u environmental protection association, and the blue prosperity coalition conducted a coral reef survey expedition across tonga to support marine spatial planning and special management area (sma) development. following this assessment, in january 2022, the eruption of the hunga tonga–hunga haʻapai (hthh) volcano generated a series of destructive tsunamis that impacted communities and ecosystems across tonga and beyond. in august 2022, the partnership conducted a second expedition, representing the first systematic post-eruption evaluation of coral reef condition across the kingdom of tonga.\r\n\r\non both expeditions, the team surveyed reef fish and benthic composition. in 2022, macroinvertebrate assemblages,  juvenile coral density, reef structural complexity, and water quality data, as well as qualitative evidence of tsunami impacts were collected at 49 forereef sites. of these, 13 sites were resurveys from 2017, allowing for comparisons of benthic cover from before and after the tsunami.\r\n\r\nfindings reveal that the eruption and tsunami caused clear but spatially variable damage to benthic communities. coral cover declined notably in haʻapai and was low at exposed sites in tongatapu & ʻeua, while vavaʻu reefs showed an increase in coral cover relative to 2017. juvenile coral densities were low across most districts, with a few exceptions, suggesting limited short-term recovery potential in some areas. coral communities were dominated by mounding and encrusting taxa indicating disproportionate loss of branching and tabular species.\r\n\r\nin contrast, reef fish communities appeared largely unaffected by the eruption, showing consistent biomass, density, and diversity patterns with previous surveys. conversely, macroinvertebrates, particularly sea cucumbers, showed substantial declines in diversity and density, likely reflecting a combination of tsunami impacts and ongoing harvesting pressure.\r\n\r\ntogether, these results provide baseline data and a cross-district assessment of post-hthh reef condition and offer critical insights to guide recovery, fisheries management, and long-term monitoring.",
