          "search_text": "lisa rodrigues seasonal metal concentration in the skeleton of the northern star coral, astrangia poculata both natural and human activities introduce heavy metals into the marine environment, pollute coral reefs, and can be toxic to marine organisms. while most studies of heavy metals have focused on corals from tropical regions, some of the most polluted areas are inhabited by understudied temperate coral species. we quantified the concentration of fourteen elements, in the skeleton of astrangia poculata from jamestown, ri collected in the fall, winter, spring, and summer seasons of 2018-19. seasonal variation was observed in most elements and concentrations of fe/ca, mn/ca, zn/ca, ni/ca, ba/ca, cu/ca, as/ca, cr/ca, and pb/ca gradually increased from fall to summer. this trend may indicate bioaccumulation of these metals into the coral skeleton through the year, with corresponding removal of some metals (zn, as, br, and pb) from coral tissue. significant correlation among cu, pb, and ni indicate pollution associated with landfills and mining sites, while zn and ni are commonly introduced from wastewater. marine-based pollution from oil spills (pb and ni) and antifouling paint from ship hulls (cu and zn) may also contribute to concentrations in the skeleton. we found that ba, fe, and zn were at least 140% higher and ni was at least 500% higher in the skeleton of a. poculata compared to other species from tropical reefs. a. poculata may have a higher tolerance for metal pollution, making it a potential indicator of pollution in the environment.",
