      "search_text": "wayamba university of sri lanka prof dileepa de croos dileepa samika thanuksha de croos a baseline assessment of hard coral assemblages reveals site-specific degradation and diversity in jaffna, sri lanka coral reefs are vital marine ecosystems, providing critical habitat and coastal protection, yet comprehensive data for northern sri lanka remains limited. this study provides a crucial, updated baseline for hard coral communities in the jaffna peninsula, building upon limited prior studies through detailed surveys at four sites: kareinagar, mandathiv, point pedro, and sakkottei. video transects, georeferenced with a gps-enabled camera, were converted into 100 still frames per transect, and 2500 randomly allocated points were analyzed using coral point counter with extensions (cpce) software to quantify benthic composition. results revealed severe degradation at mandathiv, a major fishing landing site, which exhibited the highest total coral cover (49.12%) but was predominantly dead coral (45.28%). in contrast, point pedro hosted the highest live coral cover (5.12%). kareinagar and sakkottei demonstrated higher relative live coral diversity, accounting for over 90% of the recorded genera, a condition correlated with lower anthropogenic pressure. point pedro and kareinagar hosted the highest generic richness (7 genera each), compared to five at mandathiv and three at sakkottei. statistical analysis of diversity indices (shannon-weiner, simpson's) showed no significant differences between sites but revealed significant differences between specific coral genera. non-metric multidimensional scaling ordination revealed distinct coral assemblages across sites, with community structure at mandathiv negatively correlated with sandy substrate and sakkottei positively correlated with rocky substrate. spearman's rank correlation indicated a negative trend between hard coral cover and both increasing macroalgal cover (rho = -0.167) and sandy substrate (rho = -0.296). these findings deliver an essential contemporary baseline for monitoring and targeted conservation, offering insights into reef resilience under anthropogenic stress that are critical for informing regional management strategies and contributing to global coral conservation science.",
