          "name": "Evelyne Felix",
          "search_text": "evelyne felix environmental drivers of butterflyfish diversity and assemblage structure across coral reef systems in the central philippines integrating remotely operated vehicles for benthic reef monitoring in tambisan waters, sabah, malaysia the philippines, located at the apex of the coral triangle, harbors exceptionally high marine biodiversity, particularly within the visayas region, which has long been considered a hotspot for shorefish diversity. despite this ecological prominence, the region continues to face substantial natural and anthropogenic pressures that threaten coral reef conditions. butterflyfishes (family chaetodontidae), widely used as reef health indicators, do not always exhibit straightforward relationships with high coral cover, highlighting the need to examine broader ecological drivers. this study investigates how environmental factors shape spatial patterns in chaetodontid diversity and assemblage structure across the central philippines. data were collected over a ten-year period (2010–2019) using standard non-destructive fish visual census techniques across 60 sites with triplicate surveys. a total of 36 chaetodontid species were recorded, with the lowest diversity observed in the visayas region and the highest along the eastern philippine coast, consistent with the group’s hypothesized evolutionary origins in the pacific. chaetodon octofasciatus, chelmon rostratus, and chaetodon lunulatus were the most frequently encountered species, occurring at 87%, 67%, and 58% of surveyed sites, respectively. cluster analysis of species composition and abundance revealed three major site groupings, which aligned with discriminant analysis results where 90.6% of predicted sites were correctly classified based on environmental variables. macroalgal cover, fisher density and slope degree emerged as the primary environmental predictors differentiating assemblages. these findings reveal complex spatial patterns in chaetodontid communities and emphasize the influence of both ecological conditions and human pressures on reef-associated butterflyfish populations. globally, coral reefs face significant threats from climate change and other human-induced factors. unconventional methods to monitor them especially in areas where it is not safe due to the potential hazards and other logistical constraints are much needed to understand the impact of these threats. in tambisan waters, recurring crocodile sightings restricts conventional diver-based surveys for assessing coral reef conditions. to address this challenge, we explored the feasibility of utilizing an underwater remotely operated vehicle (rov) using chasing m2 s model in the shallow fringing reefs of tambisan (approximately 2-3m depth) for benthic reef monitoring. the rov was assessed for manoeuvrability, stability, and practicality under varying currents and visibility conditions. guided by suspended transect lines to conduct the photo quadrat transect survey, the rov was equipped with a laser scaler to provide non-intrusive size references, eliminating the need for physical quadrats. preliminary findings indicate that the rov was deployable across fringing reefs along the tambisan coast, although strong currents reduced manoeuvrability and positional stability, occasionally causing current-induced drift. these limitations did not substantially affect image acquisition, and comparable image quality suitable for benthic classification was achieved. however, the image processing workflow still revealed several challenges. turbid sites reduced clarity, requiring extensive close-up imaging to ensure sufficient usable data. over uneven substrates, the laser scaler beams sometimes landed on different elevations, causing slight beam deflection. this issue was mitigated by referencing measurements to the brightest laser point, which corresponded to the original beam position prior to deflection. in cases where laser beams failed to reach the substrate in wide-angle images, wide shots were paired with corresponding close-up images where laser points were visible. overall, rov-based surveys show strong potential for expanding benthic survey coverage, provided that appropriate and workable field adaptations are implemented.",
