      "speaker_details": [
        {
          "name": "Janice Taga",
          "search_text": "tarau janice taga integrating science and traditional ecological knowledge (tek) for an effective community-based coral reef management in fiji a novel approach to identifying climate-resilient reefs in fiji for pacific island nations, the ocean is home, heritage, and a lifeline. coral reef ecosystems lie at the heart of this relationship, providing food security, livelihoods, cultural identity, and coastal protection. yet, these ecosystems and the communities that depend on them face escalating threats from climate change, extreme weather events, and unsustainable land- and sea-based activities. addressing these challenges requires approaches that meaningfully integrate scientific methods with traditional ecological knowledge (tek). when brought together, these knowledge systems strengthen community-based management and ensure practices are sustained across generations.\r\nthis study presents case studies from the i-qoliqoli (customary fishing grounds) of nakorotubu district (ra province) and dama district (bua province), fiji, where science and tek have been blended to support coral reef stewardship. historical datasets and long-standing local observations provide valuable long-term trends for understanding ecological change. between 2024–2025, we surveyed 21 sites in nakorotubu and 16 sites in dama using fisheries monitoring and benthic habitat assessments. tek identified spawning seasons and traditional closure sites, which were integrated with scientific monitoring. preliminary results show coral cover (30–45%) and fish biomass (2000–4000 kg/ha) were significantly higher in tabu areas and reef passages compared to fished areas. effectiveness of tabu areas was influenced by reef proximity to land, exposure to currents, and connectivity.\r\nthese findings demonstrate that co-produced approaches, supported by temporal datasets, can measurably enhance ecological outcomes, sustain cultural practices, and strengthen local capacity to adapt to climate change. by weaving science and tek, communities in fiji are building resilient reef management systems that safeguard both biodiversity and cultural heritage. as climate change intensifies, identifying locations of climate-resilient coral reefs and better managing them is critical to sustaining coastal ecosystems and livelihoods.  this study presents a data-driven approach to identifying climate refugia reefs using <3,680 benthic transects collected between 2009 and 2025 from a range of ngo and government agencies in fiji, stored within the open-source mermaid platform. notably, this is the first study in fiji to apply climate refugia classifications based on coral life history categories and identify sites for a climate refugia strategy according to avoidance, resistance and recovery (arr) benthic typologies. we use field records to validate and extend climate refugia locations identified in a global prioritisation, focusing on coral cover, diversity and life history categories to assess ecological resilience. we present spatial patterns of arr reef systems, including sites showing signatures of high resistance or rapid recovery potential. our findings demonstrate how existing ecological data can be combined with next generation modelling outputs to provide information pathways for national policy development and enable evidence-based adaptation in the pacific island context. these analyses provide the first national-scale evidence to guide climate-smart reef protection in fiji, supporting policy decisions, marine spatial planning, and targeted management of resilient reef areas.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "A novel approach to identifying climate-resilient reefs in Fiji",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "f00a26c9-3786-4104-997c-adbdaa7e0ca4"
            },
            {
              "title": "Integrating Science and Traditional Ecological Knowledge (TEK) for an effective Community-based Coral Reef Management in Fiji",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "15f0a906-88b2-45a0-a452-dff2368870f5"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-01706",
          "attended": true
        },
        {
          "name": "Neelam Bhan",
          "search_text": "neelam nandita bhan from community visioning to conservation action: an integrated approach to coral reef conservation in fiji fiji hosts ~7,500 km² of globally significant coral reef ecosystems, recognised for their diversity and resilience. reef ecosystems such as the great sea reef and vatu-i-ra seascape have shown the capacity to withstand and recover from climate-driven bleaching events and cyclones. coral reefs have supported the livelihoods of indigenous and non-indigenous fijian communities for generations and are the pillar of fiji’s tourism sector. despite their resilience, fiji’s reefs face escalating local threats from unsustainable fishing, pollution, and coastal development. in response, a national initiative is being undertaken to address these challenges through the development and implementation of a national action plan for coral reef conservation (napcrc). this process began with participatory community visioning workshops, which combined traditional ecological knowledge with contemporary scientific approaches to identify threats to reef ecosystems and consolidate the goals and desires of indigenous customary rights holders. through these workshops, customary rights holders articulated their vision of fiji's coral reef ecosystems as healthy and thriving, with emphasis on sustainable fishing practices and community inclusivity. to put this community vision into practice, a participatory design process brought together government agencies, coral reef experts, and community representatives to translate community aspirations into an actionable conservation strategy. the wildlife conservation society (wcs) conducted a spatial prioritisation analysis as part of this process, which involved a three-step approach: gathering data on important areas for biodiversity and communities, mapping human pressures and threats to coral reefs and associated ecosystems, and combining this information in a spatial prioritisation assessment to identify key conservation sites. this analysis identifies high-integrity, climate-resilient reef areas that require conservation, informed by scientific datasets and community knowledge. this work demonstrates how spatial planning tools can bridge indigenous knowledge systems and scientific analysis, translating community visions into geographically specific, evidence-based conservation action.",
          "talk_titles": [
            {
              "title": "From community visioning to conservation action: An integrated approach to coral reef conservation in Fiji",
              "primary": true,
              "talk_id": "f8b3b710-7b95-4735-af33-87042fb882f2"
            }
          ],
          "person_key": "icrs-p-00167",
          "attended": true
        }
      ],
