          "name": "Amy Apprill",
          "search_text": "amy apprill ecological insights into the persistent porites astreoides coral-endozoicomonas bacteria association endozoicomonas bacteria regularly associate with reef-building corals, but the nature of the association and contribution to the coral holobiont functioning and health is still uncertain. caribbean porites astreoides corals are an ideal model system for study of this relationship; they consistently either harbor high (>50%) or low (<5%) relative abundances of endozoicomonas bacteria compared to other members of their bacterial and archaeal microbiome. here, we present the results of field studies examining p. astreoides-endozoicomonas associations over time which employ a quantitative pcr assay specifically for the p. astreoides-endozoicomonas (ec10) associates. in the united states virgin islands, we identified that bleached corals lacking algal symbionts harbored either high or low endozoicomonas association patterns over three reef sites (n = 3 – 11 colonies each of high or low endozoicomonas association per site). we tracked the growth of the colonies using photogrammetry and found that the majority of colonies recovered coloration within six months, but all lost surface area and volume over this time. this  demonstrates quantitatively that endozoicomonas associate with bleached corals capable of recovery, but that corals without endozoicomonas can also recover. secondly, we conducted reciprocal transplant experiments with p. astreoides between shallow (10 m) and mesophotic (40 m) cayman island reefs (n = 10 colonies at each depth and replicated at two sites). our results showed that mesophotic colonies gain endozoicomonas when moved to the shallow reef, and that shallow colonies lose endozoicomonas when moved to the mesophotic reef. these ecological observations suggest that algal symbiont load or bleaching level (health) is not needed to maintain endozoicomonas association, and that mesophotic-related reef or host features likely prohibit endoizoicomonas from residing within p. astreoides.",
