      "search_text": "woods hole oceanographic institution amy apprill mallory kastner amy apprill ecological insights into the persistent porites astreoides coral-endozoicomonas bacteria association endozoicomonas bacteria regularly associate with reef-building corals, but the nature of the association and contribution to the coral holobiont functioning and health is still uncertain. caribbean porites astreoides corals are an ideal model system for study of this relationship; they consistently either harbor high (>50%) or low (<5%) relative abundances of endozoicomonas bacteria compared to other members of their bacterial and archaeal microbiome. here, we present the results of field studies examining p. astreoides-endozoicomonas associations over time which employ a quantitative pcr assay specifically for the p. astreoides-endozoicomonas (ec10) associates. in the united states virgin islands, we identified that bleached corals lacking algal symbionts harbored either high or low endozoicomonas association patterns over three reef sites (n = 3 – 11 colonies each of high or low endozoicomonas association per site). we tracked the growth of the colonies using photogrammetry and found that the majority of colonies recovered coloration within six months, but all lost surface area and volume over this time. this  demonstrates quantitatively that endozoicomonas associate with bleached corals capable of recovery, but that corals without endozoicomonas can also recover. secondly, we conducted reciprocal transplant experiments with p. astreoides between shallow (10 m) and mesophotic (40 m) cayman island reefs (n = 10 colonies at each depth and replicated at two sites). our results showed that mesophotic colonies gain endozoicomonas when moved to the shallow reef, and that shallow colonies lose endozoicomonas when moved to the mesophotic reef. these ecological observations suggest that algal symbiont load or bleaching level (health) is not needed to maintain endozoicomonas association, and that mesophotic-related reef or host features likely prohibit endoizoicomonas from residing within p. astreoides. mallory kastner how do sponge metabolites influence coral reef water microbes? seawater microbes play a central role in sustaining coral reef ecosystems by cycling reef nutrients, such as dissolved organic carbon (doc). sponges are ubiquitous in these ecosystems, and actively contribute to the cycling of doc by releasing transformed carbon into seawater as diverse products of metabolism ('metabolites'). the influence of sponge activity on structuring seawater microorganisms (‘picoplankton’) has only recently garnered attention. here, we examined how metabolites exuded by sponges influence the growth and composition of coral reef water microorganisms using dna stable isotope probing (dna-sip). three common caribbean sponges, xestospongia muta, niphates digitalis and verongula rigida, were incubated with 13-c labeled glucose and bicarbonate in seawater to obtain isotopically-labeled sponge metabolites. picoplankton collected from the same site were subsequently incubated with isotopically-labeled sponge metabolites. picoplankton growth during incubation was quantified by flow cytometry and show stimulated growth of non-pigmented cells, presumably heterotrophic bacteria and archaea, in the presence of sponge metabolites. the picoplankton community composition was examined using 16s rrna gene sequencing on both the bulk community and dna-sip density-separated fractions. after incubation with the sponge metabolites, we observed a significant shift in the picoplankton bulk community towards taxa that are typically fast growing and can utilize complex sources of organic carbon (often referred to as copiotrophs). sponge exudates contained significantly higher levels of ammonium and phosphate than seawater, demonstrating their nutrient contribution to the reef. future results will include sponge metabolite characterization and 16s rrna gene sequencing of dna-sip density-separated fractions to supplement current correlative results with direct evidence of labeled metabolite uptake by picoplankton. our study is building knowledge about the role of sponges on coral reef microbial and chemical dynamics, which may inform restoration practices to better support stable reef microbiota.",
