          "name": "Ikuko Yuyama",
          "search_text": "ikuko yuyama detection of digested symbiotic algae in corals corals establish symbiotic relationships only with certain genera of the family symbiodiniaceae and can sometimes break these associations, raising the possibility that they may digest the symbionts within their tissue.  to assess digestive activity toward symbionts in corals, we used gomori’s　staining to visualize the activity of acid phosphatase (acpase), a lysosomal enzyme, in corals during the early stages of symbiosis and under bleaching conditions.　firstly, juvenile polyps of acropora tenuis were inoculated with four culture strains of symbiodiniaceae belonging to genus breviolum (ccmp2459), cladocopium (ccmp2466), durusdinium (ccmp2556), and fugacium (ccmp2468). after inoculation, the cell numbers of cladocopium and durusdinium increased in the host corals, whereas those of breviolum and fugacium did not. inoculated corals subjected to gomori’s staining occasionally showed acid phosphatase activity in symbiotic breviolum, cladocopium, and durusdinium. exposure to dcmu during early siymbiosis caused fewer symbiont cells and higher acpase activity signals as compared to dcmu-untreated corals. furthermore, bleached corals under light and heat stress showed strong acpase signals around remaining colorless symbionts in the corals. these acpase signals were also observed under electron microscopy, confirming their presence around disrupted symbionts. although our results do not clearly demonstrate whether corals selectively digest specific symbiodiniaceae during early symbiosis, they suggest that corals digest algae that have lost photosynthetic activity via acpase-mediated processes.",
