      "search_text": "zhejiang university minjie hu minjie hu stress-induced transcriptional cascades in xenia sp. revealed by single-cell rna sequencing coral bleaching–the stress-induced collapse of symbiosis between corals and their dinoflagellate algae–poses an escalating threat to reef ecosystems worldwide¹. despite its ecological importance, the cellular mechanisms driving bleaching remain incompletely understood. previously, we established xenia sp. as a model system for studying coral endosymbiosis and developed an rnai-based approach to investigate lepin function during the initiation of new symbiotic relationships. here, using a menthol-induced bleaching model combined with single-cell rna sequencing at early and late stages of bleaching, we characterize the transcriptional dynamics of coral cells under stress. we show that early in the bleaching response, coral cells downregulate adhesion-related complexes, priming themself for detachment. this precedes a coordinated upregulation of membrane-trafficking pathways and lysosomal digestion machinery at later stress stage. together, our results provide a high-resolution landscape of cell-type-specific transcriptional changes during bleaching, establishing a foundation for deeper molecular insight into the mechanisms underlying coral bleaching.",
